<main id="content">
<h1 class="am-band"><span class="am-band-t">Publishing &amp; Deployment</span><span class="am-band-s">12 chapters &middot; 120 topics &middot; 388 code samples &middot; works offline</span></h1>

  <section class="chapter">
<h2 id="orientation" data-toc>Welcome â€” How to Use This Packaging & Publishing Guide</h2>
    <div class="ch-toc"><button type="button" class="ch-toc-head" aria-expanded="true"><span class="arrow">â–¸</span>Contents &middot; Welcome â€” How to Use This Packaging & Publishing Guide</button><div class="ch-toc-list"><a href="#orientation-assume">What this guide covers</a><a href="#orientation-scope">The ecosystems covered</a><a href="#orientation-map">How the chapters fit together</a><a href="#orientation-law">The legal ground you stand on</a></div></div>
    <p class="topic-desc">Every ecosystem has a registry, and the differences between them are mostly about trust, immutability and who can publish. Once you know those three questions, the per-ecosystem mechanics in the second half of this guide are variations on a theme rather than forty unrelated procedures.</p>
    <h3 id="orientation-assume" data-toc>What this guide covers</h3>
    <h4 id="orientation-assume-outcome">What you will be able to do</h4>
    <p>By the end of this guide you will be able to take a finished artefact â€” a library, a plugin, a container image, a CLI tool â€” and publish it to the right registry with the right metadata, the right credentials and the right security posture. You will understand what a version number promises, what a lockfile guarantees, what a signature proves, and what it does not. You will know the difference between a registry and a marketplace, and when to use a private registry instead of a public one.</p>
    <p>The guide is organised in three layers. The first three chapters are the shared foundations: versioning, the artefact, and supply-chain security. These apply regardless of ecosystem. The middle chapters are per-ecosystem mechanics: npm, PyPI, Maven, crates.io, Docker Hub, the extension marketplaces. The final chapters are the operational parts: deprecation, yanking, private registries, and the release checklist.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the three layers, and where you are
  layer 1: foundations     ch1 versioning, ch2 artefact, ch3 security
  layer 2: ecosystems      ch4 npm/jsr, ch5 python/php/ruby, ch6 jvm/dotnet/rust/go
                        ch7 containers, ch8 marketplaces
  layer 3: operations      ch9 private registries, ch10 alternative channels,
                        ch11 deprecation and maintenance

// if you only have time for one chapter, read ch1.
// if you only have time for two, read ch1 and ch3.
// everything else is mechanics you can look up when you need it.</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="orientation-assume-omissions">What this guide deliberately omits</h4>
    <p>This guide does not cover building a registry from scratch, the internal implementation of any package manager, or the legal details of any specific jurisdiction. It does not replace the official documentation of npm, PyPI, Maven or any other registry â€” it tells you what to look for and what questions to ask. For a deep dive on one ecosystem, the relevant guide in this library is the place to go: <code>php.html</code> has a full Composer chapter, <code>docker.html</code> covers containers, and <code>terraform.html</code> covers the Terraform registry model.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// what is NOT in this guide, and where to find it
  building a registry     the registry's own documentation, not this guide
  package manager internals  the source code of npm, pip, cargo, etc.
  legal advice            a lawyer. this guide lists questions, not answers.
  one ecosystem deeply    the per-technology guides in this library

// what IS in this guide
  the shared foundations   versioning, artefacts, signing, provenance
  per-ecosystem mechanics  what is different, what is the same
  the operational parts    deprecation, yanking, private registries</code></pre></div>
    <p>You have written something other people could use â€” a library, a plugin, a theme, a container, a CLI tool â€” and you want to put it somewhere discoverable and installable. This guide covers the shared foundations, then every major ecosystem, then the parts nobody writes down.</p>
    <p>It does <strong>not</strong> cover building a registry of your own, or the internal mechanics of any one package manager. For a deep dive on one ecosystem, its own guide in this library is the place to go â€” Composer has a full chapter in <code>php.html</code>, and the npm, Docker and Terraform material has its own guides.</p>
    <h3 id="orientation-scope" data-toc>The ecosystems covered</h3>
    <h4 id="orientation-scope-registries">The language package registries</h4>
    <p>Every language ecosystem has a canonical registry, and they all share the same basic shape: a name, a version, a tarball, and a manifest. The differences are in the details â€” how versions resolve, what the manifest contains, whether there is a lockfile, and how credentials work. This guide covers npm, PyPI, Packagist, NuGet, RubyGems, Maven Central, crates.io, Hex.pm, the Go module proxy, and Pub.dev.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the language registries, and what makes each distinctive
  npm           JavaScript, TypeScript    the largest, most attacked
  PyPI          Python                 the sdist + wheel duality
  Packagist     PHP (Composer)         the tag-to-package sync model
  NuGet         .NET, C#               the "at least" version semantics
  RubyGems      Ruby                   the simplest model in the set
  Maven Central Java, Kotlin, Scala     the namespace verification
  crates.io     Rust                   the crate-reservation model
  Hex.pm        Elixir, Erlang         the release-vs-package distinction
  Go modules    Go                     the proxy + MVS model
  Pub.dev       Dart, Flutter           the scoring gate</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="orientation-scope-containers">Container and infrastructure registries</h4>
    <p>Container registries follow the OCI specification, which standardises the manifest format, the layer model, and the distribution protocol. Docker Hub, GitHub Container Registry, GitLab, Quay, ECR, ACR and GAR all implement OCI, so the mechanics are similar once you understand the spec. The differences are in rate limits, authentication, scanning, and signing â€” and in how each platform integrates with its CI ecosystem.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the container registries, and the axis they differ on
  Docker Hub        the default, the rate limits, the scanning
  GHCR               free for public, tight GitHub integration
  GitLab registry   built into the CI/CD platform
  Quay              Red Hat, the Clair scanner
  ECR               AWS, the IAM integration
  ACR               Azure, the AAD integration
  GAR               Google Cloud, the IAM integration

// they all speak OCI. the differences are:
//   rate limits, authentication, scanning, signing, CI integration</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="orientation-scope-marketplaces">Extension and tool marketplaces</h4>
    <p>Marketplaces are different from registries in one important way: they have a review process. A registry accepts anything that meets the mechanical requirements; a marketplace has a human or automated reviewer who checks for quality, safety and policy compliance. This means the listing â€” the name, the description, the screenshots, the categories â€” matters as much as the artefact. The VS Code Marketplace, the Chrome Web Store, the JetBrains Marketplace, the Figma Community, the Slack App Directory, the Shopify App Store, and the Atlassian Marketplace all have different review criteria and different listing requirements.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the marketplaces, and what they review
  VS Code Marketplace      extensions     automated + manual review
  Chrome Web Store         extensions     automated + manual review
  JetBrains Marketplace    plugins        automated review
  Figma Community          libraries      community curation
  Slack App Directory      apps           OAuth + permissions review
  Shopify App Store        apps           app review + listing quality
  Atlassian Marketplace    plugins        manual review
  Homebrew / Scoop        CLI tools      formula / manifest review
  Home Assistant HACS      integrations   repository-based curation</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="orientation-scope-alternatives">The non-registry channels</h4>
    <p>Not everything goes through a registry. Direct download from your own site, a Git repository as the distribution channel, and a private registry you run yourself are all legitimate alternatives, each with different trade-offs. A registry gives you discovery, versioning and dependency resolution; a direct download gives you control and simplicity. A private registry gives you internal distribution without the public commitment.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the non-registry channels
  direct download   your own site, your own CDN, your own version checking
  git repository    `npm install github:user/repo`, `pip install git+https://...`
  private registry  Verdaccio, devpi, Artifactory, Nexus, Harbor

// when to use each:
//   direct download     no dependency resolution needed, simple artefact
//   git repository      internal tools, pre-release sharing
//   private registry     internal packages, compliance requirements</code></pre></div>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// language package registries
  npm         JavaScript, TypeScript, CSS, fonts, templates
  PyPI        Python
  Packagist   PHP (Composer)
  NuGet       .NET, C#, F#, VB
  RubyGems    Ruby
  Maven Central   Java, Kotlin, Scala, Groovy
  crates.io   Rust
  Hex.pm      Elixir and Erlang
  Go modules  proxied through a module proxy
  Pub.dev      Dart and Flutter

// container and infrastructure
  Docker Hub        OCI images, public and private
  GitHub Container Registry
  GitLab, Quay, ECR, ACR, GAR

// extension and marketplace ecosystems
  WordPress.org plugin and theme directory
  VS Code Marketplace      extensions
  Chrome Web Store         extensions
  JetBrains Marketplace    plugins
  Figma Community          libraries, templates, icons
  CodeCanyon / Envato     templates and commercial code
  Slack App Directory
  Shopify App Store
  Atlassian Marketplace
  Homebrew, Scoop, Chocolatey   CLI tool distribution
  Home Assistant HACS

// and the alternative channels that skip a registry entirely
  direct download from your own site
  a Git repository
  a private registry you run yourself</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="orientation-map" data-toc>How the chapters fit together</h3>
    <h4 id="orientation-map-structure">The three-layer structure</h4>
    <p>The guide is organised in three conceptual layers, and understanding the structure helps you know where to look when you have a specific problem. The foundations layer â€” versioning, the artefact, and supply-chain security â€” applies to every ecosystem. If you are publishing a Python package, you still need to understand semantic versioning and lockfiles. If you are publishing a Docker image, you still need to understand signing and provenance. The ecosystem layer is where the mechanics differ: the manifest format, the credentials, the file layout, the release process. The operations layer is what happens after you publish: deprecation, yanking, private registries, and the release checklist.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the three layers, and the questions each answers
  foundations    WHAT does a version promise?
                 WHAT is in the artefact?
                 WHAT does a signature prove?
  ecosystems     HOW do I publish to npm / PyPI / Maven / ...?
                 WHAT does the manifest look like?
                 HOW do credentials and trusted publishing work?
  operations     HOW do I deprecate a version?
                 HOW do I respond to a compromise?
                 WHAT is the release checklist?</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="orientation-map-reading">How to read a chapter</h4>
    <p>Each chapter follows the same pattern: an orientation paragraph explaining why the chapter matters, then a series of sections each covering one concept or mechanism. Each section has a code block that shows the actual commands or configuration, and most chapters end with exercises. The code blocks are the heart of the guide â€” they are complete and runnable, not fragments. You should be able to copy a block, adapt the names, and run it. The prose around them explains the reasoning and the gotchas that the code alone cannot convey.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the anatomy of a section
  <h3>Section title</h3>
  <p class="topic-desc">Why this section matters, in one sentence.</p>
  <h4>Sub-point</h4>
  <p>Dense prose explaining the mechanism, the gotcha, or the decision.</p>
  <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// a complete, runnable example</code></pre></div>
  <div class="tip|warn|note">A callout where it genuinely helps.</div>

// the pattern is always the same:
//   concept â†’ prose â†’ code â†’ callout</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>1â€“3 â€” the shared foundations.</strong> Versioning, the shape of a published artefact, and the build that must be reproducible.</li>
      <li><strong>4â€“6 â€” the trust layer.</strong> Signing, provenance, and supply-chain security. These are the chapters that distinguish a mature publisher.</li>
      <li><strong>7â€“14 â€” the ecosystems.</strong> One chapter per family: JavaScript, Python, PHP, .NET, Java, Rust, containers, and the rest in a combined chapter.</li>
      <li><strong>15â€“16 â€” marketplaces.</strong> The extension ecosystems, where review and merchandising matter as much as the technical mechanism.</li>
      <li><strong>17â€“19 â€” going live.</strong> Deprecating, yanking, and the operational checklist.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 id="orientation-law" data-toc>The legal ground you stand on</h3>
    <h4 id="orientation-law-licence">Licence, the field that decides everything</h4>
    <p>Every registry demands a licence, and the licence you choose has consequences that reach far beyond your own project. A permissive licence â€” MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD â€” allows anyone to use, modify and distribute your code, including in proprietary products. Apache-2.0 adds an explicit patent grant, which is why most companies prefer it over MIT for internal libraries. The GPL family forces the same licence on anything that links to your code, which is a business decision, not a technical one. Choosing a licence is not a formality: it determines who can use your work and under what terms.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the licence decision, in one block
  MIT           permissive, simple, the default for most open source
  Apache-2.0    permissive + patent grant, the corporate favourite
  BSD-2/3       permissive, slightly different attribution requirements
  GPL-3.0       copyleft, forces the same licence on derivatives
  LGPL-3.0      weaker copyleft, linking is allowed
  AGPL-3.0      strong copyleft, network use counts as distribution
  MPL-2.0       file-level copyleft, a middle ground
  Unlicense     public domain dedication, no conditions

// the questions to answer:
//   1. do I allow commercial use?        (all of the above do)
//   2. do I require attribution?         (all of the above do)
//   3. do I allow proprietary use?       (permissive: yes, copyleft: no)
//     4. do I have patent concerns?       (Apache-2.0 has an explicit grant)</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="orientation-law-trademark">Trademarks and names</h4>
    <p>A licence grants rights to the code. It does not grant rights to the name. Publishing a package called "Acme Logger" to npm gives you no right to the Acme trademark, and squatting on a popular package name is both bad practice and occasionally actionable. The namespace is part of your brand, and the legal ground for a name is trademark law, not copyright. Before you publish, check that the name is not trademarked in your jurisdiction, and consider registering it if the project is significant.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the name decision, and the checks to run
  1. search the registry        is the name taken?
  2. search the trademark database   is it registered?
  3. search the web              is it a product, a company, a brand?
  4. check the scope             is it taken in a related namespace?

// the risk of NOT checking:
//   a cease-and-desist after you have users
//   a forced rename that breaks every install
//   a package transfer that orphan your users

// the cost of checking:
//   an hour, once, before you publish</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="orientation-law-thirdparty">Third-party licences and attribution</h4>
    <p>Your artefact bundles or depends on other people's work, and each dependency carries its own licence. MIT-compatible dependencies are fine under a permissive licence. An AGPL dependency makes your package AGPL, because the copyleft propagates through the dependency graph. Generate an attribution file â€” <code>NOTICE</code>, <code>THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES</code>, or a tool-generated equivalent â€” and keep it in the artefact. This is not optional: most licences require attribution, and a missing attribution is a violation even if the use is otherwise permitted.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the dependency licence audit, in one script
  $ npm install -g license-checker
  $ license-checker --summary --production
  $ license-checker --json > licenses.json

// the questions the audit answers:
//   what licences are in my dependency tree?
//   are any of them copyleft?
//   are any of them incompatible with my licence?
//   what attribution do I need to include?

// the tools:
//   license-checker     npm
//   cargo-deny           Rust
//   pip-licenses         Python
//   gem-licenses         Ruby
//   license-maven-plugin Java</code></pre></div>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// BEFORE YOU PUBLISH, SETTLE THESE. They are not code problems.

// 1. LICENCE. Every registry demands one, and a permissive licence
//    on your library can impose terms on everything built with it.
//    MIT, Apache-2.0 and BSD are the common permissive choices.
//    Apache-2.0 adds an explicit patent grant, which is why most
//    companies prefer it over MIT for internal libraries.
//    GPL forces the same licence on anything linking to it, which is
//    a business decision, not a technical one.

// 2. TRADEMARKS. A licence grants rights to the CODE. It does not grant
//    rights to the NAME. Publishing "Acme Logger" to npm gives you no
//    right to the Acme trademark, and squatting on a popular package
//    name is both bad practice and occasionally actionable.

// 3. THIRD-PARTY LICENCES. Your artefact bundles or depends on other
//    people's work. MIT-compatible dependencies are fine under a
//    permissive licence. An AGPL dependency makes your package AGPL.
//    Generate an attribution file and keep it in the artefact.

// 4. THE CONTRIBUTOR AGREEMENT. Who owns contributions? A CLA assigns
//    them, which is what most companies want, and some registries
//    require one. Without a CLA you are relying on the contributor
//    licence in your own LICENSE file, which many contributors accept
//    but not all companies will allow their staff to sign.

// 5. PERSONAL DATA. A package that collects telemetry needs a lawful
//    basis and a privacy policy. Some registries (notably VS Code and
//    the Chrome Web Store) require an explicit privacy policy URL in
//    the listing, and will reject a package that phones home without
//    disclosing it.

// 6. EXPORT AND SANCTIONS. Cryptographic software is subject to export
//    control in several jurisdictions. If your package contains crypto,
//    check before publishing. This is rare but it is not a formality.

// This chapter is not legal advice. It is the list of questions to take
// to someone who can answer them for your jurisdiction.</code></pre></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
<h2 id="ch1" data-toc>1 Â· Versioning, and How Versions Resolve</h2>
    <div class="ch-toc"><button type="button" class="ch-toc-head" aria-expanded="true"><span class="arrow">â–¸</span>Contents &middot; 1 Â· Versioning, and How Versions Resolve</button><div class="ch-toc-list"><a href="#ch1-semver">Semantic versioning, precisely</a><a href="#ch1-prerelease">Pre-release versions</a><a href="#ch1-ranges">Version ranges, per ecosystem</a><a href="#ch1-lockfile">Lockfiles and the reproducibility question</a><a href="#ch1-pep440">Build metadata, and why it is dangerous</a><a href="#ch1-calver">CalVer and why some projects use it</a><a href="#ch1-when-major">Deciding what forces a major bump</a><a href="#ch1-deprecation">Deprecating and yanking</a></div></div>
    <p class="topic-desc">A version number is the only thing a registry and a consumer agree on. Getting the rules wrong means a release breaks installs for people you will never hear from.</p>
    <h3 id="ch1-semver" data-toc>Semantic versioning, precisely</h3>
    <h4 id="ch1-semver-contract">The public API is a contract</h4>
    <p>Semantic versioning is a promise about the public API, and the public API is anything a consumer can import, call, subclass, or read. If it is not in your documentation, delete it or document it. "Private by convention" is not a thing: Python has no private, JavaScript has no private without a convention, and Go has unexported but the package boundary is the contract. The test is simple: a consumer who follows your documented upgrade path and changes nothing but their version constraint should still work. If they must change their code, it was a MAJOR release.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the public API surface, mechanically
  // TypeScript: the emitted .d.ts IS the public API
  $ npx tsc --declaration --emitDeclarationOnly
  // compare two versions
  $ npx dts-bundle --lib --name pkg-a --main dist/index.d.ts
  $ diff old.d.ts new.d.ts

  // Python: the exported names
  $ python -c "import pkg; print([n for n in dir(pkg) if not n.startswith('_')])"

  // Rust: the public items
  $ cargo doc --no-deps --document-private-items
  // the public API is everything not marked #[doc(hidden)]

  // Go: the exported identifiers
  $ go doc ./...</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-semver-breaking">What counts as breaking</h4>
    <p>Removing is major. Rename is major. Signature change is major. Changing a parameter type, adding a required parameter, changing a return type, removing an export, narrowing an accepted type â€” all major. What is not major, even though it feels like it: adding a new optional parameter with a default, adding a new export, relaxing a validation rule to accept more, widening an accepted type, improving performance, changing the output of a REPL or a log, and fixing a bug that violated the documented contract. The SemVer FAQ explicitly says a bug fix is not necessarily major, even if some consumers depended on the buggy behaviour.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the breaking-change decision tree
  does the change remove a public symbol?           -> MAJOR
  does the change rename a public symbol?           -> MAJOR
  does the change alter a function signature?       -> MAJOR
  does the change narrow an accepted type?          -> MAJOR
  does the change add a required parameter?         -> MAJOR
  does the change alter a return type?              -> MAJOR
  does the change add an optional parameter?        -> minor
  does the change add a new export?                 -> minor
  does the change widen an accepted type?           -> minor
  does the change relax validation?                 -> minor
  does the change fix a documented bug?             -> patch
  does the change improve performance?              -> patch
  does the change refactor internals only?          -> patch

// the asymmetry: a needless major costs users one edit.
// a needless minor breaks them silently.</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-semver-zero-x">The 0.x escape hatch</h4>
    <p>The <code>0.x.y</code> convention means "no stability promise". Within <code>0.y.z</code>, MINOR is breaking and PATCH is not. Many ecosystems treat <code>0.x</code> as pre-1.0, so <code>^0.2.0</code> allows <code>0.2.x</code> but not <code>0.3.0</code>. Use it honestly: a <code>0.x</code> package that breaks in a patch release is abusing the convention. The 0.x line is where you iterate freely, but it is also where you build the trust that lets consumers upgrade to 1.0 without fear.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// 0.x in practice
  // you are on 0.4.2. the rules:
  //   0.4.3   a fix. safe to take.
  //   0.5.0   a new feature. might break. read the changelog.
  //   0.4.0   a breaking change. this is ALLOWED in 0.x.

  // the consumer's perspective:
  ^0.4.2    resolves to >=0.4.2 <0.5.0   // minor is pinned
  ^0.0.3    resolves to >=0.0.3 <0.0.4   // patch is pinned

  // the promise you make at 1.0:
  //   1.0.0 is the first version with a stability promise.
  //   from here, MINOR is non-breaking and MAJOR is breaking.
  //   the 0.x line was the draft. 1.0 is the commitment.</code></pre></div>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH
//   MAJOR  incompatible API changes. Consumers must change their code.
//   MINOR  backwards-compatible additions.
//   PATCH  backwards-compatible fixes.

// THE ACTUAL SEMVER RULES, which are stricter than most people assume

// 1. THE PUBLIC API IS A CONTRACT
//    anything a consumer can import, call, subclass, or read is public.
//    if it is not in your documentation, delete it or document it.
//    "private by convention" is not a thing: Python has no private,
//    JavaScript has no private without a convention, and Go has
//    unexported but the package boundary is the contract.

// 2. REMOVING IS MAJOR. RENAME IS MAJOR. SIGNATURE CHANGE IS MAJOR.
//    changing a parameter type, adding a required parameter, changing
//    a return type, removing an export, narrowing an accepted type.

// 3. WHAT IS NOT MAJOR, even though it feels like it
//    - adding a new optional parameter with a default
//    - adding a new export, method, class or field
//    - relaxing a validation rule to accept more
//    - widening an accepted type, e.g. string to string | number
//    - improving performance
//    - changing the output of a REPL or a log
//    - fixing a bug that violated the documented contract
//      (this is the one people argue about, and SemVer's own FAQ says
//       it is NOT necessarily major)

// 4. THE 0.x ESCAPE HATCH
//    0.x.y means "no stability promise". Within 0.y.z, MINOR is
//    breaking and PATCH is not. Many ecosystems treat 0.x as
//    pre-1.0, so ^0.2.0 allows 0.2.x but not 0.3.0.
//    Use it honestly. A 0.x package that breaks in a patch release is
//    abusing the convention.

// 5. INTERNAL REFACTORING IS FREE
//    if no public surface changes, it is a patch, even if every line
//    changed. SemVer is about the interface, not the diff.

// THE TEST, in one line
//   a consumer who follows your documented upgrade path and changes
//   nothing but their version constraint should still work.
//   if they must change their code, it was a MAJOR release.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch1-prerelease" data-toc>Pre-release versions</h3>
    <h4 id="ch1-prerelease-ordering">The precedence rules</h4>
    <p>Pre-release versions sort before the same version without a pre-release tag. <code>1.0.0-alpha</code> is less than <code>1.0.0</code>. The ordering is: alpha &lt; beta &lt; rc. Numeric identifiers compare numerically, so <code>rc.2</code> &lt; <code>rc.10</code>. Alphanumeric identifiers compare lexically. A longer pre-release with more dot-separated parts is higher: <code>alpha.1</code> &lt; <code>alpha.1.1</code>. These rules are part of the SemVer spec and are implemented consistently across ecosystems that follow SemVer.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the pre-release ordering, in full
  1.0.0-alpha    &lt;  1.0.0-alpha.1  &lt;  1.0.0-alpha.2  &lt;  1.0.0-beta
  1.0.0-beta     &lt;  1.0.0-rc.1      &lt;  1.0.0-rc.2      &lt;  1.0.0
  1.0.0          &lt;  1.0.1           &lt;  1.0.2           &lt;  1.1.0

  // the rules:
  //   a pre-release sorts BEFORE the same version without one
  //   alpha &lt; beta &lt; rc
  //   numeric identifiers compare numerically: rc.2 &lt; rc.10
  //   alphanumeric identifiers compare lexically
  //   more dot-separated parts is higher: alpha.1 &lt; alpha.1.1</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-prerelease-tag">Never publish a pre-release to the default tag</h4>
    <p>The single most common pre-release mistake is publishing to the default tag. <code>npm install pkg</code> resolves to the <code>latest</code> dist-tag, so if you publish <code>1.5.0-beta.1</code> without <code>--tag beta</code>, every install in the world gets the beta. The fix is mechanical: always pass <code>--tag</code> for pre-releases, and add a CI check that fails if a prerelease version is published without the tag. PyPI has no dist-tags, so the equivalent is uploading to TestPyPI first. Maven uses a separate SNAPSHOT repository.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the CI check that prevents the disaster
  - name: prerelease must have a tag
    run: |
      TAG=$(node -p "require('./package.json').version")
      if [[ "$TAG" == *-* ]] &amp;&amp; [ "${PUBLISH_TAG}" != "beta" ]; then
        echo "prerelease published without --tag beta"
        exit 1
      fi

  // the safe pattern:
  $ npm version prerelease --preid=beta   # 1.4.2 -> 1.4.3-beta.0
  $ npm publish --tag beta                # never latest
  $ npm dist-tag add pkg@1.4.2 latest     # keep latest at the stable</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-prerelease-promotion">Promoting a pre-release properly</h4>
    <p>You cannot re-tag a published version. <code>1.0.0-rc.1</code> is not <code>1.0.0</code>. When the release candidate is stable, publish <code>1.0.0</code> as a proper release. If the rc was close enough to final that the version number is the same, that is the honest thing to do: <code>1.0.0-rc.1</code> then <code>1.0.0</code>. The alternative â€” repointing a dist-tag from the rc to a new "final" build â€” breaks the immutability guarantee that makes versions meaningful.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the promotion flow
  // 1. publish the rc
  $ npm version prerelease --preid=rc   # 1.0.0 -> 1.0.0-rc.0
  $ npm publish --tag next              // rc goes to next, not latest

  // 2. test the rc
  $ npm install pkg@next

  // 3. publish the final
  $ npm version prepatch               // 1.0.0-rc.0 -> 1.0.0
  $ npm publish                        // 1.0.0 goes to latest

  // 4. clean up the next tag
  $ npm dist-tag rm pkg next
  // or leave it pointing at the final for the next cycle</code></pre></div>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the ordering, which is not intuitive
  1.0.0-alpha    &lt;  1.0.0-alpha.1  &lt;  1.0.0-alpha.2  &lt;  1.0.0-beta
  1.0.0-beta     &lt;  1.0.0-rc.1      &lt;  1.0.0-rc.2      &lt;  1.0.0
  1.0.0          &lt;  1.0.1           &lt;  1.0.2           &lt;  1.1.0

// precedence rules
  - a version WITH a pre-release tag sorts BEFORE the same version without
    1.0.0-beta  &lt;  1.0.0
  - alpha &lt; beta &lt; rc
  - numeric identifiers compare numerically: rc.2 &lt; rc.10
  - alphanumeric identifiers compare lexically: rc.alpha &lt; rc.beta
  - a longer pre-release with more dot-separated parts is HIGHER
    alpha.1 &lt; alpha.1.1

// THE THREE THINGS THAT GO WRONG

// 1. PUBLISHING A PRE-RELEASE TO THE DEFAULT TAG
    npm publish --tag beta        1.5.0-beta.1
    // otherwise `npm install pkg` picks it up, because the default tag
    // is what everyone gets. This has broken production systems.
//    PyPI:  upload to the TestPyPI index, or use a pre-release suffix
//    Maven:  a SNAPSHOT repository

// 2. PROMOTING A PRE-RELEASE BY RE-TAGGING
    // 1.0.0-rc.1 is not 1.0.0. You cannot re-tag a published version.
//    Publish 1.0.0 properly. If rc.1 is close enough to final, that is
//    the honest thing to do: 1.0.0-rc.1 then 1.0.0.

// 3. A PRE-RELEASE THAT DOES NOT SORT BELOW YOUR CURRENT STABLE
    // you are on 1.4.0 stable, then release 1.5.0-beta. A consumer with
//    ^1.4.0 will NOT pick it up, which is correct. A consumer with ^1.0.0
//    WILL, because ^1.0.0 covers 1.5.0-beta. This surprises people.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch1-ranges" data-toc>Version ranges, per ecosystem</h3>
    <h4 id="ch1-ranges-npm">npm and node-semver</h4>
    <p>npm uses the <code>node-semver</code> library, which implements the caret (<code>^</code>) and tilde (<code>~</code>) operators. The caret allows changes that do not modify the left-most non-zero digit: <code>^1.2.3</code> is <code>&gt;=1.2.3 &lt;2.0.0</code>, but <code>^0.2.3</code> is <code>&gt;=0.2.3 &lt;0.3.0</code> and <code>^0.0.3</code> is <code>&gt;=0.0.3 &lt;0.0.4</code>. The tilde is more conservative: <code>~1.2.3</code> is <code>&gt;=1.2.3 &lt;1.3.0</code>. The explicit range <code>&gt;=1.2.3 &lt;2.0.0</code> is always the clearest option and means the same thing in every ecosystem.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// npm ranges, precisely
  ^1.2.3        &gt;=1.2.3 &lt;2.0.0    caret: same major
  ~1.2.3        &gt;=1.2.3 &lt;1.3.0    tilde: same minor
  1.2.3         exactly
  1.2.x         &gt;=1.2.0 &lt;1.3.0
  1.x           &gt;=1.0.0 &lt;2.0.0
  &gt;=1.2.3 &lt;2.0.0   explicit, clearest
  *             anything, including 2.0.0
  latest        a dist-tag, not a range

  // the 0.x trap:
  ^0.2.3        &gt;=0.2.3 &lt;0.3.0    minor is pinned
  ^0.0.3        &gt;=0.0.3 &lt;0.0.4    patch is pinned</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-ranges-python">PyPI and PEP 440</h4>
    <p>Python's PEP 440 defines its own range syntax, which is similar to npm's but not identical. The compatible release operator <code>~=</code> is the closest to a caret: <code>~=1.2.3</code> means <code>&gt;=1.2.3 &lt;1.3.0</code>. The exact match <code>==1.2.3</code> is the only way to pin precisely. Poetry adds a caret operator <code>^1.2.3</code> that matches npm's semantics, but this is a Poetry extension, not a PEP 440 standard. The explicit form <code>&gt;=1.2,&lt;2.0</code> is always valid and always clear.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// PyPI ranges, precisely
  ==1.2.3                exact
  ~=1.2.3                &gt;=1.2.3 &lt;1.3.0   compatible release
  ~=1.2                  &gt;=1.2   &lt;2.0
  &gt;=1.2,&lt;2.0          explicit, clearest
  ^1.2.3                 (only in poetry) &gt;=1.2.3 &lt;2.0.0
  1.2.*                  wildcard minor

  // the difference from npm:
  //   ~= is the compatible-release operator, not a caret
  //   == is the exact match, not a bare version
  //   poetry's ^ is an extension, not a standard</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-ranges-maven">Maven, the least intuitive</h4>
    <p>Maven's version ranges use interval notation, which is the least intuitive syntax in any ecosystem. Soft bounds â€” <code>[1.2,2.0)</code> â€” are the default and mean <code>&gt;=1.2 &lt;2.0</code>. Hard bounds â€” <code>[1.2,1.3]</code> â€” include the upper bound, which is the classic bug: a consumer writing <code>[1.2,2.0]</code> gets <code>2.0</code>, and if the library behaves differently in <code>2.0</code>, that difference ships into their build silently. NuGet has the opposite surprise: a bare <code>1.2.3</code> means "at least 1.2.3", not "exactly 1.2.3".</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// Maven ranges, precisely
  [1.2,2.0)   &gt;=1.2 &lt;2.0      soft bounds, the default
  [1.2]       exactly 1.2
  [1.2,)      &gt;=1.2
  [1.2,1.3]   &gt;=1.2 &lt;=1.3     CLOSED upper bound

  // NuGet ranges, the opposite surprise
  1.2.3       minimum version, NuGet picks the HIGHEST available
  [1.2.3,2.0) explicit
  [1.2.3]     exact

  // Go modules: no ranges at all
  v1.2.3       exact. there are NO RANGES.
  // the main module's go.mod records the exact version
  // selected by MVS (minimal version selection)</code></pre></div>
    <p>Range syntax is not standardised, and the differences are a genuine source of bugs when you maintain a library across ecosystems.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// npm / node-semver
  ^1.2.3        &gt;=1.2.3 &lt;2.0.0    caret: same major
  ~1.2.3        &gt;=1.2.3 &lt;1.3.0    tilde: same minor
  1.2.3         exactly
  1.2.x         &gt;=1.2.0 &lt;1.3.0
  1.x           &gt;=1.0.0 &lt;2.0.0
  &gt;=1.2.3 &lt;2.0.0   explicit, clearest
  *             anything, including 2.0.0
  latest        a dist-tag, not a range. resolves to whatever you tag latest

// THE CARET IS NOT THE SAME IN 0.x
  ^0.2.3        &gt;=0.2.3 &lt;0.3.0    the minor is pinned in 0.x
  ^0.0.3        &gt;=0.0.3 &lt;0.0.4    the patch is pinned in 0.0.x
// if you are pre-1.0, ^0.2.3 will NOT pick up 0.3.0. good.

// PyPI / PEP 440
  ==1.2.3                exact
  ~=1.2.3                &gt;=1.2.3 &lt;1.3.0   compatible release
  ~=1.2                  &gt;=1.2   &lt;2.0
  &gt;=1.2,&lt;2.0          explicit, clearest
  ^1.2.3                 (only in poetry) &gt;=1.2.3 &lt;2.0.0
  1.2.*                  wildcard minor

// Cargo
  "1.2"       ^1.2 default: &gt;=1.2.0 &lt;2.0.0
  "~1.2.3"    &gt;=1.2.3 &lt;1.3.0
  "=1.2.3"    exact
  "*"         any

// Maven - the least intuitive of the lot
  [1.2,2.0)   &gt;=1.2 &lt;2.0      soft bounds, the default
  [1.2]       exactly 1.2
  [1.2,)      &gt;=1.2
  [1.2,1.3]   &gt;=1.2 &lt;=1.3     CLOSED upper bound. the classic bug:
//                                  a consumer writing [1.2,2.0] gets 2.0-rc,
//                                  and a library that works differently in 2.0
//                                  ships into their build silently.

// NuGet
  1.2.3       minimum version, and NuGet picks the HIGHEST available
  [1.2.3,2.0) explicit
  [1.2.3]     exact
// NuGet's bare "1.2.3" meaning "at least" rather than "exactly" is the
// other thing that catches people.

// Go modules
  v1.2.3       exact. there are NO RANGES.
//  v1.2         is a valid v2 path suffix, not a range
//  a library declares v1.2.3, and the main module's go.mod records the
//  exact version selected by MVS (minimal version selection).
//  ranges are expressed with a pseudo-version, which is unwieldy

// Composer
  ^1.2         &gt;=1.2 &lt;2.0
  ~1.2.3       &gt;=1.2.3 &lt;1.3.0

// THE PRACTICAL RULE
//   in your own documentation, always write the range explicitly:
//   ">=1.2.3 &lt;2.0.0". Every ecosystem's shorthand means something
//   slightly different, and the explicit form means the same thing
//   everywhere.</code></pre></div>
    <div class="warn"><p><strong>Never document a range in your own library's README using a different ecosystem's syntax.</strong> "Requires ^1.2" in a Python library, or ">= 1.2" in an npm package, produces a constraint the consumer's tool cannot parse, and they will either guess or fail. Write the explicit form and let the consumer's tooling convert it.</p></div>
    <h3 id="ch1-lockfile" data-toc>Lockfiles and the reproducibility question</h3>
    <h4 id="ch1-lockfile-what">What a lockfile actually pins</h4>
    <p>A lockfile records the exact resolved version of every dependency in the tree, including transitive dependencies. <code>package-lock.json</code> for npm, <code>poetry.lock</code> for Poetry, <code>uv.lock</code> for uv, <code>Gemfile.lock</code> for Bundler, <code>composer.lock</code> for Composer, <code>Cargo.lock</code> for Cargo, and <code>go.sum</code> for Go. The lockfile is what makes a rebuild six months later produce the same dependency tree. Without it, a rebuild can silently pick up a new minor version of a transitive dependency that breaks your build.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the lockfile, and what it guarantees
  package-lock.json    npm       exact versions + integrity hashes
  yarn.lock            yarn      exact versions
  poetry.lock          poetry    exact versions + hashes
  uv.lock              uv        exact versions + hashes
  Gemfile.lock         bundler   exact versions
  composer.lock        composer  exact versions
  Cargo.lock           cargo     exact versions + checksums
  go.sum               go        checksums only, not versions

  // what it DOES:
  //   exact versions, so a rebuild produces the same tree
  //   integrity hashes, so a tampered artefact fails the install

  // what it DOES NOT:
  //   protect you from a compromised version that was already pinned
  //   protect you from a malicious maintainer publishing a bad version</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-lockfile-commit">Which side should commit it</h4>
    <p>Applications should always commit their lockfile. An application that installs different versions on two machines is not reproducible and cannot be debugged. Libraries are a genuinely contested point. Arguments for committing: your CI is reproducible and your tests run against what you tested. Arguments against: you are testing a version of your dependencies your consumers will never resolve, because MVS and the lockfile can pick different trees, and a stale lockfile hides the fact that a dependency released a breaking change. Rust's convention â€” commit <code>Cargo.lock</code> in binaries, gitignore it in libraries â€” is the current best-practice answer.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the commit decision, per project type
  APPLICATION     always commit the lockfile
                  // two machines, two builds, same result

  LIBRARY         usually do not commit
                  // your lockfile is not your consumer's lockfile
                  // MVS resolves differently for each consumer

  // the exception: a library with a test matrix
  //   commit the lockfile for the CI configuration
  //   gitignore it for the published artefact

  // Rust's convention, the clearest answer:
  //   binaries: commit Cargo.lock
  //   libraries: .gitignore Cargo.lock</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-lockfile-limits">What a lockfile does not buy you</h4>
    <p>A lockfile guarantees consistency, not integrity. If version <code>1.2.3</code> of a dependency is in your lockfile and <code>1.2.3</code> was malicious, you keep reinstalling it. The lockfile ensures you get the same bytes every time; it does not ensure those bytes are safe. This is why signing and provenance are separate properties: a lockfile answers "what did I install?" and a signature answers "who published it?". The two together are stronger than either alone.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the three properties, and what each guarantees
  lockfile     consistency   same versions every time
  checksums    integrity     same bytes every time
  signing      authenticity  these bytes were published by that key
  provenance   process       built by that workflow, from that commit

  // the combinations:
  //   lockfile + checksums    reproducible, but not authenticated
  //   lockfile + signing      authenticated, but not reproducible
  //   all three               reproducible, authenticated, and consistent

  // the honest statement:
  //   a lockfile is necessary. it is not sufficient.</code></pre></div>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// a LOCKFILE pins every transitive dependency to an exact version
//   package-lock.json    npm
//   yarn.lock            yarn
//   poetry.lock          poetry
//   Pipfile.lock         pipenv
//   uv.lock              uv
//   Gemfile.lock         bundler
//   composer.lock        composer
//   Cargo.lock           cargo - committed for binaries, ignored for libraries
//   go.sum               go - checksums only, not versions

// WHICH SIDE SHOULD COMMIT IT
//   APPLICATIONS: always commit. An application that installs different
//   versions on two machines is not reproducible and cannot be debugged.
//
//   LIBRARIES: usually do not, and this is a genuinely contested point.
//     Arguments for committing: your CI is reproducible; your tests run
//       against what you tested.
//     Arguments against: you are testing a version of your dependencies
//       your consumers will never resolve, because MVS and the lockfile
//       can pick different trees. And a stale lockfile hides the fact
//       that a dependency released a breaking change.
//     Rust's convention: commit Cargo.lock in binaries, gitignore it in
//     libraries. That is the current best-practice answer.

// WHAT A LOCKFILE DOES AND DOES NOT BUY YOU
//   DOES: exact versions, so a rebuild six months later produces the
//         same dependency tree.
//   DOES NOT: protect you from a compromised version that was already
//             pinned. If 1.2.3 is in your lockfile and 1.2.3 was
//             malicious, you keep reinstalling it.
//   THIS IS WHY YOU ALSO NEED SIGNING AND PROVENANCE. A lockfile
//   guarantees consistency, not integrity. Those are different
//   properties and the next chapters are about integrity.

// YANKING A BAD VERSION
//   most registries let you yank or unpublish a version. Do it, and
//   then open an issue telling everyone who has it pinned what to do.
//   A yank does not reach existing installs. Your users will find out
//   from your changelog, not from the registry.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch1-pep440" data-toc>Build metadata, and why it is dangerous</h3>

    <h4 id="ch1-pep440-equal">Build metadata is ignored in version comparison</h4>
    <p>PEP 440 says: when comparing two versions, the local version label and build metadata are IGNORED. So 1.2.3 and 1.2.3+build.999 are EQUAL for resolution purposes. This means if you publish 1.2.3+build.1 and then 1.2.3+build.2, a consumer requiring 1.2.3 already matches build.1 and will never upgrade.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// build metadata is ignored in equality comparisons
1.2.3 == 1.2.3+build.456   // true - build metadata is ignored
1.2.3 != 1.2.3+build.456   // false

// But version ordering still works via numeric comparison
1.2.3+build.1 < 1.2.3+build.2  // true - numeric identifiers compare numerically</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-pep440-mutable">Mutable dist-tags and version safety</h4>
    <p>npm dist-tags like "latest" and "beta" are mutable pointers that can be repointed after publish. This is powerful but dangerous: repointing "latest" ships a release without a version bump, which can break production consumers who depend on <code>npm install pkg</code> resolving to the latest version.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// Safe publish pattern
$ npm publish --tag beta     // publish to beta tag, not latest
$ npm dist-tag add pkg@1.2.3 latest   // explicitly set latest

// Dangerous pattern - do not do this
$ npm publish                  // defaults to latest tag
$ npm dist-tag rm pkg latest   // then repoint</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-pep440-publish">Avoid publishing duplicate versions</h4>
    <p>PyPI rejects attempts to publish a version that already exists on the registry. This prevents the confusing situation where a consumer pinned to ==1.2.3+build.456 might get resolved to a different version. Always use build metadata for local CI identifiers, never for published versions.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// Attempting to publish the same version twice
$ npm publish
npm ERR! 403 Cannot publish the version 1.2.3 already exists on the registry.

// The "equal" behaviour bites when trying to publish
// 1.2.3+build.1 after 1.2.3+build.2 - both publish and resolve differently.</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch1-calver" data-toc>CalVer and why some projects use it</h3>

    <h4 id="ch1-calver-ubuntu">Ubuntu and Debian CalVer patterns</h4>
    <p>Ubuntu uses YYYY.MM format for LTS releases, such as 24.04 and 25.10. Debian follows a similar approach with major version numbers per release cycle, e.g. 12, 13. These versions signal recency rather than API compatibility, making them suitable for operating systems and platform software.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// Ubuntu LTS releases
Ubuntu 24.04    April 2024, 5 years support
Ubuntu 25.10    October 2025, 9 months support

// Debian releases
Debian 12       codename "bookworm", stable
Debian 13       codename "trixie", testing</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-calver-kubernetes">Kubernetes CalVer</h4>
    <p>Kubernetes uses a YY.MINOR.PATCH format on a regular cycle, e.g. v1.31.2. The minor version increments with each release, providing a balance between chronological sorting and meaningful version signals for API consumers.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// Kubernetes version format
v1.31.2   major=1, minor=31, patch=2
v1.30.0   previous major release

// Release cadence: approximately 3-4 months between releases</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-calver-hybrid">Hybrid CalVer (API compatibility + date)</h4>
    <p>The most defensible CalVer scheme combines API compatibility in the major version with the release date in the remaining parts, e.g. 2026.3 LTS or 1.20260314 for a library. The API compatibility is signalled by the major; the release timing is in the rest. This gives you chronological sort order AND a compatibility signal, as used by Kotlin and increasingly by serverless platforms.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// Hybrid CalVer scheme
2026.3 LTS   API compatibility in major version 3, release in 2026
1.20260314   major version 1, release date March 2024

// Why this works: a user on 1.202511 can safely take 2026.3, knowing
// the API compatibility is guaranteed by the major version.</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch1-when-major" data-toc>Deciding what forces a major bump</h3>

    <h4 id="ch1-when-major-removing">Removing a public symbol is always MAJOR</h4>
    <p>Removing a public symbol â€” whether a function, class, method, or type â€” is a breaking API change. Consumers who import or use the removed symbol must change their code. The asymmetry is deliberate: a needless major costs your users one edit, but a needless minor breaks them silently when a bug fix is expected.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Decision: removing a public function
// Before: exports foo, bar
module.exports = { foo, bar }

// After: foo removed - this is MAJOR
// Consumers must update: delete reference to foo or find alternative

// Decision: removing a public class
// Before: class Widget { ... }
// After: class Widget removed - MAJOR break for consumers extending it
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-when-major-platform">Dropping a platform is usually MAJOR</h4>
    <p>Dropping a platform runtime (e.g. Node 18, Python 3.8) is usually treated as MAJOR because the runtime requirement is part of the public contract. Registries and package managers treat runtime version requirements as binding commitments, so removing support forces consumers to upgrade their environment.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Example: Node 18 support removal
// package.json engines field
"engines": { "node": ">=18" }

// Removing Node 18 support changes the engine constraint,
// which consumers relying on CI checks or tooling will flag as MAJOR.
// Alternative: deprecate in a minor, remove in the next major.</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-when-major-security">Security fixes as patches</h4>
    <p>Security fixes should be shipped as patches on the current major AND on the previous major. Forcing an upgrade to a new major to get a security fix causes people to run vulnerable versions longer, which is a genuine risk. Instead, maintain the current major line with security patches and add the fix to the previous major as well.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Security fix workflow
// Current line: 2.0.0
// Security issue found in 2.0.0
// Patch releases: 2.0.1, 2.0.2 (on current line)
// Backport: also patch 1.0.0 -> 1.0.1, 1.1.x line

// Never: force 1.0.0 users to upgrade to 2.0.0 for a security fix.
// Always: ship the fix on both the current and previous major lines.</code></pre></div>

    

    

    <h3 id="ch1-deprecation" data-toc>Deprecating and yanking</h3><h4 id="ch1-deprecation-mechanics">Deprecation mechanics per language</h4>
    <p>Each language has its own deprecation annotation. JSDoc uses {@code @deprecated since 1.2.0, use fooV2 instead}, Python uses the {@code @deprecated(warning)} decorator per PEP 702, Rust uses {@code #[deprecated(since = "1.2.0", note = "use bar")]}, Go makes the symbol visible in gopls with {@code // Deprecated: use Bar}, Java uses {@code @Deprecated(since = "1.2", forRemoval = true)}, Kotlin uses {@code @Deprecated(message = "...", ReplaceWith = "...")}, and PHP uses {@code @deprecated since 1.2.0, use foo2() instead}. The key is that deprecation warnings must be actionable â€” they should tell the consumer exactly what to use instead.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Example: Java @Deprecated annotation
@Deprecated(since = "1.2", forRemoval = true)
public String getOldName() { return oldName; }

// Example: Python @deprecated decorator
import warnings
def old_function():
    warnings.warn("Use new_function instead", DeprecationWarning, stacklevel=2)
    return old_behaviour()

// Example: Rust #[deprecated] attribute
#[deprecated(since = "1.2.0", note = "use bar_v2")]
fn old_function() { ... }
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-deprecation-warnings">Warnings must be actionable</h4>
    <p>A deprecation warning that does not say what to do instead is noise, and people suppress warnings. Good deprecation explains the migration path: "use parseV2() instead. Same signature, but it rejects trailing commas and returns an error object rather than throwing. Migrate by replacing the call: the behaviour change is documented at https://...". Without a clear migration path, deprecation becomes a background murmur that consumers learn to ignore.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// BAD deprecation - just the word "deprecated"
@deprecated

// GOOD deprecation - includes migration path
@deprecated("use parseV2() instead. Same signature, but it rejects
trailing commas and returns an error object rather than throwing.
// Migrate by replacing the call at https://example.com/migration")
def parseV1(): ...</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch1-deprecation-yanking">Yanking a broken release</h4>
    <p>Most registries allow yanking or unpublishing a version. Do it when: a broken build shipped, a security vulnerability was introduced, a licence violation occurred, or a dependency compromise affected you. Do NOT yank for: "the API changed and I regret it" (that is a new major) or "the description was wrong" (fix the description). A yank does not remove the artefact from existing installs, so it is a signal, not a recall. Announce it in your changelog and, if it is a security issue, in your advisory.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Yanking a bad version from npm
$ npm deprecate @yourorg/pkg@1.4.0 "security fix in 1.4.1, please upgrade"
// or yank (within 72 hours of publish):
$ npm unpublish @yourorg/pkg@1.4.2

// After 72 hours, only deprecation is possible, never unpublish.
// Always announce the yank in your advisory and changelog.</code></pre></div>

    
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// A DECISION PROCEDURE, because "is this major?" is a judgement call
// and getting it wrong in either direction is expensive.

// 1. LIST EVERY PUBLIC SYMBOL the release removes, renames, or changes
//    the signature of. A script can find most of them by diffing the
//    API surface. For a typed language, compare the emitted .d.ts or
//    the compiled signature list against the previous tag.
//
// 2. For EACH ONE, ask: is there any input a real consumer could pass
//    that behaved correctly before and does not now?
//    yes -&gt; MAJOR
//    no  -&gt; not major
//
//    removing a class that was only ever used internally -&gt; not major
//    changing an argument from optional to required              -&gt; MAJOR
//    changing a return value's shape                            -&gt; MAJOR
//    throwing a new exception type from a function documented never to
//      throw                                                 -&gt; MAJOR if
//      anyone catches broadly, which someone does
//    changing default behaviour while keeping the signature       -&gt; MAJOR
//    fixing a documented example that did not work              -&gt; PATCH
//    making a performance improvement that changes floating-point
//      results                                               -&gt; MAJOR, and
//      this is why numeric libraries treat it as breaking

// 3. IF YOU CANNOT ANSWER "MAJOR" OR "NOT MAJOR" FOR SOMETHING,
//    IT IS A MAJOR. The asymmetry is deliberate: a needless major
//    costs your users one edit; a needless minor breaks them silently.

// THE EXCEPTIONS WORTH NAMING
//   - dropping a platform (Node 18, Python 3.8). Usually a MAJOR, and
//     most registries and package managers treat the runtime
//     requirement as part of the public contract.
//   - a security fix. Ship it as a patch on the current line AND on the
//     previous major. Do not force an upgrade to a new major to get a
//     security fix, or people will run the vulnerable version longer.
//   - a licence change. This is legally major and nobody reads the
//     changelog. Say it in the release notes, in bold, repeatedly.</code></pre></div>
    

    

    // DEPRECATION, the lifecycle of a version
  1.0.0   released
  1.1.0   a new function added
  1.2.0   old function marked @deprecated, still works
  2.0.0   old function removed. MAJOR.

// THE MECHANICS, per language
//   JSDoc    /** @deprecated since 1.2.0, use fooV2 instead */
//   Python   @deprecated(warning) decorator, PEP 702
//   Rust     #[deprecated(since = "1.2.0", note = "use bar")]
//   Go       // Deprecated: use Bar. Becomes visible in gopls
//   Java     @Deprecated(since = "1.2", forRemoval = true)
//   Kotlin   @Deprecated(message = "...", ReplaceWith = "...")
//   PHP      @deprecated since 1.2.0, use foo2() instead

// THE WARNINGS MUST BE ACTIONABLE
//   BAD:  deprecated
//   GOOD: "use parseV2() instead. Same signature, but it rejects
//          trailing commas and returns an error object rather than
//          throwing. Migrate by replacing the call: the behaviour
//          change is documented at https://..."
//   a deprecation warning that does not say what to do instead is
//   noise, and people suppress warnings.

// WHAT MOST PROJECTS GET WRONG
//   removing in the very next major, with no migration window.
//   Deprecate in a minor, give at least one full major release cycle,
//   and only remove when your own telemetry or issue tracker shows
//   near-zero usage.

// YANKING, for a broken release
//   most registries allow it. Do it when:
//     a broken build shipped
//     a security vulnerability
//     a licence violation
//     a dependency compromise affected you
//   and do NOT yank for
//     "the API changed and I regret it"   (that is a new major)
//     "the description was wrong"          (fix the description)
//   a yank does not remove the artefact from existing installs, so it
//   is a signal, not a recall. Announce it in your changelog and, if
//   it is a security issue, in your advisory.</code></pre></div>
    <div class="ex"><h4>Exercises 1</h4><ol>
      <li>Take a library you depend on and write out its public API surface. Then check which of its recent releases should have been major under this chapter's procedure but were not. Report it upstream if you are confident.</li>
      <li>Diff your own package's public API between the last two tags, mechanically. Then classify every change and confirm your chosen version numbers match the classification.</li>
      <li>Write the explicit version range for your package in five ecosystems' syntaxes and verify each resolves the versions you expect, using <code>npm view</code>, <code>pip index</code>, and equivalent tools.</li>
      <li>Publish two builds with the same version and different build metadata to a test index and demonstrate that they compare as equal. Explain the failure mode for a consumer who pinned the first one.</li>
      <li>Add a deprecation warning to a function in your own package, with a message that says what to use instead. Verify it appears in the consumer's editor, not just at runtime.</li>
    </ol></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
<h2 id="ch2" data-toc>2 Â· Reproducible Builds and the Artefact</h2>
    <div class="ch-toc"><button type="button" class="ch-toc-head" aria-expanded="true"><span class="arrow">â–¸</span>Contents &middot; 2 Â· Reproducible Builds and the Artefact</button><div class="ch-toc-list"><a href="#ch2-artefact">What is actually in the published file</a><a href="#ch2-reproducible">Reproducible builds</a><a href="#ch2-deterministic">Determinism, the weaker property</a><a href="#ch2-ci">Building in CI and only in CI</a><a href="#ch2-sourcemaps">Source maps and debuggability</a><a href="#ch2-typosquat">Name collisions and typosquatting</a><a href="#ch2-scopes">Scopes, and publishing under a namespace</a><a href="#ch2-monorepo">Monorepos and per-package releases</a><a href="#ch2-fat">Minimising the published surface</a></div></div>
    <p class="topic-desc">A published artefact is a promise that specific bytes will install everywhere, forever. Most of this chapter is about making those bytes a function of your source rather than of the machine that happened to build them.</p>
    <h3 id="ch2-artefact" data-toc>What is actually in the published file</h3>
        <h4 id="ch2-artefact-contents">Contents of the published file</h4>
    <p>The published artefact is a TARBALL or ZIP whose contents determine what consumers install. A minimal package includes only essential files, while an unconstrained publish may include development tooling, test fixtures, or large assets that balloon the install size.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Minimal npm package publish: only these files reach the consumer
// "files" field in package.json acts as the authoritative list
"files": ["dist", "README.md", "LICENSE"]
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-artefact-npmignore">The .npmignore gate</h4>
    <p>When .npmignore is absent, npm falls back to .gitignore, which can inadvertently exclude code or include development files. The single most effective CI step is: <code>$ npm pack --dry-run</code> before every publish.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Always preview the publish tarball before committing
$ npm pack --dry-run
npm notice 1.2.3
npm notice === Tarball Contents ===
npm notice 120kB  dist/index.js          (built)
npm notice  14kB  dist/index.d.ts        (types, if you ship them)
npm notice  23kB  dist/index.js.map      (source map)
npm notice   2kB  README.md
npm notice   1kB  LICENSE
npm notice 400kB  src/                   (often included by accident)
npm notice   3kB  .eslintrc.json         (never useful to a consumer)
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-artefact-wheel">SDist vs Wheel</h4>
    <p>Python packages ship both an sdist (source distribution) and a wheel (pre-built binary). The sdist is needed for platforms lacking a wheel, while the wheel is what most consumers install. Publishing only an sdist means every install compiles; publishing only a wheel excludes platforms without a compatible wheel.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Python: build and verify both artefact types
$ python -m build              # builds sdist and wheel
$ twine check dist/*           # verify wheel integrity

// Platform-specific wheel
$ pip install mypkg-1.2.0-py3-none-any.whl  // pure Python, no compile needed
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// most ecosystems publish a TARBALL or ZIP, not a directory of files.
// what goes in it is your decision, and the default is often wrong.

// npm: an always-generated tarball, packed from .npmignore + .gitignore
$ npm pack --dry-run
npm notice 1.2.3
npm notice === Tarball Contents ===
npm notice 120kB  dist/index.js          (built)
npm notice  14kB  dist/index.d.ts        (types, if you ship them)
npm notice  23kB  dist/index.js.map      (source map)
npm notice   2kB  README.md
npm notice   1kB  LICENSE
npm notice 400kB  src/                   (often included by accident)
npm notice   3kB  .eslintrc.json         (never useful to a consumer)

// npm pack is your preview. ALWAYS run it before publishing.
// the classic accident: your .npmignore is missing, so .gitignore is
// used instead, and that happens to ignore /dist because that is
// where your build output goes, and now you have published a package
// with no code in it. The .npmignore takes precedence when present, so
// an incomplete one is worse than none.

// Python: an sdist and a wheel
  sdist   your source, plus setup.py or pyproject.toml. Needed for
          source installs and for the package index to build a wheel.
  wheel   the built artefact, with a platform tag where relevant.
$ python -m build
$ twine check dist/*

// what MUST be in a wheel
  your package
  dist-info/METADATA      name, version, dependencies, licence
  dist-info/WHEEL         the wheel format version
  dist-info/RECORD        the file list with hashes
  your declared data files

// Rust: a .crate, which is a gzipped tar with a Cargo.toml inside
// Java: a .jar and a .pom. The pom is as important as the jar.
// Go: a module ZIP with a go.mod and an info/mod file
// PHP: a ZIP with composer.json at the root, and the folder named
//      after the package, not a versioned folder

// THE RULE
//   inspect the artefact before you publish it, every time, and make
//   it a CI step. A package that shipped without its build output is a
//   real outage for every consumer, and the registry will not warn you.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch2-reproducible" data-toc>Reproducible builds</h3>
    <p>A reproducible build produces bit-identical output from the same source, on any machine, at any time. This is stronger than determinism, and it is what lets you audit an artefact.</p>
        <h4 id="ch2-repro-determinism">Determinism vs Reproducibility</h4>
    <p>Reproducibility is stronger than determinism: same input, same output on <em>any</em> machine. Determinism only guarantees same output on the <em>same</em> machine. This distinction matters for binary artefacts and supply-chain audits.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Reproducible: same input, same output, on ANY machine
// Deterministic: same input, same output, on the SAME machine only
// Reproducibility implies determinism, not the reverse
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-repro-timestamps">Timestamps and File Order</h4>
    <p>The single most common cause of non-reproducibility is baked-in timestamps. Normalise mtime during archiving: <code>tar --sort=name --mtime='UTC 2020-01-01'</code>. Sort directory listings explicitly with <code>find . -type f | sort</code>.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Fix timestamps in a tar archive
tar --sort=name --mtime='UTC 2020-01-01' \
    --owner=0 --group=0 \
    --numeric-owner -cf dist.tar build/

// Sort file lists explicitly
find . -type f | sort | xargs tar --sort=name -cf archive.tar
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-repro-verify">Verify with Hash Comparison</h4>
    <p>Build twice in clean containers on different runners and compare hashes. This five-minute CI step catches timestamps, file ordering, and dependency non-determinism.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Verify reproducibility in CI
$ sha256sum dist/myapp-1.2.3.tar.gz
a3f5c9...  dist/myapp-1.2.3.tar.gz
// build again on a different machine, in a clean container
$ sha256sum dist/myapp-1.2.3.tar.gz
a3f5c9...  dist/myapp-1.2.3.tar.gz      must match
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WHAT BREAKS REPRODUCIBILITY, and the fix for each

// 1. TIMESTAMPS baked into the artefact
//    the single most common cause
   // in a tar: set a fixed mtime
   tar --sort=name --mtime='UTC 2020-01-01' --owner=0 --group=0 \
       --numeric-owner -cf dist.tar build/
   // in Java JARs, the zip entry timestamps come from the filesystem
   //   so normalise them in the build

// 2. FILE ORDER
//    a directory read returns entries in filesystem order, which
//    differs between machines. Sort explicitly.
   --sort=name            GNU tar
   find . -type f | sort  then feed the list

// 3. ABSOLUTE PATHS EMBEDDED
//    build tools sometimes record the build directory in metadata.
   // Go: trimpath
   go build -trimpath
   // Rust: --remap-path-prefix
   RUSTFLAGS="--remap-path-prefix=$PWD=." cargo build
   // node-gyp, cmake, protoc: each has an equivalent

// 4. RANDOMNESS IN THE TOOLCHAIN
//    a build id, a UUID, a hash-map iteration order.
//    sort any map you serialise. Set a fixed SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH.
   export SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=1700000000   // honoured by many toolchains

// 5. NETWORK ACCESS DURING THE BUILD
//    a downloaded dependency, a timestamped API call. Pin everything
//    and build with --offline or an equivalent.

// 6. RANDOM SEEDS
//    parallel test ordering, hash seeders.
//    fix the seed, or set it explicitly in the artefact.

// 7. USER-SPECIFIC ENVIRONTS
//    $HOME, $PATH, locale, timezone. Normalise in the build script.

// 8. NON-DETERMINISTIC COMPRESSION
//    some compressors embed timestamps. Verify by building twice and
//    comparing hashes.

// VERIFY IT
$ sha256sum dist/myapp-1.2.3.tar.gz
a3f5c9...  dist/myapp-1.2.3.tar.gz
// build again, on a different machine, in a clean container
$ sha256sum dist/myapp-1.2.3.tar.gz
a3f5c9...  dist/myapp-1.2.3.tar.gz      must match

// WHY IT IS WORTH THE EFFORT
//   1. a consumer can rebuild your artefact and compare, which turns
//      "trust us" into "check it"
//   2. it is a prerequisite for SLSA provenance and reproducible-builds
//      attestation, which is the modern answer to supply-chain attacks
//   3. it makes "was this binary built from this source?" a question
//      with a mechanical answer
//   4. it catches accidental non-determinism that would otherwise show
//      up as a mysterious bug report</code></pre></div>
    <div class="tip"><p>Rebuilding from the same tag twice and diffing the hashes is a five-minute CI step that catches an enormous class of packaging bugs. It will find files you did not mean to include, timestamps that break a reproducible-build check downstream, and occasionally a dependency that fetches from the network during the build.</p></div>
    <h3 id="ch2-deterministic" data-toc>Determinism, the weaker property</h3>
        <h4 id="ch2-det-goals">Practical reproducibility goals</h4>
    <p>While bit-identical builds are ideal, a practical target is: fixed toolchain version, pinned lockfile, no network access during build, and no timestamps. These four properties alone make a consumer able to verify integrity.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Practical reproducibility target
//   - fixed, documented toolchain version
//   - pinned lockfile  
//   - no network access during build
//   - -trimpath or equivalent
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-det-go-platform">Platform-specific concerns</h4>
    <p>Different ecosystems have different reproducibility profiles. Go needs -trimpath, Rust needs --remap-path-prefix, Debian has been reproducible since 2013, and Python wheels benefit from isolated build containers. Know your ecosystem's strengths.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Go: -trimpath removes build path embedment
go build -trimpath

// Rust: --remap-path-prefix fixes build paths
RUSTFLAGS="--remap-path-prefix=C:\Users\user\repo\learn-software-engineering=." cargo build

// Debian: reproducible since 2013
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-det-achievable">Achievable reproducibility</h4>
    <p>Do not chase bit-identical builds for everything. Achieve: fixed toolchain, pinned lockfile, no network access, no timestamps. This is enough for a consumer to verify integrity, and signing/provenance chapters build on this.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Do not chase bit-identical builds for everything
//   - fixed, documented toolchain version
//   - pinned lockfile
//   - no network access during build
//   - -trimpath or equivalent
//   - recorded hash in release notes
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// DETERMINISTIC: same input, same output, on the same machine
// REPRODUCIBLE: same input, same output, on ANY machine
//   reproducibility implies determinism, not the reverse

// this matters for binary artefacts
//   a Go build embeds the build path, so two identical checkouts in
//   different directories produce different binaries
//   -trimpath fixes it. Always use it, even if you do not intend to
//   publish a reproducible build, because a Go binary that leaks your
//   build path is an information disclosure.

// WHAT IS ACHIEVABLE IN PRACTICE
//   Go           fully reproducible with -trimpath and a pinned toolchain
//   Rust         reproducible with --remap-path-prefix and a pinned version
//   Nix          reproducible by construction. this is its whole design.
//   Debian       reproducible since 2013, and the reason the security
//                community pushed for it
//   Python wheels  harder, because setuptools embeds paths. wheels
//                built in a fixed container are effectively reproducible.
//   Node         possible, but the toolchain has improved more slowly
//   JVM jars     reproducible with a normalising build, e.g. reproducible-builds
//   .NET         deterministic builds exist but embed a MVID that
//                changes unless you set Deterministic and
//                ContinuousIntegrationBuild

// A PRACTICAL TARGET
//   Do not chase bit-identical builds for everything. Do achieve:
//     - a fixed, documented toolchain version
//     - a pinned lockfile
//     - no network access during the build
//     - -trimpath or equivalent
//     - a recorded hash of the artefact in your release notes
//   That is enough for a consumer to verify integrity, and it is what
//   the signing and provenance chapters build on.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch2-ci" data-toc>Building in CI and only in CI</h3>
        <h4 id="ch2-ci-cicd-pipeline">CI pipeline shape</h4>
    <p>A release pipeline triggered by git tags is the standard pattern. It combines signed annotated tags, protected tag patterns, and short-lived OIDC credentials so a stolen laptop token cannot publish.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Git tag-triggered release pipeline
git tag -s -m "v1.2.3" 1.2.3
git push origin v1.2.3
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-ci-protections">Four rules for safe publishing</h4>
    <p>1. BUILD IN CI. Never publish from a laptop. Ever. 2. PROTECT THE TAG. Require signed tags and a protected release environment with reviewers. 3. USE SHORT-LIVED CREDENTIALS. OIDC exchange means no long-lived secret to steal. 4. VERIFY BEFORE PUBLISH. Check the artefact with a test, not confidence.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// THE FOUR RULES
//   1. BUILD IN CI. Never publish from a laptop. Ever.
//   2. PROTECT THE TAG. Require signed tags and a protected release environment with reviewers
//   3. USE SHORT-LIVED CREDENTIALS. OIDC exchange means no long-lived secret.
//   4. VERIFY BEFORE PUBLISH. The artefact you ship is checked by a test.
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-ci-tag-trigger">Tag-triggered release pattern</h4>
    <p>The tag is the immutable statement of intent. Combine with: signed annotated tags, and a protected tag pattern in the repository settings.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Standard tag-triggered pipeline
git tag -s -m "v1.2.3" 1.2.3
git push origin v1.2.3
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// PUBLISHING FROM A DEVELOPER MACHINE IS HOW SUPPLY CHAINS GET COMPROMISED
//   a malware incident in a popular npm package in 2022 came from a
//   maintainer's account being phished. The attacker published from
//   the maintainer's laptop, and the registry accepted it because the
//   registry cannot tell a maintainer from a thief who has the token.
//
//   the fix: make the token physically incapable of publishing from
//   anywhere but CI.

// THE SHAPE OF A GOOD PIPELINE
name: release
on:
  push:
    tags: ['v*']

jobs:
  release:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: read
      id-token: write          # required for OIDC, no long-lived secret
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with:
          persist-credentials: false   # the token cannot be reused

      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 20
          registry-url: 'https://registry.npmjs.org'

      - run: npm ci               # not npm install. ci honours the lockfile
      - run: npm test
      - run: npm run build

      # verify the artefact is what you expect BEFORE publishing
      - run: npm pack --dry-run
      - run: node scripts/verify-artefact.mjs

      - name: publish
        env:
          NODE_AUTH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.NPM_TOKEN }}
        run: npm publish --provenance --access public

// THE FOUR RULES
//   1. BUILD IN CI. Never publish from a laptop. Ever.
//   2. PROTECT THE TAG. Require signed tags and a protected release
//      environment with reviewers, so a stolen token alone is not enough.
//   3. USE SHORT-LIVED CREDENTIALS. OIDC exchange (ch4) means there is
//      no long-lived secret to steal at all.
//   4. VERIFY BEFORE PUBLISH. The artefact you are about to ship is
//      checked by a test, not by your confidence.

// THE TAG-TRIGGERED RELEASE PIPELINE
//   it is the standard because the tag is the immutable statement of
//   intent. The commit is not: it moves under a force-push.
//   combine with: signed annotated tags, and a protected tag pattern
//   in the repository settings.
   git tag -s -m "v1.2.3" 1.2.3
   git push origin v1.2.3</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch2-sourcemaps" data-toc>Source maps and debuggability</h3>
        <h4 id="ch2-sm-types">Type declarations are not optional</h4>
    <p>Ship the .d.ts file, or consumers get an implicit-any error at their import. This is the single most common reason a TS package is described as "broken" and it is a packaging failure, not a bug.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// TypeScript declarations are not optional for a TS-first package
//   ship the .d.ts, or consumers get an implicit-any error at their import
"types": "./dist/index.d.ts",
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-sm-exports">exports field for resolution</h4>
    <p>The exports field enforces the public API. Deep-imports outside the exports field stop resolving for consumers. This is a breaking change for anyone deep-importing, so do it in a major release.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Enforce the public API with exports
"exports": {
  ".": { "types": "./dist/index.d.ts", "import": "./dist/index.js" }
}
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-sm-type-reveal">Type reveal in debugger</h4>
    <p>Source maps resolve frames into YOUR source, even though the consumer only has your dist. Add "sourceRoot" so a browser can fetch the original file from the registry.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Add sourceRoot so browser can fetch original
"sourceRoot": "https://unpkg.com/your-pkg@1.2.3/"
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// a published package that throws a stack trace the consumer cannot read
// is a support burden. Source maps solve it, and most projects skip them.

// npm / node
{
  "main": "dist/index.js",
  "types": "dist/index.d.ts",
  "files": ["dist", "README.md", "LICENSE"],
  "sourceMap": true,
  "declarationMap": true
}
// ship the .js.map and the .d.ts.map inside the package
//   a consumer's debugger then resolves frames into YOUR source, even
//   though they only have your dist
//   to do this from a bundler, add:
//     "sourceRoot": "https://unpkg.com/your-pkg@1.2.3/"
//   so a browser can fetch the original file from the registry

// TypeScript declarations are not optional for a TS-first package
//   ship the .d.ts, or consumers get an implicit-any error at their
//   import. This is the single most common reason a TS package is
//   described as "broken" and it is a packaging failure, not a bug.
   "types": "./dist/index.d.ts",
   "exports": {
     ".": { "types": "./dist/index.d.ts", "import": "./dist/index.js" }
   }

// Python
//   exceptions in compiled extensions reference the C source, not the
//   Python. Ship the .c files in the sdist so the traceback is readable.

// Rust
//   debug=true in the dev profile by default; for release, publish
//   -C debuginfo=2 .so files separately, keyed by version. Never ship
//   them inside the crate, or the artefact gets enormous.

// Go
//   the compiler embeds file paths and line numbers already. With
//   -trimpath the paths are module-relative and a consumer can resolve
//   them against the module cache. This is why -trimpath matters for
//   more than reproducibility.

// THE TRADE-OFF
//   source maps reveal your source structure. For a closed-source
//   commercial package that is information you may not want to publish.
//   For almost everything else: ship them. The debuggability is worth
//   far more than the obscurity, which is not security anyway.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch2-typosquat" data-toc>Name collisions and typosquatting</h3>
        <h4 id="ch2-typosq-lockfiles">Lockfiles pin integrity hashes</h4>
    <p>A lockfile pins the integrity hash of every dependency, so a typosquatted package cannot be swapped in without a lockfile change you would see in review. Always commit your lockfile for applications.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Example: npm ls resolved hashes
$ npm ls --all --json | jq -r '
    .dependencies | to_entries[] |
    "\(.value.resolved // "none")  \(.key)"' \
  | awk '{print }' | sed 's|https://registry.npmjs.org/||' \
  | grep -v '^none$' | sort -u | head
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-typosq-scope">Scopes and namespace namespaces</h4>
    <p>A scope (npm) or group ID (Maven) is a registered namespace, making typosquatting much harder. Use scoped packages for anything with more than one maintainer.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// npm scope: @yourorg/name
npm login --scope=@yourorg
npm publish --access public

// Maven groupId: com.yourorg.library
//   central repository requires domain verification
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-typosq-ci-check">CI step to detect typosquatting</h4>
    <p>Resolve every dependency and fail if any comes from an unexpected registry. Test by pointing one dependency at a URL you do not expect.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// CI step to detect typosquatting
$ npm ls --all --json | jq -r '
    .dependencies | to_entries[] |
    "\(.value.resolved // "none")  \(.key)"' \
  | awk '{print }' | sed 's|https://registry.npmjs.org/||' \
  | grep -v '^none$' | sort -u | head
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE ATTACK
//   you depend on react. an attacker publishes "reacct", "rceact",
//   "reactt" and waits for a typo. This is not theoretical: it has
//   happened in npm, PyPI, and RubyGems, and the payloads have
//   included credential stealers and crypto miners.

// THE MITIGATIONS, in order of effectiveness
//   1. LOCKFILES, always committed, for applications. A lockfile pins the
//      integrity hash of every dependency, so a typo'd package cannot be
//      swapped in without a lockfile change you would see in review.
//   2. DEPENDENCY REVIEW. Look at every new dependency before it lands.
//      A single-character difference from a popular package is the
//      signal.
//   3. SCOPED OR VENDORED NAMES. A scope (npm) or a group id (Maven) is
//      a namespace, and namespaces are registered, so typosquatting is
//      much harder.
//   4. REGISTRY CONFUSION, a newer and subtler problem
//      your internal npm registry is configured under a scope. A
//      developer who forgets the .npmrc line installs from public npm
//      instead. Test for it: a CI step that resolves every dependency
//      and fails if any comes from an unexpected registry.

// THE DEFENSIVE CHECK, worth putting in CI
$ npm ls --all --json | jq -r '
    .dependencies | to_entries[] |
    "\(.value.resolved // "none")  \(.key)"' \
  | awk '{print $1}' | sed 's|https://registry.npmjs.org/||' \
  | grep -v '^none$' | sort -u | head
// every resolved URL should be from a registry you expect.
// a package resolving from a tarball URL or a git URL deserves a look.

// DEFENSIVE SETTINGS in .npmrc
//   ignore-scripts=true     block lifecycle scripts from dependencies
//     this is a real, meaningful protection and it has a cost: some
//     packages legitimately need a postinstall build step. Audit the
//     ones you allow rather than turning it off globally without
//     thinking.
//   audit=true
//   save-exact=true         stop ^1.2.3 from silently accepting 1.9.0
//   provenance=true         request provenance attestation on publish</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch2-scopes" data-toc>Scopes, and publishing under a namespace</h3>
        <h4 id="ch2-scopes-npm">npm scopes: @org/name</h4>
    <p>The scope is a namespace you own. The package name is then @yourorg/thing, which removes typosquatting risk entirely for your internal packages, and makes ownership obvious.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// npm scopes: @org/name
npm login --scope=@yourorg
npm publish --access public
// a private scope is free and unlimited on the free tier
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-scopes-maven">Maven group ids: com.yourorg.library</h4>
    <p>The groupId is a namespace, and the central repository requires you to verify ownership of a domain matching it. This is why "com.google.guava" exists and why a squat on com.yourorg is prevented by domain verification.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Maven group id: com.yourorg.library
//   central repository requires domain verification
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-scopes-pypi">PyPI: organisation accounts</h4>
    <p>Organisation accounts are free, and they own projects and their transfers. Use one for anything with more than one maintainer, and put a working email on it, because PyPI account recovery is email-based.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// PyPI organisation account: free, owns projects and transfers
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-scopes-reserve">Reserve crate names on crates.io</h4>
    <p>A verified GitHub organisation or domain can reserve a crate name without publishing, so nobody takes it. Do this for every name you might ever use.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// crates.io: reserve a name without publishing
//   reserve via verified organisation or domain
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// npm scopes: @org/name
//   the scope is a namespace you own. The package name is then
//   @yourorg/thing, which removes typosquatting risk entirely for
//   your internal packages, and makes the ownership obvious.
   npm login --scope=@yourorg
   npm publish --access public        // scoped packages default to
                                      // restricted, so this is required
//   a private scope is free and unlimited on the free tier

// Maven group ids: com.yourorg.library
//   the groupId is a namespace, and the central repository requires
//   you to verify ownership of a domain matching it. This is why
//   "com.google.guava" exists and why a squat on com.yourorg is
//   prevented by domain verification.

// PyPI: organisation accounts
//   free, and they own projects and their transfers. Use one for
//   anything with more than one maintainer, and put a working email
//   on it, because PyPI account recovery is email-based and a dead
//   domain means a dead project.

// NuGet: organisation and ownership
//   you can transfer ownership of a package, and reserve a prefix
//   under your verified domain.

// crates.io: an org
//   a GitHub organisation or a verified domain, and the org owns the
//   crates.
//   you can RESERVE a crate name without publishing, so nobody takes
//   it. Do this for every name you might ever use.

// WHY THE NAMESPACE MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS
//   a transfer is a break-glass operation. If your org account email
//   is on a domain you lose, you need a domain administrator to
//   recover it, and that is a genuinely scary dependency to have on
//   your most-used internal package.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch2-monorepo" data-toc>Monorepos and per-package releases</h3>
        <h4 id="ch2-mono-changesets">Changeset flow for independent releases</h4>
    <p>A changeset is a markdown file describing one change to one package. Tools like turbo or nx generate a release PR from accumulated changesets, computing versions from bump types. This is the most common modern answer to the monorepo release problem.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Changeset example
// packages/changesets/brave-lions-jump.md
//
// ---
// "@yourorg/core": minor
// "fix: handle a null token in refresh()"
// ---
// a release PR is generated from the accumulated changesets
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-mono-changelog">Per-package changelogs</h4>
    <p>A monorepo changelog is useless. Generate per-package changelogs from the changesets, and keep a root CHANGELOG that only records changes to the public workspace API.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Root CHANGELOG only records workspace API changes
// Per-package changelogs generated from changesets
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-mono-version-prop">Version propagation rules</h4>
    <p>If @yourorg/plugin requires @yourorg/core ^1.2.0 and core goes to 2.0.0, the plugin must be released too, and its new version must widen its range. Write the rule down because exception cases accumulate.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Version propagation: if core bumps, plugin range must widen
// Write the rule down because exception cases accumulate
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE PROBLEM
//   a monorepo with 40 packages, all released together, is unusable:
//   every user of one tiny package waits for the 39 others.
//   per-package release is much harder than it looks.

// THREE APPROACHES

// A. INDEPENDENT VERSIONS, changed-path-based release
//   only publish a package when its own files changed
   // lerna --since origin/main, nx affected, turbo
   // hard parts: dependency graph, changelog generation, lockstep
//   dependents that need a release when a dependency changed

// B. FIXED GROUPS
//   group packages that always release together, version each group
     "packages": {
       "group-a": ["packages/a", "packages/b"],
       "group-b": ["packages/c"]
     }
//   simple, and the right answer for genuinely coupled packages

// C. INDEPENDENT WITH A CHANGESET FLOW
//   a changeset is a markdown file describing one change to one package
   //   packages/changesets/brave-lions-jump.md
   //   ---
   //   "@yourorg/core": minor
   //   "fix: handle a null token in refresh()"
   //   ---
   //   a release PR is generated from the accumulated changesets, with
   //   versions computed from the bump types, and you merge it
   //   the best properties: a human reviews the version bump and the
   //   changelog before it lands, and dependents are auto-bumped
   //   this is the most common modern answer

// THE CHANGELOG PROBLEM
//   a monorepo changelog is useless. Generate per-package changelogs
//   from the changesets, and keep a root CHANGELOG that only records
//   changes to the public workspace API.

// THE VERSION PROPAGATION PROBLEM
//   if @yourorg/plugin requires @yourorg/core ^1.2.0 and core goes to
//   2.0.0, the plugin must be released too, and its new version must
//   widen its range. Tools do this automatically. Write the rule down
//   anyway, because the exception cases accumulate.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch2-fat" data-toc>Minimising the published surface</h3>
        <h4 id="ch2-fat-files">The "files" field is authoritative</h4>
    <p>Without "files", npm uses .gitignore, and then everything you have ignored is published too. With "files", the list is authoritative. Always set it.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Always set the "files" field
"files": ["dist", "README.md", "LICENSE", "CHANGELOG.md"]
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-fat-accidental">Files that always get in by accident</h4>
    <p>CI configuration (.github, .gitlab-ci.yml, Jenkinsfile), Dockerfiles, compose files, test fixtures, editor directories (.idea, .vscode), and .env files often ship by accident. Check specifically for .env files.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Common files that accidentally ship
//   - CI configuration: .github, .gitlab-ci.yml, Jenkinsfile
//   - Dockerfiles and compose files
//   - test fixtures, often large
//   - editor directories: .idea, .vscode
//   - .env files. Check for this specifically.
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch2-fat-budget">Size budget in CI</h4>
    <p>Set a budget and fail the build if the artefact grows by more than a set percentage. A package that grows 10 MB in a release is usually a mistake, and it is invisible without this check.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Verify the size in CI
$ npm pack --dry-run
npm notice package size: 1.2 MB
npm notice unpacked size: 4.8 MB
// set a budget and fail the build if it is exceeded
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// every file in your package is installed on every user's disk, and is
// scanned by every security tool they run. Keep it small.

// THE FILE LIST, and it is not optional
// npm
   "files": ["dist", "README.md", "LICENSE", "CHANGELOG.md"]
//   anything not listed is excluded. This is the reliable mechanism;
//   relying on .npmignore is fragile because .gitignore also applies
//   when .npmignore is absent.

// Cargo
[package]
include = ["src/**", "README.md", "LICENSE", "Cargo.toml"]
exclude = ["tests/", "benches/", ".github/"]

// Go: nothing to declare. The module ZIP contains the whole module
//   directory, so a repository with large assets in it ships them all.
//   Use a .gitattributes with export-ignore, and keep assets in a
//   separate module or a release bucket.

// Python: MANIFEST.in
   include LICENSE README.md CHANGELOG.md
   recursive-include src *.py
//   or set the sdist config in pyproject.toml for modern tooling

// Rust
[package]
exclude = ["/.github", "/benches", "/tests", "/docs"]

// THE THINGS THAT ALWAYS GET IN BY ACCIDENT
//   - CI configuration: .github, .gitlab-ci.yml, Jenkinsfile
//   - Dockerfiles and compose files
//   - test fixtures, which are often large
//   - editor directories: .idea, .vscode
//   - lockfiles, when a library should not ship one
//   - .env files. Check for this specifically. It has happened.

// VERIFY THE SIZE, in CI
$ npm pack --dry-run
npm notice package size: 1.2 MB
npm notice unpacked size: 4.8 MB
// set a budget and fail the build if it is exceeded. A package that
// grows 10 MB in a release is usually a mistake, and it is invisible
// without this check.

// A PRINCIPLE WORTH STATING
//   a published artefact is a permanent, public commitment. Adding a
//   file is easy and removing one is a breaking change for anyone
//   deep-importing a path. So the file list is a public API, and it
//   deserves the same deliberation as your exported functions.</code></pre></div>
    <div class="ex"><h4>Exercises 2</h4><ol>
      <li>Run <code>npm pack --dry-run</code> or <code>python -m build</code> on one of your own packages and read the file list line by line. Remove everything a consumer does not need to import and run the function.</li>
      <li>Add a reproducible-build check to CI: build twice in clean containers on different runners and compare hashes. Fix every source of non-determinism it finds, starting with timestamps and file ordering.</li>
      <li>Convert one Go build to be reproducible with <code>-trimpath</code> and a pinned toolchain, and verify two builds produce identical hashes.</li>
      <li>Add a size budget to your release pipeline that fails when the artefact grows by more than a set percentage, and confirm it catches a deliberately added large file.</li>
      <li>Write a CI step that resolves every dependency in your lockfile and fails if any comes from an unexpected registry. Test it by pointing one dependency at a URL.</li>
    </ol></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
<h2 id="ch3" data-toc>3 Â· Signing, Provenance and Supply-Chain Security</h2>
    <div class="ch-toc"><button type="button" class="ch-toc-head" aria-expanded="true"><span class="arrow">â–¸</span>Contents &middot; 3 Â· Signing, Provenance and Supply-Chain Security</button><div class="ch-toc-list"><a href="#ch3-threats">What the threat model actually is</a><a href="#ch3-integrity">Integrity and checksums</a><a href="#ch3-signing">Signing the artefact</a><a href="#ch3-oidc">Short-lived credentials with OIDC</a><a href="#ch3-provenance">Provenance attestation</a><a href="#ch3-slsa">SLSA, in plain terms</a><a href="#ch3-2fa">Account security is the real boundary</a><a href="#ch3-verify">Verifying a dependency, and the limits</a><a href="#ch3-response">Responding to a compromise</a></div></div>
    <p class="topic-desc">Publishing is the moment your code stops being yours. This chapter is about what a consumer can verify, what you can prove, and the uncomfortable truth about where the trust boundary really sits.</p>
    <h3 id="ch3-threats" data-toc>What the threat model actually is</h3>
        <h4 id="ch3-threats-model">Threat model definition</h4>
    <p>Threat modeling identifies the assets, attack vectors, and adversaries relevant to your package. It answers: what can happen, how likely, and what the impact is. Without a model, security decisions are ad hoc.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Threat modeling, the four questions
// 1. What are we protecting? (assets, credentials, artefacts)
// 2. What can happen? ( threat actors, attack vectors)
// 3. What is the likelihood? (probability, frequency)
// 4. What is the impact? (business, reputational, legal)
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-threats-incidents">Notable incidents</h4>
    <p>The event-stream, ua-parser-js, coa, rc, colors, and faker incidents (2020-2022) share a common pattern: a maintainer's NPM token was phished via a "package migration" tool prompt, and a postinstall script exfiltrated environment variables and cloud credentials.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Incident pattern: event-stream (2022)
// Maintainer phished for "migration tool" token
// Postinstall script exfiltrated credentials and browser data
// Package had 2M+ weekly downloads
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-threats-defences">Defences that matter</h4>
    <p>The four threats in order of likelihood: 1. CREDENTIAL COMPROMISE (most common), 2. DEPENDENCY CONFUSION, 3. THE BUILD ITSELF, 4. THE REGISTRY ITSELF. Defences: OIDC, 2FA, lockfiles, and provenance attestation.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// The four threats, ordered by likelihood
// 1. CREDENTIAL COMPROMISE  (most common by wide margin)
// 2. DEPENDENCY CONFUSION
// 3. THE BUILD ITSELF
// 4. THE REGISTRY ITSELF
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the incidents, and what they actually were
//   event-stream, ua-parser-js, coa, rc, colors, faker: 2020-2022
//   the attacker got a maintainer's NPM token by phishing a login
//   prompt for a "package migration" tool
//   they published a new version with a postinstall script that exfiltrated
//   environment variables, cloud credentials and browser data
//   the package had 2 million weekly downloads
//
//   the token was LONG-LIVED and lived on a developer's laptop.
//   nothing about the registry or the package manager detected it.

// THE FOUR THREATS THAT MATTER, in order of likelihood

// 1. CREDENTIAL COMPROMISE
//    a stolen token, a phished maintainer, a leaked CI secret.
//    the most common by a wide margin.
//    this is why OIDC and 2FA matter more than signing.

// 2. DEPENDENCY CONFUSION
//    your internal package "utils" is public, and someone publishes
//    "utils" to public npm, and your build resolves to theirs.
//    a resolution hijack, an internal-registry confusion, a GitHub
//    action running on a fork.
//    the 2020 event-stream incident was a variant: a developer
//    switched a dependency from flat to nested and npm installed the
//    public version instead.

// 3. THE BUILD ITSELF
//    a compromised build runner, a malicious postinstall script in a
//    transitive dependency, a typosquatted package.
//    signing catches the last of these only if the build was clean.

// 4. THE REGISTRY ITSELF
//    a compromise of npm, PyPI or crates.io. Low probability, very
//    high impact. Signing your artefacts means a registry compromise
//    does not have to be the end of the story.

// WHAT EACH DEFENCE ACTUALLY BUYS YOU
//   checksums       integrity. the bytes are the bytes you published.
//   signing        authenticity. these bytes were signed by that key.
//   provenance     process. built by that workflow, from that source,
//                  at that commit, with that material.
//   short-lived creds  removes the stolen-token attack entirely.
//   2FA            raises the cost of account compromise.
//
//   note what none of them does: they do not make your code good, and
//   they do not stop a maintainer from publishing something bad on
//   purpose.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch3-integrity" data-toc>Integrity and checksums</h3>
        <h4 id="ch3-integrity-checksums">Checksums as baseline</h4>
    <p>Checksums prove the artefact has not changed since publication. npm verifies integrity hashes from package-lock.json on every install. PyPI uses --require-hashes. Go uses go.sum. These are the weakest defence: they do not prove authenticity.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// npm integrity hash in package-lock.json
"node_modules/express": {
    "version": "4.19.2",
    "resolved": "https://registry.npmjs.org/express/-/express-4.19.2.tgz",
    "integrity": "sha512-5/PsL6iGPdfQ/lKM1UuielYgv3BUoJfzti8kAvl8RV4Te2Jg=="
}

// PyPI --require-hashes
pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.txt
# sha256:...  package-a==1.2.3
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-integrity-limitations">Checksums do not prove authenticity</h4>
    <p>If the attacker has your token, they publish an artefact and the registry computes a valid checksum. The checksum is correct, but the content is hostile. Checksums are necessary but not sufficient.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Checksums prove integrity only, not authenticity
//   correct checksum + hostile content = valid but malicious package
//   this is why signing and provenance are separate properties
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-integrity-other-ecosystems">Checksums in other ecosystems</h4>
    <p>Cargo uses checksums in Cargo.lock, Go uses go.sum (mandatory), NuGet uses .snupkg with signature, Maven uses .asc files with -gpg. RubyGems uses .gem.aside the .gem.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Ecosystem checksum defaults
//   Go: go.sum (mandatory, checksums only)
//   Cargo: Cargo.lock (checksums)
//   npm: package-lock.json (exact versions + integrity hashes)
//   PyPI: --require-hashes (enforced by pip)
//   NuGet: .snupkg + signature
//   Maven: .asc + -gpg
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// CHECKSUMS, the baseline, and where you already have them

// npm: in package-lock.json, for every transitive dependency
  "node_modules/express": {
    "version": "4.19.2",
    "resolved": "https://registry.npmjs.org/express/-/express-4.19.2.tgz",
    "integrity": "sha512-5/PsL6iGPdfQ/lKM1UuielYgv3BUoJfzti8kAvl8RV4Te2Jg=="
  }
//   npm verifies this on every install. Change one byte and it fails.
//   This is a genuine, automatic supply-chain defence and it is why
//   committing a lockfile matters so much.

// PyPI: hashes are in the index metadata, and pip --require-hashes
//   enforces them
   pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.txt
# sha256:...  package-a==1.2.3
# sha256:...  --hash=sha256:abcdef...  package-a==1.2.3
#   with --require-hashes, pip refuses any package not pinned with a
#   hash. This is the Python equivalent of a lockfile, and most teams
#   do not use it.

// Cargo: checksums in Cargo.lock, verified on every build
// Go: go.sum, verified on every build
//   the strongest of the defaults, because go.sum is mandatory
// NuGet: a .snupkg with a signature, and packages.lock.json
// Maven: .asc signature files, verified with -gpg and
//   maven-gpg-plugin-plugin
// RubyGems: a .gem.asc signature beside the .gem

// WHAT CHECKSUMS DO NOT DO
//   they prove the artefact has not changed SINCE IT WAS PUBLISHED.
//   They do not prove it was published by you, and they do not prove
//   the contents are benign.
//   If the attacker has your token, they publish an artefact and the
//   registry computes a valid checksum for it. The checksum is
//   correct. The content is hostile.
//
//   CHECKSUMS ARE NOT ENOUGH. They are necessary, and they are the
//   weakest of the defences listed here.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch3-signing" data-toc>Signing the artefact</h3>
        <h4 id="ch3-signing-npm">npm provenance</h4>
    <p>npm publish --provenance generates a Sigstore attestation binding the artefact hash, the workflow file, git commit, and repository. Verify with <code>npm audit signatures</code>.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// npm publish with provenance
$ npm publish --provenance --access public
$ npm audit signatures
// checks every installed package against the public transparency log
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-signing-sigstore">Sigstore cross-ecosystem answer</h4>
    <p>Sigstore provides cross-ecosystem signing: <code>cosign sign-blob</code> and <code>cosign verify-blob</code> with OIDC-based identity. No key management required.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Sigstore keyless signing
$ cosign sign-blob --key env://COSIGN_PUBKEY dist/pkg-1.2.3.whl
$ cosign verify-blob --key env://COSIGN_PUBKEY \
    --certificate-identity https://github.com/yourorg/repo/.github/workflows/release.yml \
    --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
    dist/pkg-1.2.3.whl
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-signing-pleasesign">Why you should sign</h4>
    <p>The 2020-2022 npm compromise chain failed partly because signing was opt-in and nobody verified. A signed package that nobody checks is security theatre. If you sign, your CI should also verify signatures on the way in.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Why signing matters
//   Without verification, signing is security theatre
//   CI should verify every installed package signature
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// SIGNING says: "the holder of this private key vouched for these
// bytes". It is the boundary between "a package" and "a package from
// you".

// npm: provenance rather than a PGP signature
$ npm publish --provenance --access public
//   npm generates a Sigstore attestation binding:
//     the artefact hash
//     the publishing workflow file (from .github/workflows/release.yml)
//     the git commit and repository
//     the identity of the workflow runner
//   no key to manage, no key to leak, and it is verifiable with
//   npm audit signatures
$ npm audit signatures
//   checks every installed package against the public transparency log
//   the right verification tool, and almost nobody runs it

// PyPI: trusted publishing, which removes the API token entirely
//   in pyproject.toml
[project.optional-dependencies]
# or, in the dashboard: add a GitHub Actions publisher
//   you configure a one-time token on PyPI, give it a repository and
//   workflow, and PyPI mints a short-lived OIDC token per run.
//   there is no long-lived secret in your CI at all.
$ twine upload --repository pypi dist/*

// Sigstore, the cross-ecosystem answer
$ cosign sign-blob --key env://COSIGN_PUBKEY dist/pkg-1.2.3.whl
$ cosign verify-blob --key env://COSIGN_PUBKEY \
    --certificate-identity https://github.com/yourorg/repo/.github/workflows/release.yml \
    --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
    dist/pkg-1.2.3.whl
//   the two flags are the point: not "signed by someone" but "signed
//   by THAT workflow in THAT repo". A signature from an unexpected
//   pipeline is a verification failure.

// PGP, for ecosystems that still want it
$ gpg --detach-sign --armor dist/pkg-1.2.3.tar.gz
$ gpg --verify dist/pkg-1.2.3.tar.gz.asc
$ gpg --keyserver keyserver.ubuntu.com --recv-keys 0xDEADBEEF
//   publish the public key in a well-known location AND in your
//   repository, AND in a keyserver. One location is not enough: each
//   is a different failure mode.

// MANAGING KEYS, if you must use a long-lived key
//   - generate it in a hardware-backed store or an HSM, not on a laptop
//   - one key per project, not one key for everything
//   - back it up OFFLINE. Two copies, in two places, tested once a year
//   - have a revocation plan BEFORE you need it, and document the
//     public key fingerprint in your README so consumers can pin it
//   - rotate, and publish the new key before retiring the old
//   - NEVER store a signing key in the same repository as the code, or
//     in the same CI. That defeats the entire mechanism.</code></pre></div>
    <div class="warn"><p>The npm compromise chain of 2020â€“2022 failed partly because signing was opt-in and nobody verified. A signed package that nobody checks is security theatre. If you sign, your CI should also verify signatures on the way *in* â€” on every dependency in your lockfile â€” so a tampered transitive package fails your build rather than your production.</p></div>
    <h3 id="ch3-oidc" data-toc>Short-lived credentials with OIDC</h3>
    <p>This is the highest-value change most projects can make, and it removes the stolen-token attack class entirely.</p>
        <h4 id="ch3-oidc-problem">Problem: long-lived tokens</h4>
    <p>Long-lived NPM tokens stored in CI secrets, developer laptops, or shell history are valid for months. If phished, the attacker can publish as you indefinitely. This is the most common attack class.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Long-lived token (anti-pattern)
$ npm config set //registry.npmjs.org/:_authToken=npm_abc123...
// token valid for months, in CI secrets, history, screenshots
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-oidc-solution">OIDC solution</h4>
    <p>Your CI provider already has a short-lived identity token signed by the provider. Exchange it at the registry's token endpoint, which verifies the signature and checks repository/workflow matching. You get a credential valid for minutes with no secret to steal.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// OIDC exchange: short-lived, no secret
//   CI provider signs the token, registry verifies it
//   valid for minutes, no secret to steal
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-oidc-permissions">Required permissions</h4>
    <p>Only two permissions are needed: id-token: write (enables the exchange) and contents: read (all else you need). With only these, a compromised action can mint a publish token but not do more.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Minimal permissions block
permissions:
  id-token: write    # enables the OIDC exchange
  contents: read     # all else you need
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-oidc-residual">Residual risk</h4>
    <p>OIDC does not protect against an attacker who can push to your release branch or modify the release workflow. It protects against credential theft, which is the actual attack class.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// OIDC does not protect against:
//   - attacker pushing to release branch
//   - modified release workflow
// It does protect against: credential theft (phished password/TOTP)
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE PROBLEM WITH A LONG-LIVED TOKEN
   $ npm config set //registry.npmjs.org/:_authToken=npm_abc123...
   // that token is valid for months. It is in your CI secrets, in your
   // developer's ~/.npmrc, in shell history, in a screenshot in a bug
   // report. Anyone who copies it can publish as you, indefinitely.

// OIDC, the mechanism
//   your CI provider (GitHub Actions, GitLab, Buildkite, CircleCI)
//   already has a short-lived identity token, signed by the provider,
//   that names the repository, the workflow and the ref.
//   You exchange it at the registry's token endpoint, which verifies
//   the signature and checks that the repository and workflow match
//   what you pre-registered. You get a credential valid for minutes.
//   There is no secret to steal, because there is no secret.

// the permissions block, the key line
   permissions:
     id-token: write    # this is what enables the exchange
     contents: read     # and this is all else you need
//   with only id-token: write and contents: read, a compromised action
//   in your workflow can mint a publish token. With packages: write as
//   well, it could do more.

// PyPI trusted publishing, fully configured
//   1. PyPI dashboard, your project, Publishing -> add a GitHub
//      publisher: owner, repository, workflow filename, environment
//   2. pyproject.toml needs no change
   3. the workflow:
//     - uses: pypa/gh-action-pypi-publish@release/v1
//       with:
//         password: ${{ secrets.PYPI_API_TOKEN }}   omit this entirely
//   there is no token. The action exchanges the OIDC token.

// npm trusted publishing, as of 2025
//   npm supports trusted publishing on the registry side, configured in
//   the package settings, with a linked GitHub repository and
//   workflow. The workflow runs `npm publish` with no auth token set.
//   Check the current npm documentation, because this was newer than
//   the PyPI mechanism and the configuration surface has moved.

// THE RESIDUAL RISK, stated honestly
//   if an attacker can push to your release branch, or modify the
//   release workflow, they can trigger a legitimate publish.
//   OIDC does not protect against that. It protects against credential
//   theft, which is what actually happened.
//   Pair it with protected branches, signed tags, and an environment
//   that requires reviewer approval for releases.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch3-provenance" data-toc>Provenance attestation</h3>
    <p>Signing says who vouched. Provenance says how it was made: which workflow, from which commit, with which source, using which build definition.</p>
        <h4 id="ch3-prov-attestation">What provenance contains</h4>
    <p>Provenance says how the artefact was built: which workflow, from which commit, with which source, using which build definition. A typical SLSA provenance document includes buildDefinition, subject (artifact digests), and runDetails.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Provenance attestation structure
{
  "_type": "https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1",
  "subject": [{ "name": "pkg-1.2.3.tar.gz", "digest": { "sha256": "a3f5..." } }],
  "predicateType": "https://slsa.dev/provenance/v1",
  "predicate": {
    "buildDefinition": {
      "buildType": "https://github.com/actions/runner",
      "externalParameters": {
        "workflow": { "ref": "refs/heads/main", "repository": "https://github.com/yourorg/repo" }
      },
      "resolvedDependencies": [
        { "uri": "git+https://github.com/yourorg/repo@8f2a91",
          "digest": { "gitCommit": "8f2a91c4e7..." } }
      ]
    },
    "runDetails": {
      "builder": { "id": "https://github.com/actions/runner" },
      "metadata": { "invocationId": "..." }
    }
  }
}
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-prov-why-better">Why provenance is better than signing alone</h4>
    <p>Signing answers "was this signed by your key?" Provenance answers "was this built by your release workflow, from this commit, at this source?" A consumer can verify provenance without trusting the key, because identity comes from the CI provider's OIDC token.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Signing vs provenance
//   signing: "was this signed by your key?"
//   provenance: "was this built by your release workflow?"
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-prov-where-today">Where it exists today</h4>
    <p>npm publish --provenance, PyPI PEP 740, crates.io --attestations, Maven CycloneDX, containers cosign attest, NuGet signing. Consumers: $ npm audit signatures.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Ecosystems with provenance today
//   npm: npm publish --provenance
//   PyPI: PEP 740 attestations
//   crates.io: --attestations flag
//   Maven: CycloneDX via sigstore plugin
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WHAT A PROVENANCE ATTESTATION CONTAINS
{
  "_type": "https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1",
  "subject": [{ "name": "pkg-1.2.3.tar.gz", "digest": { "sha256": "a3f5..." } }],
  "predicateType": "https://slsa.dev/provenance/v1",
  "predicate": {
    "buildDefinition": {
      "buildType": "https://github.com/actions/runner",
      "externalParameters": {
        "workflow": { "ref": "refs/heads/main",
                      "repository": "https://github.com/yourorg/repo" }
      },
      "resolvedDependencies": [
        { "uri": "git+https://github.com/yourorg/repo@8f2a91",
          "digest": { "gitCommit": "8f2a91c4e7..." } }
      ]
    },
    "runDetails": {
      "builder": { "id": "https://github.com/actions/runner" },
      "metadata": { "invocationId": "..." }
    }
  }
}

// WHY IT IS BETTER THAN SIGNING ALONE
//   a signature answers "was this signed by your key?"
//   provenance answers "was this built by your release workflow, from
//   commit 8f2a91, of the source in this repository?"
//   a consumer can verify the second without trusting the key, because
//   the identity comes from the CI provider's OIDC token, which the
//   consumer can independently check against the provider.

// WHERE IT EXISTS TODAY
//   npm          npm publish --provenance. Stored in the public
//                Sigstore transparency log. Verify with npm audit
//                signatures.
//   PyPI         PEP 740 attestations. Generated by trusted publishing.
//   crates.io    crates support an --attestations flag
//   Maven        CycloneDX and in-toto attestations via
//                the sigstore plugin
//   containers    cosign attest, and the OCI referrers API
//   NuGet        signing, and newer attestation support
//   RubyGems     signing; full provenance is emerging

// THE CONSUMER SIDE, and it should be routine
$ npm audit signatures
# most packages with provenance: verified
# packages without: warn
$ npm ci --ignore-scripts
# and run a signature check in your own CI, per ch3-verify

// THE THING THAT MAKES THIS STRONG
//   the transparency log. Sigstore's Rekor log is append-only and
//   public, so a signature cannot be quietly retracted, and an entry
//   can be independently audited. An attacker who compromises your
//   publishing credential still leaves a public, permanent record.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch3-slsa" data-toc>SLSA, in plain terms</h3>
        <h4 id="ch3-slsa-levels">SLSA levels, in plain terms</h4>
    <p>SLSA levels describe how trustworthy a build's provenance is, which is a property of your pipeline, not of any one artefact.</p>
    <p>LEVEL 0/1: the artefact exists with a provenance document attached. Self-asserted, most of the ecosystem today.</p>
    <p>LEVEL 2: hosted build platform. Provenance generated by an isolated build service so the build cannot be tampered with by the submitting party. GitHub Actions with provenance enabled is in this category.</p>
    <p>LEVEL 3: hard to tamper with. Build definition is a first-class, separately reviewed artefact. Source, build file and build service are all pinned. Very few organisations today.</p>
    <p>LEVEL 4: hermetic and reproducible. Build is fully hermetic: no network, all dependencies declared, output reproducible from inputs alone. Two parties independently build and get byte-identical output. Nx and Debian aim for this.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// SLSA levels, from least to most trustworthy
//   Level 0/1: artefact exists, provenance self-asserted
//   Level 2: hosted build platform (GitHub Actions provenance)
//   Level 3: build definition under review
//   Level 4: hermetic + reproducible builds
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-slsa-achievable">What is achievable now</h4>
    <p>Steps 1-3 eliminate the credential-theft class, which is the class that actually caused every incident. Build in GitHub Actions, turn on provenance, sign your release tags.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Achievable now, in order of effort
//   1. Build in GitHub Actions, not on a laptop (hours)
//   2. Turn on provenance. npm: one flag. PyPI: dashboard checkbox (afternoon)
//   3. Sign your release tags (an hour)
//   4. Pin the build environment: fixed runner image, lockfile, no network (a day)
//   5. Make the build reproducible (a week, then maintenance)
//   6. Add a third-party verifier (a week)
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-slsa-steps">Steps 1-3 eliminate credential theft</h4>
    <p>Steps 1 to 3 eliminate the credential-theft class, which is the class that actually caused every incident in the record. Do those before worrying about level 4.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Steps 1-3: eliminate credential theft
//   1. Build in GitHub Actions, not on a laptop
//   2. Turn on provenance (npm one flag, PyPI dashboard)
//   3. Sign release tags
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// SLSA is a framework for build provenance. Its levels describe how
// trustworthy a build's provenance is, which is a property of your
// PIPELINE, not of any one artefact.

// THE LEVELS, and what each one means in practice

// BUILDS 0 and 1: the artefact exists
//   a provenance document is attached. It is self-asserted: the build
//   says what it did, and you are trusting that statement.
//   this is most of the ecosystem today. It is better than nothing.

// BUILD 2: hosted build platform
//   provenance is generated by a hosted, isolated build service, so
//   the build cannot be tampered with by the submitting party.
//   GitHub Actions with provenance enabled is in this category.
//   the properties you get: the build ran in an environment the
//   submitter does not control, and the provenance is unforgeable
//   without the platform's key.

// BUILD 3: hard to tamper with, and verifiable
//   the build definition is a first-class, separately reviewed artefact.
//   the source, the build file and the build service are all pinned.
//   a consumer can verify the chain end to end without trusting you.
//   this is very few organisations today, and the barrier is mostly
//   organisational rather than technical.

// BUILD 4: hermetic and reproducible
//   the build is fully hermetic: no network, all dependencies declared,
//   and the output is reproducible from the inputs alone.
//   two parties independently build and get byte-identical output.
//   this is what Nix and Debian aim for, and what makes the
//   vulnerability in ch2-artefact checkable rather than aspirational.

// WHAT IS ACHIEVABLE NOW, IN ORDER OF EFFORT
//   1. Build in GitHub Actions, not on a laptop. (hours)
//   2. Turn on provenance. npm: one flag. PyPI: dashboard checkbox.
//      (an afternoon)
//   3. Sign your release tags. (an hour)
//   4. Pin the build environment: a fixed runner image, a lockfile,
//      no network during the build. (a day)
//   5. Make the build reproducible. (a week, then maintenance)
//   6. Add a third-party verifier to your release process. (a week)
//
//   Steps 1 to 3 eliminate the credential-theft class, which is the
//   class that actually caused every incident in the record. Do those
//   before worrying about level 4.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch3-2fa" data-toc>Account security is the real boundary</h3>
        <h4 id="ch3-2fa-minimum">The minimum, and it is not optional</h4>
    <p>Every defence in this chapter assumes the attacker does not have your maintainer account. In every real incident, they did. So account security is the first control.</p>
    <p>Minimum requirements: 2FA on every registry account with a hardware key (YubiKey), a sign-in notification, and a separate account per person. No shared maintainer logins.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Minimum requirements for account security
//   - 2FA on every registry account, with a hardware key (YubiKey)
//   - a sign-in notification, treat unexpected as incident
//   - separate account per person, no shared maintainer logins
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-2fa-organisational">Organisational controls that actually work</h4>
    <p>Protected release environment requiring reviewer approval, signed tags, branch protection on the release branch, an audit log of publishes reviewed by someone other than the publisher, and a documented recovery path.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Organisational controls that work
//   - protected release environment requiring reviewer approval
//   - signed tags
//   - branch protection on release branch
//   - audit log of publishes, reviewed by someone other than publisher
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-2fa-bit-skipped">The bit people skip</h4>
    <p>Remove maintainers who have left every former maintainer is a permanent credential risk. Do it the day someone leaves, not at the next audit, and check every registry you have ever published to.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Remove departed maintainers
//   - the day someone leaves, not at the next audit
//   - check every registry you have ever published to
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE UNCOMFORTABLE TRUTH
//   every defence in this chapter assumes the attacker does not have
//   your maintainer account. In every real incident, they did.
//
//   so account security is not a footnote. It is the first control.

// THE MINIMUM, AND IT IS NOT OPTIONAL
   - 2FA on every registry account, with a hardware key (YubiKey or
     equivalent) rather than an app. TOTP is better than nothing; a
     phished session cookie defeats an app-based 2FA that the user
     clicks through, and a hardware key does not have that problem.
   - a sign-in notification, and treat one you did not initiate as an
     incident, not a nuisance
   - hardware-key-enforced for publishing, so a stolen password plus a
     phished TOTP is not sufficient
   - a separate account per person. No shared maintainer logins. The
     event-stream maintainer had a single point of failure in a person.

// THE ORGANISATIONAL CONTROLS THAT ACTUALLY WORK
   - a protected release environment requiring reviewer approval, so
     one person cannot publish
   - signed tags, so a release identifies its author cryptographically
   - branch protection on the release branch
   - an audit log of publishes, reviewed by someone who is not the
     person doing the publishing
   - a documented recovery path: who to email when an account is
     compromised, and how the registry's recovery works. Find out
     BEFORE you need it. PyPI recovery is email-based and goes through
     a domain administrator; npm's goes through support.

// THE BIT PEOPLE SKIP
//   remove maintainers who have left. Every former maintainer is a
//   permanent credential risk. Do it the day someone leaves, not at the
//   next audit, and check every registry you have ever published to,
//   because the one you forgot about is the one that gets used.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch3-verify" data-toc>Verifying a dependency, and the limits</h3>
        <h4 id="ch3-verify-what">What you can verify</h4>
    <p>You can verify: the bytes are the bytes published, those bytes were signed by a key you have independently obtained, the artefact was built by a named workflow from a named commit. You cannot verify: the code is safe, the maintainer is trustworthy, the build was not compromised.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// What you CAN verify
//   - bytes match what was published
//   - bytes signed by independently-obtained key
//   - built by named workflow, from named commit

// What you CANNOT verify
//   - code is safe
//   - maintainer is trustworthy
//   - build was not compromised
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-verify-pipeline">Verification pipeline in CI</h4>
    <p>A CI pipeline that checks every package resolved from expected registries, verifies provenance where expected, and checks for lifecycle scripts. Run on every push and pull request.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Verification pipeline in CI
name: verify-supply-chain
on: [push, pull_request]
jobs:
  verify:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with: { node-version: 20 }
      - run: node scripts/check-registries.mjs
      - name: verify npm provenance
        run: npm audit signatures --json > sigs.json
      - run: |
        node -e '
          const s = require("./sigs.json");
          const missing = Object.entries(s.packages ?? {})
            .filter(([name, v]) => !v.verified && v.attestations === undefined)
            .map(([name]) => name);
          if (missing.length) { console.error("no signature or provenance: " + missing.join(", ")); process.exit(1); }
        '
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-verify-ignorescripts">--ignore-scripts is highest-value flag</h4>
    <p>The payloads in event-stream and ua-parser-js were postinstall scripts. Blocking install scripts blocks most supply-chain malware, at the cost of a small number of legitimate packages that need a native build step.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// --ignore-scripts is the highest-value single flag
$ npm ci --ignore-scripts
// If you cannot turn it on globally, turn it on in CI and allow exceptions explicitly
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WHAT YOU CAN VERIFY, AND WHAT YOU CANNOT
//
// CAN:   the bytes are the bytes that were published
// CAN:   those bytes were signed by a key whose fingerprint you have
//        independently obtained
// CAN:   the artefact was built by a named workflow, in a named repo,
//        from a named commit
// CANNOT: the code is safe
// CANNOT: the maintainer is trustworthy
// CANNOT: the build was not itself compromised
// CANNOT: the dependency is needed at all, or is not malicious in a
//         way that looks like good code
//
// SAY THIS TO YOUR TEAM, because "we verify signatures" gets quoted
// back as "our dependencies are safe" and that is not what it means.

// THE VERIFICATION PIPELINE, in CI
name: verify-supply-chain
on: [push, pull_request]

jobs:
  verify:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with: { node-version: 20 }

      # 1. every package resolved from a registry we expect
      - run: node scripts/check-registries.mjs

      # 2. provenance where it is expected to exist
      - name: verify npm provenance
        run: npm audit signatures --json > sigs.json
      - run: |
          node -e '
            const s = require("./sigs.json");
            const missing = Object.entries(s.packages ?? {})
              .filter(([name, v]) => !v.verified &amp;&amp; v.attestations === undefined)
              .map(([name]) => name);
            if (missing.length) {
              console.error("no signature or provenance: " + missing.join(", "));
              process.exit(1);
            }
          '

      # 3. no install scripts, which is where most compromise payloads live
      - name: no lifecycle scripts
        run: |
          npm ci --ignore-scripts
          if grep -rE '"(pre|post)install"' package-lock.json; then
            echo "a dependency runs an install script - audit it"; exit 1
          fi

      # 4. the audit database, for known CVEs
      - run: npm audit --audit-level=high

// WHY --ignore-scripts IS THE HIGHEST-VALUE SINGLE FLAG
//   the payloads in event-stream and ua-parser-js were postinstall
//   scripts. Blocking install scripts blocks the execution of most
//   supply-chain malware, at the cost of a small number of legitimate
//   packages that need a native build step.
//   If you cannot turn it on globally, turn it on in CI and allow the
//   exceptions explicitly, in a file, reviewed.

// THE HONEST LIMIT OF ALL OF THIS
//   you have not removed trust from the system. You have made it
//   specific, checkable and revocable, which is the most that is
//   achievable without running the entire ecosystem from source. The
//   remaining trust is in the maintainers and the registry, and it
//   should be reduced deliberately: fewer dependencies, and more of
//   them first-party.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch3-response" data-toc>Responding to a compromise</h3>
        <h4 id="ch3-response-minute-0">Minute 0: stop the bleeding</h4>
    <p>Yank the malicious version immediately, revoke the publish token at every registry, invalidate CI secrets, and assume any CI secret is compromised if a maintainer account was compromised.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Minute 0: stop the bleeding
//   - yank the malicious version immediately
//   - revoke the publish token at every registry you use
//   - invalidate the CI secrets if the build was the entry point
//   - assume any CI secret is compromised if a maintainer account was compromised
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-response-minute-5">Minute 5-30: work out the scope</h4>
    <p>Read the payload exactly. Determine which versions were affected (every version published while the credential was in the attacker's hands). Be honest: if you do not know, say so publicly.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Minute 5-30: work out the scope
//   - read the payload, do not guess
//   - which versions were affected?
//   - was the build pipeline modified?
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-response-hour-1">Hour 1-6: notify</h4>
    <p>Notify: npm audit advisory, PyPI PSRT, RustSec database, GitHub Security tab, and YOUR USERS directly. Silence turns an incident into a breach.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Hour 1-6: notify
//   - npm: npm audit advisory
//   - PyPI: report to PSRT, python-security@python.org
//   - GitHub: advisory in the repository's Security tab
//   - YOUR USERS directly: changelog, advisory, email, blog post
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch3-response-day-1">Day 1-7: recover</h4>
    <p>Publish a fixed version. Consider whether previous good versions are still safe to pin. If the attack was a maintainer-account compromise, an earlier version published before the compromise is probably fine. Explicitly say so.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Day 1-7: recover
//   - publish a fixed version
//   - consider whether earlier versions are safe to pin
//   - rotate every credential you touched
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// IF ONE OF YOUR PACKAGES IS COMPROMISED, this is the order.
// Deciding in advance is worth an hour, because you will not think
// clearly in the first ten minutes.

// MINUTE 0 TO 5: STOP THE BLEEDING
   - yank the malicious version immediately. Every registry supports it.
   - revoke the publish token, at every registry you use
   - invalidate the CI secrets if the build was the entry point
   - assume any CI secret is compromised if a maintainer account was

// MINUTE 5 TO 30: WORK OUT THE SCOPE
   - what exactly did the payload do? Read it. Do not guess.
   - which versions were affected? Every version published while the
     credential was in the attacker's hands, not just the malicious one.
   - did the build pipeline run attacker code? If the release workflow
     was modified, assume the previous release is also suspect.
//   - an honest answer here is usually "we do not know yet". Say that
//     publicly rather than staying silent.

// HOUR 1 TO 6: NOTIFY
   - npm: npm audit advisory, and the registry has a security process
   - PyPI: report to the PSRT, python-security@python.org
   - Rust: crates.io has a yank-and-report flow; the advisory goes to
     the RustSec database
   - GitHub: advisory in the repository's Security tab
   - YOUR USERS DIRECTLY. A changelog entry and an advisory reach
     developers. An email, a blog post, and a HackerOne report reach
     everyone else. Silence is what turns an incident into a breach.

// DAY 1 TO 7: RECOVER
   - publish a fixed version
   - consider whether the previous good version is still safe to pin.
     If the attack was a maintainer-account compromise, an earlier
     version published before the compromise is probably fine. Say so
     explicitly, so people know whether to downgrade or upgrade.
//   - rotate every credential you touched, and any that shared a
//     mechanism with the one that leaked
//   - turn on 2FA, OIDC and provenance for this package before the
//     next release. Ship the fix and the hardening together.

// AFTERWARDS
   - write the postmortem publicly, including the timeline and the
     decisions you got wrong. The ecosystem learns from these, and
     "we were lucky" is not a finding.
//   - check whether your dependency count is a contributing factor. The
//     event-stream payload ran in the browser; a server package in a
//     container behaves differently, and that difference is design
//     information worth acting on.</code></pre></div>
    <div class="ex"><h4>Exercises 3</h4><ol>
      <li>Enable provenance on one package you publish and verify it with <code>npm audit signatures</code> and, if you use Python, the PEP 740 attestation. Read the statement and confirm it names the workflow and commit you expect.</li>
      <li>Set up a Sigstore keyless signing step and verify the artefact, including the <code>--certificate-identity</code> and <code>--certificate-oidc-issuer</code> flags. Then verify a package you did not build, and read its provenance.</li>
      <li>Configure trusted publishing for one project, remove the long-lived API token from your CI, and confirm the publish still works. Then confirm a workflow from a fork cannot publish.</li>
      <li>Write the CI verification pipeline from this chapter for your ecosystem and run it against your own lockfile. Record which of your current dependencies have no provenance.</li>
      <li>Write your own compromise runbook: the yank command, the token revocation path, the advisory contacts, and the person who decides to publish. Time yourself against a 30-minute target.</li>
    </ol></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
<h2 id="ch4" data-toc>4 Â· JavaScript: npm, jsr and the CDN ecosystems</h2>
    <div class="ch-toc"><button type="button" class="ch-toc-head" aria-expanded="true"><span class="arrow">â–¸</span>Contents &middot; 4 Â· JavaScript: npm, jsr and the CDN ecosystems</button><div class="ch-toc-list"><a href="#ch4-shape">The shape of an npm package</a><a href="#ch4-publish">Publishing to npm</a><a href="#ch4-dist">dist-tags, versions and the release dance</a><a href="#ch4-deps">Dependencies and peer dependencies</a><a href="#ch4-dual">Dual publishing: ESM and CommonJS</a><a href="#ch4-jsr">JSR, and TypeScript-first packages</a><a href="#ch4-browsers">CDN distribution and import maps</a><a href="#ch4-unpkg">The no-build path</a><a href="#ch4-mcp">Untrusted publishing and the new protections</a><a href="#ch4-trouble">Troubleshooting</a></div></div>
    <p class="topic-desc">npm is the largest package registry in the world and the most-attacked, and its tooling is correspondingly sophisticated. The mechanics are simple; the conventions are where the difficulty is.</p>
    <h3 id="ch4-shape" data-toc>The shape of an npm package</h3>
        <h4 id="ch4-shape-exports">exports field</h4>
    <p>The exports field enforces the public API. Deep-imports outside the exports field stop resolving for consumers. This is a breaking change for anyone deep-importing, so do it in a major release.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Enforce the public API with exports
"exports": {
  ".": { "types": "./dist/index.d.ts", "import": "./dist/index.js" }
}
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-shape-type">"type" field reach</h4>
    <p>"type": "module" makes every .js in the package an ES module. A package without it is CommonJS, and an ESM consumer importing it gets an ERR_REQUIRE_ESM or named-export error. Get this right on day one.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// "type": "module" makes all .js ES modules
"type": "module"

// Without it, package is CommonJS; ESM import fails
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-shape-files">"files" field</h4>
    <p>Without "files", npm uses .gitignore, and then everything you have ignored is published too. With "files", the list is authoritative. Always set it.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Always set the "files" field
"files": ["dist", "README.md", "LICENSE"]
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// package.json, the fields that matter for publishing
{
  "name": "@yourorg/http-client",     // scoped, so the namespace is yours
  "version": "1.4.2",
  "description": "A small HTTP client with retries and typed responses.",
  "license": "Apache-2.0",
  "type": "module",                     // marks this package's .js as ESM
  "main": "./dist/index.cjs",           // CJS entry, for older consumers
  "module": "./dist/index.js",          // ESM entry, for bundlers
  "types": "./dist/index.d.ts",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "types": "./dist/index.d.ts",
      "import": "./dist/index.js",
      "require": "./dist/index.cjs"
    },
    "./package.json": "./package.json"
  },
  "files": ["dist", "README.md", "LICENSE"],
  "sideEffects": false,                 // lets bundlers tree-shake
  "engines": { "node": ">=18" },
  "repository": { "type": "git", "url": "git+https://github.com/yourorg/http-client.git" },
  "publishConfig": { "access": "public", "provenance": true },
  "scripts": {
    "prepublishOnly": "npm run build &amp;&amp; npm test",
    "test": "vitest run"
  }
}

// THE THREE FIELDS PEOPLE GET WRONG

// 1. "files", or the absence of it
//    without "files", npm uses .gitignore, and then everything you have
//    ignored is published too. With "files", the list is authoritative.
//    ALWAYS set it.

// 2. "exports", and the deep-import break
    "exports": { ".": "./dist/index.js" }
//   this ENFORCES the public API. ./dist/internal.js stops resolving
//   for consumers. That is the point, and it is a breaking change for
//   anyone deep-importing, so do it in a major release and note it.
//   the escape hatch that keeps it working for most people:
    "exports": {
      ".": "./dist/index.js",
      "./package.json": "./package.json"
    }

// 3. "type", the field with the longest reach
//    "type": "module" makes every .js in the package an ES module.
//    a package without it is CommonJS, and an ESM consumer importing
//    it gets an ERR_REQUIRE_ESM or a named-export error.
//    get this right on day one. Changing it later is a major break for
//    half your consumers.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch4-publish" data-toc>Publishing to npm</h3>
        <h4 id="ch4-publish-sequence">The publish sequence</h4>
    <p>The standard publish sequence: login, version bump, publish with provenance. Always run npm pack --dry-run first to read the file list.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Standard publish sequence
$ npm login                        # or use a token in CI
$ npm version patch                # 1.4.1 â†’ 1.4.2, commits and tags
$ npm publish --provenance --access public

// A proper release, which is what you should actually do
$ npm ci
$ npm run build
$ npm test
$ npm pack --dry-run               // READ THE FILE LIST
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-publish-changelog">Changelog tooling</h4>
    <p>Run the changelot tooling to update CHANGELOG.md and package.json, then commit the release.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Changelot release tooling
$ npx changeset version            # updates CHANGELOG.md and package.json
$ npx changeset git                // commits the release
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-publish-two-package">Two-package pattern</h4>
    <p>npm couples packages only via the lockfile, so a patch release of @yourorg/core can be done without @yourorg/plugin. But git tags for a monorepo need the changesets flow.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Two-package pattern: core can be released independently
// But monorepo git tags need changesets flow (ch2-monorepo)
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE SEQUENCE
$ npm login                        # or use a token in CI
$ npm version patch                # 1.4.1 -&gt; 1.4.2, commits and tags
$ npm publish --provenance --access public

// A PROPER RELEASE, which is what you should actually do

// 1. build and verify
$ npm ci
$ npm run build
$ npm test
$ npm pack --dry-run               // READ THE FILE LIST

// 2. run the changelog tooling, if you use it
$ npx changeset version            # updates CHANGELOG.md and package.json
$ npx changeset git                // commits the release

// 3. publish
$ npm publish --provenance --access public

// 4. tag and push
$ git push origin main --follow-tags

// 5. publish the GitHub release with the changelog section

// THE TWO-PACKAGE PATTERN, and why npm forces you into it
//   you want a patch release of @yourorg/core without releasing
//   @yourorg/plugin. npm couples them only via the lockfile, so this
//   is fine. But git tags for a monorepo need the changesets flow
//   (ch2-monorepo) to be correct.

// WHAT "access" MEANS
   scoped packages default to RESTRICTED (private), unscoped to public
   --access public is required for a scoped public package
   restricted requires a paid plan

// THE FIRST 24 HOURS
   npm unpublish @yourorg/pkg@1.4.2       // within 72 hours, if you own it
   after 72 hours you can only deprecate, never unpublish
   deprecation:
     $ npm deprecate @yourorg/pkg@1.4.0 "security fix in 1.4.1, please upgrade"
   the package still installs; npm shows the warning on install and in
   `npm info`. This is usually what you want, and it is much more
   considerate than yanking.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch4-dist" data-toc>dist-tags, versions and the release dance</h3>
        <h4 id="ch4-dist-disttags">dist-tags are mutable pointers</h4>
    <p>dist-tags like "latest" are mutable pointers that can be repointed after publish. The default tag is "latest". Use --tag beta for pre-releases and --tag lts for long-term support.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// dist-tags are mutable pointers
$ npm dist-tag ls @yourorg/core
latest: 1.4.2
next:   2.0.0-beta.3
lts:    1.3.9

$ npm install @yourorg/core            // resolves "latest" â†’ 1.4.2
$ npm install @yourorg/core@next       // 2.0.0-beta.3
$ npm install @yourorg/core@1.3.9      // pinned, by version
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-dist-beta">Beta release pattern</h4>
    <p>The proper beta release uses --tag beta. Without it, the beta becomes "latest" and every user gets it. Always add a CI check.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Beta release, done properly
$ npm version prerelease --preid=beta   # 1.4.2 â†’ 1.4.3-beta.0
$ npm publish --tag beta                // note --tag. Without it, beta becomes "latest"
$ npm dist-tag add @yourorg/core@1.4.2 latest   // put latest back
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-dist-failure">The failure that happens every year</h4>
    <p>Someone runs npm publish on a prerelease version without --tag beta, and for a few hours every npm install in the world gets the beta. A CI check is the fix.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// CI check to prevent the failure
$ if [[ "" == *-* ]] && [ "" != "beta" ]; then
    echo "prerelease published without --tag beta"; exit 1
  fi
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// dist-tags are mutable pointers, and the default one is "latest"
$ npm dist-tag ls @yourorg/core
latest: 1.4.2
next:   2.0.0-beta.3
lts:    1.3.9

$ npm install @yourorg/core            // resolves "latest" -&gt; 1.4.2
$ npm install @yourorg/core@next       // 2.0.0-beta.3
$ npm install @yourorg/core@1.3.9      // pinned, by version

// THE BETA RELEASE, done properly
$ npm version prerelease --preid=beta   # 1.4.2 -&gt; 1.4.3-beta.0
$ npm publish --tag beta                # note --tag. WITHOUT IT the
                                         # beta becomes "latest" and
                                         # every user gets it.
$ npm dist-tag add @yourorg/core@1.4.2 latest   // put latest back

// THE FAILURE THAT HAPPENS EVERY YEAR
//   someone runs `npm publish` on a prerelease version without
//   --tag beta, and for a few hours every `npm install` in the world
//   gets the beta. Adding a CI check is the fix:
   - run: |
       TAG=$(node -p "require('./package.json').version")
       if [[ "$TAG" == *-* ]] &amp;&amp; [ "${{ }}..." != "beta" ]; then
         echo "prerelease published without --tag beta"; exit 1
       fi

// THE PATCH-TO-MAJOR FLOW
   1.4.2  current stable
   1.5.0  new feature, backwards compatible
   2.0.0-beta.0   the breaking change, under the next tag
   2.0.0-beta.3
   2.0.0          latest -&gt; 2.0.0
   1.5.1          a patch on the old line, if you still support it
//   keep the previous major maintained for a stated period. npm's
//   dist-tag "lts" exists for exactly this, and using it is a courtesy
//   your users notice.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch4-deps" data-toc>Dependencies and peer dependencies</h3>
        <h4 id="ch4-deps-dependencies">dependencies field</h4>
    <p>"dependencies" are installed with your package and required for it to work. They are brought in as transitive dependencies and must be satisfied for your package to function.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// dependencies: required at runtime
"dependencies": {
  "react": "^18.0.0 || ^19.0.0"
}
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-deps-devdependencies">devDependencies field</h4>
    <p>"devDependencies" are not installed for consumers; they are for you only. They are pinned by the lockfile, not the range. Do not write "*".</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// devDependencies: not for consumers
"devDependencies": {
  "typescript": "^5.4.0"      // caret in devDeps is correct
}
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-peerdependencies">peerDependencies field</h4>
    <p>"peerDependencies" are NOT installed. The consumer provides them. This matters when the host application and your package must share ONE instance, e.g. a React component library bundling React.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// peerDependencies: consumer provides
"peerDependencies": {
  "react": "^18.0.0 || ^19.0.0",
  "react-dom": "^18.0.0 || ^19.0.0"
},
"peerDependenciesMeta": {
  "react-dom": { "optional": true }
}
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-optionaldependencies">optionalDependencies field</h4>
    <p>"optionalDependencies" are installed if available, failure is tolerated. They are useful for optional native bindings or features that may not be available on all platforms.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// optionalDependencies: installed if available, failure tolerated
"optionalDependencies": {
  "sharp": "^0.32.0"
}
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE FOUR FIELDS, and getting this wrong breaks installs

  "dependencies"         installed with your package, and required
                         for it to work
  "devDependencies"      not installed for consumers; for you only
  "peerDependencies"     NOT installed. The consumer provides it.
  "optionalDependencies" installed if available, failure is tolerated
  "peerDependenciesMeta" marks a peer as optional

// WHEN TO USE A PEER DEPENDENCY
//   when the host application and your package must share ONE instance.
//   the case that matters: a React component library. If your package
//   bundles its own React, the consumer gets two Reacts, hooks fail
//   with "Invalid hook call", and it is very hard to debug.
   "peerDependencies": {
     "react": "^18.0.0 || ^19.0.0",
     "react-dom": "^18.0.0 || ^19.0.0"
   },
   "peerDependenciesMeta": {
     "react-dom": { "optional": true }
   }

// THE RANGE IN A PEER, and it is a promise about your consumers
   "^18.0.0 || ^19.0.0"     you have tested both. say so.
//   ">=16"                  you have tested some future version. you
//                            have not, and a break will find you.
//   "*"                     you make no compatibility claim, which is
//                            both useless and, in most ecosystems,
//                            a guideline violation.

// devDependencies, and the mistake everyone makes
   "devDependencies": {
     "typescript": "^5.4.0"      // the caret in devDeps is correct
   }
//   devDependencies versions are pinned by the LOCKFILE, not the
//   range, so the caret here matters less. But do not write "*":
//   your own CI then changes under you.

// THE PUBLICATION CHECK
   npm publish warns if a dependency is missing from package.json even
   though you import it. That warning catches the most common packaging
//   bug: a file that works for you because of a hoisted transitive
//   dependency that the consumer does not have.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch4-dual" data-toc>Dual publishing: ESM and CommonJS</h3>
        <h4 id="ch4-dual-build-both">Build both ESM and CJS</h4>
    <p>Node treats .js as CommonJS unless the nearest package.json says "type": "module". Bundlers prefer ESM. A modern package shipping only ESM breaks require() consumers, and a CJS-only package gets named export errors in Node ESM.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Build both ESM and CJS with tsup/rollup/esbuild
{
  "scripts": { "build": "tsup" },
  "type": "module"
}
// tsup.config.ts
export default defineConfig({
  entry: ['src/index.ts'],
  format: ['esm', 'cjs'],
  dts: true,
  sourcemap: true,
  clean: true,
  target: 'node18',
});
// this emits: dist/index.js ESM, dist/index.cjs CJS, dist/index.d.ts
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-dual-esm-trap">ESM interop trap</h4>
    <p>A CJS module can only export ONE thing to a named ESM import. module.exports = { foo, bar } works in most bundlers but fails in Node ESM named-export detection over source text. The fix: publish real ESM. Do not rely on the heuristic.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// ESM interop trap: named export from CJS
module.exports = { foo, bar }
// importing { foo } from "pkg" works in most bundlers
// but in Node's ESM the named-export detection is a heuristic over source text
// and it fails on: module.exports = { ...require('./other'), extra: 1 }
// symptom: SyntaxError: The requested module 'pkg' does not provide an export named 'foo'
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-dual-verify-both">Verify both paths</h4>
    <p>Run both entry points in CI. A package that only ever tests its ESM build will break CJS consumers without anyone noticing until a bug report.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Verify both entry points in CI
$ node -e "const m = require('./dist/index.cjs'); console.log(Object.keys(m))"
$ node --input-type=module -e "
  import { foo } from './dist/index.js';
  console.log(typeof foo);
"
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE PROBLEM
//   Node treats .js as CommonJS unless the nearest package.json says
//   "type": "module". Bundlers prefer ESM. So a modern package that
//   ships only ESM breaks require() consumers, and a package that
//   ships only CJS gets a "named export not found" error in Node ESM.

// THE SOLUTION: build both, publish both
// tsup, rollup, or esbuild. The configuration is short:
{
  "scripts": { "build": "tsup" },
  "type": "module"
}
// tsup.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from 'tsup';
export default defineConfig({
  entry: ['src/index.ts'],
  format: ['esm', 'cjs'],
  dts: true,
  sourcemap: true,
  clean: true,
  target: 'node18',
});
// this emits:
//   dist/index.js     ESM
//   dist/index.cjs    CommonJS, with the .cjs extension
//   dist/index.d.ts

// THE ESM INTEROP TRAP, which produces a very confusing error
//   a CJS module can only export ONE thing to a named ESM import:
     module.exports = { foo, bar }
   // importing { foo } from "pkg" works in most bundlers
   // but in Node's ESM the CJS named-export detection is a heuristic
   // over the source text, and it fails on:
     module.exports = { ...require('./other'), extra: 1 }
   // symptom: SyntaxError: The requested module 'pkg' does not
   // provide an export named 'foo'
   // THE FIX: publish real ESM. Do not rely on the heuristic.

// VERIFY BOTH PATHS
$ node -e "const m = require('./dist/index.cjs'); console.log(Object.keys(m))"
$ node --input-type=module -e "
  import { foo } from './dist/index.js';
  console.log(typeof foo);
"
// run both in CI. A package that only ever tests its ESM build will
// break CJS consumers without anyone noticing until a bug report.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch4-jsr" data-toc>JSR, and TypeScript-first packages</h3>
        <h4 id="ch4-jsr-types">Every exported symbol must have an explicit type</h4>
    <p>No inference on an exported function's return type, or the types are unavailable to consumers. All exported functions, classes, and interfaces must have type annotations.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Every exported symbol needs an explicit type
export async function retry<T>(
  operation: () => Promise<T>,
  options?: RetryOptions,
): Promise<T> { /* ... */ }

// Return type cannot be inferred and omitted
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-jsr-docjs">Document with JSDoc @param and @returns</h4>
    <p>Generated types and docs come from JSDoc that includes @param and @returns. No default exports in a library; use named exports.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Document with JSDoc including @param and @returns
/**
 * Retry an operation with exponential backoff.
 *
 * @param operation The operation to run. It should throw on failure.
 * @param options Retry configuration.
 * @returns The result of the first successful attempt.
 * @throws If every attempt fails.
 */
export async function retry(operation: () => Promise, options?: RetryOptions): Promise {
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-jsr-named-exports">No default exports</h4>
    <p>No default exports in a library; names collide across import styles. Use named exports only.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// No default exports in a library
export { retry }
// never: default export
// never: module.exports = { retry }
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// JSR (jsr.io) is a newer registry from the Deno team, with two
// differences that matter: it publishes TypeScript directly, and it
// scores packages on documentation and type coverage.

// WHY IT EXISTS
//   npm requires a build step before publishing, which means a JS
//   package cannot be consumed as source. JSR publishes the source
//   with types, and the consumer's tooling does the transformation.
//   The result is faster, better-typed, and a better DX for the
//   consumer's editor.

// HOW A TYPED PACKAGE NEEDS TO BE WRITTEN
//   1. every exported symbol must have an explicit type. No inference
//      on an exported function's return type, or the types are
//      unavailable to consumers.
//   2. document with JSDoc that includes @param and @returns. The
//      generated types and the docs come from this.
//   3. no default exports in a library, because the names collide
//      across import styles. Use named exports.

/**
 * Retry an operation with exponential backoff.
 *
 * @param operation The operation to run. It should throw on failure.
 * @param options Retry configuration.
 * @returns The result of the first successful attempt.
 * @throws If every attempt fails.
 */
export async function retry&lt;T&gt;(
  operation: () =&gt; Promise&lt;T&gt;,
  options?: RetryOptions,
): Promise&lt;T&gt; { /* ... */ }

// SCORING, and why it improves libraries
//   JSR scores a package on documentation completeness, type coverage,
//   and whether it builds. A package with no JSDoc scores poorly and
//   ranks lower. It is a quality gate by incentive, which is more
//   effective than a review process.

// USING IT FROM NODE AND BROWSERS
   npx jsr add @yourorg/http-client
   // and in a browser, no build step at all:
   &lt;script type="module"&gt;
     import { retry } from "https://jsr.io/@yourorg/http-client";
   &lt;/script&gt;

// THE PRACTICAL POSITION, and it is not either/or
//   publish to npm for the widest compatibility, because every
//   bundler, every CI system and every corporate firewall handles it
//   publish to JSR as well if your audience writes Deno or wants
//   source-level types. Most serious projects do both, with the npm
//   artefact built and the JSR artefact source.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch4-browsers" data-toc>CDN distribution and import maps</h3>
        <h4 id="ch4-browsers-cdn">CDN shims for browser distribution</h4>
    <p>Three CDN shims exist: esm.sh (most capable, can pin target and bundle), jsdelivr (fast, dumb, popular), and unpkg (file server, /browse). Esm.sh can resolve subpath exports and pin a target.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Three CDN shims
//   esm.sh    bundles for exact target, resolves subpath exports, can pin target: ?target=es2022, ?bundle
//   jsdelivr  pure file server with /+esm for on-the-fly ESM conversion, /npm/ prefix for bare specifiers
//   unpkg    file server only, /browse for exploring what is in the package
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-browsers-importmap">Import maps for pinning versions</h4>
    <p>Import maps allow bare specifiers to resolve with no bundler. Browsers have supported import maps since 2023. List pinned versions in the imports object.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Import map for pinning versions
<script type="importmap">
{
  "imports": {
    "react": "https://esm.sh/react@18.3.1",
    "react-dom/client": "https://esm.sh/react-dom@18.3.1/client",
    "date-fns": "https://esm.sh/date-fns@4.1.0"
  }
}</script>
<script type="module">
  import { format } from "date-fns";
</script>
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-browsers-decision">Library decision: CDN or not</h4>
    <p>A library that works from a CDN gets a try-before-install audience. If your package has a build step and cannot be consumed as source, you have given that up. "exports", a proper "browser" or "module" entry, and ESM output with no Node built-ins in the browser path make it work.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Decision: library that works from a CDN
//   gets a try-before-install audience
//   needs: "exports", "browser"/"module" entry, ESM output, no Node built-ins
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// PUBLISHING A PACKAGE THAT WORKS IN A BROWSER, NO BUILD STEP
//   this is a genuine differentiator for a utility library, and it is
//   how you reach people who are not using a bundler.

   &lt;script type="module"&gt;
     import { formatDate } from "https://esm.sh/date-fns@4";
     import debounce from "https://esm.sh/lodash-es@4.17.21";
   &lt;/script&gt;

// THE THREE CDN SHIMS, and what each does
//   esm.sh    bundles for the exact target, resolves subpath exports,
//             can pin a target: ?target=es2022, and ?bundle for a
//             single file. Most capable.
//   jsdelivr  a pure file server with /+esm for on-the-fly ESM
//             conversion, and a /npm/ prefix for bare specifiers.
//             Very fast, very dumb, which is why it is popular.
//   unpkg     a file server only, /browse for exploring what is in the
//             package. It is how you discover what a package actually
//             contains when a deep import fails.

// THE IMPORT MAP, for pinning versions without a build
   &lt;script type="importmap"&gt;
     {
       "imports": {
         "react": "https://esm.sh/react@18.3.1",
         "react-dom/client": "https://esm.sh/react-dom@18.3.1/client",
         "date-fns": "https://esm.sh/date-fns@4.1.0"
       }
     }
   &lt;/script&gt;
   &lt;script type="module"&gt;
     import { format } from "date-fns";
   &lt;/script&gt;
//   browsers have supported import maps since 2023, so bare specifiers
//   resolve with no bundler at all. This is a real option for a
//   documentation site or an internal tool, and it is not widely
//   adopted yet.

// THE DECISION
//   a library that works from a CDN gets a try-before-install audience,
//   which is how most developers actually evaluate. If your package
//   has a build step and cannot be consumed as source, you have given
//   that up for nothing.
//   "exports", a proper "browser" or "module" entry, and ESM output
//   with no Node built-ins in the browser path are what make it work.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch4-unpkg" data-toc>The no-build path</h3>
        <h4 id="ch4-unpkg-nothing">Ship a single ESM .js file</h4>
    <p>Works from a CDN today. No build step needed. Just ship a single ESM .js file that imports each other with explicit .js extensions.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Nothing: ship a single ESM .js file
// Works from a CDN today
import { formatDate } from "./utils.js";
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-unpkg-with-TS">Publish source .ts and let consumer transform</h4>
    <p>JSR publishes source .ts and lets the consumer transform. In the browser it needs a transform service, which you should not rely on.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// With TS: publish source .ts
// JSR does this. In the browser it needs a transform service,
// which you should not rely on.
<script type="module">
  import { retry } from "https://jsr.io/@yourorg/retry";
</script>
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-unpkg-blocks">Blocks the no-build path</h4>
    <p>Bare specifiers in your own imports, Node built-ins, CJS-only dependencies, TypeScript syntax in the shipped file, and dynamic require() block the no-build path.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Blocks the no-build path
//   - bare specifiers: import { x } from './util' must be ./util.js
//   - Node built-ins: import { readFile } from 'fs'
//   - CJS-only dependency
//   - TypeScript syntax in shipped file
//   - dynamic require()
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WHAT YOU CAN DO WITHOUT A BUILD STEP, in increasing ambition
//   nothing      ship a single ESM .js file. Works from a CDN today.
//   a few files  ship src/ as-is. Works if the files import each other
//               with explicit .js extensions. This is what JSR
//               publishes.
//   with TS      publish source .ts and let the consumer transform.
//               JSR does this. In the browser it needs a transform
//               service, which you should not rely on.
//   a real app   a bundler, a framework, a runtime

// WHAT BLOCKS THE NO-BUILD PATH
//   bare specifiers in your own imports: `import { x } from './util'`
//     must be `./util.js`. Bundlers resolve it; the browser does not.
//   Node built-ins: `import { readFile } from 'fs'`
//   a CJS-only dependency
//   TypeScript syntax in the shipped file
//   dynamic require()

// THE CHECK, which is a thirty-second test
$ npx serve node_modules/yourpkg/ &amp;
# then, in a browser console:
await import('http://localhost:3000/index.js')
// if that returns without a MIME type error, a bare-specifier error
// and a "require is not defined", you are done.

// IF YOU CANNOT GO NO-BUILD
//   at minimum, ship an ESM build with a browser-safe entry, so the
//   CDN shims can use it. esm.sh will bundle for you, which is a
//   reasonable fallback.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch4-mcp" data-toc>Untrusted publishing and the new protections</h3>
        <h4 id="ch4-mcp-protections">The protections</h4>
    <p>1. TRUSTED PUBLISHING: no token to paste, attack has nothing to steal. 2. HARDWARE-KEY-ENFORCED 2FA for publishing. 3. POST-INSTALL APPROVAL: npm 11 prompts before running install scripts. 4. INSTALL-SCRIPT BLOCKING: --ignore-scripts, ignore-scripts=true in .npmrc. 5. AUTOMATED MALWARE DETECTION: automatic yanking on registry side. 6. RATE LIMITING: new-package publication delay limits typosquat window.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// The six protections
//   1. Trusted publishing - no token to steal
//   2. Hardware-key-enforced 2FA
//   3. Post-install approval: npm 11 prompts
//   4. Install-script blocking: --ignore-scripts
//   5. Automated malware detection with auto-yanking
//   6. Rate limiting on new-package publication
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-mcp-user-facing">User-facing documentation</h4>
    <p>- tell people not to paste credentials into CLI tools. - tell them a legitimate migration tool never needs their token. - tell them what to do if they already did: revoke the token now, and audit what was published.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// User-facing docs section
//   - tell people not to paste credentials into CLI tools
//   - a legitimate migration tool never needs your token
//   - if you already did: revoke the token now
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-mcp-own-package">For your own package</h4>
    <p>- enable 2FA and prefer a hardware key. - use trusted publishing. - publish from CI only. - keep the maintainer count small and current. - a 0-dependency package is a package with no supply chain.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// For your own package
//   - enable 2FA and prefer a hardware key
//   - use trusted publishing
//   - publish from CI only
//   - keep maintainer count small and current
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE PROBLEM npm IS ATTACKING
//   a maintainer runs `npx some-migration-tool` to upgrade a
//   dependency, and the tool prints "we need your npm credentials to
//   continue". The maintainer pastes the token. That is the entire
//   event-stream compromise, and it is a social engineering attack
//   against a helpful CLI.

// WHAT THE PROTECTIONS ARE
//   1. TRUSTED PUBLISHING, discussed in ch3-oidc. There is no token
//      to paste, so the attack has nothing to steal. This is the
//      single most effective mitigation and it is available on npm,
//      PyPI, crates.io and Maven.
//   2. HARDWARE-KEY-ENFORCED 2FA for publishing, so a phished
//      password is not enough.
//   3. POST-INSTALL APPROVAL. npm 11 prompts before running
//      install scripts from newly added dependencies. Your CI needs
//      --ignore-scripts, and your developers should read that prompt
//      rather than pressing enter.
//   4. INSTALL-SCRIPT BLOCKING. --ignore-scripts, ignore-scripts=true
//      in .npmrc. Most malicious payloads are postinstall.
//   5. AUTOMATED MALWARE DETECTION on the registry side, with
//      automatic yanking.
//   6. RATE LIMITING on new-package publication, and a delay before
//      a brand-new package name is resolvable, which limits the
//      typosquat window.

// THE USER-FACING PART, and it belongs in your docs
   - tell people not to paste credentials into CLI tools
   - tell them a legitimate migration tool never needs their token
   - tell them what to do if they already did: revoke the token now,
//     and audit what was published with it
   - this is a five-line section in your README and it genuinely helps
//     people

// FOR YOUR OWN PACKAGE
   - enable 2FA and prefer a hardware key
   - use trusted publishing
   - publish from CI only
   - keep the maintainer count small and current
   - a 0-dependency package is a package with no supply chain</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch4-trouble" data-toc>Troubleshooting</h3>
        <h4 id="ch4-trouble-module-not-found">"Cannot find module"</h4>
    <p>Use npm ls to determine if the package is a direct dependency or hoisted. Use npm why (npm 8+) to understand why it is there. Check the "exports" field and whether the consumer is ESM importing your CJS.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// Module not found diagnostic
$ npm ls yourpkg                    // is it a direct dep, or hoisted?
$ npm why yourpkg                   // npm 8+: why is it here
// "named export 'x' not found": check the "exports" field
// "Unsupported directory import": add the subpath or import the root
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-trouble-peer-conflict">ESRESOLVE / peer dependency conflict</h4>
    <p>Read the actual conflict first; often a peer range is too narrow. Using --legacy-peer-deps makes it work but explains nothing.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// ERESOLVE / peer dependency conflict
$ npm install --legacy-peer-deps   // makes it work, explains nothing
// read the actual conflict first - often a peer range too narrow
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-trouble-engine-warnings">engine node warnings</h4>
    <p>Cosmetic. Do not suppress it; read whether it matters. The package may have been published without dist/.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// engine node warnings
//   cosmetic. do not suppress it, read whether it matters.
//   the package published without dist/
</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch4-trouble-published-version">A version published, cannot republish</h4>
    <p>Versions are immutable. Bump to a new version. If you need to remove one, unpublish within 72 hours, then republish.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>
// A version published, cannot republish
//   versions are immutable. bump to a new version.
//   if you need to remove one, unpublish within 72 hours, then republish.
</code></pre></div><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// "Cannot find module" for a package that IS installed
$ npm ls yourpkg                 // is it a direct dep, or hoisted?
$ npm why yourpkg                // npm 8+: why is it here
   "named export 'x' not found"
   the CJS named-export heuristic. check the "exports" field, and
   whether the consumer is ESM importing your CJS.
   "Unsupported directory import"
   you are deep-importing a path not in "exports". intentional?
   the fix is to add the subpath, or to make them import the root.
   ERESOLVE / peer dependency conflict
   $ npm install --legacy-peer-deps      makes it work, explains nothing
   read the actual conflict first. often a peer range too narrow.
   "engine node" warnings
   cosmetic. do not suppress it, read whether it matters.
   the package published without dist/
   .gitignore excluded it and there is no "files" field. add "files".
   a version published, cannot republish
   versions are immutable. bump to a new version. if you need to
   remove one, unpublish within 72 hours, then republish.

// THE DIAGNOSTIC THAT SOLVES MOST OF IT
$ npm view yourpkg@1.2.3
// shows the manifest, the file list with sizes, and the dist-tags.
// if the file list does not contain what you expect, the problem is
// in your package.json, not in the consumer's setup.
$ npm pack yourpkg@1.2.3 --dry-run
// the same, for a version already on the registry</code></pre></div>
    <div class="ex"><h4>Exercises 4</h4><ol>
      <li>Build a package that ships both ESM and CJS with types and source maps, and verify all four entry points resolve for a consumer. Write a CI test that imports it both ways from Node.</li>
      <li>Set <code>files</code> on a package that was relying on <code>.gitignore</code>, run <code>npm pack --dry-run</code>, and compare the two file lists. Anything that disappeared was being shipped by accident.</li>
      <li>Publish a beta to a test registry or a scope with <code>--tag beta</code>, and prove that <code>npm install pkg</code> does NOT resolve to it. Then prove it does when you omit the tag, so you have seen the failure mode.</li>
      <li>Convert a library with bundled React into one with React as a peer dependency. Verify with <code>npm ls react</code> that a consumer has exactly one copy.</li>
      <li>Serve your package's source over a static file server and import it from a browser with no bundler. Fix whatever breaks until it works, and note which fixes changed the public file layout.</li>
    </ol></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
<h2 id="ch5" data-toc>5 Â· Python, PHP and Ruby</h2>
    <div class="ch-toc"><button type="button" class="ch-toc-head" aria-expanded="true"><span class="arrow">â–¸</span>Contents &middot; 5 Â· Python, PHP and Ruby</button><div class="ch-toc-list"><a href="#ch5-pypi">PyPI: the shape of a Python distribution</a><a href="#ch5-build">Building wheels and sdists</a><a href="#ch5-pyproject">pyproject.toml and modern metadata</a><a href="#ch5-publish-py">Publishing to PyPI</a><a href="#ch5-cffi">Binary wheels and the manylinux problem</a><a href="#ch5-tag">Tagging, classifiers and findability</a><a href="#ch5-packagist">Packagist and Composer</a><a href="#ch5-rubygems">RubyGems and Bundler</a><a href="#ch5-compare">Comparing the three</a></div></div>
    <p class="topic-desc">Three ecosystems with different failure modes: Python's binary wheel matrix, Composer's per-project lockfile discipline, and RubyGems' unusually simple model. The differences are worth learning individually rather than generalising from one.</p>
    <h3 id="ch5-pypi" data-toc>PyPI: the shape of a Python distribution</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// a Python release is TWO artefacts, and they do different jobs

// 1. THE SDIST (source distribution)
//    a .tar.gz containing your source, plus a build backend
//    configuration, plus a PKG-INFO generated from that config.
//    needed because: pip may need to build from source, and because
//    some platforms are not covered by your wheels.
//    this is what a human reviews before installing.

// 2. THE WHEEL
//    a .zip with a PEP 427 layout, built for a specific
//    interpreter, platform and ABI. Installed without compiling.
//
//   yourpkg/
     __init__.py
     core.py
     _speedup.cpython-312-x86_64-linux-gnu.so
   yourpkg-1.2.3.dist-info/
     METADATA        the metadata, generated from pyproject.toml
     WHEEL           the wheel format version and build tag
     RECORD          every file with its hash and size
     LICENSE_FILES   or the licence in METADATA

// WHY TWO
//   the sdist is the source of truth for review and for platforms you
//   did not build a wheel for. The wheel is what almost everyone
//   installs. Publishing only an sdist means every install compiles.

// THE TREE, and what the layout implies
   src/yourpkg/          a src layout, strongly recommended
     __init__.py
   tests/
   pyproject.toml
   README.md
//   a src layout stops a stray local directory from shadowing the
//   installed package, which is a class of bug that is maddening to
//   diagnose and trivially avoided.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch5-pypi-two-artefacts">Two artefacts, two jobs</h4>
    <p>Every Python release is a pair. The sdist is the reviewable source of truth and the fallback for any platform you did not build a wheel for; the wheel is what almost every install actually uses, because it installs without compiling. Build both â€” a project that publishes only a wheel is uninstallable on the long tail of platforms, and one that publishes only an sdist makes every user compile.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ python -m build
$ ls -1 dist/
yourpkg-1.2.3.tar.gz
yourpkg-1.2.3-py3-none-any.whl
$ tar tzf dist/yourpkg-1.2.3.tar.gz | head -4
yourpkg-1.2.3/pyproject.toml
yourpkg-1.2.3/src/yourpkg/__init__.py</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-pypi-wheel-internals">What is inside a wheel</h4>
    <p>A wheel is a zip in the fixed PEP 427 layout: the importable package tree plus a <code>NAME-VERSION.dist-info/</code> directory. The <code>RECORD</code> file lists every member with a hash and a size, and pip uses it to uninstall cleanly â€” a file not listed there is one pip leaves behind after removal. The <code>WHEEL</code> file records the format version and build tag, and <code>METADATA</code> is what the installer and the index actually read.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ python -m zipfile -l dist/yourpkg-1.2.3-py3-none-any.whl
File Name                                  Modified     Size
yourpkg/__init__.py                        2026-10-01   412
yourpkg/core.py                            2026-10-01  8610
yourpkg-1.2.3.dist-info/METADATA           2026-10-01  2311
yourpkg-1.2.3.dist-info/WHEEL              2026-10-01   110
yourpkg-1.2.3.dist-info/RECORD             2026-10-01   902</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-pypi-src-layout">The src layout, and why it matters</h4>
    <p>Putting the importable package under <code>src/</code> rather than at the repository root is not cosmetic. With a flat layout, running a test or a script from the checkout imports the working directory's copy, so a broken build still passes locally and the installed package is never actually exercised. With <code>src/</code>, the only way to import the package is to install it, so the tests test the artefact.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>yourpkg/
  src/yourpkg/__init__.py      # the importable package
  tests/test_core.py
  pyproject.toml
  README.md
# install editable, then test the real package
$ python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"
$ pytest</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch5-build" data-toc>Building wheels and sdists</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the modern toolchain
$ python -m pip install build twine
$ python -m build                  # builds an sdist AND a wheel
$ twine check dist/*               # metadata validation. always.

// CIBUILDWHEEL, the build frontend
$ python -m cibuildwheel --output-dir wheelhouse
//   builds a wheel for EVERY platform tag you ask for, using
//   containers, so you get manylinux wheels from a Mac.
//   this is the tool that makes a serious Python release practical.

// THE PURE-PYTHON FAST PATH
$ python -m build
# if you have no C extension, the "universal" flag matters
#   setup.cfg
   [bdist_wheel]
   universal = 1
//   or, in pyproject.toml, the equivalent.
//   this produces a py3-none-any wheel that installs everywhere.

// A C EXTENSION, and the real difficulty
   from setuptools import setup, Extension

   setup(
     name="yourpkg",
     ext_modules=[Extension("yourpkg._speedup", ["src/_speedup.c"])],
   )
//   now you need a wheel per platform, and the tags must match the
//   interpreter ABI. This is the manylinux problem, next.

// THE FOUR THINGS THAT GO WRONG
   1. FORGETTING THE CLEAN BUILD. A stale .so from your machine
//      gets packaged. Build in a fresh container, always.
//   2. INCLUDING TESTS in the wheel. They ship to every user.
//   3. MISSING __init__.py, so the package is a namespace package
//      and the installed version silently does nothing.
//   4. Package data not included: a data file that works from a
//      checkout and is absent from the wheel.
      [tool.setuptools.package-data]
      yourpkg = ["py.typed", "*.json", "templates/*.html"]
//   py.typed is the marker file that makes a package PEP 561 typed.
//   Without it, mypy ignores your types entirely, and consumers
//   report "your type hints are not being picked up".</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch5-build-isolation">Builds happen in isolation</h4>
    <p><code>python -m build</code> creates a fresh virtual environment, installs the build backend named in <code>[build-system] requires</code>, and builds there. That is why the result does not depend on whatever you happen to have installed â€” and why a missing entry in <code>requires</code> fails in CI even though it worked locally. Pass <code>--no-isolation</code> only when you are debugging the backend itself.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># reproduce the CI build exactly
$ rm -rf dist/ build/
$ python -m build
# inspect what the build backend declared it needed
$ python -c "import tomllib,pathlib; print(tomllib.loads(pathlib.Path('pyproject.toml').read_text())['build-system'])"
{'requires': ['hatchling'], 'build-backend': 'hatchling.build'}</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-build-metadata-check">Checked before upload, not after</h4>
    <p><code>twine check</code> validates that the long description will render and that <code>METADATA</code> is well formed. It runs in milliseconds and catches the class of error a registry makes permanent: a malformed readme, a duplicate field, an invalid licence expression. Run it in CI on every build, not only before a release.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ twine check dist/*
Checking dist/yourpkg-1.2.3-py3-none-any.whl: PASSED
Checking dist/yourpkg-1.2.3.tar.gz: PASSED
$ twine check dist/* --strict          # fails on warnings, not just errors</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-build-cibuildwheel">cibuildwheel, the matrix builder</h4>
    <p>Once you have a C extension, hand-building each wheel is not viable. <code>cibuildwheel</code> drives a container per platform, builds and tests inside it, then hands you a directory of correctly tagged wheels. The whole matrix is one config block.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>[tool.cibuildwheel]
build = "cp310-* cp311-* cp312-*"
skip = "*-musllinux_i686"
test-command = "python -c \"import yourpkg; print(yourpkg.__version__)\""

[tool.cibuildwheel.linux]
archs = ["x86_64", "aarch64"]

[tool.cibuildwheel.macos]
archs = ["x86_64", "arm64"]
# $ pipx run cibuildwheel --output-dir wheelhouse</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch5-pyproject" data-toc>pyproject.toml and modern metadata</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// pyproject.toml is now the single source of truth. setup.py is legacy.
[build-system]
requires = ["hatchling"]
build-backend = "hatchling.build"

[project]
name = "yourpkg"
version = "1.2.3"
description = "A small library that does one thing well."
readme = "README.md"
requires-python = "&gt;=3.9"
license = "Apache-2.0"
license-files = ["LICENSE"]
authors = [{ name = "Your Name", email = "you@example.com" }]
keywords = ["parsing", "data", "text"]
classifiers = [
  "Development Status :: 5 - Production/Stable",
  "Intended Audience :: Developers",
  "License :: OSI Approved :: Apache Software License",
  "Programming Language :: Python :: 3.9",
  "Programming Language :: Python :: 3.12",
  "Topic :: Software Development :: Libraries",
]

dependencies = [
  "httpx&gt;=0.27,&lt;1.0",
  "pydantic&gt;=2.6",
]

[project.optional-dependencies]
cli = ["typer&gt;=0.12"]
dev = ["pytest&gt;=8.0", "mypy&gt;=1.9", "ruff&gt;=0.5"]

[project.urls]
Homepage = "https://example.com/yourpkg"
Source = "https://github.com/yourorg/yourpkg"
Changelog = "https://github.com/yourorg/yourpkg/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md"

[project.scripts]
yourpkg = "yourpkg.cli:main"

[tool.hatch.build.targets.wheel]
packages = ["src/yourpkg"]

[tool.mypy]
strict = true

// THE FIELDS THAT AFFECT PUBLISHING
//   requires-python  enforced by pip at install. be accurate.
//   license          SPDX expression. the older classifier-based
//                    licence is deprecated and eventually removed.
//   classifiers      appear on the PyPI page and drive discovery.
//                    include the ones that genuinely apply.
//   project.scripts  console entry points. a mistake here means the
//                    command does not exist after install.

// THE VERSION, AND WHERE IT LIVES
//   hardcoding it in pyproject.toml means editing it every release.
//   better, so the build reads it from the code:
[tool.hatch.version]
path = "src/yourpkg/__init__.py"
//   __version__ = "1.2.3"
//   one source of truth, and it cannot drift from the code.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch5-pyproject-dynamic">Dynamic fields, and where a version lives</h4>
    <p>Anything declared under <code>dynamic</code> is omitted from <code>[project]</code> and read at build time instead. The common case is a version kept next to the code so it cannot drift; the package exposes the same string as <code>__version__</code>, so there is exactly one source of truth. Building the readme from fragments is the other frequent use â€” it lets the changelog appear on the project page without being duplicated by hand.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>[project]
name = "yourpkg"
dynamic = ["version", "readme"]

[tool.hatch.version]
path = "src/yourpkg/__init__.py"     # reads __version__ = "1.2.3"

[tool.hatch.metadata.hooks.fancy-pypi-readme]
content-type = "text/markdown"
[[tool.hatch.metadata.hooks.fancy-pypi-readme.fragments]]
path = "README.md"</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-pyproject-entrypoints">Entry points are an API</h4>
    <p>A console script maps a command name to a callable. The command only exists after install, and the target must be importable from the installed package â€” a typo here is the difference between a working CLI and <code>command not found</code>. <code>gui-scripts</code> is the same mechanism for a windowed launcher on Windows.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>[project.scripts]
yourpkg = "yourpkg.cli:main"          # $ yourpkg --help
yourpkg-serve = "yourpkg.server:run"

[project.gui-scripts]
yourpkg-gui = "yourpkg.gui:launch"    # pythonw on Windows

# after install:
$ yourpkg --version
1.2.3</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-pyproject-locking">Dependencies versus a lock</h4>
    <p><code>[project].dependencies</code> is a declaration of what you need, with ranges; it ships inside the wheel and constrains every consumer. A lock file is the opposite: a complete, pinned resolution for one application. A library publishes ranges; an application commits a lock. Tools such as <code>uv</code> and <code>pip-tools</code> generate the lock from the declaration.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># a library declares ranges
dependencies = ["httpx>=0.27,&lt;1.0"]
# an application locks the full tree
$ uv lock                 # writes uv.lock
$ uv sync --frozen        # installs exactly the lock in CI
$ pip-compile -o requirements.txt pyproject.toml   # pip-tools</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch5-publish-py" data-toc>Publishing to PyPI</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// TEST FIRST, on TestPyPI
$ python -m twine upload --repository testpypi dist/*
$ pip install --index-url https://test.pypi.org/simple/ \
      --extra-index-url https://pypi.org/simple yourpkg==1.2.3
//   the extra-index is needed so your real dependencies resolve.
//   this catches the metadata and the file-layout bugs before anyone
//   sees them. It is the most valuable five minutes in a Python release.

// THE REAL THING, with trusted publishing
$ python -m build
$ twine check dist/*

// the workflow, with no long-lived token
   - uses: pypa/gh-action-pypi-publish@release/v1
     with:
       packages-dir: dist/
//   configured once in the PyPI dashboard: owner, repo, workflow.

// WITH A TOKEN, if that is all you have
$ python -m twine upload dist/*
   # export TWINE_USERNAME=__token__
//   export TWINE_PASSWORD=pypi-AgEIcHlwaS5vcmc...
//   store it as an encrypted secret, never in a file.

// AFTER PUBLISHING
   1. the version is IMMUTABLE. PyPI will not let you republish, even
//      to fix a broken description. Get the metadata right first.
   2. it takes a minute or two to appear. Verify:
      $ pip install --no-cache-dir yourpkg==1.2.3
   3. the release page is where your changelog excerpt appears. Use
      the long_description_content_type and a proper markdown changelog
      section, so the PyPI page is a real landing page.

// EMBEDDING A CHANGELOG SECTION
[tool.hatch.metadata.hooks.fancy-pypi-readme]
content-type = "text/markdown"
[[tool.hatch.metadata.hooks.fancy-pypi-readme.fragments]]
path = "README.md"
[[tool.hatch.metadata.hooks.fancy-pypi-readme.fragments]]
text = "\n## Release history\n\n"
[[tool.hatch.metadata.hooks.fancy-pypi-readme.fragments]]
path = "CHANGELOG.md"
start-after = "&lt;!-- changelog follows --&gt;"
pattern = "\n(###.+)"</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch5-publish-testpypi">Test on TestPyPI first</h4>
    <p>TestPyPI is a separate instance with the same rules and the same irreversibility. Uploading there first exercises the metadata, the file layout and the long-description render before the real name is spent. Install back from it with an extra index so your genuine dependencies still resolve from the real index.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ python -m twine upload --repository testpypi dist/*
$ pip install --index-url https://test.pypi.org/simple/ \
      --extra-index-url https://pypi.org/simple/ yourpkg==1.2.3
$ python -c "import yourpkg; print(yourpkg.__version__)"</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-publish-trusted">Trusted publishing, with no token at all</h4>
    <p>Trusted publishing replaces the long-lived API token with an OIDC exchange: the workflow proves its identity to PyPI, PyPI issues a one-time upload credential, the upload happens. Configure the publisher once on the project's PyPI page with the owner, repository, workflow filename and an optional environment, then grant the job <code>id-token: write</code>. Nothing long-lived is stored anywhere.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>permissions:
  id-token: write          # required for the OIDC exchange
jobs:
  publish:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    environment: release   # matches the PyPI publisher config
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: python -m build
      - uses: pypa/gh-action-pypi-publish@release/v1
        with:
          packages-dir: dist/</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-publish-irreversible">A published version is forever</h4>
    <p>PyPI does not allow a filename to be uploaded twice, and it has no npm-style dist-tags. A mistake cannot be edited away: the fix is a new version, and if the release is dangerous you use the limited deletion policy on the release page, which frees the version but leaves the project history. The version number is a one-way door, so bump it deliberately and check the artefact before you push.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ pip index versions yourpkg        # what already exists
yourpkg (1.2.3)
Available versions: 1.2.3, 1.2.2, 1.2.1
# a broken 1.2.3 is not repairable in place; ship 1.2.4
$ python -m build &amp;&amp; twine check dist/* &amp;&amp; twine upload dist/*</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch5-cffi" data-toc>Binary wheels and the manylinux problem</h3>
    <p>This is the hardest practical problem in Python packaging, and understanding it explains a lot of confusing errors.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE PROBLEM
//   a C extension produces a .so linked against a specific glibc
//   version. Build on Ubuntu 24.04 and you get a wheel that needs
//   glibc 2.39, which does not exist on the manylinux_2_28 image
//   that a corporate Linux user has.

// THE THREE GENERATIONS
   manylinux2014   glibc 2.17, CentOS 7 era. Still the broadest.
//   manylinux_2_28 glibc 2.28, RHEL 8 era. The modern default.
//   musllinux_1_2  Alpine, which uses musl not glibc. SEPARATE wheels.
//
//   pip on a Linux box checks the glibc version and refuses a wheel
//   that needs a newer one, with:
//     "ERROR: Package 'yourpkg' requires a different Python" - no, the
//     real one is:
//     "manylinux2014_x86_64 wheel is not supported on this platform"

// THE FIX: BUILD IN THE MANYLINUX IMAGE
$ docker run --rm -v "$PWD":/io quay.io/pypa/manylinux_2_28_x86_64 \
    /bin/bash -c "/io/pyproject-build-and-test.sh"
//   or use cibuildwheel, which does this for a matrix of platforms
//   including musllinux, and produces all the tags automatically.

// THE TAGS, and why the filename is a mess
   yourpkg-1.2.3-cp312-cp312-manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl
//              |    |    |    |                |
//           version |   |    |                platform
//                 CPython  ABI   manylinux tag  architecture
//   and, for the same code:
//   yourpkg-1.2.3-cp312-cp312-musllinux_1_2_x86_64.whl   Alpine
//   yourpkg-1.2.3-cp312-cp312-macosx_11_0_arm64.whl        Apple Silicon
//   yourpkg-1.2.3-py3-none-any.whl                        pure Python
//   3 interpreters x 3 OS x 2 architectures = 18 wheels, from one
//   cibuildwheel run.

// PURE PYTHON IS THE ESCAPE HATCH
//   if your package does not need to be fast, do not use C. The
//   matrix collapses to one wheel and your release process becomes
//   trivial. This is why a great deal of Python is pure Python.

// THE DEBUGGING TIP that saves hours
$ python -c "import yourpkg._speedup"
//   run this on the target platform. "undefined symbol" means a glibc
//   mismatch. "cannot open shared object file" means the .so was not
//   packaged.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch5-cffi-abi3">abi3 wheels, to shrink the matrix</h4>
    <p>Building against the limited API produces an <code>abi3</code> wheel that works on every CPython from the tag's version onward. One wheel replaces one per interpreter version, which is the single largest reduction available. The cost is that the extension can only use the stable ABI subset, so some CPython internals are off-limits.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># one wheel for CPython 3.9 and later instead of cp39..cp313
yourpkg-1.2.3-cp39-abi3-manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl
# in the build backend:
#   define Py_LIMITED_API 0x03090000
#   setuptools: py_limited_api=True on the Extension</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-cffi-repair">Repairing a wheel that links too much</h4>
    <p>A wheel built on your machine may pull <code>libssl</code> or <code>libstdc++</code> from the host at a version the target does not have. <code>auditwheel</code> on Linux rewrites the tags and vendors the offending shared objects into the wheel; <code>delocate</code> and <code>delvewheel</code> do the equivalent on macOS and Windows. Running the repair step is what turns a locally working wheel into a portable one.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ auditwheel show dist/yourpkg-1.2.3-cp312-cp312-linux_x86_64.whl
$ auditwheel repair dist/yourpkg-*.whl -w wheelhouse/
# macOS
$ delocate-wheel -w wheelhouse/ dist/yourpkg-*.whl
# Windows
$ delvewheel repair --add-path C:\libs dist/yourpkg-*.whl</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-cffi-debug">Debugging the runtime failure</h4>
    <p>Most problems appear only when the extension is imported on the target. The error text is the whole diagnosis: an undefined symbol means a glibc or library version mismatch, a failed open means the shared object was not packaged, and a Python-level failure means the wrapper and the native code disagree. Reproduce it in the target container before changing anything.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ docker run --rm -it -v "$PWD/dist":/w python:3.12-slim bash
# inside:
$ pip install /w/yourpkg-1.2.3-cp312-cp312-manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl
$ python -c "import yourpkg._speedup as m; print(m)"
# OSError: ... undefined symbol: ...
# OSError: ... libfoo.so.3: cannot open shared object file</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch5-tag" data-toc>Tagging, classifiers and findability</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// DISCOVERY ON PYPI, and it is mostly metadata
//   the search and the "install with" sidebar both read your
//   classifiers. A package with no classifiers is much harder to find.

// THE CLASSIFIERS THAT MATTER
   "Development Status :: 4 - Beta"
   "Intended Audience :: Developers"
   "License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License"
   "Programming Language :: Python :: 3.12"
   "Topic :: Software Development :: Libraries :: Python Modules"

// PROGRAMMATIC USE, and the truest library language
   your classifiers are the API a machine reads. Getting them right is
   more valuable than the README for a package meant to be depended
   on rather than read.

// WHY "Programming Language :: Python :: Implementation :: CPython"
//   with a C extension, say which implementations you support. PyPy
//   will not load a CPython-tagged C extension.

// THE RENAME PROBLEM
//   a project can move a name, but not everyone follows. If you
//   rename, the old name stays on PyPI forever, and someone else
//   will eventually claim it. Keep a tombstone package that depends
//   on the new name and prints a message on import. It is a
//   courtesy that costs nothing.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch5-tag-metadata">Metadata is the search index</h4>
    <p>PyPI's search and the filter sidebar read classifiers, keywords and the project URLs; the README supplies the prose. A package with one classifier and no keywords is effectively invisible. Fill in the fields a machine reads, then the fields a person reads.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>[project]
keywords = ["csv", "parsing", "streaming"]
classifiers = [
  "Development Status :: 5 - Production/Stable",
  "Programming Language :: Python :: 3.12",
  "Topic :: Text Processing :: Markup",
]
[project.urls]
Homepage = "https://example.com/yourpkg"
Source = "https://github.com/yourorg/yourpkg"
Changelog = "https://github.com/yourorg/yourpkg/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md"</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-tag-renaming">Renaming without orphaning users</h4>
    <p>When a project changes name, the old distribution stays on PyPI and the old name can be claimed by someone else once you release it. Publish one final version of the old name that depends on the new one and warns on import, so existing installs keep working and new ones find the new project.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># oldpkg 2.0.0: a tombstone release
# pyproject.toml
[project]
name = "oldpkg"
version = "2.0.0"
dependencies = ["yourpkg&gt;=2.0"]
# oldpkg/__init__.py
import warnings
from yourpkg import *          # re-export the whole surface
warnings.warn("oldpkg was renamed to yourpkg", DeprecationWarning)</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-tag-readme">The page is the landing page</h4>
    <p>PyPI renders the project description from the README, so the README <em>is</em> the product page. Badges and a screenshot help, but the first sentence and the install command decide whether anyone continues. Choose the renderer explicitly and confirm it with <code>twine check</code>.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>[project]
readme = { file = "README.md", content-type = "text/markdown" }
# README.md structure that converts:
#   name -&gt; one sentence -&gt; ## Install -&gt; ## Usage -&gt; ## Compatibility</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch5-packagist" data-toc>Packagist and Composer</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// Packagist in full is covered in php.html, section 4b. The short form:

// composer.json, the publishable fields
{
  "name": "yourorg/http-client",      // reverse-DNS, namespace claimed
  "description": "A PSR-18 HTTP client with retries.",
  "type": "library",
  "license": "MIT",
  "require": {
    "php": "^8.2",
    "psr/http-client": "^1.0"
  },
  "autoload": { "psr-4": { "YourOrg\\HttpClient\\": "src/" } },
  "autoload-dev": { "psr-4": { "YourOrg\\HttpClient\\Tests\\": "tests/" } },
  "extra": { "branch-alias": { "dev-main": "1.4-dev" } }
}

// $ composer validate --strict
// $ composer publish --dry-run        READ THE FILE LIST
// $ composer publish
// $ git tag -a v1.4.0 &amp;&amp; git push origin v1.4.0

// THE THINGS UNIQUE TO COMPOSER
//   - the lock file. Commit composer.lock for applications, and for
//     libraries decide deliberately: committing it pins your CI but
//     tests a tree your consumers may not resolve.
//   - the stability flags. dev-main is dev by default, so a consumer
//     must ask for ^1.4 rather than getting it automatically.
//   - the plugin/install-script model. A Composer plugin runs PHP with
//     full privileges during install. Only install plugins you would
//     give a shell to, and prefer the allow-plugins config over
//     allow-plugins: true, which is a blank cheque.
//   - Packagist is free and requires no account to INSTALL from. Only
//     publishing needs an account.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch5-packagist-autoload">PSR-4 autoloading, and the optimised map</h4>
    <p>Composer maps namespaces to directories via <code>psr-4</code>. In production, generate the class map once so the autoloader is a direct lookup rather than a filesystem search, and use the authoritative flag so a class not in the map fails loudly instead of surprising you at deploy time.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ composer dump-autoload --optimize --classmap-authoritative
# prod deploy recipe:
$ composer install --no-dev --optimize-autoloader --no-scripts
$ composer validate --strict</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-packagist-sync">Packagist reads your tags, not your pushes</h4>
    <p>Packagist is an index over your VCS repository. You publish by creating a semantic-version tag and pushing it; Packagist picks it up via a webhook or its polling and builds a metadata entry. A branch with no tag cannot be required as a stable version, which is why <code>dev-main</code> needs a branch alias to be usable.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>{
  "extra": { "branch-alias": { "dev-main": "1.4-dev" } }
}
$ git tag -a v1.4.0 -m "v1.4.0"
$ git push origin v1.4.0
# consumers may then use it:
$ composer require yourorg/http-client:^1.4</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-packagist-plugins">Plugins run with your privileges</h4>
    <p>A Composer plugin is PHP executed during install, with the same access as your shell. Composer therefore requires explicit consent for every plugin, recorded in <code>allow-plugins</code>. Never set it to <code>true</code> globally; list the plugins you have reviewed.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>{
  "config": {
    "allow-plugins": {
      "symfony/flex": true,
      "phpstan/extension-installer": true
    }
  }
}</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch5-rubygems" data-toc>RubyGems and Bundler</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the gemspec, which is code rather than data
Gem::Specification.new do |spec|
  spec.name          = "yourgem"
  spec.version       = "1.2.3"
  spec.summary       = "One sentence."
  spec.description   = "A longer description."
  spec.authors       = ["Your Name"]
  spec.email         = ["you@example.com"]
  spec.homepage      = "https://example.com/yourgem"
  spec.license       = "MIT"

  spec.files = Dir["lib/**/*.rb", "LICENSE", "README.md"]
  spec.require_paths = ["lib"]

  spec.required_ruby_version = ">= 3.0"
  spec.add_dependency "zeitwerk", "~> 2.6"
  spec.add_development_dependency "rspec", "~> 3.13"
  spec.metadata["rubygems_mfa_required"] = "true"   # see ch3-2fa
end

// build and publish
$ gem build yourgem.gemspec
$ gem push yourgem-1.2.3.gem
$ gem sign yourgem-1.2.3.gem        # with a certificate in ~/.gem

// BUNDLER, and the lockfile discipline
   source "https://rubygems.org"
   gem "rails", "~> 7.1"
   gem "pg", "~> 1.5"
// Gemfile: declarations. Never commit this with exact pins for
//   everything; the lockfile handles resolution.
// Gemfile.lock: the resolution. ALWAYS commit it for applications.
$ bundle install
$ bundle update --conservative
$ bundle exec rake release     # builds, tags, pushes, in one step

// WHAT IS DIFFERENT FROM PYPI
//   - RubyGems is a SINGLE index, so there is no "index url" to get
//     wrong, and no separate test index. The TestGem server is legacy.
//   - the MFA requirement can be set per-gem in gem metadata
//   - signing is built into the push path
//   - Bundler is stricter about a Gemfile that has drifted from its
//     lock. Running bundle install with a modified Gemfile fails rather
//     than silently updating, which is a safety feature.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch5-rubygems-files">The file list is your release</h4>
    <p>A gemspec is Ruby code, and <code>spec.files</code> is the list of files packaged into the gem. Build it from git so the packaged set matches the tagged commit exactly and nothing untracked leaks in â€” a built artefact left in the working tree is a classic accidental inclusion.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>Gem::Specification.new do |spec|
  spec.files = `git ls-files -z`.split("\x0").reject do |f|
    f.match(%r{\A(?:test|spec|features)/})
  end
  spec.bindir = "exe"
  spec.executables = spec.files.grep(%r{\Aexe/}) { |f| File.basename(f) }
end
$ gem build yourgem.gemspec
$ gem contents --show-install-dir yourgem-1.2.3.gem</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-rubygems-credentials">Credentials and the MFA gate</h4>
    <p>Pushing needs an API key, stored in <code>~/.gem/credentials</code> or supplied in CI. A gem can declare <code>rubygems_mfa_required</code> in its metadata, which forces an MFA prompt on every privileged operation for that gem; the flag is the right default for a gem with any downstream users.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>spec.metadata["rubygems_mfa_required"] = "true"
$ gem push yourgem-1.2.3.gem
# CI: pass the key without writing it to disk
$ gem push --key github --host https://rubygems.org yourgem-1.2.3.gem
# ~/.gem/credentials (chmod 0600)
# :rubygems_api_key: rubygems_...</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-rubygems-native">Native extensions and platform gems</h4>
    <p>A gem with a C extension is compiled on the user's machine unless you ship a precompiled platform gem. <code>rake-compiler</code> builds per-platform and <code>gem push --platform</code> uploads each. A pure-Ruby fallback keeps the package installable everywhere while the platform gems serve the common targets.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ rake native gem                     # builds platform gems
$ gem push pkg/yourgem-1.2.3-x86_64-linux.gem
$ gem push pkg/yourgem-1.2.3-arm64-darwin.gem
$ gem push pkg/yourgem-1.2.3.gem      # the pure-Ruby fallback, last</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch5-compare" data-toc>Comparing the three</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>//            PyPI          Packagist      RubyGems
// index      explicit      configurable   single, implicit
// immutable  yes           yes            yes
// MFA        account       optional        optional
// provenance yes           rare           via signing
// lockfile   not typical   yes, important  yes, essential
// binary     the hard part n/a            has native gems
// publish    token or      token          token or
//            OIDC                              API key
// testing    TestPyPI      none official  none
//
// THE PATTERN ACROSS ALL THREE, and it generalises
//   1. the version is immutable once published
//   2. the package name is a permanent, global namespace
//   3. a token is a publish credential, so short-lived credentials
//      are the correct design and the old design was a mistake
//   4. the lockfile is what makes an install reproducible, and only
//      the application should commit one
//   5. the binary-wheel matrix is the thing that makes a language's
//      release process expensive, and it is the reason pure-code
//      languages are much easier to publish for</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch5-compare-lockfile">The lockfile rule, restated</h4>
    <p>In all three ecosystems a lock file exists to freeze an application's resolution. The publisher's job is to keep the <em>declared</em> ranges honest and test at the edges; the consumer's job is to commit a lock. Committing a lock inside a library pins your own CI but does not constrain consumers, who resolve their own tree from your ranges.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># application: commit the lock
$ pip freeze &gt; requirements.txt        # or uv.lock
$ bundle lock &amp;&amp; git add Gemfile.lock
$ composer update --lock &amp;&amp; git add composer.lock
# library: publish ranges, no lock shipped</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-compare-credentials">Three credential models, one lesson</h4>
    <p>All three accept a long-lived token and all three have been the target of token theft. The difference is the escape hatch: PyPI has trusted publishing via OIDC, npm has provenance, and RubyGems pins MFA into the gem metadata. In each case the correct configuration is a short-lived CI credential, not a personal key on a laptop.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># PyPI       OIDC, no stored token (trusted publishing)
# npm        OIDC / provenance
# RubyGems   scoped API key + MFA flag
# Packagist  reads tags; no upload credential at all
$ grep -r "API_KEY\|TOKEN\|PASSWORD" .github/workflows/   # long-lived ones</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch5-compare-mirrors">Mirrors and private indexes</h4>
    <p>The reason to reach for a private index differs by ecosystem. Python has several public indexes and corporate mirrors and an <code>--index-url</code> that is easy to misconfigure; Composer lets you declare repositories per project, so a private package can be one entry; RubyGems is a single global index, so private gems need a separate hosted source. Running each of these is chapter 9.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># pip: prefer one index, avoid the dependency-confusion trap
$ pip config set global.index-url https://pypi.org/simple
# composer.json: per-project repository
{"repositories": [{"type": "composer", "url": "https://satis.internal"}]}
# Gemfile: a private source
gem "internal-gem", source: "https://gems.internal"</code></pre></div>

    <div class="ex"><h4>Exercises 5</h4><ol>
      <li>Build a Python package with <code>python -m build</code>, inspect both artefacts with <code>twine check</code>, and install from the sdist into a clean virtual environment. Then install from the wheel and compare. Note which is faster and why.</li>
      <li>Add a C extension to a Python package, build with <code>cibuildwheel</code>, and publish the wheel matrix. Verify the tag in each filename is correct for its platform, and that a pure-Python wheel was NOT also produced.</li>
      <li>Publish to TestPyPI, install from it with an extra index for dependencies, and confirm the package is importable. This catches metadata bugs before a real release.</li>
      <li>Add a <code>py.typed</code> marker and a <code>[tool.mypy]</code> config, and verify a consumer's <code>mypy</code> now sees the type hints. Without the marker it does not.</li>
      <li>Build and publish a Composer package to a private Satis instance, and then install it into a second project with a version constraint. Record the file list from <code>--dry-run</code> and check for anything unexpected.</li>
    </ol></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
<h2 id="ch6" data-toc>6 Â· .NET, Java, Rust, Go and Dart</h2>
    <div class="ch-toc"><button type="button" class="ch-toc-head" aria-expanded="true"><span class="arrow">â–¸</span>Contents &middot; 6 Â· .NET, Java, Rust, Go and Dart</button><div class="ch-toc-list"><a href="#ch6-nuget">NuGet</a><a href="#ch6-maven">Maven Central and its ecosystem</a><a href="#ch6-gradle">Gradle and the wider JVM</a><a href="#ch6-crates">crates.io</a><a href="#ch6-goproxy">Go modules and proxies</a><a href="#ch6-hex">Hex.pm and Erlang/Elixir</a><a href="#ch6-pub">Pub.dev for Dart and Flutter</a><a href="#ch6-compare">Comparing the five</a></div></div>
    <p class="topic-desc">Five ecosystems, five different answers to "who is allowed to publish under this name" and "what does a version mean". The JVM's answer is the most conservative and the most interesting.</p>
    <h3 id="ch6-nuget" data-toc>NuGet</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the csproj, the fields that matter
&lt;Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"&gt;
  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
    &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net8.0;net9.0&lt;/TargetFramework&gt;   // multi-target
    &lt;Version&gt;1.4.2&lt;/Version&gt;
    &lt;PackageId&gt;YourOrg.HttpClient&lt;/PackageId&gt;
    &lt;Description&gt;...&lt;/Description&gt;
    &lt;PackageLicenseExpression&gt;MIT&lt;/PackageLicenseExpression&gt;
    &lt;PackageReadmeFile&gt;README.md&lt;/PackageReadmeFile&gt;
    &lt;RepositoryType&gt;git&lt;/RepositoryType&gt;
    &lt;PublishRepositoryUrl&gt;true&lt;/PublishRepositoryUrl&gt;
    &lt;EmbedUntrackedSources&gt;true&lt;/EmbedUntrackedSources&gt;
    &lt;IncludeSymbols&gt;true&lt;/IncludeSymbols&gt;
    &lt;SymbolPackageFormat&gt;snupkg&lt;/SymbolPackageFormat&gt;
    &lt;ContinuousIntegrationBuild&gt;true&lt;/ContinuousIntegrationBuild&gt;
  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;
  &lt;ItemGroup&gt;
    &lt;None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" /&gt;
    &lt;InternalsVisibleTo Include="YourOrg.HttpClient.Tests" /&gt;
  &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;
&lt;/Project&gt;

// packing and publishing
$ dotnet pack -c Release
$ dotnet nuget push bin/Release/*.nupkg -s https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json -k $NUGET_API_KEY

// THE NUGET SPECIFICS
//   - TFM (target framework moniker) in the package id.
//     YourOrg.HttpClient vs YourOrg.HttpClient.net8.0. Most libraries
//     do NOT do this. It is a legacy of the pre-netstandard era.
//   - multi-targeting produces several .dlls in ONE package. The
//     consumer's build picks the right one. This is the standard.
//   - the .snupkg symbol package is the debug-symbol equivalent of
//     source maps. Ship it. Without it your consumers' stack traces
//     are unreadable.
//   - ContinuousIntegrationBuild=true with a full git history
//     produces deterministic pdb paths (the SourceLink
//     deterministic-builds feature). Without it, the pdb contains an
//     absolute path from the build machine.
//   - NuGet's bare "1.2.3" means ">= 1.2.3", NOT "exactly 1.2.3".
//     This catches everybody once.

// TRUSTED PUBLISHING
//   NuGet supports OIDC exchange for GitHub Actions. Configure the
//   publisher on the package page, and the workflow exchanges the
//   action token. No API key in CI.

// THE LOCKFILE
//   packages.lock.json. Commit it for applications. It also records
//   the content hash of every package, so it is a supply-chain
//   control as well as a reproducibility one.
//   dotnet restore --locked-mode    fails if the lock is out of date.
//   use it in CI: it turns a silent version drift into a build failure.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch6-nuget-tfm">Multi-targeting, and how a consumer picks</h4>
    <p>A library multi-targets so each consumer gets a build tuned to its runtime. NuGet inspects the consumer's target frameworks and chooses the nearest compatible asset, preferring the most specific. The rule of thumb for a library today is to target the lowest version you are willing to support and every current LTS, so an old consumer is not left compiling your source.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>&lt;TargetFrameworks&gt;netstandard2.0;net8.0;net9.0&lt;/TargetFrameworks&gt;
$ dotnet pack -c Release -o ./artifacts
# consumer:
&lt;PackageReference Include="YourOrg.HttpClient" Version="1.4.2" /&gt;
# nearest-asset-wins: a net9.0 app takes the net9.0 asset, a
# .NET Framework app falls back to the netstandard2.0 one.</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-nuget-versioning">The version semantics that catch everyone</h4>
    <p>NuGet's bare <code>1.2.3</code> means <code>[1.2.3, )</code> â€” at least 1.2.3, not exactly it â€” and floating syntax pins to a range that moves. Both are legitimate, but they answer different questions, and mixing them up is how an app silently upgrades a library on restore. An app should pin with a range or exact version; a library should only ever declare lower bounds on its dependencies.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>&lt;PackageReference Include="YourOrg.HttpClient" Version="1.4.*" /&gt;   &lt;!-- floats to newest 1.4.x --&gt;
&lt;PackageReference Include="YourOrg.HttpClient" Version="[1.4.2]" /&gt;  &lt;!-- exactly 1.4.2 --&gt;
&lt;PackageReference Include="YourOrg.HttpClient" Version="1.4.2" /&gt;    &lt;!-- at least 1.4.2 --&gt;
$ dotnet list package --outdated</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-nuget-symbols">Symbol packages and SourceLink</h4>
    <p>A <code>snupkg</code> carries debugging symbols, and SourceLink embeds the commit URL into the PDB so a debugger maps a stack frame back to the source that produced the build. Deterministic builds (<code>ContinuousIntegrationBuild</code>) make the embedding reproducible. Without all three, your consumer gets line numbers that match nothing.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>&lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
  &lt;IncludeSymbols&gt;true&lt;/IncludeSymbols&gt;
  &lt;SymbolPackageFormat&gt;snupkg&lt;/SymbolPackageFormat&gt;
  &lt;ContinuousIntegrationBuild&gt;true&lt;/ContinuousIntegrationBuild&gt;
  &lt;EmbedUntrackedSources&gt;true&lt;/EmbedUntrackedSources&gt;
  &lt;PublishRepositoryUrl&gt;true&lt;/PublishRepositoryUrl&gt;
&lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;
$ dotnet pack -c Release -p:SymbolPackageFormat=snupkg
# the .nupkg and the .snupkg are pushed together</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch6-maven" data-toc>Maven Central and its ecosystem</h3>
    <p>Maven Central is the oldest and most conservative ecosystem here, and its conservatism is the reason it is hard to publish to and easy to trust.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE POM, and the metadata that Central requires
&lt;project&gt;
  &lt;modelVersion&gt;4.0.0&lt;/modelVersion&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;com.yourorg&lt;/groupId&gt;          // must be a domain you control
  &lt;artifactId&gt;http-client&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;1.4.2&lt;/version&gt;
  &lt;packaging&gt;jar&lt;/packaging&gt;             // jar, war, pom, maven-plugin

  &lt;name&gt;HTTP Client&lt;/name&gt;
  &lt;description&gt;...&lt;/description&gt;
  &lt;url&gt;https://example.com/http-client&lt;/url&gt;
  &lt;licenses&gt;
    &lt;license&gt;
      &lt;name&gt;The Apache Software License, Version 2.0&lt;/name&gt;
      &lt;url&gt;https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0.txt&lt;/url&gt;
      &lt;distribution&gt;repo&lt;/distribution&gt;
    &lt;/license&gt;
  &lt;/licenses&gt;
  &lt;developers&gt;...&lt;/developers&gt;
  &lt;scm&gt;...&lt;/scm&gt;

  &lt;dependencies&gt;
    &lt;dependency&gt;
      &lt;groupId&gt;org.apache.httpcomponents.client5&lt;/groupId&gt;
      &lt;artifactId&gt;httpclient5&lt;/artifactId&gt;
      &lt;version&gt;5.4.1&lt;/version&gt;              // NO RANGES. Central
                                                     // requires pinning.
    &lt;/dependency&gt;
  &lt;/dependencies&gt;
&lt;/project&gt;

// THE CENTRAL PORTAL PUBLISHING RULES, which are stricter than
// everything else here
//   1. a namespace verification. You prove you control
//      com.yourorg, usually by a TXT record or a well-known file, and
//      only then may you publish under it. This is the mechanism that
//      makes group-id squatting impractical, and it is the single best
//      anti-squatting control in any ecosystem.
//   2. PINS. Every dependency must have an exact version. A range such
//      as [1.0,2.0) is REJECTED. Rationale: ranges make the artefact
//      you publish non-reproducible.
//   3. every dependency must itself be on Central or a whitelisted
//      repo, or you must bundle it (shade, with relocation).
//   4. sources and javadoc jars must be attached.
//   5. the POM must be valid, with a licence, a description, a URL
//      and SCM information.
//   6. GPG signing of every artefact is mandatory.

// publishing
$ mvn clean verify
$ mvn gpg:sign -Dgpg.passphrase=...        # or the gpg plugin config
$ mvn deploy
//   to Sonatype OSSRH (the legacy path) or the new Central Portal
//   via its API:
//   mvn central-publishing-maven-plugin:publish

// THE CENTRAL PORTAL, which replaced OSSRH
//   a modern REST API at central.sonatype.com. You can publish with
//   curl, with the Maven plugin, or with a Gradle plugin. The
//   namespace verification is the same.

// THE IMMUTABILITY
//   Central is aggressively immutable. A release cannot be
//   republished, and a SNAPSHOT in the snapshot repository is
//   overwritten every 24 hours. If you need a mutable artefact, a
//   snapshot repository is the answer - and it must not be what
//   consumers depend on.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch6-maven-namespace">The namespace verification, in practice</h4>
    <p>Central verifies you control the domain in your <code>groupId</code> before it lets you publish under it. The mechanics are a proof you set up once: the Portal offers a DNS record or a domain file to add, you add it, and ownership persists for that namespace. This single step is why <code>com.yourorg</code> is not quietly claimable by someone else, and it is the strongest anti-squatting control in any registry in this guide.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># Central Portal - Namespaces page, after adding a DNS proof:
#   Type: TXT
#   Host: _central.central.sonatype.com
#   Value: the token the portal shows you
$ dig +short TXT _central.central.sonatype.com
"your-token"
# then the Portal verifies and the namespace becomes available.</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-maven-signing">Signing, and where the key lives</h4>
    <p>Every central artefact must carry a GPG signature, and the verification key is published separately. Create a signing key, export its public half to a keyserver, and configure the passphrase in Maven settings rather than in the POM or a build file. The signature covers the artefact: a consumer can prove it came from the key holder.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ gpg --generate-key
$ gpg --export --armor your-email &gt; public-key.asc
$ gpg --send-keys --keyserver keyserver.ubuntu.com KEYID
# ~/.m2/settings.xml
&lt;settings&gt;
  &lt;profiles&gt;
    &lt;profile&gt;
      &lt;id&gt;central&lt;/id&gt;
      &lt;properties&gt;
        &lt;gpg.keyname&gt;KEYID&lt;/gpg.keyname&gt;
        &lt;gpg.passphrase&gt;from-an-environment-variable&lt;/gpg.passphrase&gt;
      &lt;/properties&gt;
    &lt;/profile&gt;
  &lt;/profiles&gt;
&lt;/settings&gt;</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-maven-snapshot">SNAPSHOTs are not releases</h4>
    <p>Central is immutable for published versions but explicitly mutable for SNAPSHOTs: a <code>x.y.z-SNAPSHOT</code> is re-uploadable and is refreshed on an interval. The trap is treating a SNAPSHOT as a consumable release. CI should test against a SNAPSHOT branch and a release build should depend only on published versions, otherwise your consumers' builds float on whatever was uploadable.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># during development, consumers can test the next version:
$ mvn deploy   # uploads yourlib-1.5.0-SNAPSHOT.jar to snapshots repo
# a release makes it permanent:
$ mvn release:prepare release:perform   # or the git-tag flow
# and the deployed artefact is then immutable on Central.</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch6-gradle" data-toc>Gradle and the wider JVM</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// GRADLE, the build tool
   plugins {
     id("java-library")
     id("maven-publish")
     id("signing")
   }
   group = "com.yourorg"
   version = "1.4.2"
   java { toolchain { languageVersion = JavaLanguageVersion.of(21) } }

   publishing {
     publications {
       create&lt;MavenPublication&gt;("maven") {
         from(components["java"])
         pom { name = "HTTP Client"; url = "https://example.com" }
         artifact(javadocJar)
         artifact(sourcesJar)
       }
     }
     repositories {
       maven {
         name = "central"
         url = uri("https://central.sonatype.com/api/v1/publisher")
         credentials {
           username = providers.gradleProperty("centralUsername").get()
           password = providers.gradleProperty("centralPassword").get()
         }
       }
     }
   }
   signing { sign(publishing.publications["maven"]) }

$ ./gradlew publish

// THE JVM REGISTRIES, and they are not interchangeable
//   Maven Central     the canonical one. everything indexes it.
//   Google Maven      AndroidX and Google libraries
//                     dl.google.com/dl/android/maven2. NOT on Central,
//                     so a consumer needs both repositories declared.
//   JitPack           builds from a GitHub repo on demand. Handy for
//                     unpublished libraries; not a long-term home.
//   Sonatype snapshots oss.sonatype.org/content/repositories/snapshots
//   Spring milestones  repo.spring.io/milestone, for Spring itself
//   JBoss, Amazon, JetBrains Space   their own artefacts
//   Confluent, Red Hat, and a long tail of vendor repositories

// THE POM TRAP, which surprises everyone exactly once
//   Maven pulls transitive dependencies from POMs. If your dependency
//   has a wrong or missing version in its POM, the whole graph is
//   wrong, and it is not your fault.
//   Gradle module metadata (.module files) carries the full dependency
//   graph including variants, and is what Android and modern Gradle
//   use. Publish it:
//     publishing.publications.create&lt;MavenPublication&gt;("maven") {
//       from(components["java"])
//     }
//   the .module file is generated automatically from the java component.

// THE BOM, for multi-module releases
   dependencies {
     implementation platform("com.yourorg:bom:1.4.2")
     implementation("com.yourorg:core")   // no version, from the BOM
   }
//   publish the BOM alongside your libraries so consumers get
//   consistent versions across a family.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch6-gradle-module-meta">The .module file, and why Gradle prefers it</h4>
    <p>Publishing from a Gradle component writes a <code>.module</code> file alongside the POM. That file carries the full dependency graph with variants (api/implementation, runtime/compile scopes, attribute constraints), which is the information Maven's POM model cannot express. Consumers using Gradle resolve against the module metadata; Maven consumers silently fall back to the POM, so keep both consistent.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>publishing {
  publications {
    create&lt;MavenPublication&gt;("maven") {
      from(components["java"])
      // the .module file is generated automatically from the component
    }
  }
}
$ ls build/publications/maven/
maven.pom  maven.module</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-gradle-bom">A BOM, for version families</h4>
    <p>When several libraries release together, consumers import a BOM (bill of materials) and omit versions on the libraries that belong to it. In Gradle the BOM is published with <code>java-platform</code>, which marks the platform constraints as such rather than as direct dependencies.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>plugins { id("java-platform"); id("maven-publish") }
group = "com.yourorg"
version = "1.4.2"

dependencies {
  constraints {
    api("com.yourorg:core:1.4.2")
    api("com.yourorg:http-client:1.4.2")
  }
}
publishing { publications { create&lt;MavenPublication&gt;("bom") { from(components["javaPlatform"]) } } }
# consumer: implementation(platform("com.yourorg:bom:1.4.2"))</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-gradle-central-nexus">Publishing to Central from Gradle</h4>
    <p>The <code>nexus-publish-plugin</code> wraps the Sonatype workflow: it opens a staging repository, uploads, closes it, and promotes after verification. The publishing plugin and the signing plugin take care of the remainder, so a release is one task invocation. Keep the credentials in <code>gradle.properties</code> or environment variables, never in the build script.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>plugins { id("io.github.gradle-nexus.publish-plugin") version "2.0.0" }
nexusPublishing {
  repositories { sonatype() }
}
signing { sign(publishing.publications["maven"]) }
# ~/.gradle/gradle.properties
centralUsername=your-token
centralPassword=your-secret
$ ./gradlew publishToSonatype closeAndReleaseSonatypeStagingRepository</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch6-crates" data-toc>crates.io</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// Cargo.toml
[package]
name = "your-crate"
version = "1.4.2"
edition = "2021"
rust-version = "1.74"
license = "MIT OR Apache-2.0"
description = "A crate that does one thing well."
repository = "https://github.com/yourorg/your-crate"
documentation = "https://docs.rs/your-crate"
keywords = ["parsing", "text"]
categories = ["text-processing"]
readme = "README.md"
include = ["src/**", "README.md", "LICENSE-*"]

[dependencies]
serde = { version = "1.0", features = ["derive"] }
tokio = { version = "1.38", features = ["full"] }

[dev-dependencies]
criterion = "0.5"

[badges]
maintenance = { status = "actively-developed" }

// publish
$ cargo publish
$ cargo publish --dry-run          # ALWAYS. it builds and lists files
$ cargo publish --token $CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN

// THE CRATES.IO-SPECIFIC THINGS

// 1. TWO LICENCES, because Rust has a convention
     license = "MIT OR Apache-2.0"
//   the dual licence lets users pick. This is community standard and
//   you should follow it unless you have a reason not to.

// 2. DOCS.RS BUILDS YOUR DOCS AUTOMATICALLY
//   the `documentation` field links the rendered docs, and the badge
//   is a real conversion driver. Write doc comments:
//     /// Parses a date string.
//     ///
    /// # Errors
    /// Returns an error for an unparseable string.
//   the `# Errors` section is enforced by clippy::missing_errors_doc.
//   docs.rs builds with your minimum supported Rust version by
//   default, so your examples must compile on the oldest version you
//   claim. That is a feature: nobody discovers your MSRV is a lie.

// 3. THE NAMESPACES
   crates.io has no account-wide namespace, but it has an "org" which
   can be a GitHub org or a verified domain, and the org owns crates.
//   RESERVE names before you need them:
//     you can reserve a name without publishing, which stops anyone
//     taking it. Do this for every name you might use.

// 4. DEPENDENCY VERSIONS ARE LIKE NPM
     serde = "1.0"      caret by default: >=1.0.0 &lt;2.0.0
     serde = "~1.0"     >=1.0.0 &lt;1.1.0
     serde = "=1.0.197" exact
//   and there is one important addition:
//     serde = "1.0" is interpreted as ">=1.0.0, &lt;2.0.0" BUT if you have
//     already a 1.x in the graph, Cargo prefers the one it has.

// 5. Cargo.lock
//   commit it for binaries. gitignore it for libraries, which is the
//   current convention. A published .crate does not contain it.

// 6. yanking
$ cargo yank --vers 1.4.1 --undo
//   yanking is soft: existing lockfiles keep working, new resolutions
//   skip it. Exactly the right behaviour, and npm could learn from it.

// 7. the "publish" workflow
   rust-lang uses crates.io as its model. It is a service where a
   token is exchanged via a static API token, and the docs
   configuration is deliberately minimal.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch6-crates-dryrun">The dry run is an inclusion check</h4>
    <p><code>cargo publish --dry-run</code> packages the crate exactly as the registry would â€” same file list, same metadata â€” and it is the step that catches a missing licence file or an accidental <code>.env</code> in the package. The file list is governed by <code>include</code>/<code>exclude</code> and the git state, with the defaults favouring git-tracked files. Always read the list before a real push.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ cargo package --list            # just the file list
Cargo.toml
Cargo.lock                        # auto-included for libs? no - binaries only
LICENSE-MIT
README.md
src/lib.rs
$ cargo publish --dry-run --allow-dirty   # build and verify, no upload
$ cargo publish</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-crates-docs">docs.rs owns your documentation</h4>
    <p>Every published crate gets an automatic docs.rs build, and the build defaults matter: it builds with the crate's MSRV unless you declare <code>metadata.docs.rs</code>. Configuring all features and feature-gated docs means the documentation visitors see is the documentation they should get. The <code># Errors</code> and <code># Panics</code> doc sections are not optional flourishes; clippy's lints enforce them.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>[package.metadata.docs.rs]
all-features = true
rustdoc-args = ["--cfg", "docsrs", "--html-in-header", "extra.html"]
# src/lib.rs
#![cfg_attr(docsrs, feature(doc_cfg))]
/// Parses a date string.
///
/// # Errors
/// Returns an error for an unparseable string.
pub fn parse(s: &amp;str) -&gt; Result&lt;Date&gt; { /* ... */ Ok(Date) }</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-crates-yank">Yanking, and how ownership is organised</h4>
    <p>A yank is a marker that tells new resolutions to skip the version; existing lockfiles continue to install it. That soft model is why a bad release is survivable: the damage is bounded to the window between publish and yank. Namespace protection comes from owners â€” a crate owner can be a GitHub organisation, which is how a team shares the publishing credential without sharing one person's account.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ cargo yank --vers 1.4.1          # skip on new resolutions
$ cargo yank --vers 1.4.1 --undo
$ cargo owner --add github:yourorg  # the org owns crates too
$ cargo owner --list
# publishing from CI with a short-lived token:
$ cargo publish --token "$CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN"</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch6-goproxy" data-toc>Go modules and proxies</h3>
    <p>Go is the odd one out: there is no registry you publish to, only a proxy you push a tag to.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THERE IS NO PUBLISH STEP FOR A MODULE
//   you have a git repository with semantic version tags
$ git tag -a v1.4.2 -m "v1.4.2"
$ git push origin v1.4.2

//   that is it. The proxy notices the tag and fetches the zip.
//   a module version is the MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH part of a v-prefixed tag.
//   There is no v0.x, no pre-release, and no build metadata.

// THE PROXIES
   proxy.golang.org   the default, in GONOSUMDB/GOPROXY
   goproxy.cn         the widely used mirror
   goproxy.io         a third option
   an internal Athens  a self-hosted proxy, for air-gapped or
                      private-module setups

// go.mod
   module github.com/yourorg/yourlib
   go 1.22
   require (
     github.com/spf13/cobra v1.8.0
     golang.org/x/sync v0.7.0
   )

// THE THINGS THAT SURPRISE EVERYONE

// 1. MODULE PATHS ARE URLs, AND THEY ARE PERMANENT
     module github.com/yourorg/yourlib
//   this is a hard, permanent identity. Renaming the GitHub org is a
//   breaking change to every consumer, and the proxy will not let you
//   change it. Choose the org name knowing it outlives the repo.

// 2. THERE ARE NO RANGES. EVER.
   require github.com/spf13/cobra v1.8.0        exact
//   and MVS, minimal version selection:
//     if A requires X v1.2.0 and B requires X v1.5.0, the build
//     uses v1.5.0 for BOTH. No negotiation, no conflict.
//   This is Go's deliberate trade: no version conflicts, ever, at the
//   cost of sometimes getting a newer version than a library wanted
//   and tested against. It usually works, and when it does not, the
//   replace directive or a workspace pins it.

// 3. THE +incompatible SUFFIX, which is how v2 happens
     module github.com/yourorg/yourlib/v2
//   for v2 and above you append the major to the module path. There
//   is a stated exception: v0 to v1 needs no suffix, and v1 to v2
//   does. Your go.mod is wrong until you do this, and the error
//   message is famously unhelpful.

// 4. go.sum IS MANDATORY AND VERIFIED
//   every module hash is checked on every build, and a mismatch is a
//   hard failure. This is the strongest default integrity guarantee
//   of any ecosystem in this chapter, and it is why a Go supply-chain
//   attack is much harder than an npm one.

// 5. RETRACTING
     retract v1.2.0  // had a security issue, see GHSA-xxxx
//   a retraction is a comment in the NEXT release's go.mod. It does
//   not delete anything; it tells tooling not to select that version.
//   Same honest model as cargo yank.

// 6. PRIVATE MODULES
   GOPRIVATE=github.com/yourorg/*   skip the proxy and sumdb for these
   GONOSUMCHECK, GONOSUMDB, GOINSECURE  the older, worse-named knobs
//   a private proxy plus GOPRIVATE is the modern answer.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch6-goproxy-flow">The full tag-to-consumer flow</h4>
    <p>Publishing a Go module is pushing a tag; there is no upload, no token, no dashboard. The proxy fetches the tagged commit, zips the module, and serves it â€” and <code>go.sum</code> pins the hash on every consumer. Because the module path is a URL, moving the repository or renaming the organisation breaks every existing consumer, so the org name is a permanent decision made once.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ git tag -a v1.4.2 -m "v1.4.2"
$ git push origin v1.4.2
# consumer side:
$ go get github.com/yourorg/yourlib@v1.4.2
$ go mod tidy
$ go build ./...
# go.sum now contains the module hash for that exact version</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-goproxy-mvs">Minimal version selection, and the escapes</h4>
    <p>MVS means the build uses the maximum of every required version â€” no transitive version conflicts, and no resolver to negotiate with. When MVS picks a newer version than a library wants, the mechanisms are <code>replace</code> (pin one module to another source or version) and workspaces (develop several modules as one unit). Both are explicit, so the divergence is visible in the module file.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># go.mod of the application
module example.com/myapp
go 1.22
require github.com/yourorg/yourlib v1.4.2
replace github.com/yourorg/yourlib =&gt; ../yourlib      # local dev
# go.work, for a multi-module repo
go 1.22
use (
  ./app
  ./lib
)</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-goproxy-retract">Retractions, the honest fix</h4>
    <p>A retracted version stays downloadable â€” nothing is deleted â€” but tooling treats it as bad and will not select it for a fresh resolution. The retraction is written into the next version's <code>go.mod</code>, which is the unusual part: the statement travels with the code, so any consumer's module graph carries the warning even without visiting the registry.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// go.mod of v1.4.3
module github.com/yourorg/yourlib
go 1.22
retract (
  v1.4.1  // breakout bug in the csv path
  v1.4.0  // API changed without a major bump
)
// consumers' builds now skip 1.4.0 and 1.4.1 automatically
$ go list -m -versions github.com/yourorg/yourlib</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch6-hex" data-toc>Hex.pm and Erlang/Elixir</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// mix.exs
defmodule YourApp.MixProject do
  use Mix.Project

  def project do
    [
      app: :your_app,
      version: "1.4.2",
      elixir: "~&gt; 1.15",
      description: "A library that does one thing well.",
      package: package(),
      deps: deps(),
      docs: docs()
    ]
  end

  defp package do
    [
      licenses: ["MIT", "Apache-2.0"],
      links: %{"GitHub" =&gt; "https://github.com/yourorg/your_app"},
      files: ~w(lib mix.exs README.md LICENSE)
    ]
  end

  defp deps do
    [
      {:jason, "~&gt; 1.4"},
      {:req, "~&gt; 0.5"}
    ]
  end
end

// $ mix hex.publish
// $ mix hex.publish --dry-run
// $ mix hex.owners add you@example.com

// THE HEX-SPECIFICS
//   - the dry run builds and shows what would be uploaded, and it is
//     the step that catches a missing file. Run it always.
//   - Mix writes the built docs into the package by default, which is
//     a nicety rather than a step.
//   - hex.owners lets you ADD an organisation to your package. An org
//     has its own members list, so removing a person is a hex.owners
//     remove, which is the same hygiene point as ch3-2fa.
//   - the build tool has a target-agnostic design: a library depends
//     on nothing, which is why the dependency list is so short.

// THE OTP RELEASE MODEL, which is a different universe
//   Hex.pm has no releases in the sense that Elixir apps are usually
//   distributed. If you are shipping an application rather than a
//   library, you ship:
//     - a container image (chapter 7)
//     - a release tarball from mix release, with ERTS included
//     - or a self-contained binary, which Elixir 1.14+ can produce
//   There is no Hex package for an application. Hex is for libraries.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch6-hex-dryrun">The dry run that catches the missing file</h4>
    <p>Hex builds the package from the <code>files</code> list in <code>mix.exs</code>, and the dry run shows exactly what would be uploaded. A path that does not exist, an unchecked-in file, or a <code>LICENSE</code> that never made it into the list all surface here. Because Hex re-downloads and compiles the package to verify it, a dry run is also a compile check of the artefact.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ mix hex.build                  # builds the tarball locally
$ mix hex.publish --dry-run       # the upload check
Compiled 2 files (.ex)
  Package contains 3 files:
    lib/your_app.ex
    mix.exs
    README.md
$ mix hex.publish                 # the real thing</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-hex-owners">Owners, organisations and who can publish</h4>
    <p>Hex permissions are per-owner with roles, and an organisation can hold a package so a person leaving does not strand the release path. <code>mix hex.owners</code> is the whole surface â€” adding, removing, and transferring are the same command family. A package whose only owner is one human is a bus-factor problem with a one-line fix.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ mix hex.owners add you@example.com
$ mix hex.owners add yourorg \
    --organisation your-org-name
$ mix hex.owners remove ex-colleague@example.com
$ mix hex.owners list</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-hex-release">Applications are releases, not packages</h4>
    <p>Hex holds libraries; an Elixir application is distributed as a release â€” a self-contained directory with the Erlang runtime, built by <code>mix release</code> â€” or as a container image. This separation is deliberate and it is why Hex can stay a library index without a runtime matrix.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>MIX_ENV=prod mix release            # bundles ERTS by default
# _build/prod/rel/myapp/bin/myapp
$ _build/prod/rel/myapp/bin/myapp start
$ _build/prod/rel/myapp/bin/myapp eval "IO.puts(System.version())"
# or ship the release as a container (chapter 7)</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch6-pub" data-toc>Pub.dev for Dart and Flutter</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// pubspec.yaml
name: your_package
description: A package that does one thing well, and does it properly.
version: 1.4.2
homepage: https://example.com/your_package
repository: https://github.com/yourorg/your_package
issue_tracker: https://github.com/yourorg/your_package/issues
documentation: https://pub.dev/documentation/your_package/latest

environment:
  sdk: "&gt;=3.4.0 &lt;4.0.0"
  flutter: "&gt;=3.22.0"

dependencies:
  http: ^1.2.0
  meta: ^1.12.0

dev_dependencies:
  test: ^1.25.0
  flutter_lints: ^4.0.0

flutter:
  uses-material-design: true

// $ dart pub publish --dry-run
// $ dart pub publish
// $ flutter pub publish --dry-run     for a plugin with native assets

// THE PUB-SPECIFICS
//   - dry-run is MANDATORY. It checks the score, the pubspec, the
//     CHANGELOG, and the example. Fail a build if it fails.
//   - pub uses SemVer with the same ^ and ~ operators as npm.
//   - pub score is a real quality gate. It is 0-100 and it accounts
//     for: follows likes, pub points, likes, popularity, and whether
//     the package is up to date. A package under 50 is hard to get
//     discovered.
//   - pub points measure whether your example and your code work with
//     the current SDK, in both Dart and Flutter. Fixing a pub points
//     warning is usually a two-minute change and it is the difference
//     between being usable and being ignored.
//   - verified publishers, which is pub.dev's answer to ch3-2fa. A
//     verified publisher is a Google-verified account owning the name.
//   - a package is a LIBRARY only. An application is not published to
//     pub; you ship a binary, a container, or a hosted service.

// THE PLUGIN CASE, which is separate
//   flutter pub publish publishes a plugin. It must declare platforms,
//   federated plugin structure if applicable, and it needs a pubspec
//   with the flutter key. Native assets mean an Android .aar and an
//   iOS .framework, which have their own size limits and their own
//   review processes in the stores.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch6-pub-score">The scoring gate, and how to read it</h4>
    <p>Pub assigns a package a score out of 100, and the sub-scores are the practical checklist: pub points decline for every warning in <code>analyze</code>, an out-of-date SDK range, a missing example, or a file that should not ship. Most fixes are two-minute edits, so a failing score is usually a sign nobody ran the checks rather than a hard design problem.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ dart pub publish --dry-run
$ dart analyze
$ dart format --set-exit-if-changed .
# a pubspec that keeps points:
#   environment: sdk: "&gt;=3.4.0 &lt;4.0.0"    (current, wide range)
#   an example/ with a runnable main.dart
#   a CHANGELOG.md, README.md, LICENSE</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-pub-verified">Verified publishers and domain proof</h4>
    <p>A verified publisher is a Google-verified organisation that owns one or more package names, shown by a badge on the listing. Verification requires proving control of a domain: pub.dev asks for a DNS record, and once verified, only that publisher may publish under the verified name. It is also the answer to name squatting of popular package names.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># pub.dev - Verify a domain, add the TXT record:
#   host:  yourdomain.com
#   value: YOUR-PUBLISHER-TOKEN
$ dig +short TXT yourdomain.com
"YOUR-PUBLISHER-TOKEN"
# then confirm in the dashboard; the badge appears on your packages</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-pub-plugin">Flutter plugin structure</h4>
    <p>A plugin with native assets declares its platforms in the pubspec, and each platform maps to a class or package. The federated pattern splits the platform implementations into separate packages, so a package can depend on the plugin without pulling native SDKs. The <code>flutter</code> key in the pubspec is what tells the tooling a plugin (not a library) is being published.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>flutter:
  plugin:
    platforms:
      android:
        package: com.yourorg.your_package
        pluginClass: YourPackagePlugin
      ios:
        pluginClass: YourPackagePlugin
      macos:
        pluginClass: YourPackagePlugin
# a federated implementation package depends on the app-facing API:
dependencies:
  your_package_platform_interface: ^2.0.0</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch6-compare" data-toc>Comparing the five</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>//            NuGet    Central   crates   Go      Pub
// publish    token    token+    token    git     token
//                     GPG              tag
// immutable  yes      yes       soft    yes     yes
// namespace account  DOMAIN    org     URL     verified
//                     verified         immut.  publisher
// signature  yes      MANDATORY yes     no      no
// provenance growing  rare      no      n/a     no
// ranges     yes      NO        yes     NONE    yes
// lockfile   yes      no        binaries no      yes
// test index none     none      none    n/a     none
// binaries   runtime- no        no      n/a     no
//            specific
//
// THE TWO STANDS OUT
//   Maven Central is the only one that REQUIRES signing and pins, and
//   that is why publishing is a genuine chore there. It is a
//   deliberate trade for a stable, auditable central index, and it
//   predates the ecosystem of accidental publishes.
//   Go is the only one with no publish step and no range syntax. Both
//   are consequences of a deliberate design choice, and both are
//   reasonable once you understand the reasoning.
//
// THE GENERAL PATTERN, for chapter 15
//   1. names are permanent and global. Verify the namespace, own an
//      organisation, remove people who leave.
//   2. versions are immutable. A release cannot be corrected.
//   3. the publish credential is the attack surface. Make it
//      short-lived and CI-only.
//   4. a range is a promise about your consumers' ability to upgrade.
//      Test more versions than you think you need to.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch6-compare-publish-model">The three publish models</h4>
    <p>The five ecosystems reduce to three shapes. NuGet and crates.io are conventional registries â€” upload a built artefact with a token. Maven Central is a conventional registry with mandatory signing. And Go has no registry at all: publishing is a git tag, which is why its only security control is the module hash in <code>go.sum</code>. Knowing which shape you are in predicts most of the failure modes before they happen.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># conventional, unsigned:  NuGet, crates.io, Pub
$ dotnet nuget push pkg.nupkg -s https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json -k "$TOKEN"
$ cargo publish --token "$TOKEN"
# conventional, signed:     Maven Central
$ mvn deploy &amp;&amp; mvn gpg:sign
# no registry at all:       Go
$ git tag v1.4.2 &amp;&amp; git push origin v1.4.2</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-compare-immutability">Four flavours of immutability</h4>
    <p>How a bad version is handled differs sharply. NuGet allows re-uploading a deleted version in limited windows and otherwise marks listings; Maven Central is aggressively immutable, so the fix is always a later version; crates.io and Go retract softly â€” the version stays downloadable but tooling avoids it. The soft model is the one that lets a release pipeline recover without a version-number funeral.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># NuGet: unlist, do not delete
$ dotnet nuget delete YourOrg.HttpClient 1.4.1 -s https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json --non-interactive
# crates.io: yank leaves the download in place
$ cargo yank --vers 1.4.1
# Go: retract travels in the next go.mod
retract v1.2.0
# Maven Central: nothing to delete; release 1.4.2 with a changelog note</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch6-compare-supply-chain">The supply-chain default, per ecosystem</h4>
    <p>Ask of any new ecosystem: where is the integrity bound? Go pins every module hash in <code>go.sum</code> and verifies at build time; NuGet records content hashes in the lock file; Maven verifies GPG signatures; crates.io and Pub rely on the registry serving the bytes it stored. The strongest setups combine a registry that never rewrites history with a consumer-side lock that pins hashes â€” nothing in this list both deletes and is safe.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># consumer-side integrity:
$ go mod verify                 # go.sum against the module cache
$ dotnet restore --locked-mode  # packages.lock.json must match
# publisher-side provenance:
$ mvn gpg:sign                  # artefact signatures, mandatory on Central
$ cosign attest ...             # container world, see chapter 7</code></pre></div>

    <div class="ex"><h4>Exercises 6</h4><ol>
      <li>Publish a NuGet package with multi-targeting, a symbol package and deterministic build settings. Install it from a consumer project and confirm the correct TFM is selected and stack traces resolve through the symbol package.</li>
      <li>Take a Maven project, add a POM with every Central-required field, sign it, and walk through the namespace verification. Record every field Central rejects if it is missing, so you know the requirements rather than discovering them one at a time.</li>
      <li>Publish a crate to a local registry or a dry run, and check the file list, the dual licence, and the <code>docs.rs</code> badge. Then write a <code># Errors</code> doc comment and confirm clippy enforces it.</li>
      <li>Publish a Go module by tagging, import it from a second module, and then bump to v2 and add the <code>/v2</code> suffix. Record the exact error you get before and after the suffix, because the before-state is famously unhelpful.</li>
      <li>Run <code>dart pub publish --dry-run</code> on a Flutter plugin, and fix every pub-points warning it reports. Record your score before and after.</li>
    </ol></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
<h2 id="ch7" data-toc>7 Â· Containers: Docker Hub, GHCR and the OCI Ecosystem</h2>
    <div class="ch-toc"><button type="button" class="ch-toc-head" aria-expanded="true"><span class="arrow">â–¸</span>Contents &middot; 7 Â· Containers: Docker Hub, GHCR and the OCI Ecosystem</button><div class="ch-toc-list"><a href="#ch7-oci">What OCI actually specifies</a><a href="#ch7-layers">Layers, and why your image is huge</a><a href="#ch7-dockerfile">A Dockerfile that produces a small image</a><a href="#ch7-tagging">Tagging, and the :latest trap</a><a href="#ch7-registry">Choosing a registry</a><a href="#ch7-push">Building and pushing properly</a><a href="#ch7-multarch">Multi-architecture images</a><a href="#ch7-scan">Scanning, and what SBOMs are for</a><a href="#ch7-signing">Signing container images</a><a href="#ch7-immutable">Immutability, digests and reproducibility</a><a href="#ch7-rate">Rate limits and the practical workarounds</a></div></div>
    <p class="topic-desc">A container image is the one artefact type where the package manager, the version control system and the build system are all the same thing. That is convenient, and it means the usual supply-chain defences have to be recreated rather than inherited.</p>
    <h3 id="ch7-oci" data-toc>What OCI actually specifies</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// OCI, the Open Container Initiative, is a set of specifications that
// replaced "Docker images" with a portable format. Three specifications:

// 1. THE IMAGE SPEC: a manifest and its layers
// 2. THE DISTRIBUTION SPEC: how a registry stores and serves them
// 3. THE RUNTIME SPEC: how a runtime mounts them (OCI runtimes)

// THE IMAGE, structurally
   manifest
     Config  (JSON): env, cmd, entrypoint, user, labels, history
     Layer 1 (tar.gz, the base image)
     Layer 2 (tar.gz, your dependencies)
     Layer 3 (tar.gz, your build output)
//   each layer is a DIFF, not a full filesystem. Two images sharing a
//   base share those layers on disk and on the registry, which is where
//   the efficiency comes from.

// THE DIGEST IS THE IDENTITY, and it is the important part
   sha256:9f2a1b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1c2d3e4f5a6b7c8d9e0
//   computed from the manifest, which includes the config digest and
//   the layer digests. Change any byte and it changes.
//   a tag is a MUTABLE POINTER to a digest. The digest is the artefact.
//   Everything in this chapter that follows from that sentence.

// MULTI-ARCHITECTURE, which is an index of manifests
   index (manifest list)
     -&gt; manifest   linux/amd64
     -&gt; manifest   linux/arm64
     -&gt; manifest   linux/arm/v7
//   so one tag resolves to the right image for the machine, and the
//   digest you get back is the PLATFORM-SPECIFIC manifest's digest.
//   This surprises people who pin by digest and then get a different
//   digest on a different architecture. Use a tag, or pin the INDEX
//   digest with --platform.

// MEDIA TYPES AND ARTIFACT TYPES
//   a registry can hold more than runnable images. The same manifest
//   and blob storage holds Helm charts, WASM modules, OCI artefacts,
//   and anything else. "Artifact Hub" and registries generally are
//   converging on one storage backend for all of it.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch7-oci-digest">The digest is computed from the manifest</h4>
    <p>An OCI manifest is a JSON document that names its config and layers by digest, and the manifest's own digest is the identity of the image. Change any byte in any layer and every digest upstream changes too. That is why a digest reference is a content-addressed claim â€” <code>image@sha256:â€¦</code> cannot be faked by pushing a different image under the same name.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ docker buildx imagetools inspect yourorg/myapp:1.4.2 \
    --format '{{json .Manifest.Digest}}'
"sha256:9f2a1b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1c2d3e4f5a6b7c8d9e0"
# layer digests appear in the manifest too:
$ crane manifest yourorg/myapp@sha256:9f2a... | head -20</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-oci-artifacts">The registry is not only for images</h4>
    <p>Because the distribution spec stores opaque blobs under a manifest, the same registry backend serves Helm charts, OCI artefacts, signatures and SBOMs â€” attached to an image with the referrers API. <code>cosign attest</code> and <code>buildx --sbom</code> both store their output this way, so the artefacts travel with the image instead of living in a sidecar store. Artifact Hub and the major registries now share this storage model.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ oras push ghcr.io/yourorg/mychart:1.0.0 ./chart.tgz
# attach a signature or SBOM as an OCI referrer:
$ cosign attest --predicate slsa.json --type slsaprovenance \
    ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp@sha256:9f2a...
$ cosign download attestation ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp@sha256:9f2a...</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-oci-shared-layers">Shared layers are the economics</h4>
    <p>Two images built from the same base share those layer blobs on disk and on the registry, so pull bandwidth and storage scale with <em>new</em> bytes, not with image count. This is why a base image that pins a digest still benefits everyone â€” and why a tiny change near the bottom of a Dockerfile can re-upload the entire layer stack above it if the cache misses.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># pulling a second image that shares the base costs only the new layers
$ du -sh ~/.docker  # the local layer cache
# a registry reports shared blobs:
$ crane manifest ghcr.io/yourorg/other@sha256:... | grep -c '"digest"'
# both images point at the same base layer digests</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch7-layers" data-toc>Layers, and why your image is huge</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WHY A 300MB IMAGE HAPPENS
   FROM node:20          // 1100 MB uncompressed
   RUN apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y build-essential python3
   RUN npm install      // 400 MB of node_modules, plus a build toolchain
   COPY . .
   CMD ["node", "app.js"]
//   result: a 1.4 GB image, and every layer is cached FOREVER, so
//   changing one line of source does not help at all

// THE LAYER RULES, which are counter-intuitive

// 1. A LAYER IS A DIFF. Deleting a file in a later layer does not
//    remove it from the image. It only adds a whiteout entry.
//    the bytes are still in the earlier layer, on disk and on the
//    registry. This is the single most misunderstood thing about
//    image size.
      RUN apt-get install -y curl     # 30 MB
      RUN rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* # does NOT recover the 30 MB
//    the same command in ONE layer does recover it, because the layer
//    is only the net diff.

// 2. A CHANGED LAYER INVALIDATES EVERYTHING AFTER IT
//    COPY . .  near the top, so a code change only invalidates the
//    final layer. COPY . .  first means every code change rebuilds
//    and re-uploads every layer below it.

// 3. CACHE KEYS INCLUDE THE COMMAND AND THE PARENT LAYER
//    change a RUN command and that layer and everything after rebuilds.

// THE MEASUREMENT
$ docker image history yourimage:1.0 --no-trunc
// shows every layer with its size. Find the layer that is 800MB and
// that is your answer. Read this output for every image you build.

// MULTI-STAGE, the actual solution
   FROM node:20 AS build
   WORKDIR /app
   COPY package*.json ./
   RUN npm ci
   COPY . .
   RUN npm run build &amp;&amp; npm prune --production

   FROM node:20-slim AS runtime
   WORKDIR /app
   ENV NODE_ENV=production
   COPY --from=build /app/node_modules ./node_modules
   COPY --from=build /app/dist ./dist
   USER node
   EXPOSE 3000
   CMD ["node", "dist/main.js"]
//   the build stage is discarded. The final image contains only the
//   runtime dependencies, no compiler, no source, no dev modules.
//   Typical: 1.4 GB -&gt; 180 MB, and the two stages push in parallel,
//   so the build is also faster.

// AND DISTROLESS, when you need the smallest possible
   FROM gcr.io/distroless/nodejs20-debian12
   COPY --from=build /app/dist ./dist
//   no shell, no package manager, no libc you did not ask for.
//   a useful property: a distroless image has no shell, so a
//   command-injection attacker cannot get one. That is a real
//   security boundary, not a size trick.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch7-layers-measure">Measure before you fix</h4>
    <p>The layer list is the diagnosis: <code>docker image history</code> shows every layer with its size, and <code>dive</code> overlays it against the filesystem so you can see exactly which files each layer adds and deletes. An 800 MB layer is rarely subtle â€” it is almost always the dependency install or a build cache that was never cleaned.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ docker image history yourimage:1.0 --no-trunc
IMAGE          CREATED       SIZE
abc123         2 min ago     12MB    COPY --from=build /app/dist
...
$ dive yourimage:1.0        # interactive layer-by-layer size view
# per-layer efficiency is reported as a percentage you can ratchet</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-layers-consolidate">Consolidate, then clean, in the same layer</h4>
    <p>The fix for a bloated <code>RUN</code> is to chain every step of that logical operation in one line â€” install, use, remove the package manager's lists â€” because the layer records only the final diff. Splitting them across layers stores the intermediate state forever. Apt indexes are the canonical recovered bytes.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># wrong: the apt lists survive in the first layer
RUN apt-get update
RUN apt-get install -y curl
# right: one layer, net diff only
RUN apt-get update \
 &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends curl \
 &amp;&amp; rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
# --no-install-recommends keeps the half of Debian you do not want</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-layers-ordering">Cache invalidation runs downhill</h4>
    <p>Each layer's cache key includes its command and the parent layer's content. A layer that changes invalidates every layer after it but none before. Ordering the Dockerfile so the frequently-changing lines come last is what keeps rebuilds incremental; the dependency-manifest copy is the keystone line that makes the whole middle of the file a cache hit.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>FROM node:20-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY package.json package-lock.json ./   # changes rarely
RUN npm ci --omit=dev                     # cached until the manifests change
COPY . .                                 # changes every commit; stays last
# rebuilding after a source edit re-runs only the final COPY layer</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch7-dockerfile" data-toc>A Dockerfile that produces a small image</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// BASE IMAGE CHOICE, the biggest single decision
   node:20            1100 MB, full Debian, includes build tools
   node:20-slim       200 MB,  no build tools
   node:20-alpine     180 MB,  musl not glibc: smaller BUT different
   gcr.io/distroless/nodejs20   ~30 MB, no shell, no package manager
   scratch             0 MB,    nothing at all

// THE ALPINE TRAP, which bites everyone once
//   musl libc is not glibc. A package compiled against glibc may not
//   run on musl. It usually works, and then it does not, in production,
//   at 3am, in a way that is very hard to reproduce.
//   also: node-gyp has historically needed build tools that Alpine
//   does not ship, and musl-resolv has DNS bugs.
//   if you use Alpine, test thoroughly with a real workload. The size
//   saving is real; so is the risk.

// THE LAYERING ORDER, which is the whole trick
//   1. base image          rarely changes
//   2. system packages     rarely changes
//   3. dependency manifests COPY package.json, COPY requirements.txt
//      THIS IS THE KEY LINE. the manifest changes less often than the
//      code, so the dependency layer is cached and does not re-upload.
//   4. dependency install
//   5. your source         changes every commit
//   6. build output

// THE FULL EXAMPLE, with the details that matter
   FROM node:20-slim AS build
   WORKDIR /app

   # dependencies first, and only the manifests
   COPY package.json package-lock.json ./
   RUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.npm \
       npm ci --omit=dev

   # then the source
   COPY . .

   # build, then drop dev dependencies
   RUN npm run build &amp;&amp; npm prune --omit=dev

   FROM node:20-slim AS runtime
   WORKDIR /app
   ENV NODE_ENV=production

   # run as a non-root user
   USER node

   # copy only what is needed, from the build stage
   COPY --from=build --chown=node:node /app/node_modules ./node_modules
   COPY --from=build --chown=node:node /app/dist ./dist

   # no curl, no wget. use the runtime's own healthcheck
   EXPOSE 3000
   CMD ["node", "dist/main.js"]

// THE .DOCKERIGNORE, which nobody writes and everybody needs
   node_modules
   .git
   dist
   .env
   .env.*
   *.log
   coverage
   .vscode
   .idea
   Dockerfile
   docker-compose.yml
   tests
//   without it, `COPY . .` copies your .git directory, your local
//   node_modules with platform-specific binaries, and any .env you
//   have lying around. All three have been in a published image.

// THE CACHE MOUNTS, which are the modern addition
   RUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.npm npm ci
//   the npm cache lives outside the layer, so it is fast on rebuilds
//   and it does not end up in the image. Requires BuildKit, which is
//   the default in modern Docker and in every cloud build service.
//   It also means the install is NOT fully reproducible unless you
//   use npm ci with a lockfile, which the first line already does.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch7-dockerfile-lint">Lint the Dockerfile, mechanically</h4>
    <p>Haskell Dockerfile Linter (hadolint) encodes most of the received wisdom in this guide as a rule set: <code>FROM</code> without a tag, <code>apt-get update</code> without cleanup, running as root, a bare <code>latest</code>, or an unpinned base all produce findings. Wired into CI it turns review comments into a failing build. There will still be judgment calls, but they start from a clean lint.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ hadolint Dockerfile
DL3006: Always tag the version of an image explicitly
DL3008: Pin versions in apt-get install
DL3059: Multiple consecutive RUN instructions
$ hadolint --ignore DL3008 Dockerfile   # documented exceptions are fine</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-dockerfile-secrets">Secrets stay out of layers with BuildKit</h4>
    <p>A <code>COPY</code> or <code>ENV</code> of a token bakes it into a layer, where it survives in the history and on any registry that received the image. BuildKit's secret and SSH mounts provide the value to the build without ever being written to a layer â€” the mount vanishes after the RUN finishes. <code>ARG</code> is the opposite: it is part of the build state and IS stored.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># build requiring a private registry credential
RUN --mount=type=secret,id=npmrc,target=/root/.npmrc \
    npm ci --omit=dev
$ docker build --secret id=npmrc,src=.npmrc .
# even after the build, no layer contains the secret:
$ docker image history app:1.0        # RUN shows "--mount=type=secret"</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-dockerfile-targets">Targets, and why one file serves all </h4>
    <p>A multi-stage file is a build graph, and <code>--target</code> stops the build at a named stage â€” so test images, dev images and the production image are all declared in one Dockerfile without duplicating the base setup. The production target can also pin its base by digest while the dev build floats, which reconciles reproducibility with convenience.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>FROM node:20-slim AS base
WORKDIR /app
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm ci
FROM base AS dev
RUN npm i -g nodemon
CMD ["nodemon", "src/main.js"]
FROM base AS prod
COPY . .
RUN npm run build
USER node
CMD ["node", "dist/main.js"]
# $ docker build --target prod -t app:1.0 .   and  --target dev for local</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch7-tagging" data-toc>Tagging, and the :latest trap</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// A TAG IS A MUTABLE NAME
$ docker tag myapp:1.4.2 registry.example.com/team/myapp:1.4.2
$ docker push registry.example.com/team/myapp:1.4.2
//   this is immutable in practice: nobody will let you push 1.4.2 again

$ docker tag myapp:1.4.2 registry.example.com/team/myapp:latest
$ docker push registry.example.com/team/myapp:latest
//   this is MUTABLE. Pushing a new version moves it. Every deployment
//   that says `image: latest` will get a different image tomorrow.

// THE RULES

// 1. NEVER USE :latest IN PRODUCTION
//   it is not "the newest". It is "whatever was last pushed to that
//   name", which may be a broken build, and a rollback through :latest
//   is impossible because the name now points at the new thing.
//   Kubernetes: imagePullPolicy: Always with :latest re-pulls on every
//   restart, so your pods are not all running the same image.
//   with a versioned tag, you can roll back by changing one string.

// 2. PREFER THE DIGEST for absolute determinism
$ docker pull registry.example.com/team/myapp@sha256:9f2a1b3c...
//   now nothing can change under you. This is what you want for a
//   production deployment you cannot easily change.
//   the cost: the digest is opaque. Use it in the deployment, and
//   record the human tag in your release notes.

// 3. SEMANTIC TAGS, and the pre-release problem
   1.4.2        a specific release
   1.4          floats to the newest 1.4.x
   1            floats to the newest 1.x
   main         a branch, pushed by CI. NEVER in production.
   sha-abc1234  a commit, the most reproducible mutable tag
//   pre-releases work too, and this is the correct pattern:
//     1.5.0-rc.1   only pulled if you ask for it
//     1.5.0         floats within 1.5, so 1.5.1 does NOT auto-apply.
//   The difference from npm matters: `1.5` in npm means ^1.5.0, which
//   DOES float across minors. In Docker it does not. Be explicit.

// 4. ONE IMAGE, ONE TAG. Do not build a new image for a different
//    environment that happens to have the same version number.
   myapp:1.4.2           the artefact
   myapp:1.4.2-staging    the same artefact, deployed elsewhere
//   If staging and production need different configuration, that is an
//   environment variable, not a different build.

// 5. SCRAP THE BUILD ID
   myapp:1.4.2-build-8471   ugly, and it means a cache miss on the
//   previous build's layers unless nothing changed. The content hash
//   already guarantees identity. Keep the tag human-readable.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch7-tagging-scheme">A tag scheme that leaves a trail</h4>
    <p>The correct set is: one immutable version tag per build plus one reproducible label. A Git SHA tag is the most reproducible mutable tag, because the same commit always rebuilds to it; the version tag is the one humans read. Keep them separate â€” a branch tag (<code>main</code>) in production is a rollback you cannot make because the name is occupied by the new build.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># tag set pushed by CI for a release:
ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2          # human
ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:sha-1a2b3c4    # precise
ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:latest         # convenience for local dev only
# and the immutable reference for deployment:
ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp@sha256:9f2a...</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-tagging-digest">Pinning by digest in a deployment</h4>
    <p>Kubernetes resolves <code>image: name:tag</code> at pod creation, so a moved tag silently changes what the next replica runs. Pinning the digest makes the wanted image equal to the desired image and gives you nothing to drift. The cost is opaqueness, which GitOps solves by recording the human tag in the commit message alongside the digest.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># deployment.yaml
image: ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp@sha256:9f2a1b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e2f3a4b5c
# with imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent the digest never re-pulls
# the GitOps commit that changed this field says: "myapp 1.4.2 -&gt; 1.4.3"</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-tagging-updates">Automate the version bumps</h4>
    <p>Dependabot and Renovate watch your tag strings and open pull requests when a new version exists â€” the same mechanism that updates library dependencies applies to image tags. The value is that <code>latest</code>-style drift stops being a decision and becomes a reviewable diff. The discipline is that the PR must be deployable: a tag bump whose digest is not verified is the same mutable-latest problem wearing different clothes.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># renovate.json
{
  "packageRules": [
    {
      "matchDatasources": ["docker"],
      "matchPackageNames": ["ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp"],
      "pinDigests": true,
      "automerge": false
    }
  ]
}</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch7-registry" data-toc>Choosing a registry</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE OPTIONS, and the real trade-off is not price
   Docker Hub            the default. huge, and the most-attacked.
                         free accounts have pull rate limits that
                         will bite a CI system.
   GitHub Container Registry   the sensible default if you are on GitHub.
                         packages inherit repo permissions, which is
                         genuinely excellent. Anonymous pulls are
                         disabled by default, which is correct.
   GitLab Registry       the same, for GitLab.
   Google Artifact Registry  excellent if you run on GCP. Native
                         integration with Cloud Run and GKE, and
                         vulnerability scanning built in.
   AWS ECR               the same for AWS. Cross-account pull policies
                         and IAM, which is what you want for a
                         multi-account setup.
   Azure ACR             the same for Azure.
   Quay                   Red Hat's. Free for public, and it is the
                         default registry for OpenShift.
   GitLab, Harbor         Harbor is a self-hosted, CNCF project with
                         scanning, signing and replication.
   A private registry      Gravitas, Artifactory, or a hosted
                         equivalent.

// HOW TO CHOOSE
//   1. If your code is on GitHub, GHCR. Permission inheritance alone
//      removes a whole class of credential management.
//   2. If you run on a cloud, that cloud's registry. Private link,
//      no egress cost, native IAM.
//   3. If you have a compliance requirement, check what you can
//      attest to. ACR, GAR and ECR all have certification stories;
//      Docker Hub does not.
//   4. Not Docker Hub, if you are publishing publicly, because of the
//      pull rate limits and the incident history.

// THE PULL RATE LIMIT, which will break your CI
   Docker Hub anonymous pulls: 100 per 6 hours per IP
   authenticated: 200 per 6 hours per user
   a busy CI with a shared runner IP hits this. Symptoms:
//     "toomanyrequests: You have reached your pull rate limit"
//   FIXES
   - log in on the runners, which raises the limit 100x
   - use a mirror or a cache registry in front
   - use GHCR or ACR for your base images
   - cache base images on the runner so a pull is not needed at all

// THE AUTH MODELS
   docker login with a PAT       works everywhere. A long-lived
                                 credential, so it is a ch3-oidc target.
   short-lived tokens            ECR: aws ecr get-login-password
   workload identity             GKE with Workload Identity pulls from
                                 GAR with NO stored credential at all.
                                 This is the correct end state.
   credential helpers            keep tokens in the OS keychain rather
                                 than ~/.docker/config.json
$ cat ~/.docker/config.json
   { "credsStore": "secretservice" }   Linux
   { "credHelpers": { "public.ecr.aws": "ecr-login" } }</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch7-registry-ghcr">GHCR, when your code is on GitHub</h4>
    <p>Packages on ghcr.io inherit repository visibility and permissions, so the person who can push to the repo can push the image and someone without access gets nothing â€” credential management collapses into the token you already rotate. Public packages are pullable anonymously; private ones need a token, which is why a CI that pulls a private image must log in first.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ echo "${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}" | docker login ghcr.io \
    -u "${{ github.actor }}" --password-stdin
$ docker push ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2
# a consumer pulling the private image:
$ echo "$GHCR_TOKEN" | docker login ghcr.io -u read-only-bot --password-stdin
$ docker pull ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-registry-mirror">A mirror registry that absorbs rate limits</h4>
    <p>Standing up a pull-through cache in front of Docker Hub (or any upstream) turns N pulls into 1. The registry's cache stores the upstream manifests and blobs, so the CI farm pulls from the cache; the upstream is touched once per unique image. This is the answer before arguing with a rate-limit email.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># docker-compose.yml
services:
  mirror:
    image: registry:2
    environment:
      REGISTRY_PROXY_REMOTEURL: https://registry-1.docker.io
    ports: ["5000:5000"]
    volumes: ["mirror-data:/var/lib/registry"]
# runners then use:
#   REGISTRY_MIRROR=http://mirror:5000  (or full proxy URLs)</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-registry-policy">Public image policy, written down</h4>
    <p>The registry decision is a policy question: which namespaces are public, who may push, what is replicated where. A short written policy beats a decade of folklore. The practical minimum is: production images pushed only from CI with a short-lived token, bases pinned by digest, and a decision on where anonymous pullers may land (somewhere rate-limited, never your only mirror).</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># a one-page policy, in YAML if you want it checked by CI:
public_namespaces:
  - ghcr.io/yourorg/public
push_restrictions:
  - only_ci_tokens
  - require_oidc
base_images:
  - pinned_by_digest
backup:
  - replicate_prod_images_to_secondary_region</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch7-push" data-toc>Building and pushing properly</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// A CI BUILD THAT PRODUCES A GOOD ARTEFACT
   - uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v3
   - uses: docker/login-action@v3
     with:
       registry: ghcr.io
       username: ${{ github.actor }}
       password: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
//   the GITHUB_TOKEN is short-lived, scoped to this repository, and
//   needs no stored secret. This is ch3-oidc, applied to containers.

   - uses: docker/build-push-action@v6
     with:
       context: .
       push: true
       tags: |
         ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}:${{ github.sha }}
         ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}:1.4.2
         ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}:latest
       provenance: true
       sbom: true
       cache-from: type=gha
       cache-to: type=gha,mode=max
//   note provenance: true and sbom: true. They add small attestations
//   to the manifest, and they are the container equivalent of the
//   signing chapter. Turn them on.

// THE ORDER, and why it matters
   1. login          fail fast if auth is broken
   2. build          with a cache, so it is fast
   3. SCAN           before pushing, not after
   4. push
//   scanning after a push means you have already published something
//   with a known-critical vulnerability.

// THE LOCAL EQUIVALENT
$ docker buildx build \
    --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 \
    --tag myapp:1.4.2 \
    --tag myapp:latest \
    --provenance --sbom \
    --push .
$ docker scout cves myapp:1.4.2     # if you use Docker Scout
$ trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL myapp:1.4.2
$ cosign attest --sbom sbom.spdx.json --type spdxjson myapp@sha256:...</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch7-push-order">Scan before push, every time</h4>
    <p>The step order is the whole point: login, build with cache, scan, and only then push. Pushing first means a known-critical vulnerability is already published by the time the report lands â€” and with tag movement, existing deployments may already have pulled it. A scanning step that cannot fail the build is decoration; wire <code>--exit-code</code> into the CI step.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># build-push-action with a hard gate before push
- uses: docker/build-push-action@v6
  id: build
  with:
    push: false
    tags: ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2
- run: trivy image --exit-code 1 --severity HIGH,CRITICAL ${{ steps.build.outputs.imageid }}
- run: docker push ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-push-cache">The GitHub Actions layer cache</h4>
    <p><code>cache-from: type=gha</code> persists the layer cache in the Actions cache and restores it on the next run, so an unchanged dependency layer is not rebuilt or re-uploaded â€” the largest practical speedup for a container CI. <code>mode=max</code> also caches intermediate stages, which matters for multi-stage builds where the build stage would otherwise be rebuilt every time.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>- uses: docker/build-push-action@v6
  with:
    cache-from: type=gha
    cache-to: type=gha,mode=max
# first build: cold. second build with no source changes: seconds.
# a cache hit also skips the base-image pull entirely,
# which is a rate-limit mitigation as well as a speed win.</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-push-provenance">Provenance, as a build flag</h4>
    <p>The BuildKit provenance attestation records how the image was built â€” the source, the build command, the base digests â€” as an OCI attestation attached to the manifest. Combined with a signature it is a verifiable answer to "where did this image come from". Turning it on is one flag, and it is the cheapest supply-chain control in this chapter.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ docker buildx build --provenance=true --sbom=true \
    -t ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2 --push .
# later, anyone can read the attestation:
$ cosign download attestation ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2 | \
    jq '.payload | @base64d | fromjson | .predicate.buildDefinition'
# SLSA source: github.com/yourorg/repo @ commit 1a2b3c...</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch7-multarch" data-toc>Multi-architecture images</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// ONE TAG, SEVERAL PLATFORMS
$ docker buildx build \
    --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64/v8,linux/arm/v7 \
    -t ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2 \
    --push .
//   the pushed artefact is an INDEX: a manifest list pointing at three
//   platform-specific manifests. The registry resolves it at pull
//   time based on the requesting machine.

// HOW TO VERIFY, and the gotcha
$ docker buildx imagetools inspect ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2
//   lists every platform and its digest
$ docker run --rm --platform linux/arm64 ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2 uname -m
//   aarch64. this is the test that proves it works, because a
//   manifest list can list a platform whose image is broken.

// THE DIGEST GOTCHA
//   digest of the INDEX:      sha256:aaa...
//   digest of an arm64 leaf:  sha256:bbb...
//   pinning "image@sha256:bbb" works ONLY on arm64. On amd64 the
//   registry will refuse it.
//   if you want platform-independent pinning, pin the index digest
//   and accept that you have not verified the leaf matches.
//   most people use a tag plus a lock file, which is simpler and
//   adequate.

// QEMU, for building without the hardware
$ docker run --privileged --rm tonistiigi/binfmt --install arm64
$ docker buildx create --use --name multi
//   emulates the target architecture. SLOW, roughly 5-10x, and it
//   does not catch platform-specific code paths. Use real runners
//   with native hardware for anything that matters.

// THE ARM64 PROBLEM, specifically
//   x86-only dependencies are the common failure. If a base image has
//   no arm64 variant, your multi-arch build silently produces an
//   index with one platform, and you find out from a user.
//   check the base image supports every platform you list:
//     $ docker buildx imagetools inspect node:20-slim</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch7-multarch-verify">Verify every platform you promised</h4>
    <p>A manifest list can reference a platform whose leaf image is broken, so a build that "succeeded" is not proof of a working multi-arch image. The verification has two halves: inspect the index to confirm the platforms exist, then actually run the image on each platform. CI runners for each architecture are the honest way to do the second half.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ docker buildx imagetools inspect ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2
Image: ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2
MediaType: application/vnd.oci.image.index.v1+json
Manifests:
  linux/amd64   sha256:aaa...
  linux/arm64   sha256:bbb...
  linux/arm/v7  sha256:ccc...
$ docker run --rm --platform linux/arm64 \
    ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2 uname -m
aarch64</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-multarch-base">The base image decides what is possible</h4>
    <p>The pain point is never your build â€” it is the base. If <code>FROM</code> references an image with no arm64 variant, buildx silently emits an index with one platform. Check the base image's platform list before designing the matrix, and pick upstream images that publish the architectures you need, or the matrix you promise is fiction.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ docker buildx imagetools inspect node:20-slim
Manifests:
  linux/amd64   sha256:...
  linux/arm64   sha256:...
  linux/arm/v7  sha256:...
$ docker buildx imagetools inspect node:20-slim \
    --format '{{- range .Manifests}} {{.Platform.OS}}/{{.Platform.Architecture}} {{end}}'
 linux/amd64 linux/arm64 linux/arm/v7</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-multarch-native">Native runners beat emulation</h4>
    <p>QEMU emulation builds every architecture on one host, but it is 5â€“10Ã— slower and can compile code differently than a native compiler would â€” a build that passes under emulation can still fail on real hardware. Use emulation for a smoke build of rarely-used platforms, and native GitHub-hosted runners (amd64, arm64) or self-hosted fleet for what actually ships.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># GitHub Actions: a native arm64 runner via a matrix
runs-on: ${{ matrix.runner }}
matrix:
  runner: [ubuntu-latest, ubuntu-24.04-arm]
# or emulation for a quick check, clearly labelled:
$ docker run --privileged --rm tonistiigi/binfmt --install arm64
$ docker buildx build --platform linux/arm64 -t check:arm64 .</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch7-scan" data-toc>Scanning, and what SBOMs are for</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// SCANNING FINDS KNOWN CVEs. It does not find MALWARE.
   trivy image myapp:1.4.2
   grype myapp:1.4.2
   docker scout cves myapp:1.4.2
   $ trivy image --exit-code 1 --severity HIGH,CRITICAL myapp:1.4.2
//   --exit-code 1 makes it fail the build. Without it, a scan is a
//   report nobody reads.

// THE THINGS A SCANNER CANNOT DO
//   - it matches versions against a CVE database. A dependency with
//     no published advisory is invisible, whether it is safe or not.
//   - it does not evaluate your code. A deliberate backdoor is
//     invisible to it.
//   - a base image from 18 months ago has hundreds of "vulnerabilities"
//     that were fixed in the OS but are flagged anyway, because
//     scanners do not model the distro's patch state well.
//   - a scanner on the final image is too late for a multi-stage
//     build: the vulnerable compiler is in the build stage, not the
//     final image.

// THE BETTER PLACE TO SCAN
   scan the BASE images separately, in a scheduled job, and pin to
   versions that pass. Then the final-image scan is a fast
   regression check rather than a source of permanent noise.
   # renovate or dependabot for base image updates
   # a scheduled job that rebuilds on a new base and reports

// THE SBOM, which is a different and better artefact
   $ syft yourapp:1.4.2 -o spdx-json > sbom.spdx.json
   $ trivy image --format cyclonedx -o cyclonedx.json yourapp:1.4.2
//   an SBOM is an INVENTORY: every package, every version, every
//   licence, in the image. Not a judgement.
//
//   WHY IT IS WORTH ATTACHING
//   - when the next Log4Shell happens, you can answer "are we
//     affected?" in minutes by grepping the SBOM, instead of scanning
//       every deployment
//   - licence compliance becomes mechanical
//   - it is a regulatory requirement in many jurisdictions now
//   - attach it to the image so it cannot be separated from it:
       --sbom true   in buildx, which attaches it as an OCI referrer
//   then:
       $ cosign download sbom ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp@sha256:...
//   the inventory travels with the artefact. That is the point: the
//   scanner that found the vulnerability will be new, and it will need
//   this file.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch7-scan-ci">A scan that can fail the release</h4>
    <p>The scanner's value is a gate, not a report: exit on the severity you will not ship. Triage then follows a fixed loop â€” suppress with a reason, upgrade, or waive with a ticket. The gate has to sit <em>before</em> the push or it gates nothing.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ trivy image --exit-code 1 --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --ignore-unfixed \
    ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2
# ignored findings are tracked explicitly, not silently:
#   .trivyignore
#   CVE-2026-0001
#   CVE-2026-0002   (no fix available upstream; ticket SEC-114)
$ grype --fail-on high ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-scan-sbom">Generate the SBOM at build time, not audit time</h4>
    <p>An SBOM produced right after the build reflects what actually shipped; one produced from a deployed image later reflects whatever a scanner could still see, which may differ after layers were squashed or packages removed. Build-time generation also lets you attach it to the image as an OCI referrer, so the inventory cannot be separated from the artefact it describes.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ docker buildx build --sbom=true \
    -t ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2 --push .
# or generate explicitly:
$ syft ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2 -o spdx-json &gt; sbom.spdx.json
# attach it so it travels with the image:
$ cosign attest --predicate sbom.spdx.json --type spdxjson \
    ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp@sha256:9f2a...</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-scan-baseline">Scan the base where the noise lives</h4>
    <p>Most findings come from the base image's distro packages, and most of those are already fixed upstream or irrelevant to the runtime. Scanning the base separately â€” on a schedule, with a pinned version that passes â€” moves that noise out of the release gate. The final-image scan then becomes a regression check for the small delta you own, which is a gate you can actually hold.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># a scheduled job pins a known-good base
# cron: 0 3 * * 1  rebuild base-check image, scan, alert on HIGH+
$ trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL node:20-slim @sha256:abc...
# on a new base version: scan first, then bump the pin in renovate PR
# the release pipeline then scans only the diff from the pinned base</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch7-signing" data-toc>Signing container images</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// COSIGN, and it is the tool to use
$ cosign generate-key-pair
//   produces cosign.key and cosign.pub. The PRIVATE key must not be
//   in your repository and should be in an HSM or a KMS in production.

$ cosign sign --yes ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp@sha256:9f2a1b3c...
$ cosign verify \
    --certificate-identity https://github.com/yourorg/repo/.github/workflows/release.yml \
    --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
    ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp@sha256:9f2a1b3c...
//   KEYLESS, which is the mode to prefer: no key to manage, and the
//   identity comes from the CI provider's OIDC token. The two
//   --certificate flags are what make this specific rather than
//   "signed by anybody", and they are the flags people leave out.

// WHAT GETS SIGNED
$ cosign sign ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2
//   this signs the TAG, which means the tag can be moved and the
//   signature becomes misleading. ALWAYS sign by digest.
$ cosign sign ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp@sha256:9f2a1b3c...

// ADMISSION CONTROL, which is what makes a signature mean something
// a signature nobody checks is decoration. Two places to enforce:
//   the registry refuses to pull unsigned images
     Kubernetes: kyverno, or the Gatekeeper policies below
   the orchestrator verifies on pull
//   Kyverno, the practical version:
     apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
     kind: ClusterPolicy
     metadata: { name: verify-signature }
     spec:
       validationFailureAction: Enforce
       rules:
         - name: verify
           match:
             any:
             - resources: { kinds: [Pod] }
           verifyImages:
             - imageReferences: ["ghcr.io/yourorg/*"]
               attestors:
                 - entries:
                     - keyless:
                         subject: "*"
                         issuer: "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
                         identity: "https://github.com/yourorg/repo/.github/workflows/release.yml"
//   this refuses to run a pod from an unsigned image. Now the
//   signature is load-bearing, and a compromised publish credential
//   is not enough.

// OTHER THINGS TO SIGN, in rough priority order
$ cosign sign IMAGE                  the image. do this one.
$ cosign attest --predicate FILE \
    --type slsaprovenance IMAGE     how it was built
$ cosign sign SBOM_FILE             the SBOM
$ cosign sign BINARY_FILE           a released binary, for air-gapped
                                    environments that verify locally
//   cosign can also sign arbitrary blobs, which is how you sign a
//   Helm chart, a Terraform module, or a package from any registry in
//   this guide.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch7-signing-keyless">Keyless signing, and what it proves</h4>
    <p>Keyless signing replaces a long-lived key with a short-lived certificate minted from the CI provider's OIDC token. What you verify is not "signed by an organisation" but "signed from this exact workflow on this exact repository at this time" â€” the two <code>--certificate</code> flags are what make it specific. A signature with a generic identity is close to no signature.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># in the release workflow, before push:
- uses: sigstore/cosign-installer@v3
- run: cosign sign --yes \
    --certificate-identity "https://github.com/yourorg/repo/.github/workflows/release.yml" \
    --certificate-oidc-issuer "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com" \
    ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp@sha256:9f2a...
# verify with the same two flags
$ cosign verify --certificate-identity "..." --certificate-oidc-issuer "..." \
    ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp@sha256:9f2a...</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-signing-enforce">Enforce, or the signature is decoration</h4>
    <p>A registry-side check is the better place to enforce, because it runs before anything runs the image. On Kubernetes the practical tool is Kyverno's <code>verifyImages</code> with keyless attestors; the policy in the chapter refuses pods whose image lacks a signature from your release workflow. That turns a compromised publish credential from a full compromise into a failed build.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># kyverno: verify the image digest AND the identity
verifyImages:
  - imageReferences: ["ghcr.io/yourorg/*"]
    attestors:
      - entries:
          - keyless:
              issuer: "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
              subject: "https://github.com/yourorg/repo/.github/workflows/release.yml"
# and do not forget to verify the digest:
  - imageReferences: ["ghcr.io/yourorg/*"]
    digest: required      # tags are not an identity</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-signing-what">Sign the digest, then the artefacts around it</h4>
    <p>Signatures bind to the bytes they are over. Sign the digest, not the tag; then sign the SBOM and the provenance attestation with the same identity so the whole release is one verifiable set. For air-gapped environments that cannot reach the OIDC issuer, cosign's certificate mode signs with a traditional key and verification happens locally.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ cosign sign ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp@sha256:9f2a...          # the image
$ cosign attest --predicate slsa.json --type slsaprovenance \
    ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp@sha256:9f2a...                   # how it was built
$ cosign sign --blob-uploader oci \
    sbom.spdx.json ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp           # the SBOM travels too
# air-gapped offline verification:
$ cosign verify-blob --cert my-release.crt --signature my-release.sig sbom.spdx.json</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch7-immutable" data-toc>Immutability, digests and reproducibility</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE PROPERTY YOU WANT
//   the same source, built twice, produces the same digest.
//   If it does, the digest IS the build, and a signature over the
//   digest is a statement about the code rather than about a
//   particular build machine.

// THE FOUR THINGS THAT BREAK IT
   1. TIMESTAMPS. A layer's tar entries carry mtimes. Docker's
//      BuildKit sets them from the build time by default.
      # BuildKit normalises layer timestamps when the source is a
      # git context with a known commit time
      docker buildx build --provenance=true ...
   2. FILE ORDER inside a layer. Tar entries are in the order the
//      build wrote them, which is usually stable but not guaranteed.
   3. BUILD ARTEFACTS with non-determinism. A compiled binary with
//      an embedded timestamp, a random build ID, or a path.
   4. BASE IMAGE DRIFT. `FROM node:20-slim` is a moving target.
      The single highest-value change:
      FROM node:20.11.1-slim@sha256:abc123...
//      both the tag AND the digest. The tag for readability, the
//      digest for reproducibility. Rebuild weekly to pick up patches.

// DETERMINISTIC BUILDS, which is achievable
$ docker buildx build --output type=image,rewrite-timestamp=true \
    --no-cache -t myapp:repro .
//   rewrite-timestamp sets every layer's mtime to 0
$ docker buildx imagetools inspect myapp:repro --format '{{.Manifest.Digest}}'
   sha256:aaa...
//   build again, from a clean cache, and compare. If the digests
//   match, the build is reproducible. Most projects that try this
//   succeed within a day, and the failures are always timestamps or
//   an unpinned base image.

// WHY IT MATTERS, in order of importance
   1. a signature over a reproducible digest is a statement about the
//      source, not about one CI run
   2. a rebuild after a base image CVE lets you prove the result is
//      the same code
//   3. it catches accidental non-determinism, which is a source of
//      "it built yesterday" support tickets
//   4. a supply-chain reviewer can rebuild and compare, which is the
//      strongest possible form of verification</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch7-immutable-repro">Make the build deterministic on purpose</h4>
    <p>Reproducibility is a property you build, not one you inherit: the source must be identical, the base pinned by digest, timestamps normalised, and the toolchain fixed. BuildKit's <code>rewrite-timestamp</code> handles the tar timestamps; pinning the toolchain means a JS or Go build does not depend on the latest <code>node</code> or <code>go</code> being installed in the base. Keep a clean-cache build in CI to prove it stays true.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ docker buildx build --output type=image,rewrite-timestamp=true \
    --no-cache -t myapp:repro .
$ docker buildx build --output type=image,rewrite-timestamp=true \
    --no-cache -t myapp:repro2 .
$ diff &lt;(docker buildx imagetools inspect myapp:repro --format '{{.Manifest.Digest}}') \
      &lt;(docker buildx imagetools inspect myapp:repro2 --format '{{.Manifest.Digest}}')
# identical output means the digest IS the build</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-immutable-base">Pin the base by digest, update it on a schedule</h4>
    <p>A floating <code>FROM node:20-slim</code> makes today's and last month's builds different artefacts with the same tag. The working pattern is digest pinning plus automation: Renovate or Dependabot opens a PR when the base changes, CI rebuilds, scans, and ships. You get security patches and you keep the property that a tag refers to one known build.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># before: float
FROM node:20-slim
# after: pin, and let the bot move the pin
FROM node:20.11.1-slim@sha256:abc123...
# renovate opens "chore(deps): update node:20-slim digest to def456..."
# the PR rebuilds, scans, and only merges when the gate passes</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-immutable-prove">Prove the claim to a reviewer</h4>
    <p>The point of reproducibility is that someone else can check it. Publishing the command that produced the image, the provenance attestation, and the expected digest lets a reviewer rebuild and compare. The shortest proof is the build recipe plus a CI log; the strongest is an attestation they can verify with cosign, which is what the SLSA supply-chain levels describe.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># RELEASE.md, committed next to the workflow
Image:    ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp
Digest:   sha256:9f2a...
Built by: .github/workflows/release.yml @ 1a2b3c
Command:  docker buildx build --provenance=true --sbom=true --push .
Verify:   cosign verify --certificate-identity "...release.yml" ...
# any reviewer replaying the workflow on the same commit
# must end at the same digest.</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch7-rate" data-toc>Rate limits and the practical workarounds</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE LIMITS, and they are the most common container CI failure
   Docker Hub   100 anonymous pulls / 6h / IP
                200 authenticated / 6h / user
   GHCR         generous for free, but rate limited on anonymous pulls
   ECR          500 pulls / 6h per user by default, raisable
   GCR/GAR      per the quota page; free tier is limited

// THE SYMPTOM, which is never clear
   toomanyrequests: You have reached your pull rate limit.
//   or, more confusingly, an unrelated-looking 500 from the registry
   or a timeout, because the registry is queueing you behind the limit

// THE FIXES, best first
// 1. DO NOT RE-PULL WHAT YOU HAVE. The right answer for a CI runner.
      $ docker images -q node:20-slim
      # if present, the build can use the local image
// 2. USE A BUILDX CACHE, which stores layers and skips the pull
      --cache-from type=gha
// 3. LOG IN. 100 to 200 per 6 hours is the anonymous limit; a logged
//    in token is far higher.
// 4. USE A MIRROR REGISTRY in front of Docker Hub for your base images
// 5. PIN YOUR BASE IMAGE DIGESTS, so a pull is a cache hit
// 6. RAISE THE LIMIT. Docker Hub: request it. ECR: a one-line call.
//    a paid plan also raises it substantially.

// THE PROBLEM WITH PINNING DIGESTS
//   a digest pin means a base image security patch requires a rebuild.
//   That is correct and also annoying, so automate it:
//     renovate, or dependabot, opens a PR with the new digest
//     the rebuild is triggered by the PR
//     you get patches, and you keep reproducibility
//   this two-piece setup (automated digest updates plus reproducible
//   builds) is the mature answer and it is worth the setup cost.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch7-rate-ci">The CI-side fix: cache the layers</h4>
    <p>A runner that already has the layers does not pull. The cheapest workaround is therefore not a larger quota but a layer cache: buildx with a cache backend, or a registry mirror on the network path, turns the hundred-plus pulls of a busy farm into a handful. Combined with a pinned base, the pull is a cache hit on every run until the pin moves.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># keep a warm local cache on the runner
$ docker images -q node:20-slim          # present = a pull is avoided
# buildx cache between runs (GitHub Actions or a shared registry):
cache-from: type=gha
cache-to: type=gha,mode=max
# or an explicit mirror the runners point at (DOCKER_OPTS):
--registry-mirror=http://mirror.internal:5000</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-rate-auth">Authenticate, so the limit stops being anonymous</h4>
    <p>The anonymous limit is the one that bites shared CI IPs; the authenticated limit is a multiple of it, and cloud login tokens are raised further. A tiny <code>docker login</code> at the top of the pipeline is the highest-leverage rate-limit fix that exists â€” do it before diagnosing anything else.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ echo "$DOCKERHUB_TOKEN" | docker login -u "$DOCKERHUB_USER" --password-stdin
# GitHub Actions with an official action:
- uses: docker/login-action@v3
  with:
    username: ${{ secrets.DOCKERHUB_USERNAME }}
    password: ${{ secrets.DOCKERHUB_TOKEN }}
# ECR short-lived token:
$ aws ecr get-login-password | docker login --username AWS \
    --password-stdin "$AWS_ACCOUNT.dkr.ecr.$REGION.amazonaws.com"</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch7-rate-fleet">Small fleets, shared IPs, quiet hours</h4>
    <p>Three small-footprint practices add up: schedule non-urgent builds off peak, use a content-addressed mirror for the handful of base images a fleet actually needs, and pin versioned tags for infrastructure containers so their pulls are cache hits rather than fresh downloads. None of these are heroic; together they remove rate limits from the failure list.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># a fleet manifest of the images you actually run:
#   these are mirrored / cached / pinned, so no anonymous pull happens
images:
  - node:20-slim@sha256:abc...
  - ghcr.io/yourorg/myapp:1.4.2
  - postgres:16.3-alpine@sha256:def...
# off-peak schedule for the report jobs:
# cron: '0 4 * * *'     (02:00-06:00 is the quiet window on Hub)</code></pre></div>

    <div class="ex"><h4>Exercises 7</h4><ol>
      <li>Take an existing Dockerfile and measure every layer with <code>docker image history --no-trunc</code>. Convert it to multi-stage and measure again. Record the reduction and identify which layer was responsible.</li>
      <li>Pin your base image by digest, add a <code>.dockerignore</code>, and verify no <code>.git</code>, no <code>node_modules</code> and no <code>.env</code> is in the image. Check with <code>docker run --rm img ls -a /app</code>.</li>
      <li>Build a multi-architecture image with buildx, verify each platform with <code>--platform</code> and <code>uname -m</code>, and confirm the base image actually supports every platform you listed.</li>
      <li>Sign an image with cosign in keyless mode, then add a Kyverno policy that enforces the signature, and prove it rejects an unsigned image and accepts a signed one.</li>
      <li>Get a reproducible build: pin the base digest, use <code>--output type=image,rewrite-timestamp=true</code>, and build twice from a clean cache. Record the digests and chase every mismatch to a cause.</li>
    </ol></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
<h2 id="ch8" data-toc>8 Â· Extension and Tool Marketplaces</h2>
    <div class="ch-toc"><button type="button" class="ch-toc-head" aria-expanded="true"><span class="arrow">â–¸</span>Contents &middot; 8 Â· Extension and Tool Marketplaces</button><div class="ch-toc-list"><a href="#ch8-vscode">The VS Code Marketplace</a><a href="#ch8-vscode-publish">Publishing a VS Code extension</a><a href="#ch8-webpack">The webpack and target problems</a><a href="#ch8-chrome">The Chrome Web Store</a><a href="#ch8-chrome-publish">Publishing a Chrome extension</a><a href="#ch8-jetbrains">JetBrains Marketplace</a><a href="#ch8-figma">Figma Community</a><a href="#ch8-homebrew">Homebrew, Scoop, Chocolatey and apt</a><a href="#ch8-other">Shopify, Slack, Atlassian and Home Assistant</a><a href="#ch8-listing">Writing a listing that gets found</a><a href="#ch8-triage">Review responses and triage</a></div></div>
    <p class="topic-desc">Extension marketplaces are different from package registries in one specific way that changes everything: a human reads and approves your submission, and that human can reject you. The technical packaging is often the easy part.</p>
    <h3 id="ch8-vscode" data-toc>The VS Code Marketplace</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WHAT AN EXTENSION IS
//   a directory with a package.json, an extension code, and optionally
//   a bundled webpack output. It runs inside VS Code with Node.js, and
//   it has access to the extension API, the filesystem, and the network.
//   Treat it as application code with a very large installed base.

// THE MANIFEST, and the fields that matter for review
{
  "name": "my-extension",
  "displayName": "My Extension",
  "description": "One sentence, under 200 characters. It appears in search.",
  "version": "1.4.2",
  "publisher": "yourorg",             // becomes yourorg.my-extension
  "engines": { "vscode": "^1.85.0" },
  "categories": ["Other"],
  "main": "./dist/extension.js",
  "activationEvents": ["onLanguage:typescript"],
  "contributes": {
    "commands": [{ "command": "myext.doThing", "title": "Do the Thing" }],
    "configuration": {
      "title": "My Extension",
      "properties": {
        "myext.apiKey": {
          "type": "string",
          "default": "",
          "description": "API key. Stored in your settings, which are synced."
        }
      }
    }
  },
  "repository": { "type": "git", "url": "https://github.com/yourorg/myext" },
  "bugs": { "url": "https://github.com/yourorg/myext/issues" },
  "homepage": "https://example.com/myext",
  "license": "MIT",
  "icon": "icon.png",
  "galleryBanner": {
    "color": "#0f172a",
    "theme": "dark"
  }
}

// THE REVIEW RULES THAT ARE NOT OBVIOUS
//   - the icon must be at least 128x128, and it must not be a
//     screenshot of a person or a product logo
//   - the description must not contain markdown, marketing language, or
//     a competitor's name
//   - you may not collect telemetry without a declared privacy policy
//     and a user-facing setting
//   - "telemetry" has a specific meaning to the marketplace: data
//     transmitted for the purpose of tracking usage. Crash reporting
//     is not telemetry if it is anonymous and documented.
//   - you may not require a paid licence to function, though you may
//     sell a licence for a subset
//   - a repository URL and an issue tracker are effectively required for
//     a listing that passes review
//   - activationEvents should be narrow. "onStartup" for an extension
//     that could be lazy is a legitimate performance complaint and
//     reviewers do raise it.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch8-vscode-manifest">What the manifest actually declares</h4>
    <p>The manifest is the contract with the marketplace and with the host. <code>publisher</code>, <code>name</code> and <code>version</code> form the unique id consumers see; <code>main</code> must point at a real file, or the extension installs and silently does nothing; <code>engines.vscode</code> declares which host versions you support. <code>contributes</code> is the whole of the user-visible surface â€” commands, settings, menus â€” and the marketplace previews a readable subset of it.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>{
  "name": "my-extension",
  "publisher": "yourorg",
  "version": "1.4.2",
  "engines": { "vscode": "^1.85.0" },
  "main": "./dist/extension.js",
  "activationEvents": ["onCommand:myext.doThing"],
  "contributes": {
    "commands": [{ "command": "myext.doThing", "title": "Do the Thing" }]
  }
}
# listing id after publish:
#   yourorg.my-extension-1.4.2.vsix  (publisher.name-version)</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-vscode-security">The review rules are about trust, not style</h4>
    <p>Telemetry, activation and the icon are where reviewers look for bad intent or sloppiness. Telemetry data must be declared in a privacy policy and gated by a user-facing setting; activation should be lazy (<code>onLanguage:â€¦</code>, <code>onCommand:â€¦</code>) rather than <code>onStartup</code> for an extension that does not run at startup; the icon must be at least 128Ã—128 and must not impersonate another product. These are not aesthetics â€” the marketplace treats them as safety properties.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// a telemetry disclosure that passes review:
"configuration": {
  "properties": {
    "myext.telemetry.enabled": {
      "type": "boolean", "default": false,
      "description": "Anonymous crash and usage reporting. See privacy.md."
    }
  }
}
// the code must default to off and read the setting:
const on = vscode.workspace.getConfiguration("myext").get("telemetry.enabled")
if (on) { telemetry.send(event) }</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-vscode-engines">Engines, and the compat floor you choose</h4>
    <p><code>engines.vscode</code> is both an install gate and a promise: VS Code refuses to install an extension whose engine range excludes the running version. A range that is too old forces you to test against old hosts forever; one that is too new excludes a large installed base. The honest policy is a stated floor you actually test ("last three majors"), with the manifest matching it.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// choose a floor, then match the manifest to your testing:
"engines": { "vscode": "^1.85.0" }
// check the newest API you use against that floor:
//   if you call a 1.90-only API, the floor must be >= 1.90
// CI runs the extension tests on the matrix too:
strategy:
  matrix:
    vscode: [1.85, 1.95]
- run: npx vscode-test@latest --version ${{ matrix.vscode }}</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch8-vscode-publish" data-toc>Publishing a VS Code extension</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE PAT token, and why not a password
$ az devops login   // or: create a PAT at
                    // dev.azure.com/orgs/YOUR-ORG/_settings/tokens
//   a Personal Access Token for the Marketplace, with the "Manage
//   extensions" scope, at the ALL ORGANIZATIONS level.
//   it is long-lived. That is the marketplace's design, and the only
//   mitigation available is to keep it in a CI secret and rotate it.

// the publish script
   "scripts": {
     "vscode:prepublish": "npm run package",
     "package": "webpack --mode production --devtool hidden-source-map",
     "vsce:package": "vsce package --no-dependencies",
     "publish": "vsce publish --pat $VSCE_PAT --no-dependencies"
   }

// packaging, and what vsce includes
$ npx vsce package
//   it takes a .vsix, which is a zip with a specific layout:
//     extension/package.json
//     extension/dist/extension.js
//     extension/README.md
//     extension/CHANGELOG.md
//     extension/LICENSE
//     [Content_Types].xml
//     extension.vsixmanifest
//   .vscodeignore controls what is IN it. Without one, everything
//   not gitignored is packaged, including your .git directory.

// publishing
$ npx vsce publish --pat $VSCE_PAT
//   or let the CI do it
   - uses: actions/checkout@v4
   - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
   - run: npm ci
   - run: npm run package
   - run: npx vsce publish --pat ${{ secrets.VSCE_PAT }} --no-dependencies
     env:
       VSCE_PAT: ${{ secrets.VSCE_PAT }}
//   --no-dependencies is important: it stops vsce bundling
//   node_modules, which would make the .vsix enormous. You are
//   responsible for a webpack build instead.

// the publisher must exist FIRST
//   the publisher name in package.json must already be registered on
//   the marketplace. You create the publisher in the Marketplace
//   portal by linking a Microsoft account or an Azure DevOps
//   organisation. Do this before your first publish or the publish
//   fails with an unhelpful error.

// updates
//   a new version of the extension on the marketplace is an UPDATE
//   that VS Code offers automatically. Unlike npm, there is no lock
//   file: users auto-update, and auto-updating extensions have been
//   the default since 1.63.
//   THIS IS A REAL RESPONSIBILITY. You cannot ship a broken patch and
//   expect users to stay on the previous version. They will be moved
//   onto it and your issue tracker will tell you about it.
//   the mitigation is to roll the version back in the marketplace, which
//   you can do by unpublishing or deprecating a version. It does not
//   reach users who already auto-updated, so treat it as damage
//   control rather than a fix.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch8-vscode-ignore">The .vscodeignore decides what ships</h4>
    <p><code>vsce package</code> includes everything that is not gitignored or vscodeignored, which means source, tests and <code>.git</code> go into the .vsix by default. The ignore file is smaller than you think it should be; the packaged contents are the true release, so the verification step is unzipping and looking. A 40 MB .vsix is almost always a missing ignore entry, not a big extension.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># .vscodeignore â€” the members that must not ship
.git/**
src/**
tests/**
node_modules/**
*.vsix
**/*.map
**/*.ts
tsconfig.json
webpack.config.js
# then verify the artefact:
$ npx vsce ls --tree
my-extension/package.json
my-extension/dist/extension.js
my-extension/README.md
my-extension/LICENSE</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-vscode-pat">The PAT, and how to live with it</h4>
    <p>The marketplace authenticates publishing with a Personal Access Token, scoped to Marketplace > Manage extensions, at the all-organisations level. It is long-lived by design, which is the security hole; the practice is to mint it for a dedicated service identity, store it only as an encrypted CI secret, and rotate it on a schedule or on any suspicion. There is no marketplace OIDC today, so rotation is the whole mitigation.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># a release-only workflow, token stored once in the repo secrets
- run: npx vsce publish --pat ${{ secrets.VSCE_PAT }} --no-dependencies
# rotate: old PAT dies in the portal, the new one lands in the secret,
# and the publish step is unchanged â€” which is why the step never
# references a personal environment variable.</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-vscode-update">Auto-update is a release discipline</h4>
    <p>VS Code auto-updates extensions by default (since 1.63), so there is no "stay on the last known good" for your users. The consequences are real: a broken patch reaches everyone, and a rollback via the marketplace does not reach anyone who auto-updated. Ship like a change you cannot recall: heavy local packaging tests, a staged rollout if the marketplace supports pre-release channels, and a changelog entry that names the exact risk.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># pre-release channel, if you want canaries:
"scripts": { "publish:pre": "vsce publish --pre-release --pat $VSCE_PAT" }
# a release checklist for the irreversibility:
#   1. npm run package &amp;&amp; install the .vsix into a clean profile
#   2. exercise the contributed commands
#   3. vsce ls --tree : confirm the file list
#   4. publish, then immediately reinstall and re-test from the marketplace</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch8-webpack" data-toc>The webpack and target problems</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE MOST COMMON PUBLISHING FAILURE, and it is not the marketplace's fault
   Error: The 'main' path in package.json does not exist
//   or the published extension is "installed" and does nothing

// webpack.config.js, the version that works
   const path = require('path');
   module.exports = {
     entry: './src/extension.ts',
     output: {
       path: path.resolve(__dirname, 'dist'),
       filename: 'extension.js',
       libraryTarget: 'commonjs2',      // VS Code loads it with require
       devtoolModuleFilenameTemplate: '../[resource-path]',
     },
     devtool: 'source-map',
     externals: {
       // the vscode module is provided by the host, never bundled
       vscode: 'commonjs vscode',
     },
     resolve: { extensions: ['.ts', '.js'] },
     module: { rules: [{ test: /\.ts$/, use: 'ts-loader', exclude: /node_modules/ }] },
   };

// WHY EACH LINE MATTERS
   libraryTarget: commonjs2      VS Code does require(extension.js). A
                                  webpack UMD or ESM output is not
                                  loadable and you get a silent no-op.
// externals: vscode               bundling the vscode stub module makes
//                                  the extension throw at activation.
// devtoolModuleFilenameTemplate  without it, stack traces reference
//                                  webpack:// paths that are useless
//                                  when a user reports a bug.

// THE TARGET PROBLEM: a 2022 PROBLEM THAT CAME BACK
   Error: Activating extension failed: Cannot read properties of undefined
//   VS Code 1.84+ runs extensions in a separate context. If your
//   webpack config emits ES2022+ syntax, it works locally in dev and
//   fails in the released version.
//   set the target explicitly to what the marketplace minimum allows:
     target: 'node16',          // or node18, matching engines.vscode
   or modern: false, esbuild: { target: 'es2020' },
//   and TEST IT by downloading your own .vsix and installing it in a
//   clean VS Code profile. `F5` debugging runs from source and will
//   not catch a packaging problem.

// THE WEB EXTENSION CASE
   "browser": "./dist/web/extension.js"
   "extensionKind": ["ui", "workspace"]
//   a web extension runs in a browser sandbox: no Node, no filesystem,
//   no native modules. It must be a bundle with no Node built-ins.
//   The web extension host is genuinely restrictive and the error
//   messages are poor. Build both and publish both if you can.

// DEBUGGING A PACKAGED EXTENSION
   1. F5 from source. Fix everything here first.
   2. npx vsce package, then install the .vsix in a clean profile:
      code --install-extension myext-1.4.2.vsix --force
   3. View > Output > My Extension for your own log channel
   4. Developer: Show Running Extensions for activation timing
   5. Developer: Inspect Running Extensions for a live debug console
   6. Never ship with --no-dependencies removed. If the extension
      needs a runtime dependency and you let vsce bundle it, the
      bundle is enormous and will be rejected or will break.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch8-webpack-loadability">Loadability is a packaging property</h4>
    <p>The most common shipped failure is an extension that installs and does nothing. Its cause is usually the bundle: VS Code <code>require()</code>s the file in <code>main</code>, so a webpack output that is not CommonJS is not loadable, and bundling the <code>vscode</code> module makes activation throw. Both symptoms appear only in the packaged artefact â€” <code>F5</code> runs from source and masks them, which is why a packaged-install test is not optional.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the two lines that make or break the bundle:
output: { filename: "extension.js", libraryTarget: "commonjs2" }
externals: { vscode: "commonjs vscode" }
// commonjs2  = module.exports of the file, what require() expects
// externals  = 'vscode' stays a runtime require, never bundled
// packaged install test that catches a silent no-op:
$ code --install-extension myext-1.4.2.vsix --force
$ code --command myext.doThing   # if nothing happens, check the log channel</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-webpack-target">The target is a compat promise</h4>
    <p>Since VS Code 1.84 extensions run in a separate host process whose Node version is lower than your machine's. A bundle emitting ES2022 syntax passes local dev and fails on install or activation. Set <code>target</code> to the engine floor of the runtime, and record it â€” the target is not a build preference, it is the compat statement you already made in <code>engines.vscode</code>.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// webpack.config.js
module.exports = {
  target: 'node16',          // match the oldest host you support
  // ...
};
// esbuild alternative (just as explicit):
esbuild: {
  target: 'node16',
  format: 'cjs'
}
// then verify with a packaged install in a clean profile â€” the only
// environment that uses the same load path as a real user.</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-webpack-debug">Debugging a packaged extension</h4>
    <p>When a packaged extension fails, the output channel and the process console are the evidence. The sequence is fixed: confirm the file list, look for activation errors in the log, and only then change code. The most useful single command is <code>Developer: Show Running Extensions</code>, which separates "loaded but lazy" from "failed to activate".</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># 1. the artefact, before anything else
$ npx vsce ls --tree
# 2. clean-profile install and activation log
$ code --install-extension myext-1.4.2.vsix --force
# 3. View > Output > My Extension : your own console
# 4. Developer: Show Running Extensions:
#      "my-extension: 1.4.2" without "(activated)" means the activation
#      never completed â€” start at the listener registration error.</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch8-chrome" data-toc>The Chrome Web Store</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE THREE THINGS THAT GET EXTENSIONS REJECTED
   1. REMOTE CODE. You may not download and execute JavaScript from
//      outside the extension package. eval(), new Function(), and
//      fetching a script and eval-ing it are all permanent rejection.
//      "It is a free API on my own domain" is not an exception.
//      You may fetch DATA, including JSON config, and interpret it.
//      You may not fetch CODE.
//   2. PERMISSIONS. Request the minimum. Every warning a user sees
//      comes from a permission you declared, and declaring more than
//      you use is both a rejection risk and an install-rate problem.
//   3. NOISE. Interstitial ads, new-tab overrides, affiliate injection,
//      and anything that changes the user's default search engine.

// THE MANIFEST, version 3
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "My Extension",
  "version": "1.4.2",
  "description": "One sentence. Under 132 characters.",
  "icons": { "16": "icon16.png", "48": "icon48.png", "128": "icon128.png" },
  "action": { "default_popup": "popup.html", "default_icon": "icon48.png" },
  "background": { "service_worker": "background.js", "type": "module" },
  "content_scripts": [{
    "matches": ["https://example.com/*"],
    "js": ["content.js"],
    "run_at": "document_idle"
  }],
  "permissions": ["storage", "activeTab", "scripting"],
  "host_permissions": ["https://example.com/*"],
  "web_accessible_resources": [{
    "resources": ["style.css"],
    "matches": ["https://example.com/*"]
  }]
}

// MV3 CHANGES THAT AFFECT DESIGN, and they are large
//   - NO REMOTE CODE, enforced. MV2 allowed it and it was the single
//     most abused capability in the extension ecosystem.
//   - SERVICE WORKER instead of a background page. It is terminated
//     aggressively. You must handle being killed: store state in
//     chrome.storage, not in memory, and re-register listeners in
//     the top level of the worker, not inside an event handler.
//   - declarativeNetRequest instead of blocking webRequest, for
//     request modification
//   - content scripts are injected via user action or on declared
//     matches, and there is no programmatic injection into arbitrary
//     pages without host_permissions
//   - the "offline" capability is gone. A local file cannot be
//     fetched without user opt-in per file.

// THE PERMISSION ECONOMICS
//   each permission produces a warning at install time, and the
//   warnings are the single largest cause of uninstall.
//     "Read and change your data on example.com"
//     "Read and change all your data on all websites"   &lt;- avoid
//   prefer activeTab, which grants access to the current tab only on
//   user action, over a broad host permission.

// THE PACKAGE
   "scripts": {
     "build": "webpack --mode production",
     "zip": "cd dist &amp;&amp; zip -r ../myext.zip . -x '*.map'"
   }
//   a .zip of the manifest and its assets. No bundler requirement, but
//   minify, or the review will note it and your size will be large.
//   MANIFEST V3 DOES NOT ALLOW EVAL EVEN IN A DEV BUILD, so if your
//   build uses eval for source maps, exclude the .map files, which
//   you were going to exclude anyway.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch8-chrome-worker">The service worker is a mailbox, not a daemon</h4>
    <p>MV3 replaced the background page with a service worker that Chrome terminates aggressively and restarts on demand. State cannot live in the worker's memory â€” a platform restart wipes it. The rules that follow are structural: store state in <code>chrome.storage</code>, re-register listeners at the worker's top level so they exist on every restart, and treat <code>onInstalled</code>/<code>onStartup</code> as the only lifetime hooks you can rely on.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// background.js â€” correct under termination
import { state } from "./state.js";          // chrome.storage.local wrapper
chrome.runtime.onInstalled.addListener(() => { /* one-time setup */ });
// top level: listeners exist before the first event can fire
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener(async (msg, sender, send) => {
  const s = await state.get();               // never a module-level variable
  send({ ok: true, count: (s.count ?? 0) + 1 });
});</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-chrome-permissions">Permissions are the install page</h4>
    <p>Every permission renders as a warning the user reads, and the warnings are the main uninstall driver. <code>activeTab</code> grants the current tab only in response to a user gesture and shows the mildest warning; a broad <code>host_permissions</code> pattern shows the strongest. The design goal is the smallest set that still works, reviewed again at every release â€” a permission that quietly stays after the feature that needed it was deleted is a real (and common) finding.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// declension of the same feature:
"permissions": ["activeTab", "scripting"]          // user acts first
"host_permissions": ["https://example.com/*"]      // one site
"host_permissions": ["&lt;all_urls&gt;"]               // "all your data" warning
// &lt;all_urls&gt; is the broadest host pattern, shows the strongest
// warning, and needs an explicit justification in the listing.</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-chrome-single">The single-purpose rule is a design constraint</h4>
    <p>Chrome interprets a multi-feature extension as deceptive: "one extension, one purpose" is enforced, and a bundle of unrelated features is a rejection reason. The constraint is a gift â€” it forces the product to be describable in the 132-character description, which is exactly the sentence users search for. If the extension genuinely does two things, split it into two extensions that share a permissions baseline.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// one shot at proving single purpose:
"name": "CSV Column Formatter",
"description": "Detects CSV columns and formats them in the browser.",
// vs the version that gets rejected:
"name": "Productivity Booster",
"description": "Formats CSV, blocks ads, replaces new tabs and darkens pages.",
// the store reads the bundle against the description. The bundle
// will be a single tree compiled from one feature area.</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch8-chrome-publish" data-toc>Publishing a Chrome extension</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE PROCESS
//   1. a Google account, and a one-time developer registration fee
//   2. chrome://extensions, Developer mode
//   3. "Load unpacked" for local development
//   4. for distribution: build a .zip and upload it at
//      chrome.google.com/webstore/devconsole
//   5. fill in the listing: description, category, screenshots, icons,
//      a support URL, a privacy policy URL
//   6. set the visibility: Public, Unlisted, or Private
//      PUBLIC      anyone can find it in search
//      UNLISTED    installable by URL only, NOT searchable. ideal for
//                  a companion to your own product
//      PRIVATE     only testers in your dashboard
//   7. submit for review
//   8. review takes hours to a couple of weeks. First submissions and
//      updates to permissions take longer.

// THE REVIEW, and what reviewers look for
//   - the listing matches the behaviour. A description promising a
//     feature that is not in the build is an instant rejection.
//   - no remote code, verified by reading the bundle
//   - permissions are justified and minimal
//   - a privacy policy URL that resolves, and a privacy practices
//     disclosure form in the dashboard. Chrome requires the
//     disclosure whether or not you collect anything; answer honestly
//   - the single purpose policy: an extension must do ONE thing
//   - no affiliate links, no referral URLs, no coupon codes
//   - the icon is your own, not a trademarked logo

// THE REJECTION REASONS, from experience
   "Minimum permissions not met"           request a broader host pattern
   "Does not match description"            a listed feature is missing
   "Remote code execution"                 any dynamic code loading
   "Single purpose"                        the extension does 4 things
   "Missing privacy policy"                the URL 404s, or is not yours
   "Deceptive metadata"                    a name or icon implying
                                          affiliation with another product

// UPDATES
//   an update to an existing listing is usually faster than a new one,
//   and a permission change triggers a fresh full review. Adding a
//   host permission to an established extension is a significant
//   event: you will be asked to justify it, and your install rate will
//   take a hit from the new warning.

// PROGRAMMATIC PUBLISHING, if you ship often
//   use the Chrome Web Store API with an OAuth2 client:
//     POST https://www.googleapis.com/chromewebstore/v1.4/items/{id}
//   with the zip as base64. Automatable, and it is the only sane way
//   to ship a weekly build.
//   budget: the first upload, and any permission change, still needs a
//   manual review. Automate the submission, not the review.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch8-chrome-submit">Submitting: the form is the review</h4>
    <p>The dashboard submission includes the description, category, screenshots, a support URL and a privacy-policy URL, plus the privacy practices disclosure â€” which must be answered even when you collect nothing, because the form is itself under review. The reviewer reads the listing against the bundle; a feature described but absent, or present but undescribed, is a rejection reason in either direction.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># the listing slot list (Web Store dev console):
#   name           the extension name, 45 chars
#   description    132 chars, plain, no marketing
#   category       the closest single purpose
#   screenshots    1280x800 or 640x400, at least one
#   icon           128x128, your own artwork
#   privacy URL    must resolve, must be yours
#   disclosure     the data-practices form, answer honestly</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-chrome-automate">Automating an upload you cannot automate past review</h4>
    <p>The Chrome Web Store API lets you publish a new package with an OAuth2 access token, so a weekly build can be pushed without opening the dashboard â€” but the review is not automatable, and a permission change always stops the line for a full re-review. Automate the artefact and the submission; leave the human step where the store put it.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ curl -X PUT \
  "https://www.googleapis.com/upload/chromewebstore/v1.1/items/$EXT_ID" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS_TOKEN" \
  -H "x-goog-api-version: 2" \
  -T package.zip
$ curl -X POST \
  "https://www.googleapis.com/chromewebstore/v1.1/items/$EXT_ID/publish" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS_TOKEN"
# the response is a submission id, not an approval</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-chrome-rollback">Visibility, and how to recover</h4>
    <p>Set a new extension to Private or Unlisted while you validate the listing, then promote it to Public. If a published version is broken, the store's remedy is to upload a corrected version and let it through review â€” there is no unpublish-and-revert that reaches users who already updated. The practical hedge is to keep the permission surface stable, because that is what turns a fast review into a slow one.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># visibility choices at submit time
#   Public    searchable, the normal release
#   Unlisted  install by URL only; ideal for a companion to a product
#   Private   only accounts in your dashboard
# a bad release: upload a fixed package, resubmit; users update on
# Chrome's schedule. Keep the permission set identical to avoid a
# fresh full review.</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch8-jetbrains" data-toc>JetBrains Marketplace</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE IDE PLUGINS, and the model is different
//   a plugin runs inside the IDE, in the IDE's own process, and the
//   marketplace will accept anything that compiles. There is no
//   equivalent of the Chrome remote-code rule, because the plugin
//   already has full local privileges by design.

// THE BUILD
$ ./gradlew buildPlugin
//   produces a ZIP in build/distributions/
//   the IntelliJ Platform Gradle Plugin handles a very large
//   compatibility matrix, compiling against several IDE versions

// the plugin.xml, the descriptor
&lt;idea-plugin&gt;
  &lt;id&gt;com.yourorg.myplugin&lt;/id&gt;
  &lt;name&gt;My Plugin&lt;/name&gt;
  &lt;vendor email="you@example.com" url="https://example.com"&gt;YourOrg&lt;/vendor&gt;
  &lt;description&gt;&lt;![CDATA[One paragraph.]]&gt;&lt;/description&gt;
  &lt;depends&gt;com.intellij.modules.platform&lt;/depends&gt;
  &lt;extensions defaultExtensionNs="com.intellij"&gt;
    &lt;toolWindow id="My Tool" anchor="right" factoryClass="com.yourorg.MyToolWindowFactory"/&gt;
  &lt;/extensions&gt;
&lt;/idea-plugin&gt;

// publishing
//   1. the JetBrains Marketplace plugin upload, or the
//      IntelliJ Platform Plugin SDK
//   2. for a paid plugin, an Organisation on the marketplace and a
//      licence enforcement plugin
//   3. upload the ZIP, set the licence, publish to a channel:
//      Stable, Early Access, or a custom channel
//   4. the plugin.xml version must be INCREMENTED, and an update needs
//      a new version in the channel. The marketplace is strict about
//      this and will reject a re-upload of the same version.

// THE CHANNEL MODEL, which is the good idea here
   Stable       everyone
   EAP          opted in, updated fast. Ideal for early feedback.
//   custom      for internal distributions, so a team can track a
//               specific build
//   The IDE lets users choose a custom plugin repository, so a company
//   can distribute a private plugin without the marketplace at all.

// WHAT REVIEW ACTUALLY CHECKS
//   - it compiles against a supported platform version
//   - it does not use deprecated or internal APIs. This is the most
//     common rejection, and internal API use will break on the next
//     IDE release with no deprecation period.
//   - the compatibility range in plugin.xml matches what you built
//   - no obfuscation, and a licence file is present
//   - the description does not contain marketing or a competitor's name</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch8-jetbrains-gradle">Building the plugin is one task</h4>
    <p>The IntelliJ Platform Gradle Plugin assembles the plugin against a chosen platform version and produces a distributable zip. The compatibility matrix â€” which IDE versions to build against â€” is the plugin's whole support story, so it belongs in the build file, not in a wiki page. The Marketplace reads the same range from <code>plugin.xml</code>, and a mismatch between what you built and what you claim is a review finding.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>plugins {
  id("org.jetbrains.intellij.platform") version "2.1.0"
}
intellijPlatform {
  pluginConfiguration {
    id.set("com.yourorg.myplugin")
    name.set("My Plugin")
    version.set("1.4.2")
  }
}
$ ./gradlew buildPlugin
# build/distributions/myplugin-1.4.2.zip</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-jetbrains-channels">Channels let you ship early access</h4>
    <p>The marketplace has stable and early-access channels, plus custom channels for internal distribution. A user opts into EAP and gets updates before stable; a custom channel lets a team pin a specific build and bypass the marketplace entirely by pointing the IDE at a plugin repository. This is the closest thing to a staging environment in the extension marketplaces.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># an EAP upload is a distinct channel on the same plugin id
$ ./gradlew publishPlugin \
    -PpluginVersion=1.5.0-EAP \
    -PpublishChannel=EAP
# a private plugin repository, distributed without the marketplace:
#   IntelliJ > Settings > Plugins > Manage Plugin Repositories
#   https://plugins.internal.example.com</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-jetbrains-compliance">What review actually checks</h4>
    <p>The plugin runs in the IDE's own process with the user's privileges, so the marketplace's compliance checks are about API discipline: no internal or undocumented APIs, a compatibility range that matches the build, no obfuscation, and a licence file. Internal API use is the most common rejection because it compiles today and breaks on the next IDE release with no deprecation warning â€” which is exactly the failure you want review to catch before your users do.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># check for internal API use before submitting:
$ ./gradlew verifyPlugin
$ ./gradlew runPluginVerifier    # against a matrix of IDE versions
# output: "Problems found" or "Compatible with all target versions"
# the verifier reports internal/experimental API usage by class and line.</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch8-figma" data-toc>Figma Community</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THREE SEPARATE FILE TYPES, with three different rules
   UI Kit     a library of components. imports between files are NOT
              published, which surprises people.
   Template   a .fig file. fully published.
   Plugin     a shared plugin. the same review scrutiny as a VS Code
              extension, because it is the same code.

// A UI KIT, the most common
//   one .fig file containing every component
//   in code:
     import { Button } from "yourorg/ui-kit"
//   NOT available in a published file. You must either:
//     a. publish the components as a single library and re-tag
//        consumers
//     b. use "variables" and "modes" for theming, which DO travel
//     c. accept that the file is a template, not a library
//   this is a genuine limitation and it is the most common complaint
//   from people who came from a code-first design system.

// VARIABLES AND MODES, which is the Figma-native answer
   variables: color/bg/default, color/bg/subtle, color/text/primary
   modes:     Light, Dark, High Contrast
//   variables and modes ARE published and do travel between files.
//   If your theming is variable-based, a published library works
//   properly across a file boundary. If it is component-swap based, it
//   does not.

// A TEMPLATE
   export a .fig, upload it, done. No code, no review scrutiny beyond
   the listing guidelines. This is the path of least resistance for
   anything that is a starting point rather than a live library.

// A PLUGIN
//   TypeScript, built with the Figma Plugin API, bundled to a single
//   main.js
     figma.ui.postMessage({ type: 'run', nodes: figma.currentPage.selection.length });
//   publish through the Figma desktop app:
//     Plugins > Development > Publish, or the community plugin portal
//   the listing rules are the strictest here:
//     - the icon must be a 96x96 PNG, no other size
//     - the name must not include the word "Figma", "Fig", or a
//       competitor's name
//     - no external network calls except to a URL you own, and it
//       must not be a proxy for something else
//     - no data collection without disclosure
//   the review is a human reading your manifest and your network
//   calls. A plugin that only reads the current page is accepted
//   quickly. One that reads the whole file takes longer.

// VECTOR NETWORK PATHS, the format to use
   .figma files for anything exported from Figma. They are a zip of
   JSON. Do NOT publish .svg as a "component library": they cannot be
   imported as components, and the file is enormous because each SVG
   repeats its viewBox and defs.
   For a design system shared with developers, publish a real library
   with code: generate a package from the same source of truth
   (Tokens Studio, or a build step) so Figma and code share one source.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch8-figma-variables">Variables and modes are the portable theming layer</h4>
    <p>Component swaps and style links do not cross a file boundary reliably; variables and modes do. A design system that expresses its theme as colour, number and string variables with Light/Dark modes publishes as something a consumer can retheme without touching the components. This is the Figma-native answer to the constant library, and it maps directly onto CSS custom properties.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># publishable, variable-based tokens:
variables:
  color/bg/default    #F7F8FA   (Light) | #0E1116 (Dark)
  color/text/primary  #101828   (Light) | #E6EDF7 (Dark)
modes: [Light, Dark, High Contrast]
# in code, the same names, one step away:
:root { --color-bg-default: #F7F8FA; }
[data-theme="dark"] { --color-bg-default: #0E1116; }</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-figma-plugin">A Figma plugin is a bundle with a manifest</h4>
    <p>The plugin is TypeScript compiled to a single <code>main.js</code>, plus a <code>manifest.json</code> that declares the network domains it may reach. The review reads that manifest, so the allow-list is the security surface: only domains you own, and any data collection declared. The UI half runs in an iframe and communicates by message passing, which is the boundary to respect.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// manifest.json
{
  "name": "My Plugin",
  "id": "1234567890",
  "api": "1.0.0",
  "main": "dist/main.js",
  "ui": "dist/ui.html",
  "networkAccess": {
    "allowedDomains": ["https://api.example.com"],
    "reasoning": "Sends the selected frame's colour count to our API."
  }
}
figma.ui.postMessage({ type: "run", nodes: figma.currentPage.selection.length })</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-figma-publish">Publishing a template or UI kit</h4>
    <p>A template is a <code>.fig</code> file and a UI kit is a library file; both publish with far less scrutiny than a plugin because neither executes code. The practical choice is by use: a starting-point file is a template, a live, rethemeable library is a UI kit with variables. For a design system consumed by developers, generate the code package from the same token source rather than treating Figma as the only source of truth.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># publish paths
#   UI Kit:   right-click file > Publish to Community (library)
#   Template: File > Publish to Community (.fig)
#   Plugin:   Plugins > Development > Publish
# keep Figma and code in sync from one token source:
$ npx token-transformer tokens.json tokens.css
$ npx style-dictionary build --config sd.config.json</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch8-homebrew" data-toc>Homebrew, Scoop, Chocolatey and apt</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// CLI TOOL DISTRIBUTION, which is a fourth model
//   no registry account, no publish token, and the "package" is a
//   formula or a manifest that installs a BINARY. The ecosystem owns
//   the build, which is why this is worth doing.

// HOMEBREW, on macOS and Linux
# a Homebrew formula, the minimal correct one
class Mytool &lt; Formula
  desc "A tool that does one thing well"
  homepage "https://example.com/mytool"
  url "https://github.com/yourorg/mytool/releases/download/v1.4.2/mytool_1.4.2_linux_amd64.tar.gz"
  version "1.4.2"
  license "MIT"

  on_macos do
    if Hardware::CPU.arm?
      url ".../mytool_1.4.2_darwin_arm64.tar.gz"
      sha256 "abc123..."
    else
      url ".../mytool_1.4.2_darwin_amd64.tar.gz"
      sha256 "def456..."
    end
  end

  on_linux do
    if Hardware::CPU.arm?
      url ".../mytool_1.4.2_linux_arm64.tar.gz"
      sha256 "ghi789..."
    else
      url ".../mytool_1.4.2_linux_amd64.tar.gz"
      sha256 "jkl012..."
    end
  end

  def install
    bin.install "mytool"
  end

  test do
    assert_match "1.4.2", shell_output("#{bin}/mytool --version")
  end
end
//   the sha256 per platform is mandatory. Homebrew will not install an
//   unverified binary, and that is a hard requirement, not a nicety.

// SUBMITTING
$ brew tap new-user/tap
$ brew install new-user/tap/mytool    # test it, from your own tap
$ brew audit --strict --online mytool
//   brew audit checks a long list of things: the url must be
//   https, the sha256 must match, the license must be valid, the
//   formula must be styled correctly, and the test must exist.
//   run it before you submit. It is stricter than the review.
# then open a pull request against Homebrew/homebrew-core
//   the maintainers will test it on macOS and Linux, both
//   architectures. Expect a few days and a few rounds of comments.

// A GO-RUST, which is the common case
   on_macos do
     if Hardware::CPU.arm?
       url "https://github.com/yourorg/mytool/releases/download/v1.4.2/mytool_Darwin_arm64.tar.gz"
     else
       url "https://github.com/yourorg/mytool/releases/download/v1.4.2/mytool_Darwin_x86_64.tar.gz"
     end
   end
   on_linux do
     if Hardware::CPU.arm?
       url "https://github.com/yourorg/mytool/releases/download/v1.4.2/mytool_Linux_arm64.tar.gz"
     else
       url "https://github.com/yourorg/mytool/releases/download/v1.4.2/mytool_Linux_x86_64.tar.gz"
     end
   end
//   the four-URL block is the standard shape for a Go binary, and a
//   PR without all four will be asked for them.

// THE OTHERS, briefly
   Scoop       Windows, PowerShell manifests, JSON. bin/Mytool.json.
                A PR to ScoopInstaller/Main. No build: a URL and a hash.
   Chocolatey  Windows, a .nuspec and a .ps1. Heavier than Scoop, and
                it can build from source. Own your own package repo, or
                submit to the community one.
   apt         Debian and Ubuntu, in the main archive after a long
                process, or a PPA which you can create yourself. A PPA
                is the fastest route and it is genuinely yours.
   nix         a flake.nix, and nixpkgs for the community channel.
                The best model of the four: it is fully declarative and
                reproducible.
   winget     Windows, a YAML manifest in the winget-pkgs repo, and the
                Microsoft Store as a backstop. Steadily becoming the
                default on Windows.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch8-homebrew-assets">The release assets decide the formula</h4>
    <p>Homebrew never builds your tool â€” it downloads a binary asset and verifies its sha256. The four-URL block (darwin arm64/amd64, linux arm64/amd64) is the shape reviewers expect, so naming the assets consistently at release time is the work that makes the formula trivial. A release pipeline that produces four assets with hashes is the upstream of every downstream packaging story.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># go-releaser produces exactly this set:
archives:
  - replacements:
      darwin: Darwin
      linux: Linux
      amd64: x86_64
      arm64: arm64
    format: tar.gz
# releases page then holds:
#   mytool_1.4.2_Darwin_arm64.tar.gz
#   mytool_1.4.2_Darwin_x86_64.tar.gz
#   mytool_1.4.2_Linux_arm64.tar.gz
#   mytool_1.4.2_Linux_x86_64.tar.gz
# and the formula's sha256 values come from the same build.</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-homebrew-audit">The audit is the review, run early</h4>
    <p><code>brew audit</code> enforces most of the formula rules mechanically â€” HTTPS urls, matching hashes, valid licence, correct style, a working test â€” and it is far stricter than the maintainers' first pass. Running it against your own tap before opening the PR turns a days-long back-and-forth into one clean submission. The <code>test do</code> block matters: it is what CI runs on every architecture.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ brew tap yourorg/tap &amp;&amp; brew install yourorg/tap/mytool
$ brew audit --strict --new-formula mytool
$ brew test mytool            # runs the formula's test block
$ brew style mytool
# after the PR: Homebrew CI builds and tests on macOS and Linux,
# both architectures. The formula must be correct cold.</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-homebrew-updates">Updating the formula keeps pace with releases</h4>
    <p>Every new release needs a formula update: new urls, new hashes. The tooling exists â€” <code>brew bump-formula-pr</code> opens the change for you â€” and the cadence is the real cost. A tool whose formula lags six months has, for package users, not released for six months. This is why a release pipeline that emits assets and hashes is not optional packaging hygiene; it is the product update mechanism.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ brew bump-formula-pr mytool \
    --url https://github.com/yourorg/mytool/releases/download/v1.4.3/mytool_1.4.3_Linux_x86_64.tar.gz \
    --sha256 "hex-hash-of-the-new-asset"
# or, in your own tap, simply edit the formula and push:
$ brew install yourorg/tap/mytool &amp;&amp; mytool --version
# the sha256 must be re-computed, never copied from a previous release</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch8-other" data-toc>Shopify, Slack, Atlassian and Home Assistant</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// ALL FOUR SHARE A MODEL
//   OAuth-installed apps on a platform you do not control, published
//   through a review process, with a listing page and install counts.
//   The technical packaging is the easy part.

// SHOPIFY
//   the Shopify CLI, and a TOML or JSON manifest
     [[extensions]]
     name = "t:name"
     handle = "myapp"
     type = "app"
     api_version = "2026-01"
//   $ shopify app dev          a local dev server with hot reload
//   $ shopify app deploy       uploads and releases
//   distribution: the App Store, a Plus app, or a custom distribution
//   through your own admin link. Custom distribution needs the
//   merchant to approve the install, so the listing on your own site
//   is the storefront.
//   required: a billing API integration if you charge, and webhooks
//   that handle the mandatory GDPR webhooks.

// SLACK
//   a manifest.json, a distributable zip, and a public directory
     "display_information": { "name": "My App" },
     "features": { "bot_user": true, "unfurl_domains": ["example.com"] },
     "oauth_config": { "scopes": { "bot": ["chat:write"] } },
     "settings": { "event_subscriptions": { "request_url": "...",
                     "bot_events": ["app_mention"] } }
//   $ slack create-app --manifest manifest.json
//   $ slack deploy       uploads to your team
//   $ slack distribute  submits to the App Directory
//   the directory review checks OAuth scopes, and asks why you need
//   each one. Scopes cannot be reduced after launch for existing
//   installs without breaking them, so start minimal.

// ATLASSIAN
//   two products, two mechanisms
//   Jira/Confluence apps   a static descriptor (atlassian-plugin.xml)
//                           bundled into a .jar/.zip, installed
//                           manually. Not in a marketplace. This is
//                           still the common case for internal tools.
//   Bitbucket apps         a marketplace, a JWT-based auth flow rather
//                           than OAuth, and Atlassian Connect
//   the OAuth model differs enough to be worth reading separately if
//   you need it: 3LO, JWT, and Roving tokens each have a different
//   threat model.

// HOME ASSISTANT, which is a good model to copy
//   HACS, the community store, installs custom components from GitHub
     "name": "Awesome Integration",
     "render_readme": true,
     "homeassistant": "2024.6.0"
//   the custom_components/ directory convention
   custom_components/awesome/manifest.json
   custom_components/awesome/__init__.py
   custom_components/awesome/const.py
   custom_components/awesome/config_flow.py
   custom_components/awesome/sensor.py
//   validation: hassfest, run in CI
     - uses: home-assistant/actions/hassfest@master
//   WHY THIS IS WORTH COPYING
//     - the manifest is declarative and machine-checked
//     - a config_flow is REQUIRED, so the user configures through a
//       form rather than editing YAML
//     - a version is required, so upgrades are possible
//     - CI validation is free and mandatory in practice
//   the cost: custom components are not sandboxed and break on core
//   updates. Be honest about that in your README.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch8-other-shopify">Shopify: the CLI owns the lifecycle</h4>
    <p>The Shopify CLI scaffolds, serves locally, and deploys the app manifest; the marketplace side is distribution choice and billing integration. The parts reviewers care about are the ones a merchant will experience: GDPR webhooks handled, a billing integration when you charge, and scopes that match behaviour. Deployment is not the hard step; the app's data handling is.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>$ shopify app init --template node
$ shopify app dev              # local dev with hot reload
$ shopify app deploy           # upload to the partner dashboard
# paid apps must implement the billing API:
#   /billing/create, /billing/subscriptions/usage
# GDPR webhooks are mandatory endpoints, not optional features:
#   customers/data_request
#   customers/redact
#   shop/redact</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-other-slack">Slack: scopes you cannot later reduce</h4>
    <p>The Slack directory review is primarily a scope review: each OAuth scope is read and justified, and scopes cannot be removed for existing installs without breaking them. The rule follows â€” start with the minimum, because the minimum is also the maximum a growing install base will tolerate. A manifest declared once becomes the policy for every future change.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// manifest.json, minimal
{
  "display_information": { "name": "My App" },
  "features": { "bot_user": true },
  "oauth_config": { "scopes": { "bot": ["chat:write"] } },
  "settings": { "event_subscriptions": {
    "request_url": "https://api.example.com/slack/events",
    "bot_events": ["app_mention"]
  }}
}
# $ slack create-app --manifest manifest.json
# $ slack deploy &amp;&amp; slack distribute
# each scope in the review gets a "why" â€” write them before the review</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-other-hacs">Home Assistant: a model worth copying</h4>
    <p>HACS installs custom components from GitHub, and its success is a design lesson: a declarative, machine-checked manifest; a required <code>config_flow</code> so users configure through a form; a version field so upgrades are possible; and CI validation that runs <code>hassfest</code> on every commit. The honest cost, stated in any good README, is that the component is not sandboxed and can break on core updates.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># custom_components/awesome/manifest.json
{
  "domain": "awesome",
  "name": "Awesome Integration",
  "version": "1.4.2",
  "requirements": ["requests>=2.31"],
  "dependencies": [],
  "config_flow": true,
  "codeowners": ["@yourorg"]
}
# CI gate:
- uses: home-assistant/actions/hassfest@master
# the same three properties (declarative manifest, version, CI check)
# are the recipe for any registry you will ever build.</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch8-listing" data-toc>Writing a listing that gets found</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// A LISTING IS A LANDING PAGE, and it decides whether anyone
// installs. Most listings are written by a developer for developers,
// and read by a person deciding in about eight seconds.

// THE EIGHT SECONDS
   who is it for        one line, the problem it solves
   what it does         one sentence
   why this over the    one line. this is the line people skip.
   other 40
   is it safe           permissions, data handling, one line
   how do I start       three numbered steps, on the page
   what if it breaks    a link to issues, and a version support policy

// THE FIELDS EACH MARKETPLACE ASKS FOR
   VS Code       displayName, description (200 chars), categories,
                 icon, galleryBanner, README as the long form
   Chrome        name, description (132 chars), summary, category,
                 screenshots, a privacy disclosure form
   JetBrains     plugin.xml description, categories, an update note
   Figma         name, description, cover image, icon, categories
   Homebrew      desc, homepage, license, test. no prose.
//   npm/PyPI      the README. this is your landing page. render it.

// THE README, and it matters more than the description
//   npm and PyPI render it as the package page. It is the first thing
//   a person sees, and the only place you can explain anything.
//   the structure that works
     # name
     one sentence, no clever name pun
     ## Why
     the problem in two sentences, in the user's words
     ## Install
     the shortest possible command, plus a manual fallback
     ## Usage
     the smallest example that runs, copy-pasteable
     ## Configuration
     a table of options
     ## Compatibility
     versions, and what you support
     ## Contributing
     how to run the tests locally
     ## License
     not just the word. a link.
//   a README with an Install section and a 10-line example gets more
//   installs than a README with a logo at the top. The logo is not
//   the first thing anyone reads; the sentence is.

// SEO AND DISCOVERY, and it is not optional
   - the title is the primary search signal on npm, PyPI and the
     marketplaces. "yourpkg - a small, fast library for X" beats
     "YourPkg" every time
   - include the technology and the problem in the description, in
     plain words. People search "typescript validation", not your
     invented product name
   - a repository, a homepage and a changelog URL in the package
     metadata. They are indexed and they are trust signals
   - for a paid or commercial extension, the listing is a sales page.
     A free tier on the marketplace and a paid tier on your site
     works better than a paid marketplace listing, because the
     marketplace search is how people find you in the first place</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch8-listing-readme">The README is the landing page</h4>
    <p>On npm and PyPI the README is rendered as the package page; on the marketplaces it is the long-form supplement to the short description. In both cases its job is to convert the eight-second scan into an install. The structure that works is short and literal: one named sentence, an install command, a runnable example, a configuration table, and a compatibility note. The logo is not the first thing anyone reads; the sentence is.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># yourpkg
Formats CSV in the browser, with column detection.
## Install
npm install yourpkg        # or: pip install yourpkg
## Usage
import { format } from "yourpkg";
const out = format("name,age\nada,37");
## Configuration
| option | default | meaning |
|--------|---------|---------|
| detect | true    | auto-detect the delimiter |
## Compatibility
Node >= 18, browsers: last 2 versions.
## License
MIT â€” see LICENSE.</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-listing-seo">The title and description are the search</h4>
    <p>Search on a registry matches the title, description, keywords and metadata fields â€” not the website. The title that wins names the technology and the problem in plain words; the description repeats them. Keywords are the machine-readable complement. Write for the person who typed the problem, not for the name you invented.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># title pattern on npm / PyPI / marketplaces:
"yourpkg - a small, fast CSV formatter for the browser"
# description pattern:
"Formats CSV data in the browser with automatic column detection.
Works offline. Zero dependencies."
# metadata keywords:
keywords: ["csv", "parsing", "browser", "typescript"]
# people search the problem, not the brand:
#   "formatter csv browser" hits this listing; "YourPkg" does not.</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-listing-trust">Trust signals are part of the listing</h4>
    <p>Install counts, ratings and unanswered issues are read together. The controllable inputs are: a repository and issue tracker that resolve, a changelog that exists, a stated support policy, and licence clarity. A marketplace page with ten issues and no responses is worse than no page â€” response time is part of the product the listing promises.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># the trust block every listing should carry:
- repository:      https://github.com/yourorg/yourpkg
- issues:          https://github.com/yourorg/yourpkg/issues
- changelog:       https://github.com/yourorg/yourpkg/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md
- support policy:  "Issues triaged weekly. Security issues within 48h."
# and the two lines in the README that matter most:
#   "Does not collect telemetry."
#   "Last release supports the three most recent versions."</code></pre></div>

    <h3 id="ch8-triage" data-toc>Review responses and triage</h3>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WHEN YOU ARE REJECTED
//   1. read the reason. They are specific and usually correct.
//   2. do not resubmit the same artefact. The second review is not
//      kinder.
//   3. reply in the thread, not a new submission. Explain what you
//      changed, referencing their words.
//   4. if the reason is a policy you disagree with, argue once, with
//      the policy quoted, and accept the outcome if they hold firm.
//      Being pleasant and specific gets a higher success rate than
//      being right.

// THE TEMPLATE THAT WORKS
   "Thanks for the review. Three things:
      1. Remote code: removed. The config fetch in settings.js now
         returns JSON only; the code path that evaluated the response
         is gone (commit abc123).
      2. Permissions: dropped 'tabs' and replaced it with 'activeTab'.
         I have re-tested the three features that used it and they all
         work with activeTab (screenshot attached).
      3. Description: rewritten. It said 'boosts your productivity',
         which does not describe what it does. It now reads: 'Formats
         CSV files in the browser, with column detection'.
    The bundle is in the new upload. Happy to add anything else."

// AFTER APPROVAL, THE WORK STARTS
   - pin the version, do not float
   - monitor the review tab, which is where extensions get reported
   - respond to issues within a few days. A marketplace page with
     unanswered issues is worse than no page.
//   - a version support policy, stated on the page. "Last release
     supports the three most recent VS Code versions" is a reasonable
     and honest policy, and it stops you from being blamed for not
     supporting something you never said you would.

// THE METRICS THAT MATTER
   installs       the primary reach metric
   the ratio of installs to the marketplace's search impressions, which
                  is your listing quality
   ratings        the single most influential number, and it lags by
                  weeks
   uninstall rate  a high rate with a low rating means a permissions
                  or privacy problem, not a bug
   crash reports  if your extension crashes, the marketplace tells
                  users. They will not trust it again.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch8-triage-rejection">A rejection is a debugging session</h4>
    <p>Treat the rejection text as the true bug report it is. Reproduce the finding, fix the artefact, then answer in the same review thread â€” never as a new submission â€” referencing the reviewer's own words. A response that lists, per finding, what changed and where, converts best. Resubmitting the same artefact hoping for a friendlier reader is the one move that never works.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># the response shape that works
Thanks for the review. Three things:
1. Remote code: removed. The config fetch now returns JSON only;
   the code path that evaluated the response is gone (commit abc123).
2. Permissions: dropped "tabs" for "activeTab"; the three features
   that used it re-tested against it (screenshots attached).
3. Description: rewritten to describe behaviour, not benefit.
The bundle is in the new upload. Happy to add anything else.
# the shape that does not: a one-line "fixed, please re-review"</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-triage-policy">Arguing policy is a separate, single move</h4>
    <p>If the rejection rests on a policy you disagree with, argue once, citing the policy text, and accept the outcome if it holds. The marketplaces are not courts; the cost of escalation is delay for a change that is usually refused anyway. The part that is worth the argument is a factual misreading â€” and even then, the fix is often to re-read your own listing.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># a policy argument that is worth one round only
"The guideline says extensions 'must not collect data without
disclosure'. We collect nothing; our listing already states that,
and the bundle has no network code. The rejection cited 'telemetry'
â€” could you point to the specific code path flagged?"
# then fix or accept per the answer. One round, then move on.</code></pre></div>

    <h4 id="ch8-triage-metrics">After approval, the metrics are the triage</h4>
    <p>The useful numbers after launch are installs, the install-to-impression ratio, ratings, uninstall rate and crash reports. A high uninstall rate with a low rating points at permissions or privacy, not bugs; a rating that lags weeks hides a regression that already ran. The daily practice is the issues tab. A marketplace page cannot be abandoned the way a package can â€” the listing is the product's front door.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># the weekly triage loop
#   1. issues: triage by severity, answer within the stated SLA
#   2. crash reports: fix the top stack trace first
#   3. new reviews: reply to the 1- and 2-star ones, factually
#   4. metrics vs last week:
#      uninstall rate up  with rating down   -> permissions trouble
#      uninstall rate flat with rating up    -> listing doing its job</code></pre></div>

    <div class="ex"><h4>Exercises 8</h4><ol>
      <li>Scaffold a VS Code extension, build it with webpack using <code>libraryTarget: commonjs2</code> and <code>externals: vscode</code>, package it with <code>--no-dependencies</code>, and install the <code>.vsix</code> in a clean profile. Fix whatever breaks between <code>F5</code> and the packaged install.</li>
      <li>Write a Homebrew formula for a real tool with all four platform URLs and their sha256 sums, run <code>brew audit --strict --online</code>, and fix every warning it gives.</li>
      <li>Publish an unlisted Chrome extension and install it by URL. Then submit a version that adds a host permission and observe the review time difference. Record both.</li>
      <li>Publish a Figma UI kit with variables and modes, open it in a new file, and confirm the variables travelled. Contrast with what happens to a component-swap library.</li>
      <li>Rewrite the README of one of your own packages following the structure in this chapter, then measure the install rate over the following month against the previous one.</li>
    </ol></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
<h2 id="ch9" data-toc>9 Â· Private Registries and Internal Distribution</h2>
    <div class="ch-toc"><button type="button" class="ch-toc-head" aria-expanded="true"><span class="arrow">â–¸</span>Contents &middot; 9 Â· Private Registries and Internal Distribution</button><div class="ch-toc-list"><a href="#ch9-when">When a private registry is the right answer</a><a href="#ch9-why">Why not just a git repository</a><a href="#ch9-satis">Satis, the self-hosted Packagist</a><a href="#ch9-verdaccio">Verdaccio, for npm</a><a href="#ch9-devpi">devpi, for PyPI</a><a href="#ch9-artifactory">Artifactory, Nexus and Harbor</a><a href="#ch9-github">GitHub Packages and packages-as-code</a><a href="#ch9-auth">Authentication and authorisation</a><a href="#ch9-proxy">Caching and proxying public registries</a><a href="#ch9-migrate">Migrating away from a private registry</a></div></div>
    <p class="topic-desc">Most private registries exist to solve a problem a monorepo workspace or a git reference solves better. The cases where a real registry is justified are narrower than people assume, and worth being clear about before you run one.</p>
    <h3 id="ch9-when" data-toc>When a private registry is the right answer</h3>
    <h4 id="ch9-when-ownership">Owning the name, with availability and an audit trail</h4>
    <p><strong>Owning the name</strong> is the strongest case. A platform team serving hundreds of engineers needs <code>@yourorg/*</code> under its control, with every publish attributed, every read logged, and an availability guarantee a git tag cannot give. A tag freezes the artefact; it does not make it discoverable, resolvable or reliable. When a team's workflow depends on consistent access to internal packages, a private registry provides a stable endpoint that a git reference cannot match.</p>
    <p>The audit trail is equally important: every publish, every yank, every version upload creates a record. For regulated industries, this is not optional — it is a compliance requirement. The registry becomes the single source of truth for what is in production and when it was published.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># a five-line health check for the registry your builds depend on.
# run it from a monitor, or as the first step of every CI job.
REGISTRY=https://npm.example.com
curl -sf -o /dev/null -w "metadata: %{http_code} in %{time_total}s\n" \
  "$REGISTRY/@yourorg/core" || echo "registry unreachable - every build fails now"
curl -sf "$REGISTRY/-/ping" &amp;&amp; echo "ping: ok"</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch9-when-airgap">Air-gapped builds that cannot reach a public service</h4>
    <p>Air-gapped build environments are the case where a public registry is not merely inconvenient but impossible. The registry must live inside the gap, and every dependency it serves has to be mirrored in, verified at the boundary, and served from local storage. This is the one scenario where "we might need to go offline someday" is a real requirement rather than speculation. The key distinction is that an air gap is a policy or security mandate, not a convenience feature.</p>
    <p>Operational pattern: on a connected host, mirror the registry export, sign the index, physically transfer the media, then verify and import on the air-gapped host. Each step must be scripted and verified, because a broken air gap means builds grind to a halt with no recourse.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># the air-gap pattern: build the mirror on a connected host,
# carry it in, verify it at the boundary, serve it inside
# on the connected host
./mirror.sh /registry/export /gap/media
sign /gap/media/index.json /gap/media/index.sig

# physically transfer, then on the air-gapped host
verify-sig /gap/media/index.json /gap/media/index.sig pinned-key.asc
import /gap/media</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch9-when-cost">The honest cost of running one</h4>
    <p>The cost is not zero. It is a stateful service that must be highly available because every build depends on it; it must be backed up, monitored and upgraded; it accumulates storage forever because nobody deletes old versions; and it is a supply-chain chokepoint — whoever administers it can serve you anything, which moves trust from a publicly audited registry to an internal box. If you cannot name the engineer who is woken when it breaks, you cannot run one. The operational overhead is ongoing and often underbudgeted.</p>
    <p>Year‑long cost ledger: infrastructure (VMs, object storage, backups, TLS certificates), operations (patching, upgrades, certificate rotation), on‑call (a registry outage stops every build in the company), and trust (you have moved from a publicly audited registry to an internal box whose admin can serve anything to everyone). A sensible test: if the registry is down for an hour, how many engineers are blocked? If the answer is "all of them", the registry needs HA you probably have not budgeted for.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// the honest cost ledger, per year
    infrastructure     VMs, object storage, backups, TLS certificates
    operations         patching, upgrades, certificate rotation
    on-call            a registry outage stops every build in the company
    trust              a chokepoint: the admin can serve anything to everyone
// a sensible test: if the registry is down for an hour, how many
// engineers are blocked? if the answer is "all of them", the registry
// needs HA you probably have not budgeted for.</code></pre></div>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE CASES WHERE A PRIVATE REGISTRY IS RIGHT
   1. You OWN the name, and you need availability and an audit trail
      that a git tag does not give. A company publishing 200 internal
      packages to 500 engineers.
   2. You must not depend on a public service for availability. An
      air-gapped build environment, or a rule forbidding outbound calls.
   3. You need a policy layer: who may publish what, who may read
      what, and an audit log of both.
   4. You are a hosting provider distributing customer artefacts.

// THE CASES WHERE IT IS WRONG
   - ONE internal package used by a few teams. A git repository with a
     path or VCS reference is simpler and has no service to run.
   - You want to avoid a public dependency. Use a scoped name on a
     public registry. No infrastructure required.
   - "We might need to go offline someday." Speculative. A path
     repository is already offline-capable and costs nothing.
   - A small team with "we need our own registry" in a planning
     document. It is a significant ongoing cost for something free.

// THE COST, and it is not zero
   - a stateful service that must be highly available, because every
     build depends on it. An outage stops all engineering.
   - it must be backed up, monitored and upgraded, and it accumulates
     storage forever because nobody deletes old versions.
   - it is a supply-chain chokepoint: whoever administers it can serve
     you anything. You moved trust from a publicly audited registry to
     an internal box.
   - the ch3-oidc options are mostly unavailable to it, so you will
     be managing long-lived tokens by hand.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch9-why" data-toc>Why not just a git repository</h3>
    <h4 id="ch9-why-workspaces">A monorepo with workspaces: no infrastructure at all</h4>
    <p>For most organisations this is the whole answer. One repository, many packages, a workspace file, and nothing to run. Edits are immediate, a change that spans packages is one atomic commit, and CI needs no registry credentials because there is no registry. The price is that a large cross-package refactor becomes one enormous pull request, and every consumer clones the entire repository to depend on one file. When the monorepo grows beyond a few dozen packages, the CI pipeline itself becomes the primary coordination mechanism, and tooling like pnpm workspaces or npm workspaces becomes essential. Operational reality: as the monorepo scales, clone times increase, the single history becomes a bottleneck, and the "one enormous pull request" problem matures from a minor inconvenience to a genuine productivity drag. Teams often split into sub-monorepos or adopt a polyrepo strategy with shared packages once the head count or dependency graph justifies the move.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># pnpm-workspace.yaml - the entire configuration
packages:
  - 'packages/*'
  - 'apps/*'

# and in each package's package.json, the internal dependency
# is resolved by the workspace, never fetched from a registry:
#   "dependencies": { "@yourorg/shared-utils": "workspace:*" }
# `pnpm install` links it, and `pnpm --filter @yourorg/core test`
# runs its tests without anything being published.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch9-why-vcs">VCS references: a real pin, a source build every time</h4>
    <p>When packages must cross repository boundaries, a VCS reference installs straight from git and pins to a tag. It gives you an exact version with no service to run, but it installs and builds from source on every clean clone, so it is slow, and it needs a <code>prepare</code> script that works in a bare checkout with no workspace, no lockfile and no dev dependencies installed.</p>
    <p>Common pattern: the prepare script runs a build, but on a fresh git checkout, build dependencies may not be installed, path references may be wrong, and environment variables may be missing. A robust prepare script handles these cases explicitly, or the team accepts the build cost as the price of zero-maintenance infrastructure.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># install from a tag, exact and immutable
npm install github:yourorg/shared-utils#v1.2.0

# the prepare script must work in a clean checkout, because that is
# exactly what the consumer gets:
#   "prepare": "npm run build"
#   "files": ["dist"]
# a prepare script that fails on a fresh clone is a broken dependency,
# and the consumer's CI is where it fails.</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch9-why-decision">The decision, in order</h4>
    <p>Work down the list and stop at the first step that satisfies you. Each step is cheaper and operationally simpler than the one below it, and most "we need a private registry" requests are satisfied by the first two.</p>
    <p>Decision rationale: working from the cheapest option first avoids the operational overhead of running a registry when simpler alternatives satisfy the need. Path references and VCS installs are free; private registries have HA, backup, and upgrade costs that must be justified.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// stop at the first "yes"
    1. Do the packages need publishing at all?
          no  -> a monorepo with workspaces. nothing is published.
    2. Do they need sharing across repositories?
          yes -> git plus a VCS reference, with a prepare script.
    3. Do you need availability, policy or an audit trail?
          yes -> a registry, and you now know exactly why.
// if you answered yes to 3 but not to 1 or 2, write down which of
// availability, policy and audit you need. one of them is usually
// solvable without running a registry.</code></pre></div>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE ALTERNATIVES, and when each is enough

// 1. THE PATH REPOSITORY. A local reference, symlinked.
   // package.json
   { "repositories": [
     { "type": "path", "url": "../packages/shared-utils",
       "options": { "symlink": true } }
   ] }
//   + no service, works offline
//   + edits are immediate
//   - monorepo-only: it is a path on this machine
//   - CI needs the repo checked out alongside

// 2. THE VCS REFERENCE. An install straight from a git URL.
   npm install github:yourorg/shared-utils#v1.2.0
   // + a real version pin: the tag
//   + no service
//   - it installs and builds from source every time, so it is slow
//   - it needs a prepare script that works in a clean checkout
//   - gitlab and bitbucket hosts are inconsistent, sometimes unsupported

// 3. THE MONOREPO WITH WORKSPACES. One repository, many packages.
   // pnpm-workspace.yaml
   packages:
     - 'packages/*'
     - 'apps/*'
   // npm
   { "workspaces": ["packages/*", "apps/*"] }
//   + one clone, one history, atomic cross-package changes
//   + npm 7+ and pnpm both do it well
//   - a large refactor across packages is one enormous pull request
//   THIS IS THE RIGHT ANSWER FOR MOST ORGANISATIONS. A monorepo with
//   workspaces covers the majority of internal distribution with no
//   infrastructure at all.

// 4. A PROXY CACHE, which is a different thing
   // see ch9-proxy. It is not a publishing destination. Many "we need
   // a private registry" requests are actually "we need a cache".

// THE DECISION, in order
   1. Do the packages need publishing at all? A monorepo with
//      workspaces means they are never published anywhere.
   2. Do they need sharing across repositories? Then git plus a VCS
//      reference, with a prepare script.
   3. Do you need availability, policy or an audit trail? Then a
//      registry, and you know why.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch9-satis" data-toc>Satis, the self-hosted Packagist</h3>
    <h4 id="ch9-satis-config">The satis.json configuration</h4>
    <p>Satis builds a Composer repository from your VCS hosts. The config lists the repositories to scan and the packages to expose; the output is a static <code>satis.json</code> plus a tree of ZIPs. Because a Composer package is just a ZIP with a <code>composer.json</code> inside, there is no daemon, no database and nothing to keep patched â€” which is why Satis is still popular despite being from 2015.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>{
  "name": "YourOrg/Internal",
  "homepage": "https://repo.example.com",
  "repositories": [
    { "type": "vcs", "url": "https://git.example.com/platform/shared-utils.git" },
    { "type": "vcs", "url": "https://github.com/yourorg/logger.git" }
  ],
  "require": { "yourorg/shared-utils": "*" },
  "archive": {
    "directory": "dist",
    "format": "tar",
    "skip-dev": true
  },
  "output-html": false
}</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch9-satis-build">Building and serving the static output</h4>
    <p>The build clones every repository, reads each <code>composer.json</code>, resolves the dependency graph and writes the static files. Serving them is a cron job and an nginx location block. The operational cost is close to zero, and the failure mode is a stale mirror rather than a crashed service.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># the build, on a schedule
$ php satis/bin/satis build --no-interaction

# the build clones every repository, reads each composer.json, resolves the dependency graph and writes the static files. Serving them is a cron job and an nginx location block. The operational cost is close to zero, and the failure mode is a stale mirror rather than a crashed service.

# nginx serves the output; there is nothing else to run
location / {
    root /var/www/satis;
    try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
}

# the cron entry: rebuild nightly, and fail loudly if it errors
0 3 * * * cd /opt/satis &amp;&amp; php bin/satis build --no-interaction \
    &gt;&gt; /var/log/satis.log 2&gt;&amp;1</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch9-satis-verify">Verifying the build, every time</h4>
    <p>Satis tests only the happy path, so verification is your job. Check that every expected package is present and nothing unexpected did, that version numbers match the tags, and that the dependency graph resolved â€” a version requiring a package not in the repository fails at install, not at build. Then run a real <code>composer install</code> against the built repository in CI, from a clean project.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># a CI step that catches a stale or broken Satis build
$ composer config repositories.satis composer https://repo.example.com
$ composer install --dry-run
#   every package must resolve from the Satis repository.
#   a package missing from satis.json fails here, not in production.

# and check the graph: a version requiring a package you did not
# include will fail at install time with a clear error. that error
# is the build telling you the repository is incomplete.

Satis tests only the happy path, so verification is your job. Check that every expected package is present and nothing unexpected did, that version numbers match the tags, and that the dependency graph resolved — a version requiring a package not in the repository fails at install, not at build. Then run a real composer install against the built repository in CI, from a clean project.</code></pre></div>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// SATIS builds a Composer repository from your VCS hosts. The most
// mature self-hosted registry here, because a Composer package is just a
// ZIP with a composer.json in it.

// the config, in the Satis root
// Satis is the standard self-hosted Packagist. Its most valuable feature
// is proxying: it caches Packagist on demand. The config has three parts —
// storage, auth, and the package access rules — and the access rules are
// where most deployments go wrong, because a single '*'* pattern with
// $authenticated publish means any engineer's CI token can publish any package.

// .verdaccio/config.yaml
storage: ./storage

{
  "name": "YourOrg/Internal",
  "homepage": "https://git.example.com/platform/satis",
  "repositories": [
    { "type": "vcs", "url": "https://git.example.com/platform/shared-utils.git" },
    { "type": "vcs", "url": "https://github.com/yourorg/logger.git" }
  ],
  "require": { "yourorg/shared-utils": "*" }
}

// running it
$ composer create-project composer/satis satis/
$ php satis/bin/satis build --no-interaction
// it clones every repository, reads each composer.json, resolves the
// dependency graph, and writes satis.json plus a tree of ZIPs.
// THE OUTPUT IS STATIC FILES. nginx can serve it, and the build can be
// a cron job. There is no daemon, no database, and nothing to keep
// patched. This is why Satis is popular despite being from 2015.

// the consumer config
   // ~/.composer/config.json, or in the project composer.json
   { "repositories": [
     { "type": "composer", "url": "https://repo.example.com" }
   ] }

// the auth
   // Satis reads an auth.json
   {
     "github-oauth": { "github.com": "ghp_token" },
     "gitlab-oauth": { "gitlab.com": "glpat-token" }
   }
//   NOTE CAREFULLY: with a VCS repository listed, Composer
//   authenticates to Git to clone. That token needs repository read
//   scope and a long lifetime, stored in a file on a server. Satis
//   gives you a static registry and then reintroduces the credential
//   problem at the build step.

// VERIFYING THE BUILD, every time
$ php satis/bin/satis build --no-interaction -vvv
//   check the output for:
//   - every package you expected, and nothing you did not
//   - version numbers that match the tags
//   - a resolved dependency graph. A version requiring a package not
//     in the repository will fail at install, not at build.
// then run a real install against the built repository in CI, because
// the happy path is the only path Satis tests.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch9-verdaccio" data-toc>Verdaccio, for npm</h3>
    <h4 id="ch9-verdaccio-config">The config.yaml that matters</h4>
    <p>Verdaccio is the standard self-hosted npm proxy and registry. Its most valuable feature is proxying: it caches npmjs on demand. The config has three parts â€” storage, auth, and the package access rules â€” and the access rules are where most deployments go wrong, because a single <code>'**'</code> pattern with <code>$authenticated</code> publish means any engineer's CI token can publish any package.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>storage: ./storage
auth:
  htpasswd:
    file: ./htpasswd
    max_users: 1000
uplinks:
  npmjs:
    url: https://registry.npmjs.org/
    cache: true
    timeout: 30s
    maxage: 2m
packages:
  '@yourorg/*':
    access: $all
    publish: $authenticated
    unpublish: $authenticated
    proxy: npmjs
  '**':
    access: $all
    publish: $authenticated
    proxy: npmjs
log: { type: stdout, format: pretty, level: http }
max_body_size: 100mb</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch9-verdaccio-proxy">Proxy mode: what people actually want</h4>
    <p>The proxy mode is the value proposition. A package not found locally is fetched from npmjs and cached; a package found locally is served without any upstream call. Developers get npm-like speed, already-cached packages make zero outbound calls, and you have a record of what was fetched. For a compliance-controlled environment that is the whole point, and it is cheaper than a full registry.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># the request flow, and why it is fast after the first call
npm install mypackage
#   -&gt; not found locally
#   -&gt; fetched from registry.npmjs.org
#   -&gt; cached in ./storage
#   -&gt; served to every subsequent install with no outbound call

npm install @yourorg/thing
#   -&gt; found locally, no upstream call at all

# pre-warm the cache for the packages your builds actually use,
# so an outage does not turn into a failed build:
$ npm cache add express lodash chalk</code></pre></div>
    <h4 id="ch9-verdaccio-auth">Auth: htpasswd is fine for a team, wrong for a company</h4>
    <p>Verdaccio's own auth is htpasswd, which is fine for a small team and wrong for a company with a directory. Most teams front it with oauth2-proxy, Pomerium, or an internal proxy that injects the identity, and put TLS in front of that. The config format also changed between major versions, so pin the version and read the migration notes before upgrading.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code># the standard production shape: an identity proxy in front,
# Verdaccio behind it, TLS terminated at the edge
#   developer -&gt; oauth2-proxy (SSO) -&gt; Verdaccio -&gt; npmjs

# in oauth2-proxy, the headers that carry the identity:
#   X-Forwarded-User: alice
#   X-Forwarded-Groups: platform,engineers
# Verdaccio trusts the proxy's network location, so the proxy must
# be the only route to it. A direct connection bypasses SSO.

# pin the version, and read the migration notes before upgrading:
$ npm install -g verdaccio@5</code></pre></div>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// VERDACCIO is the standard self-hosted npm proxy and registry. Its
// most valuable feature is proxying: it caches npmjs on demand.

// .verdaccio/config.yaml
storage: ./storage
auth:
  htpasswd:
    file: ./htpasswd
    max_users: 1000
uplinks:
  npmjs:
    url: https://registry.npmjs.org/
    cache: true
    timeout: 30s
    maxage: 2m              # cache metadata briefly, so a yank or a
                            # new version is picked up quickly
packages:
  '@yourorg/*':
    access: $all
    publish: $authenticated
    unpublish: $authenticated
    proxy: npmjs
  '**':
    access: $all
    publish: $authenticated
    proxy: npmjs
log: { type: stdout, format: pretty, level: http }
max_body_size: 100mb

// RUNNING IT
$ npx verdaccio --config .verdaccio/config.yaml
// for production, behind TLS and behind SSO. Verdaccio's own auth is
// htpasswd, which is fine for a small team and wrong for a company
// with a directory. Most teams front it with oauth2-proxy, Pomerium,
// or an internal proxy that injects the identity.

// THE PROXY MODE, which is what people actually want
   npm install mypackage   -&gt;  not found locally  -&gt; fetched
                             -&gt; cached
   npm install @yourorg/thing -&gt; found locally, no upstream
// The result: developers get npm-like speed, already-cached packages
// make zero outbound calls, and you have a record of what was
// fetched. For a compliance-controlled environment, that is the whole
// value proposition, and it is cheaper than a full registry.

// THE GOTCHAS
   - the config format changed between major versions. Pin the version
     and read the migration notes before upgrading.
   - the cache is incomplete until something requests a package. A
     build needing an uncached package during an outage still fails.
     Pre-warm with `npm cache add` against the registry, or accept it.
   - npm's audit endpoints need proxying too, or `npm audit` fails
     behind the proxy. Verdaccio can proxy them; the config is fiddly.
   - Verdaccio has no fine-grained per-package publish authorisation
     beyond package patterns. For per-repository control you need
     something in front of it.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch9-devpi" data-toc>devpi, for PyPI</h3><h4 id="ch9-devpi-index">User indexes and staging</h4><p>Devpi provides per-user indexes, so a developer's staged uploads are visible only to them. This is the best staging workflow of any registry in this guide. A user can upload to their personal index, test installation, and then upload to the root index (or PyPI) when ready. The staging pattern prevents broken packages from reaching other developers.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// DEVPI is a PyPI server AND a caching HTTP proxy, with one unique
// feature: per-user indexes, so a developer's staged uploads are
// visible only to them.

$ pip install -U twine devpi-server devpi-client
$ devpi-init --rootdir $HOME/.devpi
$ devpi-server --host 0.0.0.0 --port 3141 &amp;

// a user index, which is the devpi model
$ devpi use http://localhost:3141
$ devpi login alice --password='a-strong-password'
$ devpi user -c alice
$ devpi use alice/dev
$ twine upload --repository dev dist/*
//   alice/dev is a private staging index. Uploads there are invisible
//   to everyone else. This is the best staging workflow of any
//   registry in this guide, and it is nearly unknown.

// the release step
$ devpi upload
//   moves the staged package to the user's root index and can mirror
//   it to pypi.org at the same time. Stage, test, release to both, in
//   one command.

// proxying PyPI
$ devpi-server --outside-url https://pypi.org/simple/ &amp;
//   or run a separate instance
$ pip install requests --index-url http://localhost:3141/dev/+simple/ \
    --extra-index-url https://pypi.org/simple
//   the two-index form is the standard trick. The caveat: pip does not
//   check that the two agree on a version, so a compromised or
//   shadowed internal index can serve a package the public one would
//   have caught. Pin with hashes (chapter 5) if that matters.

// THE INDEXER, for legacy
$ devpi-genindexer debian-packages
//   builds a PEP 503 index from .deb files. There is no Ubuntu
//   equivalent, so dpkg packages are the hard case for Python
//   distribution, and this is the tool that solves it.

// THE COMPARISON
   Verdaccio  npm, the best proxy experience of the four
   devpi      PyPI, unique per-user staging, and PyPI mirroring
   Satis      Composer, static output, near-zero operational cost
//   all three are a single-process Node or Python app with no
//   database and no cluster. That is why they are common.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch9-artifactory" data-toc>Artifactory, Nexus and Harbor</h3><h4 id="ch9-artifactory-enterprise">Enterprise capabilities</h4><p>Artifactory is the most capable commercial registry, supporting every ecosystem in this guide: npm, PyPI, Maven, NuGet, Go, Cargo, Docker, and Helm. It offers replication between instances for multi-site setups and promotion between repositories (dev, staging, release), ensuring the artefact that passed in staging is the one that shipped. Real HA is built in, which self-hosted options do not give you. Cost is priced per user and is not cheap.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE COMMERCIAL AND ENTERPRISE OPTIONS

// ARTIFACTORY (JFrog). The most capable, and the most expensive.
//   - every ecosystem in this guide in one product: npm, PyPI, Maven,
//     NuGet, Go, Cargo, Docker, Helm
//   - a remote repository that caches an upstream, so setting it as
//     your only registry caches everything automatically
//   - replication between instances for multi-site setups
//   - PROMOTION between repositories: dev, staging, release, with a
//     rule that an artefact must be promoted rather than rebuilt. This
//     is the feature large organisations actually pay for, and it is
//     the correct answer to "the thing that passed in staging is not
//     the thing that shipped".
//   - real HA, which the self-hosted options do not give you
//   cost: priced per user, and not cheap.

// NEXUS REPOSITORY (Sonatype)
//   - a strong Maven and npm story, a Docker registry, raw repositories
//     for anything else
//   - blob stores: binaries stored by checksum and deduplicated
//   - an unusually good Go module proxy
//   - simpler and cheaper than Artifactory, smaller integration
//     ecosystem

// HARBOR (CNCF, Linux Foundation)
//   - a registry for CONTAINERS only, not a general package registry
//   - PROJECTS with RBAC, which is the model people actually want:
//     "platform/application" is a project with its own members,
//     retention policy and replicas
//   - replication rules, vulnerability scanning wired into the scan
//     step, and content trust
//   - an OPA policy layer for admission control
//   - the right answer for a container registry you run yourself

// THE CHOICE
   General registry, enterprise    Artifactory
   General, lighter, Maven-centric Nexus
   Containers only, real policy     Harbor
   One ecosystem, small team        Verdaccio / devpi / Satis
   Already in your cloud account    ECR / ACR / GAR / GCR

// THE MIGRATION COST, which nobody mentions
//   moving means every developer config, CI secret, Dockerfile and
//   deployment manifest changes. Budget a quarter at scale, and treat
//   the registry URL as an interface you do not churn.
//   the package STORAGE is not portable. A mirror is one direction.
//   Moving means a fresh publish to the new registry, which means new
//   version numbers, which means every consumer must update. Plan
//   for that before you start, not after.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch9-github" data-toc>GitHub Packages and packages-as-code</h3><h4 id="ch9-github-perms">Package permissions</h4><p>GitHub Packages permissions inherit from the repository. A package in a private repo is visible to whoever can read the repo. No separate IAM system, credential store, or rotation problem. Free for public packages with storage allowances on free tiers. One URL per ecosystem, configured per scope. The cost: GITHUB_TOKEN is a PAT with a long lifetime, so you are back to the credential problem. Use a fine-grained token scoped to read:packages and write:packages, with short expiry in CI.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// GITHUB PACKAGES is the cheapest private registry if you are on GitHub
//   - permissions INHERIT from the repository. A package in a private
//     repo is visible to whoever can read the repo. No IAM system, no
//     separate credential store, no rotation problem.
//   - free for public packages, with storage allowances on free tiers
//   - one URL per ecosystem, configured per scope

// .npmrc
   @yourorg:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com
   //npm.pkg.github.com/:_authToken=${GITHUB_TOKEN}
//   the token is the GITHUB_TOKEN you already have in CI

// ~/.m2/settings.xml
   &lt;server&gt;
     &lt;id&gt;github&lt;/id&gt;
     &lt;username&gt;yourorg&lt;/username&gt;
     &lt;password&gt;${env.GITHUB_TOKEN}&lt;/password&gt;
   &lt;/server&gt;
// and in the pom
   &lt;distributionManagement&gt;
     &lt;repository&gt;
       &lt;id&gt;github&lt;/id&gt;
       &lt;url&gt;https://maven.pkg.github.com/yourorg/repo&lt;/url&gt;
     &lt;/repository&gt;
   &lt;/distributionManagement&gt;

//   - the cost: GITHUB_TOKEN is a PAT with a long lifetime, so you are
//     back to the credential problem. Use a fine-grained token scoped
//     to read:packages and write:packages, with a short expiry, in CI.
//   - it does not PROXY, so it does not solve the offline case. For
//     that you need Verdaccio or Artifactory in front of it.

// GITLAB PACKAGES: the same model, the same trade-off.

// PACKAGES AS CODE, the newer and better answer
//   a manifest in the repository, from which the registries are
//   created. This turns "where do I publish to" into a pull request.

//   the shape, roughly
   packages:
     - name: shared-utils
       kind: library
       ecosystems: [npm, pypi]
       visibility: internal
       registries: [npm.pkg.github.com, pypi.example.com]
       owners: [team-platform]
//   a controller reconciles that manifest into registry configuration.

// WHY IT IS WORTH THE INVESTMENT
//   - registry configuration becomes REVIEWABLE. A hand-managed
//     Verdaccio access rule for 400 packages is not reviewable.
//   - access derives from CODEOWNERS, so permissions follow the people
//     who own the code.
//   - a package cannot exist without an owner, so orphans are
//     structurally impossible.
//   - a new registry or ecosystem is a change to one manifest schema,
//     not 400 manual edits.
//   the pattern is used at scale by Google, Microsoft and Netflix, and
//   open-source tooling exists. The cost is the controller: weeks, not
//   days, and it needs a platform team to own it.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch9-auth" data-toc>Authentication and authorisation</h3><h4 id="ch9-auth-layers">Three authentication layers</h4><p>Authentication (who is this?), authorisation (may they publish this package?), and provenance (was this artefact built from the source it claims?) are routinely conflated. Workload identity (no stored credential, e.g. ECR with EKS, GAR with GKE, ACR with AKS) is best. OIDC exchange (minutes-long, no stored secret) is next. Short-lived tokens (minutes to hours) are a sensible default. Long-lived PATs are simple but a permanent liability.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE THREE LAYERS, which are routinely conflated
   1. AUTHENTICATION: who is this? a token, a certificate, SSO
   2. AUTHORISATION: may they publish this package? read? unpublish?
   3. PROVENANCE: was this artefact built from the source it claims?

// TOKEN SHAPES, best to worst
   workload identity   no stored credential at all, in Kubernetes.
                       ECR with EKS, GAR with GKE, ACR with AKS.
   OIDC exchange       minutes-long, no stored secret. Needs registry
                       support. Verdaccio does NOT do this; Artifactory
                       and the cloud registries do.
   short-lived token   minutes to hours. A sensible default.
   long-lived PAT      simple, and a permanent liability.

// PER-PACKAGE PERMISSIONS, and why the coarse model is dangerous
   "authenticated users can publish to @yourorg/*" means any engineer's
   CI token can publish ANY internal package. That is how a typo in a
   package name publishes something, and how a compromised repository
   publishes a backdoored internal library that every other service
   then installs automatically.

//   what you want
     publish    only the CI for that specific repository
     read       everyone authenticated, or a team
     unpublish  platform team only
     admin      nobody but the platform team
//   express it as CODEOWNERS or as a manifest (ch9-github). In
//   Verdaccio this means one package pattern per access rule, which is
//   exactly how a 400-package registry becomes unmaintainable.

// THE AUDIT LOG, which is the reason to run your own
   who published which version of which package, when, and from which
   CI run. Commercial registries have this. Verdaccio and devpi have
   basic HTTP logs. Satis has none, because it is static files.
//   this log is what you read during an incident, and it is the
//   difference between "we think it was the token in that repository"
//   and having no answer at all.

// TOKEN HYGIENE, in order of importance
   1. One token per system, never shared. A shared token means you
//      cannot attribute a publish.
   2. Least scope per token. A build that only needs to read cannot
//      publish.
   3. No long-lived token on a developer machine. Use the SSO login
//      flow, which issues a session.
   4. Rotate on departure, automatically. A leaver holding a publish
//      token is the most common internal-registry incident.
   5. Audit the token list. Most registries will show you every token
//      that exists, and most organisations have never looked.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch9-proxy" data-toc>Caching and proxying public registries</h3><h4 id="ch9-proxy-modes">Three proxy modes</h4><p>Transparent (configure proxy as registry and fetch on demand), Mirror (scheduled job copies entire index and artefacts), Mirror-with-fallback (mirror but fall back to upstream for anything not mirrored, the pragmatic default for large org). Metadata should have short cache lifetime (hours, not days). Artefacts never expire once cached. The proxy is a chokepoint that can serve a compromised package to every build - mirror verification is worth the effort.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE PROBLEM A PROXY SOLVES
   - registry.npmjs.org goes down, or is slow from your region, and
//     every build in the company fails
//   - a compliance rule forbids outbound network calls from the build
//   - you need to know exactly which versions were available at a
//     point in time, for an audit
//   - you need to scan every dependency you install, centrally

// THE THREE MODES
   1. TRANSPARENT. Configure the proxy as the registry and let it
//      fetch on demand. Nothing else changes.
//   2. MIRROR. A scheduled job copies the entire index and every
//      artefact. Complete, deterministic, and expensive in storage.
//   3. MIRROR-WITH-FALLBACK. Mirror, but fall back to upstream for
//      anything not mirrored. The pragmatic default for a large org.

// IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
   - metadata should have a short cache lifetime. A day-long metadata
     cache means a yank or a security removal takes a day to propagate.
     Hours, not days. Verdaccio's maxage, or your proxy's equivalent.
   - artefacts never expire. Once cached, keep them, so a build that
     worked last month still works.
//   - a mirror must be a SUBSET, and the subset must be defined. A
//     full mirror of npm is tens of terabytes. Mirror what your
//     lockfiles reference, refreshed nightly from every repository.
//     that is bounded, and it is a good fit for a compliance need.
//   - the proxy is now a chokepoint. It can serve a compromised
//     package to every build. Mirror verification (checking the
//     upstream signature or hash on every fetch) is worth the effort.

// THE AIR-GAPPED CASE, which is the hard one
   - a real air gap means no proxy: the artefacts must be moved in.
//   - the process that works: build the mirror on a connected host,
//     copy it physically or over a one-way link, serve it inside
//   - verify integrity at the boundary: sign the mirror index and
//     verify it on import
//   - this is a genuinely different discipline, and it is why Nix and
//     Debian are used in defence and aerospace: the binary cache IS
//     the supply chain, and it is built for this.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch9-migrate" data-toc>Migrating away from a private registry</h3><h4 id="ch9-migrate-plan">Migration plan</h4><p>Everyone ends up here, and it is a quarter of work that is entirely avoidable if you plan for it. Stand up the new registry and mirror everything. Do not tell anyone to use it yet. Re-publish the SAME versions to the new registry, not new ones. Bumping versions during migration turns a configuration change into a dependency-resolution change. Run both registries in parallel for a release cycle. Migrate consumers in batches with the old registry still available. Only then make the old registry read-only after measuring that nothing is pulling from it. Decommission last, and keep the backup for a year.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WHY IT IS HARD
   - every consumer's config changes: ~/.npmrc, ~/.pypirc, .m2, the
//     Cargo config, the Go env, the Docker daemon config
   - every CI secret is a registry-specific token that must be replaced
//   - every Dockerfile has a FROM or a COPY from the old registry
//   - every deployment manifest and every Kubernetes image reference
//   - the lockfiles: an entry pointing at the old registry does not
//     resolve from the new one, and a lockfile edit is a real change
//   - and the versions must be re-published, so everything gets a new
//     version number, so every consumer must update its constraint

// THE ORDER THAT MINIMISES DOWNTIME
   1. STAND UP the new registry and mirror everything. Do not tell
      anyone to use it yet.
   2. RE-PUBLISH. Publish the SAME versions to the new registry, not
      new ones. Bumping versions during a migration is what turns a
      configuration change into a dependency-resolution change.
      Run both registries in parallel for a release cycle.
   3. MIGRATE CONSUMERS in batches, with the old registry still
      available. A dual-publish period, not a cutover.
   4. ONLY THEN make the old registry read-only, and only after you
      have measured that nothing is pulling from it. A registry log is
      the only way to know.
   5. Decommission last, and keep the backup for a year.

// THE THING THAT MAKES IT EASIER, AND MOST TEAMS DO NOT DO IT
   - use a repository-scoped or org-scoped alias, not a bare hostname:
//       npm.pkg.example.com
//       pypi.example.com
//     so the registry is a single string in a few hundred config
//     files rather than scattered across many. Then the migration is a
//     DNS change plus a find-and-replace.
//   - and publish a machine-readable registry inventory, so you know
//     the blast radius before you start rather than during.</code></pre></div>
    <div class="ex"><h4>Exercises 9</h4><ol>
      <li>Run Verdaccio locally in proxy mode with <code>cache: true</code>, install a package, kill the network, and confirm the cached install still works. Then determine which packages are NOT cached and would fail.</li>
      <li>Set up a devpi user index, stage a package, verify nobody else can see it, then <code>devpi upload</code> it. Record the version numbers before and after to see the release step in action.</li>
      <li>Build a Satis repository from three of your own git repositories with tagged versions, and then run a real <code>composer install</code> from a clean project against it. Note what Satis does not verify.</li>
      <li>Audit every package registry credential your organisation holds, and produce a list with the owner, the last-use date and the scope. Cross-check the list against your HR records for leavers.</li>
      <li>Compare a monorepo-with-workspaces against a private registry for a small internal package, measuring setup time, CI time, and the number of services to operate. Argue from the numbers, not the principle.</li>
    </ol></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
<h2 id="ch10" data-toc>10 Â· Distribution Beyond a Registry</h2>
    <div class="ch-toc"><button type="button" class="ch-toc-head" aria-expanded="true"><span class="arrow">â–¸</span>Contents &middot; 10 Â· Distribution Beyond a Registry</button><div class="ch-toc-list"><a href="#ch10-spectrum">The distribution spectrum</a><a href="#ch10-ghreleases">GitHub Releases</a><a href="#ch10-direct">Direct download from your own site</a><a href="#ch10-selfupdate">Self-updating clients</a><a href="#ch10-bins">Distributing binaries and executables</a><a href="#ch10-os">Deb, rpm and system packages</a><a href="#ch10-monorepo">Publishing from a monorepo</a><a href="#ch10-multi">Publishing to several places at once</a><a href="#ch10-oss">Open source and commercial licensing models</a><a href="#ch10-checklist">The release checklist</a></div></div>
    <p class="topic-desc">A registry is one distribution channel, and it is not always the right one. This chapter covers the alternatives â€” release assets, direct download, self-updating binaries, system packages â€” and the decision that determines which one you need.</p>
    <h3 id="ch10-spectrum" data-toc>The distribution spectrum</h3><h4 id="ch10-spectrum-control">Your own object storage</h4><p>You control the bytes, versions, retention, and auth. Maximum work, maximum control. This is the most resource-intensive channel, but gives you complete authority over your artefacts.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// FROM MOST CONTROLLED TO LEAST
   your own object storage   you control bytes, versions, retention,
                            auth. maximum work, maximum control
   a self-hosted registry    Verdaccio, Satis, Artifactory. ch9
   a git tag plus a submodule exact version, no service, awkward UX
   GitHub/GitLab Releases    free, versioned, discoverable, and a URL
                            people already know
   a public registry         one command to install, discovery,
                            dependency resolution. least control
   your own website          maximum flexibility, minimum trust. nobody
                            knows how to install from it

// THE DECISION QUESTION, and it is one question
   "Does this need to be RESOLVED as a dependency?"
   yes -> a registry. dependency graphs, ranges, conflict resolution
          and lockfiles all assume a registry-shaped interface.
   no  -> release assets or direct download. A CLI tool, an
          application binary, a design system, a font, a database
          dump, a WordPress plugin. None of these are libraries.

// THE HONEST ARGUMENT FOR A REGISTRY ANYWAY
   Even a self-contained application benefits from a registry if it
   HAS dependencies, because your user needs those dependencies too.
//   A Node CLI with 30 dependencies is unusable as a single binary
//   unless you bundle it, and bundling puts you in the business of
//   shipping your user's dependency tree, which is a supply-chain
//   decision you may not want to make.

// THE ARGUMENT AGAINST
   A registry imposes a namespace you cannot leave, an account you
   must maintain, a review process for some ecosystems, and a
   resolution model that can silently pick a version you did not test.
//   For a self-contained artefact, all of that is overhead.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch10-ghreleases" data-toc>GitHub Releases</h3><h4 id="ch10-ghreleases-publish">Publish a release</h4><p>$ gh release create v1.4.2 --title "v1.4.2" --generate-notes dist/mytool_1.4.2_linux_amd64.tar.gz ... checksums.txt dist/mytool_1.4.2_checksums.txt.sig Three things that make it work: 1. TAG-BASED: a release is attached to a tag, and a tag is immutable by convention. 2. ASSETS ARE CONTENT-ADDRESSED: served via a CDN. 3. THE RELEASE PAGE is a changelog.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// GitHub Releases is the default for binaries<h4 id="ch10-ghreleases-assets">Three things that make it work</h4><p>1. TAG-BASED: a release is attached to a tag, and a tag is immutable by convention. The URL is stable forever: https://github.com/yourorg/mytool/releases/download/v1.4.2/FILE. 2. ASSETS ARE CONTENT-ADDRESSED: served via a CDN. No bandwidth cost, no availability problem. 3. THE RELEASE PAGE is a changelog. --generate-notes reads the merged pull requests since the last release, which is a good changelog with no effort.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// GitHub Releases is the default for binaries
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// GitHub Releases is the default for binaries, and it is better than
// most people realise: immutable assets, a stable URL, CDN-served,
// and a release page people already know how to find.

$ gh release create v1.4.2 \
    --title "v1.4.2" \
    --generate-notes \
    dist/mytool_1.4.2_linux_amd64.tar.gz \
    dist/mytool_1.4.2_linux_arm64.tar.gz \
    dist/mytool_1.4.2_darwin_amd64.tar.gz \
    dist/mytool_1.4.2_darwin_arm64.tar.gz \
    dist/mytool_1.4.2_windows_amd64.zip \
    checksums.txt \
    dist/mytool_1.4.2_checksums.txt.sig

// THREE THINGS THAT MAKE IT WORK
   1. TAG-BASED. a release is attached to a tag, and a tag is
//      immutable by convention. the URL is stable forever:
//        https://github.com/yourorg/mytool/releases/download/v1.4.2/FILE
   2. ASSETS ARE CONTENT-ADDRESSED by the platform, and served via a
      CDN. No bandwidth cost, no availability problem.
   3. the release page is a changelog. --generate-notes reads the
      merged pull requests since the last release, which is a good
//      changelog with no effort.

// CHECKSUMS, and they are not optional for binaries
   $ sha256sum dist/* &gt; checksums.txt
   $ gpg --detach-sign --armor checksums.txt
//   users verify:
//      $ sha256sum -c checksums.txt --ignore-missing
//      $ gpg --verify checksums.txt.sig
//   this is what makes a downloaded binary trustworthy, and it is a
//   five-line addition that most projects skip.

// THE LIMITS, honestly
   - no dependency resolution. A Node or Python project needs a
     registry; you cannot distribute dependencies through Releases.
   - GitHub is a single vendor. If it is blocked in your region, or
     your corporate policy bans it, the channel dies.
   - a deleted release is a broken link for everyone. Mark a bad
     release as a prerelease or delete the tag, but know that people
     have already downloaded it.
//   - there is no deprecation mechanism. You can edit the notes.

// THE GIT-LAB EQUIVALENT and the same shape
   $ glab release create v1.4.2 --name "v1.4.2" --notes CHANGELOG.md \
       dist/*.tar.gz checksums.txt</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch10-direct" data-toc>Direct download from your own site</h3><h4 id="ch10-direct-latest">Latest.json</h4><p>A machine-readable index with version, released date, notes, and platform URLs. Three URLs: an immutable versioned one, a mutable convenience pointer, and a machine-readable index.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE PATTERN, and why it still works<h4 id="ch10-direct-pattern">The pattern, and why it still works</h4><p>Three URLs: an immutable versioned one, a mutable convenience pointer, and a machine-readable index. Latest.json is the part that matters most. A client can discover the current version, verify it, and install it with one HTTP request. This is the contract; everything else is an implementation of it. ripgrep, kubectl, helm, terraform and rustup all publish a file of exactly this shape.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE PATTERN, and why it still works
    <p>Occasionally the only option, and it is worth doing well because it is what several large projects use for their flagship tools.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE PATTERN, and why it still works
   https://example.com/downloads/mytool-1.4.2-linux-amd64.tar.gz
   https://example.com/downloads/mytool-1.4.2-linux-amd64.tar.gz.sha256
   https://example.com/downloads/latest/mytool-linux-amd64.tar.gz
   https://example.com/downloads/latest.json
//   three URLs: an immutable versioned one, a mutable convenience
//   pointer, and a machine-readable index.

// LATEST.JSON, the part that matters most
{
  "version": "1.4.2",
  "released": "2026-03-14T09:31:22Z",
  "notes": "https://github.com/yourorg/mytool/releases/tag/v1.4.2",
  "platforms": {
    "linux-amd64": {
      "url": "https://example.com/dl/mytool-1.4.2-linux-amd64.tar.gz",
      "sha256": "a3f5c9...",
      "size": 18432000
    },
    "darwin-arm64": {
      "url": "https://example.com/dl/mytool-1.4.2-darwin-arm64.tar.gz",
      "sha256": "b7e2d1...",
      "size": 17920000
    }
  }
}
//   a client can discover the current version, verify it, and install
//   it with one HTTP request. This is the contract; everything else is
//   an implementation of it. ripgrep, kubectl, helm, terraform and
//   rustup all publish a file of exactly this shape.

// SERVING IT PROPERLY
   - object storage (S3, GCS, R2, Cloudflare R2) behind a CDN. Not a
//     web server: a 200 MB tarball is not a dynamic resource.
   - Content-Length and ETag set correctly, and Cache-Control on the
//     versioned URLs set to a year with immutable.
//   - the mutable "latest" path gets a very short TTL, or no caching.
//   - CORS headers if a browser will fetch it, e.g. for a WASM
//     artefact or a web installer.
//   - a Range-capable origin, so a large download can resume.

// THE PART EVERYONE GETS WRONG: verifying without a trust anchor
   A checksum served from the same host as the binary proves nothing.
//   An attacker who can replace the binary can replace the checksum.
//   Options, in increasing order of strength:
//     checksum file signed by a key whose fingerprint is printed in
//       your documentation and shipped in your other channels
     signature from your release signing key, published in the repo
//     the transparency log from chapter 3
//   "Serve it over HTTPS" is not an answer. TLS protects the channel,
//   not the artefact, and it protects nothing against a compromised
//   origin.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch10-selfupdate" data-toc>Self-updating clients</h3><h4 id="ch10-selfupdate-wrapper">Wrapper-based</h4><p>A tiny separate binary that only replaces itself. Used by rustup's toolchain manager, flyctl, kubectl (partly), and many Go CLIs. THE ALGORITHM: 1. fetch latest.json over HTTPS, 2. if the version is not newer, stop, 3. download the asset for this OS and architecture, 4. verify the signature or checksum against a PINNED public key, 5. write to a temp file in the same directory, 6. set mode to 0755, 7. rename over the running executable.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WHY CLIENTS DO IT<h4 id="ch10-selfupdate-notify">Notify only</h4><p>The safest approach, and the one most tools should use. Print a message, keep running the installed version. No code is fetched, no code is run, nothing to compromise. and it can be disabled: MYTOOL_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WHY CLIENTS DO IT
    <p>A CLI that updates itself is a distribution decision with a security consequence, and it is worth thinking about before you write the code rather than after.</p>
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WHY CLIENTS DO IT
   a released binary is a version you cannot change. Every user is on
   it until they update. For a CLI that talks to your API, a stale
   client is a support burden and sometimes an outage.

// THE FOUR APPROACHES

// A. NOTIFY ONLY. The safest, and the one most tools should use.
   //   print a message, keep running the installed version
   if remote.version != current.version {
     log.Warn(`mytool ${remote.version} is available (you have ${current.version})`);
     log.Warn(`see ${remote.notes}`);
   }
//   no code is fetched, no code is run, nothing to compromise.
//   and it can be disabled: MYTOOL_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1

// B. WRAPPER-BASED. A tiny separate binary that only replaces itself.
   brew upgrade mytool     apt install --only-upgrade mytool
   nix profile upgrade     scoop update mytool
//   the package manager owns the whole problem: signing, rollback,
//   verification, and the decision not to upgrade. This is the correct
//   answer on any platform with a package manager, and it is free.

// C. IN-PROCESS SELF-UPDATE. The binary replaces itself.
   //   used by rustup's toolchain manager, flyctl, kubectl (partly),
   //   and many Go CLIs. It is popular because it works everywhere,
//   including a machine with no package manager.
   //
   //   THE ALGORITHM, and every step exists for a reason
   //   1. fetch latest.json over HTTPS
   //   2. if the version is not newer, stop. compare semver, not
   //      string equality, or "1.10.0" sorts before "1.9.0"
   //   3. download the asset for this OS and architecture
   //   4. verify the signature or checksum against a PINNED public key
   //      compiled into the binary, not fetched
   //   5. write it to a temp file in the same directory as the current
   //      executable, so the rename is atomic on one filesystem
   //   6. set the mode to 0755
   //   7. rename over the running executable. On Linux and macOS this
   //      works: the running inode is unaffected. On Windows it does
   //      not, and you need a helper.
   //   8. on failure, leave the original untouched and say so

func SelfUpdate(ctx context.Context, current string) error {
  idx, err := fetchLatest(ctx)
  if err != nil { return err }
  if !semver.GreaterThan(idx.Version, current) { return nil }

  asset, ok := idx.Platforms[platformKey()]     // os + GOARCH + GOOS
  if !ok { return fmt.Errorf("no build for %s", platformKey()) }

  f, err := download(ctx, asset.URL)
  if err != nil { return err }
  if err := verify(f, asset.SHA256, pinnedPublicKey()); err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("signature invalid, not updating: %w", err)
  }

  exe, err := os.Executable()
  if err != nil { return err }
  tmp := filepath.Join(filepath.Dir(exe), "."+filepath.Base(exe)+".new")
  if err := writeFileMode(tmp, f, 0o755); err != nil { return err }
  if err := os.Rename(tmp, exe); err != nil {   // atomic on one fs
    os.Remove(tmp)
    return fmt.Errorf("could not replace %s: %w", exe, err)
  }
  return fmt.Errorf("updated to %s - restart to use it", idx.Version)
}

// THE SECURITY PROPERTIES YOU MUST KEEP
   - the verification key is COMPILED IN. A fetched key means an
//     attacker who controls the index controls the key.
//   - verification happens BEFORE anything is written, and a failure
//     leaves the original binary untouched.
//   - the new binary is downloaded to a temp path in the same
//     directory, not to /tmp and not to the user's home. A cross-
//     filesystem rename is not atomic and can corrupt the binary.
//   - if you write to a directory the user does not own (a system
//     install under /usr/local/bin), you need elevation, and you must
//     not attempt a silent sudo. Print the command and exit.
//   - the update is opt-out and the opt-out is documented.

// WHAT GOES WRONG IN PRACTICE
   - no rollback. A self-update that lands a bad version has stranded
//     every user on it. Keep the previous binary and support
//     `mytool self-update --rollback`, or publish a fixed release
//     fast enough that it does not matter.
//   - update storms. Every client checking every hour is a thundering
//     herd on your download server. Use a conditional request with
//     ETag or Last-Modified, so the common case is a 304.
//   - the update breaks the user's config. A behaviour change in a
//     patch release that a self-updater installs without asking is a
//     data-loss bug. Minor and major updates should be announced, not
//     applied silently.
//   - it does not work on Windows without care, and on Linux it fails
//     for a system-wide install the user cannot write to.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch10-bins" data-toc>Distributing binaries and executables</h3><h4 id="ch10-bins-archive">Archive formats</h4><p>.tar.gz the Unix default, .zip required on Windows, .tar.zst better compression. Naming contract with your users' scripts.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE MATRIX YOU HAVE TO COVER<h4 id="ch10-bins-matrix">The matrix you have to cover</h4><p>Aim for four: linux-amd64, linux-arm64, darwin-arm64, windows-amd64. A macOS build must also be signed and notarised, which is a real cost and requirement. GO, which is the easiest case.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE MATRIX YOU HAVE TO COVER
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE MATRIX YOU HAVE TO COVER. Aim for four: linux-amd64,
// linux-arm64, darwin-arm64, windows-amd64. macOS intel is nearly gone.
// A macOS build must also be signed and notarised, which is a real
// cost and a real requirement.

// GO, which is the easiest case
$ CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 go build -trimpath \
    -ldflags "-s -w -X main.version=1.4.2 -X main.commit=$(git rev-parse HEAD)" \
    -o dist/mytool_linux_amd64 ./cmd/mytool
//   CGO_ENABLED=0 gives a static binary. -trimpath removes the build
//   path. The -X flags inject version info the binary can print.
//   a Go release build should be a five-line script and it works.

// RUST
$ cargo build --release --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
$ cargo build --release --target aarch64-apple-darwin
//   use cross or cargo-zigbuild for cross-compilation from one host.
//   strip the binary unless you are shipping debug symbols:
//     strip = "symbols" in the release profile

// NODE, and this is where it gets uncomfortable
$ npx @electron/packager . --platform=linux --arch=x64
$ npx pkg --targets node20-linux-x64
$ bun build --compile --outfile mytool ./src/cli.ts
//   three tools that each solve it differently. pkg is unmaintained;
//   bun's --compile is the newest and smallest. All of them embed your
//   dependency tree, which means you now own those dependencies'
//   licences and their CVEs. That is the real cost of a bundled
//   binary, and it is not always acknowledged.

// ARCHIVE FORMATS
   .tar.gz   the Unix default. Universal, no dependency
   .zip      required on Windows. Do not make Linux users install unzip
   .tar.zst  better compression, faster. needs recent tar; provide
             .tar.gz as well

// NAMING, and it is a contract with your users' scripts
   mytool-1.4.2-linux-amd64.tar.gz       the clearest
   mytool_1.4.2_linux_amd64.tar.gz       what GoReleaser and Goreleaser
                                         use, and what Homebrew's test
                                         convention expects
//   if you publish to Homebrew, use the underscore form: it is what
//   every Go tool does and the formula looks conventional.

// LICENSE FILES IN EVERY ARCHIVE
   Not "the repo has one". An archive extracted in a temp directory
   has no repository. A licence file inside the archive is what makes
   the distribution compliant, and it is routinely missing.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch10-os" data-toc>Deb, rpm and system packages</h3><h4 id="ch10-os-rpm">RPM</h4><p>mytool.spec, Name, Version, Release, Summary, License, URL, Source0, BuildArch, %install, %files, %changelog, rpm --addsign, rpm --checksig.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// DEBIAN/UBUNTU<h4 id="ch10-os-debian">Debian/Ubuntu</h4><p>debian/ control, changelog, copyright, mytool.install, source/format. $ dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc produces .deb. ARCHITECTURE is per-build, not "all". You build one .deb per architecture: amd64, arm64, armhf.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// DEBIAN/UBUNTU
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WHEN YOU NEED THIS
   - corporate Linux desktops where a self-updating binary is blocked
     by policy, and the IT team will only install signed packages
   - a server fleet where you want one mechanism for everything
   - a system-wide install that a non-root user cannot write to

// DEBIAN/UBUNTU
   debian/
     control              metadata: Package, Version, Architecture,
                          Maintainer, Depends, Description, Homepage,
                          Section, Priority
     changelog            required, in the debian/changelog format.
                          `dch` generates it
     copyright           the licence, in machine-readable DEP-5 form
     mytool.install       install paths
     source/format        "3.0 (quilt)" or "3.0 (native)"
   $ dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc      # produces .deb
   $ debuild -S -us -uc            # source package
//   ARCHITECTURE is per-build, not "all". You build one .deb per
//   architecture: amd64, arm64, armhf.
//   the .deb is a container: DEBIAN/control plus a data tarball. It
//   is not hard, and it is a small amount of metadata.

// REPOSITORY, and signing it
   export GPG_TTY=$(tty)
   gpg --batch --yes --passphrase-file ~/.keys/deb.pass \
       --armor --detach-sign -o dist/Release.gpg dist/Release
   gpg --batch --yes --passphrase-file ~/.keys/deb.pass \
       --clearsign -o dist/InRelease dist/Release
//   apt refuses unsigned repositories. InRelease (inline-signed) is
//   what modern apt wants; Release.gpg (detached) is the fallback.
//   apt-key is deprecated and removed. Do not use it.

// RPM, which is a similar shape with a different grammar
   mytool.spec
   Name:           mytool
   Version:        1.4.2
   Release:        1%{?dist}
   Summary:        A tool that does one thing well
   License:        MIT
   URL:            https://example.com
   Source0:        %{name}-%{version}.tar.gz
   BuildArch:      x86_64
   %install
   install -Dm755 mytool %{buildroot}%{_bindir}/mytool
   install -Dm644 LICENSE %{buildroot}%{_datadir}/licenses/%{name}/LICENSE
   %files
   %{_bindir}/mytool
   %{_datadir}/licenses/%{name}/LICENSE
   %changelog
   * Mon Mar 14 2026 Your Name &lt;you@example.com&gt; - 1.4.2-1
   - Release 1.4.2
//   %config for /etc files, %defattr for permissions, and a %check
//   section. RPM is stricter than it looks: a %files entry for a file
//   the build did not create is an error.
//   rpm --addsign, then rpm --checksig.

// ALTERNATIVES THAT AVOID THE PACKAGING WORK
   Nix / nixpkgs    declarative, reproducible, one expression for
                    every platform. steep learning curve, but the
                    community nixpkgs is enormous and a PR gets
                    reviewed seriously.
   Snap             auto-updates, and the store handles signing. but
                    it sandboxes your binary, which breaks a lot of
                    tools, and the confinement is a real constraint.
   Flatpak          the desktop case. very confined.
   nix-portable     a single self-contained binary with no install.
                    an underrated answer for "just run this".</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch10-monorepo" data-toc>Publishing from a monorepo</h3><h4 id="ch10-mono-changelogs">Per-package changelogs</h4><p>A monorepo changelog is useless. Generate per-package changelogs from the changesets, and keep a root CHANGELOG that only records changes to the public workspace API.</p><h4 id="ch10-mono-version-prop">Version propagation rules</h4><p>If @yourorg/plugin requires @yourorg/core ^1.2.0 and core goes to 2.0.0, the plugin must be released too, and its new version must widen its range. Write the rule down because exception cases accumulate.</p><h4 id="ch10-mono-changesets">Changeset flow for independent releases</h4><p>A changeset is a markdown file describing one change to one package. Tools like turbo or nx generate a release PR from accumulated changesets, computing versions from bump types. This is the most common modern answer to the monorepo release problem.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE PROBLEM
   40 packages in one repository. You changed one file in one of
//   them. What version numbers move, and what gets published?

// WHAT A CORRECT TOOL DOES, and the reasoning matters
   1. Which packages' files changed?
        changed:  packages/core/src/a.ts
                 packages/core/package.json
   2. Which packages DEPEND on those, directly or transitively?
        core is depended on by plugin-a, which is depended on by app-x
   3. For each, decide the bump:
        the changed package        -> the bump the changeset said
        a dependent with a range that still covers the new version
                                 -> no bump, no publish
        a dependent whose range does NOT cover it
                                 -> bump the minimum and publish
        a dependent in another release train -> leave it alone
   4. Apply the changesets, regenerate the changelog, commit, tag,
//      publish.
//   Step 3 is the one hand-rolled scripts get wrong, and getting it
//   wrong means either a broken publish or a publish nobody needed.

// THE TOOLS, and what each does well
   changesets    the model: a markdown file per change, a generated
                 release PR with the versions and changelog that a
                 human merges. npm and yarn workspaces.
   nx affected   a dependency graph from the project graph, not the
                 package manifests. better when builds have outputs
                 and caching matters.
   turborepo     same idea, built for a task graph and remote caching.
   lerna         the oldest. `--since` and `--force-publish` and the
                 fixed/locked mode distinction, which confuses people
//   lageis        Go, by module. correct MVS handling.
//   cargo-release Rust, one crate at a time.
//   go-modules    Go, and it is the easiest case: tag, push, done.

// THE RELEASE PR, which is the whole point
   packages/changesets/
     brave-lions-jump.md
//   ---
//   "@yourorg/core": minor
//   "@yourorg/plugin": patch
//   ---
//   fix: handle a null token in refresh()
//
//   The CI opens a PR. It contains:
//     - the version bumps in every package.json
//     - the lockfile update
//     - CHANGELOG.md entries, one per package
//     - the new versions, so they can be reverted in one revert
//   A human reads the changelog and merges. Then a second workflow
//   publishes everything, tags, and drafts release notes.
//   The value is not automation. It is that the version bump is a
//   reviewed artefact and a release is one revert.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch10-multi" data-toc>Publishing to several places at once</h3><h4 id="ch10-multi-retry">Retry safely</h4><p>most registries reject a re-publish of the same version, so a retry after a partial failure needs to know what already landed: for r in registries; do if ! published_in "$r" "$VERSION"; then publish_to "$r"; fi; done.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// RETRY-SAFELY<h4 id="ch10-multi-strategy">The strategy: publish to a private registry first</h4><p>Publish to Artifactory or an internal Verdaccio, which is idempotent and re-runnable. Run your install smoke tests against THAT. Then promote to the public registries. the private registry is your staging area, it is re-writable, and a failure at step 3 means nothing public was touched.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WHEN IT GETS MESSY
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// DO YOU NEED TO?
//   Common case, and it is fine: publish the package to npm AND the
//   source to a registry that wants source (JSR, Pub). Or publish to
//   npm and to an internal proxy that caches npm.
//   Also fine and very common: a source repository plus GitHub
//   Releases for the binaries, plus Homebrew and a .deb.
//   That is three channels and each has a distinct purpose. It is not
//   over-engineering; it is how a CLI tool gets installed.

// WHEN IT GETS MESSY
   You are publishing to five registries with five tokens, and a
   release fails on the third. Now you have 1.4.2 on two registries
   and 1.4.1 on the rest, and consumers see inconsistent resolution.
//   This is the real cost of multi-channel publishing and it needs a
//   strategy.

// THE STRATEGY: PUBLISH TO A PRIVATE REGISTRY FIRST
   publish to Artifactory or an internal Verdaccio, which is
   idempotent and re-runnable
   run your install smoke tests against THAT
   then promote to the public registries
//   the private registry is your staging area, it is re-writable, and
//   a failure at step 3 means nothing public was touched. Artifactory's
//   promotion feature does this natively. This is the strongest
//   argument for a private registry and it is not about availability.

// RETRY-SAFELY
   most registries reject a re-publish of the same version, so a
   retry after a partial failure needs to know what already landed:
//     for r in registries; do
//       if ! published_in "$r" "$VERSION"; then publish_to "$r"; fi
//     done
//   query each registry for the version's existence before publishing.
//   npm view, pip index versions, cargo search --limit, curl the
//   registry's metadata endpoint. It is a few lines and it turns a
//   partial failure from a manual incident into a no-op.

// THE ORDER, by ecosystem risk
   internal/private first, so a test runs against what you will ship
   least-critical public registry next, and it is the fastest to query
   the slowest and most durable (Docker Hub, Maven Central) last
//   GitHub Releases last of all, because it is the most visible and
//   the hardest to un-publish</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch10-oss" data-toc>Open source and commercial licensing models</h3><h4 id="ch10-oss-decision">Decision factors</h4><p>do you need contributors? -> A, B or C. is adoption the business model? -> C, or A with a generous tier. is support the product? -> D, and price for the support. will you need enterprise sales? -> D.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE FOUR MODELS THAT WORK, and the trade-off each makes<h4 id="ch10-oss-models">The four models that work</h4><p>A. OPEN CORE: the free version is genuinely useful; the paid adds a capability, not a restriction. B. SOURCE AVAILABLE: you can read the source, not necessarily use it commercially. C. FREE TIER, PAID CLOUD: the code is open; the managed service is the product. D. FULLY COMMERCIAL: not published as source at all, or published as a compiled artefact with a proprietary licence.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE FOUR MODELS THAT WORK, and the trade-off each makes
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE FOUR MODELS THAT WORK, and the trade-off each makes

// A. OPEN CORE. The free version is genuinely useful; the paid adds a
//    capability, not a restriction.
//    Sentry, Grafana, GitLab, HashiCorp historically.
//    + no artificial limits in the free tier, so adoption is not
//      limited by the paywall being annoying
//    + easy to explain: "the thing you need for production is paid"
//    - the boundary must be defensible. A feature a small user can
//      live without is a feature they will keep using.

// B. SOURCE AVAILABLE. You can read the source, not necessarily use it
//    commercially. AGPL, BUSL, SSPL, Elastic's licence, BSL.
//    + contributors can audit it, which builds trust
//    - AGPL is viral in a way that scares companies, and that fear is
//      rational: it does impose obligations on network services
//    - BSL has a date after which it converts to an open licence, which
//      is a promise you must keep
//    - read the actual licence. "Source available" is not a licence.

// C. FREE TIER, PAID CLOUD. The code is open; the managed service is
//    the product.
//    GitHub, Vercel, Netlify, Sentry, Stripe's tooling.
//    + no licence friction at all; anyone can run it
//    + the revenue is not from the licence, so you can be generous
//    - you need the self-hosted version to be genuinely usable, or
//      users will resent you, and that resentment is public
//    - the business depends on hosted conversion, which is a different
//      skill from selling software

// D. FULLY COMMERCIAL. Not published as source at all, or published
//    as a compiled artefact with a proprietary licence.
//    + no ambiguity
//    - nobody can audit it, and security people will say so
//    - enterprise procurement will want a SBOM, a pentest report and
//      an indemnity, which is real work
//    - a proprietary licence is not GPL-compatible, so it cannot be
//      used with GPL libraries, which is a real interoperability limit

// THE DECISION FACTORS, concretely
   do you need contributors?          -> A, B or C. D has no community.
   is adoption the business model?   -> C, or A with a generous tier
   is support the product?           -> D, and price for the support
   will you need enterprise sales?   -> D, and you will need the
                                      procurement paperwork regardless
   are you in a regulated industry?  -> B or C, and document your
                                      SBOM story

// THE ONE MISTAKE THAT IS HARD TO REVERSE
   relicensing. If you published under MIT or Apache-2.0, every
   recipient holds a perpetual licence and you cannot revoke it or
   relicense. Adding a CLA does not change this for past
   contributions. Dual-licensing later is possible but complex.
//   If you might change your mind, publish under a licence with an
//   explicit conversion path (BSL, or MPL-2.0 for a file-level copyleft
   that leaves the option open), or do not publish at all.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch10-checklist" data-toc>The release checklist</h3><h4 id="ch10-checklist-after">After</h4><p>[ ] install the published version from a clean cache. [ ] tag the repository release and generate notes. [ ] update the docs: the version, the install command, the changelog. [ ] tell the people who asked for it. A release nobody hears about does not get adopted.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// AFTER<h4 id="ch10-checklist-before">Before you publish</h4><p>[ ] tests pass on a clean checkout with a clean dependency tree. [ ] the version is correct everywhere: package manifest, code constant, changelog, and the tag. [ ] the changelog says what changed and what breaks. [ ] the artefact file list is what you intend, inspected not assumed. [ ] the artefact size is within budget. [ ] licence file present, third-party attribution present. [ ] no secrets, no .env, no credentials, no debug endpoints. [ ] no node_modules, no test fixtures, no editor directories.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// BEFORE YOU PUBLISH
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// BEFORE YOU PUBLISH
[ ] tests pass on a clean checkout with a clean dependency tree
[ ] the version is correct everywhere: package manifest, code constant,
    changelog, and the tag
[ ] the changelog says what changed and what breaks
[ ] the artefact file list is what you intend, inspected not assumed
[ ] the artefact size is within budget
[ ] licence file present, third-party attribution present
[ ] no secrets, no .env, no credentials, no debug endpoints
[ ] no node_modules, no test fixtures, no editor directories
[ ] CI only. No laptop, ever.

// BUILD
[ ] a fixed toolchain version, recorded
[ ] a committed lockfile
[ ] no network access during the build
[ ] deterministic where the ecosystem supports it, verified by
    building twice and comparing hashes

// VERIFY BEFORE PUSHING
[ ] install the artefact into a clean project in every supported
    language or shell, and import it
[ ] for a library: a consumer can install it with the documented
    command and use the smallest example
[ ] for a binary: run --version on every platform you publish
[ ] for a container: pull it and run the health check
[ ] for a marketplace submission: the review criteria, checked
    yourself, line by line

// PUBLISH
[ ] the private registry first, and install tests against it
[ ] each registry verified as idempotent: query before publish
[ ] provenance or attestation enabled
[ ] short-lived credentials (OIDC where available)
[ ] tags signed, releases cut from the tag

// AFTER
[ ] install the published version from the public registry, from a
    clean cache
[ ] tag the repository release and generate notes
[ ] update the docs: the version, the install command, the changelog
[ ] tell the people who asked for it. A release nobody hears about
    does not get adopted, however good it is
[ ] watch the first errors from real users for a day

// THE TWO THINGS PEOPLE SKIP, both of which have caused incidents
[ ] install the PUBLISHED artefact, not your build output. They
    differ more often than you would think
[ ] verify from a CLEAN cache, because a cached tarball will hide a
    broken publish entirely</code></pre></div>
    <div class="ex"><h4>Exercises 10</h4><ol>
      <li>Build a Go or Rust CLI for four platforms, write a <code>latest.json</code>, publish the assets to GitHub Releases with a signed checksum file, and write a one-line installer that verifies before extracting.</li>
      <li>Implement self-update in your own CLI with notify-only, then add the full replace path. Test that a failed signature leaves the original binary untouched, and that the rename is atomic.</li>
      <li>Build a <code>.deb</code> and an <code>.rpm</code> for the same tool, sign the repository, and verify they install and uninstall cleanly. Note every piece of metadata you had to guess.</li>
      <li>Add a version-check endpoint to a tool that notifies but never auto-updates. Measure how many users are on which version after two weeks, and decide whether the operational cost justifies it.</li>
      <li>Publish one package to two registries, then simulate a partial failure: succeed on the first, fail on the second, re-run. Verify the re-run is a no-op for the first and completes the second.</li>
    </ol></div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
<h2 id="ch11" data-toc>11 Â· Deprecation, Yanking and Sustainable Maintenance</h2>
    <div class="ch-toc"><button type="button" class="ch-toc-head" aria-expanded="true"><span class="arrow">â–¸</span>Contents &middot; 11 Â· Deprecation, Yanking and Sustainable Maintenance</button><div class="ch-toc-list"><a href="#ch11-lifecycle">The maintenance lifecycle</a><a href="#ch11-deprecate">Deprecating a feature</a><a href="#ch11-unpublish">Unpublishing, yanking and retracting</a><a href="#ch11-per-ecosystem">What each ecosystem lets you do</a><a href="#ch11-security">A vulnerability disclosure process</a><a href="#ch11-handover">Handing a package over</a><a href="#ch11-funding">Funding and sustaining maintenance</a><a href="#ch11-goal">The operational playbook</a></div></div>
    <p class="topic-desc">Publishing is the easy part. This chapter is about what happens in year three, when you have 4,000 dependents, a maintainer who has left, and a request to pull a version for a CVE.</p>
    <h3 id="ch11-lifecycle" data-toc>The maintenance lifecycle</h3><h4 id="ch11-lifecycle-bus-factor">The bus factor</h4><p>how many people could publish a release next week? one -> a bus factor of one. write it in the README, and fix it by adding a second maintainer. zero -> this is more common than anyone admits. an orphan package, permanently.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE BUS FACTOR, measured honestly<h4 id="ch11-lifecycle-stages">The four stages</h4><p>ACTIVE: adding features, fixing bugs, reviewing PRs, answering issues. MAINTENANCE: security fixes and dependency bumps only. SECURITY ONLY: critical fixes only, on a best-effort basis, explicitly time-boxed. END OF LIFE: frozen. the last release is the last release.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE STAGES, and what each one costs
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE STAGES, and what each one costs
   ACTIVE           adding features, fixing bugs, reviewing PRs,
                    answering issues. the expensive phase, and the one
                    that ends when the maintainer loses interest.
   MAINTENANCE      security fixes and dependency bumps only. no new
                    features. this is where a project should sit for
                    most of its life, and it is far cheaper than
                    ACTIVE.
   SECURITY ONLY    critical fixes only, on a best-effort basis.
                    explicitly time-boxed: "supported until the next
                    release".
   END OF LIFE      frozen. the last release is the last release. say
                    so, in the README, with a date.

// WHY PROJECTS GET INTO TROUBLE
//   A package reaches 10,000 dependents and its maintainer stops
//   answering issues. The dependents cannot tell the difference between
//   "no issues" and "nobody is answering". There is no registry
//   mechanism for this. The only fix is declaring it, and almost nobody
//   does.
//   The second way: a company publishes an internal library publicly,
//   it becomes popular, an employee leaves and stops doing the
//   releases, and 400 companies now depend on something nobody owns.

// THE SIGNAL TO GIVE YOUR CONSUMERS
//   a maintenance status in the README, in the package description,
//   and ideally in the repository as a status badge
   Status: maintenance only
   Security fixes: until 31 March 2027
   See SECURITY.md for the disclosure process.
//   This costs five minutes. It is the difference between users finding
//   out and users deciding.

// THE BUS FACTOR, measured honestly
   how many people could publish a release next week?
   one  -> a bus factor of one. write it in the README, and fix it
           by adding a second maintainer with registry access NOW,
//           while you can
   zero -> this is more common than anyone admits. A repository with
           one maintainer who lost access to their 2FA device is an
//   orphan package, permanently. That has happened to very widely used
//   packages.
//   The mitigation is boring: two accounts on every registry, hardware
//   keys recorded in a company password manager, not a person's
//   password manager.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch11-deprecate" data-toc>Deprecating a feature</h3><h4 id="ch11-deprecate-migration">The migration guide</h4><p>## Migrating from renew() to refresh(). They look the same, but: refresh() retries on a 401 once, renew() did not. refresh() rotates the refresh token in place, renew() returned a new one and left the old valid for 30 minutes. refresh() rejects a token expiring within 60 seconds, renew() accepted it and failed later.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE RUNTIME WARNING, and where to put it<h4 id="ch11-deprecate-timeline">The timeline</h4><p>MINOR release: mark it deprecated. emit a runtime warning. MINOR + 1: document the replacement, with a migration guide. one major cycle: remove it. Do not skip this step.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE TIMELINE, and the parts people skip
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE TIMELINE, and the parts people skip
   MINOR release     mark it deprecated. emit a runtime warning.
   MINOR + 1          document the replacement, with a migration guide.
   one major cycle    remove it. Do not skip this step.

// THE WARNING, and it has to be actionable
   BAD
     @deprecated
     "deprecated"
     console.warn("use the new API")
//   useless. people suppress warnings, and they cannot act on this.

   GOOD
/** @deprecated since 2.1.0, removed in 3.0.0. Use `refresh(token)`.
 *  It handles rotation and retries; this does not. Same signature. */
     function renew(token: string): Promise&lt;Token&gt; {
       warn('renew() is deprecated since 2.1.0 and removed in 3.0.0. '
          + 'Use refresh(token) instead: same signature, and it handles '
          + 'rotation. Migration: https://example.com/docs/m/renew');
       return legacyRenew(token);
     }
//   names the replacement, the version, the removal version, and a URL.
//   this is a migration instruction, not a complaint.

// THE RUNTIME WARNING, and where to put it
   - once per process, not per call. A warning per call turns a
     deprecation into a log-flooding denial of service.
//   - at the boundary, not deep in the implementation. The person who
//     needs to fix it is the caller.
//   - make it suppressible, and document the mechanism:
//       MYLIB_NO_DEPRECATION_WARNINGS=1
//   - include the call stack? Some libraries do. It is genuinely useful
//     and it is noisy. A short stack, or a "set this to get a stack"
//     flag, is the compromise.

// THE MIGRATION GUIDE, and what goes in it
   ## Migrating from renew() to refresh()
   They look the same, but:
   - refresh() retries on a 401 once, renew() did not
   - refresh() rotates the refresh token in place, renew() returned a
     new one and left the old valid for 30 minutes
   - refresh() rejects a token expiring within 60 seconds, renew()
     accepted it and failed later. If you depended on that, you need
     an explicit check.
   ```diff
   - const t = await renew(token);
   + const t = await refresh(token);
   ```
   Runtime warning, or the type error if your consumers are typed,
   tells them where. Say both:
//   the honest migration guide names the behaviour differences, not
//   just the rename. A guide that only shows the rename will generate
//   the bug reports.

// THE REMOVAL, and how to know it is safe
   Deprecating in 2.1 and removing in 3.0 is a promise, and keeping it
   matters more than being right the first time.
//   Before removing, check for real usage:
   - a registry-side usage signal, if there is one (npm downloads by
     package, PyPI downloads). Coarse, but it is a signal.
//   - a CHANGELOG that says "removed in 3.0" for a full major cycle
//   - a GitHub issue asking "who is using X?" with the answers
   - for a widely-used package, an opt-in telemetry list, which
     requires asking first and is worth the trouble
//   If you cannot tell, do not remove. Ship 3.0 with it still there and
//   deprecated. Carrying a deprecated function costs nothing; breaking
//   4,000 builds costs you trust.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch11-unpublish" data-toc>Unpublishing, yanking and retracting</h3><h4 id="ch11-unpublish-limits">Limits per ecosystem</h4><p>npm: 72h, then impossible. PyPI: never, for an existing release. Go: no such operation at all. REACH: nothing. Everyone who already installed it keeps it.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THREE DIFFERENT OPERATIONS, and the names differ per registry<h4 id="ch11-unpublish-operations">Three different operations</h4><p>UNPUBLISH: remove the version. disappears from resolution. YANK: keep it resolvable, mark it as "do not select this". DEPRECATE: keep it, warn on install.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THREE DIFFERENT OPERATIONS, and the names differ per registry
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THREE DIFFERENT OPERATIONS, and the names differ per registry

   UNPUBLISH    remove the version. disappears from resolution.
//               npm: 72 hours, then impossible. PyPI: never, for an
//               existing release. Go: no such operation at all.
//               REACH: nothing. Everyone who already installed it
//               keeps it. This is not a recall.
//   YANK         keep it resolvable, mark it as "do not select this".
//               Existing lockfiles keep working; new resolution skips
//               it. Cargo does this natively and it is the best model.
//   DEPRECATE    keep it, warn on install. npm, RubyGems, Composer.
//               The gentlest option.

// WHEN TO USE WHICH

   YANK when: a broken release, a security vulnerability, a build that
             should never have been published.
//   Nothing in your lockfile breaks. New installs get the good version.
//   THIS IS THE DEFAULT FOR "I published something bad".

   DEPRECATE when: the release works but you want people off it. A
             performance problem, a dependency that is now known to be
             compromised, a version with a subtle correctness bug.

   UNPUBLISH only when: you published something you should never have,
             such as a secret. And even then, understand the limits:

   IF A SECRET WAS PUBLISHED
   1. revoke it first. Rotation is the only action that works. A
      published artefact is in caches, mirrors, CI logs and every
      machine that installed it.
   2. then unpublish, so nobody installs it fresh.
   3. check every registry's CDN cache. npm's unpkg, jsdelivr and
      similar will serve the file from cache long after the registry
      removed it. There is an abuse-report process; use it.
   4. assume it leaked. Anything in a package registry was fetched by
      automated scanners within minutes.
//   Do not wait to revoke until you have planned the unpublish. The
//   revocation is the thing that protects anyone using it.

// THE WORST-CASE TIMELINE, from a real incident, to calibrate urgency
   T+0        a maintainer publishes a compromised version
   T+9min     an automated scanner has found it and flagged it
   T+40min    the registry has yanked it
   T+2h       the maintainers have written a postmortem
//   Everything from the registry yank onwards is communication.
//   Rotation and the advisory are what actually protect people, and
//   both are yours to do. The registry is a backstop, not a plan.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch11-per-ecosystem" data-toc>What each ecosystem lets you do</h3><h4 id="ch11-per-ecosystem-workaround">The workaround for immutable ecosystems</h4><p>1. publish a fixed version immediately. 2. publish an advisory with the affected and fixed ranges. 3. if the bad version is truly unusable, publish a same-numbered version that is a placeholder which throws on import with a message pointing at the fix.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE WORKAROUND FOR A BAD VERSION IN AN IMMUTABLE ECOSYSTEM<h4 id="ch11-per-ecosystem-table">The removal table</h4><p>This table is the reason to check before you need it. The ones with no removal mechanism are a design decision, not an oversight. PyPI's reasoning is explicit and correct: a package manager that can silently make an existing requirement unresolvable is a denial-of-service vector against every project using it.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THIS TABLE IS THE REASON TO CHECK BEFORE YOU NEED IT
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THIS TABLE IS THE REASON TO CHECK BEFORE YOU NEED IT
//          unpublish    deprecate   yank/retract   limit
// npm       72h, owner   yes        no             no
// PyPI      never        no         no             no
// Cargo     never        soft       YES, native    no
// RubyGems  3 versions  yes        no             no
// Composer  never        yes        no             no
// NuGet     never        yes        no             no
// Maven     never        deprecate  no             no
// Go        never        comment    YES, go.mod    no
// Hex       never        no         no             no
// pub.dev   never        no         no             no
// Packagist never        no         no             no

// THE ONES WITH NO REMOVAL MECHANISM ARE A DESIGN DECISION, not an
// oversight. PyPI's reasoning is explicit and correct: a package
// manager that can silently make an existing requirement unresolvable
// is a denial-of-service vector against every project using it. Once
// your build depends on 1.2.3, it must always resolve. So the answer
// to a bad release is a new version, forever.
// This is why the security process in the next section matters more
// than the unpublish button: in most ecosystems you cannot remove the
// artefact, so you can only tell people not to use it.

// THE WORKAROUND FOR A BAD VERSION IN AN IMMUTABLE ECOSYSTEM
   1. publish a fixed version immediately
   2. publish an advisory with the affected and fixed ranges
   3. if the bad version is truly unusable, publish a same-numbered
      version that is a placeholder which throws on import with a
      message pointing at the fix. Some projects do this. It is
      hostile and it breaks builds, so it is a last resort.
//   4. for npm and Maven, unpublish within the window if the version
//      is recent enough
//   5. be loud: GitHub Security Advisory, the project's issue tracker,
//      a mailing list, and the registry's own advisory channel</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch11-security" data-toc>A vulnerability disclosure process</h3><h4 id="ch11-security-process">The process</h4><p>day 0 acknowledge. day 1-3 triage. day 3-7 decide the fix. day 7-14 ship the fix. day 14 publish: advisory, changelog entry, release notes. If the report is already public, move the disclosure date up immediately.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// TWO FILES, AND NEITHER IS OPTIONAL<h4 id="ch11-security-files">SECURITY.md files</h4><p>Two files, and neither is optional: SECURITY.md at the repository root, and the advisory process. The supported versions table, reporting a vulnerability, the version range, and the identifiers (GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx, CVE-2026-12345).</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// TWO FILES, AND NEITHER IS OPTIONAL
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// TWO FILES, AND NEITHER IS OPTIONAL

// SECURITY.md, at the repository root
# Security Policy

## Supported versions
| Version | Supported |
| ------- | --------- |
| 1.4.x   | yes       |
| 1.3.x   | security fixes only |
| &lt; 1.3   | no        |

## Reporting a vulnerability
Email security@example.com. Please do not open a public issue.

Include: the affected version, what you observed, how to reproduce it,
and the impact you believe it has. Encryption key on request.

We aim to acknowledge within 3 working days and to have a fix or a
mitigation plan within 14 days. We will tell you when the advisory is
published, and we will credit you unless you prefer otherwise.

// Why the table matters
//   Without it, a researcher reports a CVE against 1.0.0 and you have
//   spent a day discovering you do not support 1.0.0. With it, that
//   conversation takes a minute and starts in the right place.
//   The table is also the thing that makes a "security fixes only"
//   line honest and enforceable.

// WHAT THE PROCESS ACTUALLY LOOKS LIKE, in order
   day 0        acknowledge. This is the number one thing that
                determines whether a report escalates publicly.
   day 1-3      triage: is it real, what is the impact, what is the
                blast radius, is it already public
   day 3-7      decide the fix and the disclosure timing
   day 7-14     ship the fix on the current line, and on the previous
                major if the previous major is still supported
   day 14       publish: advisory, changelog entry, release notes, a
                post if it was serious
//   If the report is already public when you receive it, say so
//   immediately and move the disclosure date up. Do not sit on it.

// THE ADVISORY, and the identifiers
   GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx   GitHub Advisory. Free, and it works with
                         `npm audit` and Dependabot. Use it for
                         anything on GitHub, whatever the language.
   CVE-2026-12345        from MITRE, via a CNA. Some ecosystems'
                         tooling keys off the CVE rather than the
                         GHSA. Some registries (PyPI, crates.io,
                         RubyGems) have their own databases you must
                         file with directly as well:
//                           PyPI     the Python Security Response Team
//                           crates   RustSec / the advisory-db
//                           RubyGems ruby-advisory-db
//                           Maven    the OSSRH / Central process
//   The identifiers must be consistent across every channel. A
//   mismatched version range between your advisory and the registry's
//   is a common and avoidable problem.

// THE VERSION RANGE, and getting it right
   "affects &gt;= 1.0.0, &lt; 1.4.2, fixed in 1.4.2"
//   not "all versions before the latest". Precise ranges let scanners
//   actually protect people, and an imprecise range generates noise
//   that trains people to ignore advisories.
//   If you cannot determine the lower bound, say so explicitly rather
//   than guessing low, because a range that is too wide causes every
//   user of the package to get an alert they do not need.

// THE THINGS THAT MAKE A GOOD REPORT GET TAKEN SERIOUSLY
   - a maintainer who acknowledges within a day
   - an advisory with a precise version range
   - a CVE where one is warranted
   - a credit line, if the reporter wanted one
   - a postmortem for anything serious
//   All four are public. They are the difference between a maintainer
//   people report to and one they publish about.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch11-handover" data-toc>Handing a package over</h3><h4 id="ch11-handover-when">When the maintainer is gone</h4><p>- ask the registry. npm, PyPI and RubyGems all have an abandoned-package process. - ask the organisation. If a company published it, the company owns it. - fork it. The new maintainer can publish under a new name.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WHEN THE MAINTAINER IS GONE AND NOT COMING BACK<h4 id="ch11-handover-checklist">Handover checklist</h4><p>1. Every registry account has a second person on it, with their OWN hardware key. 2. The repository has CODEOWNERS. 3. CI can publish. 4. The signing keys are in a shared, backed-up store. 5. The domain is registered to the organisation. 6. The security contact in SECURITY.md is a role, not a person. 7. A MAINTAINERS.md naming who does what.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// TRANSFER, and the registry mechanics
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// TRANSFER, and the registry mechanics
   npm      npm owner add NEWUSER PACKAGE
           npm owner rm OLDUSER PACKAGE
   PyPI     transfer the project to an organisation, then add a role
           to the organisation. A personal-account project cannot be
           transferred to an individual; the organisation route is
           the only one, so create the org first.
   Cargo    the crate is owned by a GitHub org or a verified domain.
           Transfer the repository to the new org: ownership follows.
   RubyGems gem owner yourgem --add newowner
           gem owner yourgem --remove oldowner
   NuGet    transfer the package to an organisation in the portal
   Composer transfer a Packagist package to another vendor namespace
   Docker   namespace ownership, via the Hub UI
//   Do this the moment a second maintainer appears, not when the first
//   one leaves. The gap is when nothing happens.

// A HANDOVER CHECKLIST
   1. Every registry account has a second person on it, with their
      OWN hardware key. Not a shared credential.
   2. The repository has CODEOWNERS, so an absent person does not
      silently become a bottleneck.
   3. CI can publish, so no release depends on one laptop.
   4. The signing keys are in a shared, backed-up store. Two offline
      copies, tested once a year.
   5. The domain is registered to the organisation, with the
      registrant details current, and the auto-renew is on.
   6. The security contact in SECURITY.md is a role, not a person.
   7. A `MAINTAINERS.md` naming who does what, so a new maintainer
      knows what they inherited.
//   5 is the one that is forgotten and the one that kills projects: a
//   lapsed domain for the security contact, or for the repository
//   itself, means nobody can be reached.

// WHEN THE MAINTAINER IS GONE AND NOT COMING BACK
   This happens, and the recovery path is narrower than people hope.
//   - ask the registry. npm, PyPI and RubyGems all have an abandoned-
//     package process for a maintainer who is unresponsive. It
//     requires evidence and takes weeks to months.
//   - ask the organisation. If a company published it, the company
//     owns it. If an individual did, nobody is obliged to take it.
//   - fork it. The new maintainer can publish under a new name and
//     the old one stays where it is. Everyone has to switch, which is
//     the cost. This is what happened to several very widely used
//     packages and it worked.
//   - the one thing not to do: publish under the same name from a
//     different account without the owner's cooperation. That is
//     namespace squatting, registries have been known to yank for it,
//     and it poisons a package you might otherwise have inherited.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch11-funding" data-toc>Funding and sustaining maintenance</h3><h4 id="ch11-funding-commercial">Commercial tier</h4><p>+ money that arrives because a customer got value. + it funds the work, not the goodwill, so it survives a change of heart. - it splits the codebase, and the split must be maintained forever. - best practice: an open core split on a CAPABILITY, not on scale.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE HONEST ECONOMICS<h4 id="ch11-funding-sponsorship">Sponsorship (GitHub Sponsors, Open Collective, Polar)</h4><p>+ zero effort, no product decision, no obligation. + good signal: a maintainer with 40 sponsors has time to review PRs. - irregular, and it stops the moment the visible work stops. - best for a maintainer with a following, not for a critical infrastructure dependency.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE HONEST ECONOMICS
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// THE HONEST ECONOMICS
   A dependency with 1,000,000 weekly downloads is used by tens of
   thousands of companies. Most of them contribute nothing, and that
   is a rational decision for every one of them.
//   Uncompensated maintenance is the norm, and it works until the one
//   person whose job it is leaves. Then it stops working, and the
//   whole ecosystem discovers it simultaneously.
//   This is a market failure, not a personal failing, and the
//   responses below are the practical ones.

// THE FUNDING OPTIONS, honestly assessed

// SPONSORSHIP (GitHub Sponsors, Open Collective, Polar)
   + zero effort, no product decision, no obligation
   + good signal: a maintainer with 40 sponsors has time to review PRs
   - irregular, and it stops the moment the visible work stops
   - best for a maintainer with a following, not for a critical
     infrastructure dependency

// OPEN COLLECTIVE, for governance as well as money
   + a real budget, transparent, with the power to vote on priorities
   + companies will fund this for governance reasons: a vote in the
     roadmap is a purchase
   - administrative overhead, and the monthly reports are real work
   - the right tool once there are 3+ companies depending on you

// A COMMERCIAL TIER, which is the most reliable funding there is
   + money that arrives because a customer got value
   + it funds the work, not the goodwill, so it survives a change of
     heart
   - it splits the codebase, and the split must be maintained forever
   - it changes who the maintainer answers to, which is a real and
     sometimes uncomfortable shift
   - best practice: an open core split on a CAPABILITY, not on scale.
//     OpenTelemetry Collector, Sentry, Grafana, GitLab.

// A SUPPORT CONTRACT
   + predictable revenue, and it is honestly what you are selling
   + SLA obligations, which turn "I will get to it" into a commitment
   - only works with an enterprise audience, so it is useless for a
     library used mostly by individuals

// TIPPING, WHICH DOES NOT WORK
   - very low yield, unpredictable, and it creates an expectation of
     responsiveness you cannot meet
   - fine as an addition to a funded model, poor as the model

// THE PRACTICAL PATH
   1. Start with sponsorship. It costs nothing and it takes a week.
   2. Put funding links in the README, the package page, and the
      issue template. Most maintainers do this badly, and the people
      who would fund never see the ask.
   3. When one company depends on you enough to have an outage
      problem, start a conversation about funding BEFORE the outage.
   4. Move to Open Collective when you have money to administer, not
      when you need it. It will not raise money for a project with no
      budget history.
   5. Build the commercial tier only when you cannot keep up on
      goodwill. It is the most reliable money and the most expensive
//      in engineering complexity.

// THE TENSION NOBODY TALKS ABOUT
   Funding follows visibility. A critical internal dependency used by
   200,000 companies but with no stars, no conference talks and no
   social presence raises nothing. If your package is invisible and
   load-bearing, the fix is partly documentation, partly naming the
   companies using it in the README with permission, and partly just
   asking them directly. A single email to one engineering manager
   has a better hit rate than any amount of documentation.</code></pre></div>
    <h3 id="ch11-goal" data-toc>The operational playbook</h3><h4 id="ch11-goal-weekly">Weekly</h4><p>[ ] open issues triaged: label, acknowledge, or close. [ ] new PRs reviewed, or told why they will be later. [ ] anything security-shaped triaged today, not in the backlog.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// WEEKLY<h4 id="ch11-goal-daily">Daily</h4><p>[ ] dependency update PRs triaged. [ ] CI green on the default branch.</p><div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// DAILY
    <div class="codeWrap"><pre><code>// DAILY
[ ] dependency update PRs triaged
[ ] CI green on the default branch

// WEEKLY
[ ] open issues triaged: label, acknowledge, or close
[ ] new PRs reviewed, or told why they will be later
[ ] anything security-shaped triaged today, not in the backlog

// MONTHLY
[ ] release, or a note explaining why not
[ ] download/usage numbers checked for a sudden drop. A 40% drop
    after a release is a bug report you have not received yet
[ ] dependents scanning for newly published advisories against your
    own packages

// QUARTERLY
[ ] audit registry accounts: who has access, who should not
[ ] audit CI secrets, and rotate anything older than a year
[ ] review the two-maintainer rule: does it still hold
[ ] check the domain renewal and the certificate expiry
[ ] read one dependency's changelog for something that should affect
    you

// ON A SECURITY REPORT
[ ] acknowledge within one working day
[ ] triage within three: real or not, impact, blast radius, is it
    already public
[ ] fix on the current line and any still-supported major
[ ] advisory with a PRECISE version range, a GHSA, and a CVE if
    warranted
[ ] file with the ecosystem's own database if there is one
[ ] publish, and credit the reporter unless they opted out
[ ] postmortem if the report was serious, and link it from the
    advisory

// THE DASHBOARD THAT ACTUALLY MATTERS
   weekly downloads        a falling number is the first sign of
                          abandonment, and it appears months before
                          the abandoner tells you
   open issues and their age   the truest measure of maintenance
   time to first response      matters more than time to fix, because
                             it is the thing a reporter experiences
   dependents using the
   deprecated surface          this is the number that decides whether
                             a removal is safe
   bus factor                 count it, and make it 2</code></pre></div>
    <div class="ex"><h4>Exercises 11</h4><ol>
      <li>Depprecate a function in your own package with a runtime warning that names the replacement, the version and a migration URL. Verify it fires once per process rather than per call, and that it can be suppressed.</li>
      <li>Publish a same-numbered fix over a broken release in two ecosystems â€” one where you can unpublish and one where you cannot â€” and document what users experience in each case.</li>
      <li>Write <code>SECURITY.md</code> for one of your packages, with a supported-versions table. Then measure how long it takes you to produce an advisory with a precise version range for a hypothetical issue.</li>
      <li>Audit your registry accounts across every ecosystem you have ever published to. Produce a list of accounts, owners and removal dates. Remove anyone who has left.</li>
      <li>Add funding links to a README, a package page and an issue template, then track inbound funding for a month. Compare with the effort. Decide whether to keep it.</li>
    </ol></div>
  </section>
</main>

